window.COURSE = {"course":"CS313 — Theory of Computation","student":"","brandcfg":{"key":"cs313.v1","brand":"Automaton","code":"CS313","sub":"CS313 · Theory of Computation<br>study traveler","eyebrow":"State register · 2026/27 Fall · Dr. K. Oladejo","hero":"CS313 Study Traveler","tagline":"Every chapter is a state. Read it, quiz it, reach the accepting state.","testHint":"In-Class Quiz 1: Mon 26.10, 3rd hour, 11:00 sharp. Drill the ★ Quiz 1 Prep chapter and chapter 04 (Lecture 4: NFAs, NFA→DFA, new this year)."},"deadlines":[{"date":"2026-10-26","label":"In-Class Quiz 1 (10%) — 3rd hour, starts 11:00 sharp · DFA/NFA, formal definitions, NFA→DFA (★ Quiz 1 Prep chapter)","pts":10},{"date":"2026-11-09","label":"In-Tutorial Quiz 1 (10%) — week 7 · regex + pumping lemma (b2)","pts":10},{"date":"2026-11-16","label":"Midterm Exam (25%) — date TBA, estimate wk 8","pts":25},{"date":"2026-12-14","label":"In-Class Quiz 2 (10%) — week 12 · decidability (b5)","pts":10},{"date":"2026-12-28","label":"In-Tutorial Quiz 2 (10%) — week 14 · complexity (b6)","pts":10},{"date":"2027-01-18","label":"Final Exam (35%) — date TBA, estimate wk 17 · b6 has the revision map","pts":35}],"chapters":[{"id":"00","title":"Course Map & Grading","slides":"Lecture 1, slides 1–4","md":"# CS313 — Theory of Computation (Course Map)\n\n**Professor:** Dr. Kazeem Oladejo — IUS, 2026/27 Fall\n**Textbooks:** Maheshwari & Smid, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation* (Carleton, 2024 — free online); Sipser, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*, 3rd ed. (2012, the classic).\n\n## Grading (100%) — what an A requires\n\n| Component | Weight | When |\n|-----------|--------|------|\n| In-Class Quiz 1 | 10% | **Monday 26.10.2026, 3rd hour, starts promptly at 11:00** — arrive early (prof. email 25.10). Covers DFAs and NFAs: prep sheet solved → `study/quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md`; Lecture 4 → `04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md`; NFA→DFA conversion is new this year |\n| In-Tutorial Quiz 1 | 10% | Week 7 (~9–13 Nov 2026). **2 questions announced: (1) Regular Expressions, (2) Pumping Lemma** → `study/quizzes/TQ1-prep.md` (tutorial sheet solved, 2 sheet errors corrected, 12 pumping proofs, mock quiz) |\n| Midterm Exam | 25% | **Week 8** (Lecture 5 slide 3; exact date TBA) |\n| In-Class Quiz 2 | 10% | Week 12 (~14–18 Dec 2026) |\n| In-Tutorial Quiz 2 | 10% | Week 14 (~28 Dec–1 Jan) |\n| Final Exam | 35% | TBA (likely week 17) |\n\n**Key structural fact: 40% of the grade is quizzes**, spread across four checkpoints — this course punishes cramming and rewards steady weekly review far more than ENS208 does. No project or homework component was announced in Lecture 1.\n\n## Course questions (the professor's framing, slide 5)\n\n1. What can and what cannot be computed?\n2. What is an algorithm?\n3. When should an algorithm be considered practically feasible?\n\n## Announced roadmap\n\nLecture 1 slide 37 promised: set theory (cont.) → proof techniques → **deterministic finite machines**. Lecture 2 delivered the middle two. Expected arc of the course: sets/proofs → DFA/NFA/regular languages → context-free grammars → Turing machines → decidability → complexity.\n\n## Lectures covered so far\n\n| Week | Lecture | Topics | Digest |\n|------|---------|--------|--------|\n| 1 | Lecture 1 (38 slides) | Course intro; sets, operations, power set, Cartesian product | `01-sets-and-operations.md` |\n| 2 | Lecture 2 (60 slides) | Relations, graphs of relations, equivalence, countability, functions | `02-relations-and-functions.md` |\n| 2 | Lecture 2 (cont.) | Seven proof techniques; set theory laws; De Morgan proved | `03-proof-techniques.md` |\n| 3 | Lecture 3 | DFAs (5-tuple, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure) — ⚠️ **deck not received yet**; Tutorial 3 stands in for it | `quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md` (Tutorial 3 part) |\n| 4 | Lecture 4 (44 slides) | NFAs: nondeterminism, formal definition, acceptance, ε-closure, DFA–NFA equivalence, subset construction (worked example with pruning) | `04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md` + `lessons/04-lesson.md` |\n| 5 | Lecture 5 (43 slides, given 26.10 before Quiz 1) | Regular operations, closure under ∪/∘/\\* (NFA constructions), regular expressions (definition, 16 identities), regex ⟺ regular, regex → NFA for (ab ∪ a)\\*, closure under complement and ∩ | `05-regular-expressions.md` + `lessons/05-lesson.md` · tutorial `tutorials/Tutorial5/Tutorial_RegEx.pdf` → `quizzes/TQ1-prep.md` |\n\n**Status 25.10.2026:** Lecture 4 ingested (all 44 slides verified by code; ten text errata, no errors in the worked examples). **Lecture 3's deck is still missing** — when it arrives, ingest it as the next free chapter number (05) and keep its site row next to 04. Next in the arc: regular expressions and the pumping lemma (In-Tutorial Quiz 1, week 7) — pre-read `book/B2` §2.7–2.9 and `book-sipser/S1` §1.3–1.4.\n\n**Status 02.11.2026:** Lecture 5 + Tutorial_RegEx ingested (all 43 slides read from images; every regex, identity and the (ab ∪ a)\\* NFA verified by brute force; only cosmetic slide errata). The pumping lemma is NOT in Lecture 5 — the prep uses M&S §2.9. Lecture 3's deck is still missing; if it arrives, give it chapter id `03b` (05 is now Lecture 5).\n\n⚠️ **Lecture 2 contains an unusual number of slide errors** — about a dozen, listed inline in chapters 02 and 03 and collected in `exam-prep.md`. Several are in material that will be examined (the codomain exercise, the induction labelling, the irrational-powers proof). Study from the digests, not from the raw slides.\n\n## Materials\n\n- `CS313 TComp - Lecture 1.pptx` — week 1 lecture (38 slides): course intro + set theory. Converted to PDF for slide rendering.\n- `CS313 TComp - Lecture 2.pptx` — week 2 lecture (60 slides): relations and functions, then proof techniques. Converted with LibreOffice.\n- `CS313 TComp - Lecture 4.pptx` — week 4 lecture (44 slides): nondeterministic finite automata. Received 25.10.2026 as \"last lecture material for the previous week\"; converted with LibreOffice to `CS313 TComp - Lecture 4.pdf`; all 44 pages render on the site (prefix L4s).\n- `CS313 TComp - Lecture 5.pptx` — week 5 lecture (43 slides): regular expressions and regular languages. Received 02.11.2026 (Downloads name `CS313 TComp - Lecture5.pptx`); converted with LibreOffice to `CS313 TComp - Lecture 5.pdf`; all 43 pages render on the site (prefix L5s).\n- `tutorials/Tutorial5/Tutorial_RegEx.pdf` — 4-page scanned tutorial sheet (exercises from Rich's book with answers). Two model answers are wrong (FSM M: \"no ba\" → no **ab**; 3b: ends in **ba**); corrected in `quizzes/TQ1-prep.md`. Pages render on the site (prefix T5p).\n- **Lecture 3 deck: missing.**\n- ⚠️ The Sipser PDF is no longer in this folder; its page images survive in `website/slides/`, and `build_site.py` reuses them rather than failing. Drop the PDF back in if pages ever need re-rendering.\n- Sipser book: the standard reference; Maheshwari & Smid is freely available as PDF from Carleton — worth downloading.\n\n## Class rules (slide 2)\n\nPhones silent, no food/drink, no disruption (IUS rulebook sanctions), questions welcomed.\n","lesson":"","cards":[],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/L1s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/L1s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/L1s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/L1s004.jpg"}]},{"id":"01","title":"Sets & Operations on Sets","slides":"Lecture 1, slides 5–38","md":"# 01 — Sets and Operations on Sets (Lecture 1, slides 5–36)\n\n_Week 1. This is the language layer for the whole course — DFAs, grammars, and Turing machines are all defined as tuples of sets. Quiz 1 (week 5) material._\n\n## Why theory (slides 5–8)\n\n- Three driving questions of the course: what **can/cannot be computed**; what **is an algorithm**; when is an algorithm **practically feasible**.\n- A *theory* = well-substantiated, evidence-based, **testable/falsifiable**, predictive, dynamic (open to change) explanation.\n- Math here is **discrete** — finite sets and sequences, not real numbers and continuous variables. Motivation: ironclad guarantees (compilers must translate correctly; programs should terminate).\n- In a nutshell: ToC provides **abstract models of computation**; **discrete mathematics is its language**; discrete math deals with **set theory**.\n\n## Sets — basics (slides 9–12)\n\n- **Set** = collection of objects; order and repetition don't matter: {a, b, d} = {d, b, a}.\n- **Membership:** a ∈ A; c ∉ A. **Empty set:** {} or ∅.\n- Sets can contain sets: A = {a, ∅, {a, ∅}} has exactly **3 elements**: a, ∅, and {a, ∅}.\n- A = {(a, b), h}: the *pair* (a, b) ∈ A, but a ∉ A, b ∉ A, (b, a) ∉ A, ∅ ∉ A; h ∈ A.\n- **Set-builder notation:** B = {x ∈ A : P(x)} — the subset of A whose elements satisfy property P.\n  - Over natural numbers N: {n ∈ N : n < 0} = ∅; {n ∈ N : n < 3} = {0, 1, 2} (note: **0 ∈ N in this course**).\n\n## Subset, equality, disjointness (slides 15–18)\n\n| Relation | Definition |\n|----------|-----------|\n| Inclusion A ⊆ B | ∀a (a ∈ A → a ∈ B) |\n| Equality A = B | A ⊆ B **and** B ⊆ A (prove both directions!) |\n| Proper subset A ⊂ B | A ⊆ B and A ≠ B |\n| Disjoint | A ∩ B = ∅ (no common elements) |\n\n- Subset examples: A={2,3}, B={1,2,3,4}, C={1,3,4,5}: A ⊆ B; A ⊄ C (2 ∉ C); B ⊄ A.\n- **∅ is a subset of every set** — but not necessarily an *element* of it.\n- ⚠️ Slide 18 as written defines disjoint via \"{x : x ∉ A and x ∉ B}\" — that's a slide typo; correct meaning: A and B share no elements (¬∃x: x ∈ A ∧ x ∈ B).\n\n## Set operations (slides 19, 25)\n\n| Operation | Definition | Example |\n|-----------|-----------|---------|\n| Union A ∪ B | {x : x ∈ A **or** x ∈ B} | {1,3,9} ∪ {3,5,7} = {1,3,5,7,9} |\n| Intersection A ∩ B | {x : x ∈ A **and** x ∈ B} | {1,3,9} ∩ {3,5,7} = {3} |\n| Difference A − B | {x : x ∈ A and x ∉ B} | {1,3,9} − {3,5,7} = {1,9} |\n| Complement −A (or Aᶜ) | U − A, defined only relative to a universe U ⊇ A | x ∈ −A iff x ∉ A |\n\nBig union/intersection (slide 20, figure): ⋃ᵢ Aᵢ / ⋂ᵢ Aᵢ over an index set.\nPartition (slide 24, figure): splitting a set into non-empty, pairwise-disjoint subsets whose union is the whole set.\n\n## Power set (slides 21–23)\n\n- **P(A) or 2^A = {B : B ⊆ A}** — the set of ALL subsets, including ∅ and A itself.\n- |A| = n ⟹ |2^A| = **2ⁿ**.\n- 2^{c,d} = {∅, {c}, {d}, {c,d}}.\n- Worked: A = {1, a, 3} → 2^A = {∅, {1}, {a}, {3}, {1,a}, {1,3}, {a,3}, {1,a,3}} — 8 subsets.\n\n## Ordered pairs & Cartesian product (slides 26–30)\n\n- Ordered pair (a, b): **(a,b) = (c,d) iff a = c and b = d**. Order matters: (a,b) ≠ (b,a), while {a,b} = {b,a}.\n- Cartesian product A × B = all ordered pairs; Aⁿ = A × A × … × A.\n- |A| = m, |B| = k ⟹ |A × B| = m·k; |Aⁿ| = |A|ⁿ.\n- A = {3,1}: A² = {(3,3), (3,1), (1,3), (1,1)}.\n- A³ has 2³ = 8 triples: (3,3,3), (3,3,1), (3,1,3), **(3,1,1)**, (1,3,3), (1,3,1), (1,1,3), (1,1,1).\n  - ⚠️ Slide 29 lists (1,1,1) twice and omits (3,1,1) — professor's typo; the correct list is above.\n- {3,1,9} × {b,c,d} = {(3,b),(3,c),(3,d),(1,b),(1,c),(1,d),(9,b),(9,c),(9,d)} — 9 pairs.\n\n## Q&A slides (31–36, figures)\n\nSlide 33 answers: False / True / True. Slide 34 justification pattern: {∅,{1},{2},{1,2}} ∩ {1,2} = ∅ because the first set's elements are *sets* and the second's are *numbers* — no element is in both. This \"type of the elements\" argument is a recurring exam pattern.\n\n## Next class (slide 37)\n\nSet theory cont. → proof techniques → deterministic finite machines.\n","lesson":"# Lesson 01 — Set Theory, with the tricks\n\n_You're a senior software engineer, so I'll teach this through types: a set is an unordered collection with no duplicates (`Set<T>`), a tuple is ordered and typed (`(T, U)`). Nearly every exam trap in this chapter is a **type confusion** the professor plants deliberately._\n\n## The one skill this chapter actually tests\n\n**Element (∈) vs subset (⊆).** These are different relations, and mixing them is THE classic quiz kill:\n\n- x **∈** A asks: is x one of the objects in the box?\n- X **⊆** A asks: is every object in box X also in box A?\n\nTest yourself on A = {a, ∅, {a, ∅}}:\n- ∅ ∈ A? **Yes** — ∅ is literally listed inside.\n- ∅ ⊆ A? **Yes** — vacuously, always true for any set.\n- {a} ∈ A? **No** — {a} isn't listed. {a} ⊆ A? **Yes** — a ∈ A.\n- {a, ∅} ∈ A? **Yes** (it's the third element). {a, ∅} ⊆ A? **Yes** too (both a and ∅ are elements). Rare double-yes!\n\n⚠️ **Trick:** \"∅ is a subset of every set but not necessarily an element of it\" — the professor wrote this on slide 17 verbatim. He WILL test it. `B = {c, ∅}` has ∅ as BOTH element and subset.\n\n## Sets are `Set<T>`, pairs are tuples\n\n- {a,b} = {b,a} but (a,b) ≠ (b,a). Sets: unordered, no duplicates. Pairs: ordered, positional.\n- From A = {(a,b), h}: `a ∉ A`. The pair is one opaque object; its coordinates are not elements of A. Like a list containing a tuple — `a in [(a,b), h]` is False in Python.\n- ⚠️ **Trick (slide 34's Q&A pattern):** {∅,{1},{2},{1,2}} ∩ {1,2} = **∅**. Looks like they overlap, but the left set's elements are *sets* and the right's are *numbers*. Intersection compares elements, not \"content\". Type mismatch ⟹ empty intersection. Expect a variant of this on Quiz 1.\n\n## Power set = all subsets, count = 2ⁿ\n\nThink bitmask: each element is in or out → 2ⁿ subsets. |A|=3 ⟹ |2^A| = 8.\n⚠️ **Trick:** under exam stress everyone forgets **∅ and A itself** when listing 2^A. Count first (2ⁿ), then list, then verify the count.\nBonus trap: is ∅ ∈ 2^A? **Always yes** (∅ ⊆ A for any A). Is A ∈ 2^A? **Always yes**. So 2^A is never empty — |2^∅| = 1 = {∅}.\n\n## Cartesian products = nested loops\n\nA × B is the double loop `for a in A: for b in B: yield (a,b)` → sizes multiply: |A×B| = |A|·|B|, |Aⁿ| = |A|ⁿ.\n\n⚠️ **Professor typo #1 (slide 29):** his A³ list for A={3,1} contains **(1,1,1) twice and is missing (3,1,1)**. The correct 8: (3,3,3), (3,3,1), (3,1,3), (3,1,1), (1,3,3), (1,3,1), (1,1,3), (1,1,1). If a quiz answer looks like his slide, count for duplicates — enumerate systematically (first coordinate 3s, then 1s) like binary counting.\n\n⚠️ **Professor typo #2 (slide 18):** the \"disjoint\" line defines A ∩ B = ∅ with \"{x : x ∉ A and x ∉ B}\" — wrong as written (that's the complement of the union). Disjoint means: no x is in both. If asked to define disjoint, write **A ∩ B = ∅**, don't reproduce the slide's set-builder.\n\n## Definitions you must produce verbatim\n\n1. A ⊆ B ⟺ ∀a (a ∈ A → a ∈ B)\n2. A = B ⟺ A ⊆ B ∧ B ⊆ A ← this is also the *proof method* for set equality: two inclusion proofs\n3. A ⊂ B ⟺ A ⊆ B ∧ A ≠ B\n4. A ∪ B = {x : x ∈ A ∨ x ∈ B}; A ∩ B = {x : x ∈ A ∧ x ∈ B}; A − B = {x : x ∈ A ∧ x ∉ B}\n5. −A = U − A, **only defined relative to a universe U** ← \"what's the complement of {1,2}?\" is unanswerable without U; saying so is worth marks\n6. 2^A = {B : B ⊆ A}\n7. (a,b) = (c,d) ⟺ a = c ∧ b = d\n\n## Small print that costs points\n\n- **0 ∈ N in this course** (slide 12 used {0,1,2} for n<3). Some books start N at 1 — use the professor's convention.\n- Duplicates collapse: {a, a, b} = {a, b}; when you build a product or power set, dedupe before counting.\n- The − in A − B is *relative* complement: only removes what's actually in A; {1,3,9} − {3,5,7} = {1,9} (the 5 and 7 do nothing).\n\n## Course-strategy note\n\n40% of your grade is four quizzes (weeks 5, 7, 12, 14) — steady weekly drills beat cramming here. And Lecture 1's closing slide says **deterministic finite machines** are ~2 lectures away: set tuples like (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) will carry the rest of the semester, which is exactly why he's drilling ∈ vs ⊆ now.\n\n## Six flash-facts\n\n∈≠⊆ · ∅ ⊆ everything, ∈ only if listed · (a,b)≠(b,a) but {a,b}={b,a} · |2^A|=2ⁿ, never forget ∅ and A · |A×B|=|A||B| · set equality = two inclusion proofs.\n","cards":[{"q":"The three fundamental questions of Theory of Computation?","a":"What can/cannot be computed; what is an algorithm; when is an algorithm practically feasible."},{"q":"Why is the math of ToC \"discrete\"?","a":"It works with finite sets and sequences rather than real numbers/continuous variables — needed for ironclad guarantees about computation."},{"q":"How many elements does A = {a, ∅, {a, ∅}} have, and what are they?","a":"Three: a, ∅, and the set {a, ∅}."},{"q":"For A = {(a,b), h}: is a ∈ A? Is (b,a) ∈ A?","a":"No and no — only the pair (a,b) and h are elements; order matters in pairs."},{"q":"{n ∈ N : n < 0} = ? and {n ∈ N : n < 3} = ?","a":"∅ and {0, 1, 2} (0 counts as natural here)."},{"q":"Define A ⊆ B formally.","a":"∀a (a ∈ A → a ∈ B)."},{"q":"How do you prove A = B?","a":"Prove both inclusions: A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A."},{"q":"Proper subset A ⊂ B means…?","a":"A ⊆ B and A ≠ B."},{"q":"Is ∅ a subset of every set? Is it an element of every set?","a":"Subset: yes, always. Element: only if the set explicitly contains ∅."},{"q":"When are A and B disjoint?","a":"A ∩ B = ∅ — they share no elements."},{"q":"{1,3,9} ∪ {3,5,7}, ∩, and − ?","a":"∪ = {1,3,5,7,9}; ∩ = {3}; − = {1,9}."},{"q":"What is the complement −A and what does it require?","a":"−A = U − A; it is only defined relative to a universe U with A ⊆ U."},{"q":"Define the power set and its size.","a":"2^A = {B : B ⊆ A}, all subsets including ∅ and A; |2^A| = 2^|A|."},{"q":"Write out 2^{c,d}.","a":"{∅, {c}, {d}, {c,d}}."},{"q":"|2^A| for A = {1, a, 3}? List two subsets people forget.","a":"8; people forget ∅ and A itself."},{"q":"When are ordered pairs (a,b) and (c,d) equal?","a":"iff a = c and b = d."},{"q":"(a,b) vs {a,b} — the key difference?","a":"(a,b) ≠ (b,a) in general (order matters); {a,b} = {b,a} (sets are unordered)."},{"q":"|A × B| in terms of |A| and |B|? |Aⁿ|?","a":"|A|·|B|; |A|ⁿ."},{"q":"A = {3,1}: list A².","a":"{(3,3), (3,1), (1,3), (1,1)}."},{"q":"Why is {∅,{1},{2},{1,2}} ∩ {1,2} = ∅?","a":"The first set's elements are sets, the second's are numbers 1 and 2 — no object is an element of both, so the intersection is empty."}],"figs":[{"n":5,"src":"slides/L1s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/L1s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/L1s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/L1s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/L1s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/L1s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/L1s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/L1s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/L1s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/L1s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/L1s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/L1s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/L1s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/L1s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/L1s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/L1s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/L1s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/L1s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/L1s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/L1s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/L1s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/L1s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/L1s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/L1s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/L1s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/L1s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/L1s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/L1s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/L1s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/L1s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/L1s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/L1s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/L1s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/L1s038.jpg"}]},{"id":"02","title":"Relations & Functions","slides":"Lecture 2, slides 2–32","md":"# 02 — Relations and Functions (Lecture 2, slides 2–32)\n\n_Week 2. Everything in this course is defined with relations and functions: a DFA's transition **function** δ, the \"same state\" **equivalence relation** behind the Myhill–Nerode theorem, and the **countability** argument that proves some languages have no recogniser at all. Quiz 1 (week 5) material._\n\n## Relations as sets (slides 2–3)\n\nThe idea: a relation is not a new kind of object. It **is a set** — the set of exactly those combinations for which the relation holds.\n\n- \"Less than\" over numbers = the set of all pairs whose first component is smaller than the second. (4, 7) is in it; (4, 2) and (4, 4) are not.\n- Pairs must be **ordered**, because {4, 7} = {7, 4} as a set but the relation distinguishes them. So we use the ordered pair **(4, 7)**, whose two parts are called its **components**.\n\n⚠️ **Slide 3 erratum.** It writes \"R = (a, b)\". A relation is a **set of** ordered pairs, so it should be R = {(a, b), …} or R ⊆ A × B. A single pair is an *element* of R, not R itself.\n\n## Binary relations, domain and range (slides 4–5)\n\n> **Binary relation:** any set R with **R ⊆ A × B**.\n\n| Term | Definition (as corrected) |\n|------|---------------------------|\n| Domain D_R | {a ∈ A : **there exists** b with (a, b) ∈ R} — the first components actually used |\n| Range V_R | {b ∈ B : **there exists** a with (a, b) ∈ R} — the second components actually used |\n\n⚠️ **Slide 4 erratum.** It writes D_R = {a ∈ A : (a, b) ∈ R} with a free, unquantified b. Read it as \"∃b\". Without the quantifier the definition does not parse.\n\n**Worked example (slide 5).** R = {(a, 3), (c, 2), (b, 5), (d, 3)}\n- Domain = {a, c, b, d}\n- Range = {3, 2, 5} — the repeated 3 is written **once**, because a range is a set.\n\n## n-ary relations (slide 6)\n\n- **Ordered tuple:** given sets A₁ … Aₙ, an element (a₁, …, aₙ) with aᵢ ∈ Aᵢ.\n- **Cartesian product:** A₁ × ⋯ × Aₙ = {(a₁, …, aₙ) : aᵢ ∈ Aᵢ}.\n- **n-ary relation:** any R ⊆ A₁ × ⋯ × Aₙ.\n- When every Aᵢ is the same set A, we say R is an n-ary relation **on A**: R ⊆ Aⁿ.\n\nWhy it matters later: a DFA transition is a relation on Q × Σ × Q, and it is a *function* exactly when the machine is deterministic.\n\n## Relations as directed graphs (slides 7–8)\n\nFor a relation **on a single set** (R ⊆ A × A) there is a standard picture:\n\n- each element of A is a **node** (small circle),\n- draw an **edge** (arrow) from a to b **iff** (a, b) ∈ R.\n\nSlide 8's example: R = {(a,b), (b,a), (a,d), (d,c), (c,c), (c,a)} — note (c, c) is a **self-loop**, and a ↔ b is a two-way pair.\n\nThe point of the picture is that the four properties below become things you can *see*.\n\n## The four properties (slides 9–16)\n\n| Property | Definition | What to look for in the graph |\n|----------|-----------|-------------------------------|\n| **Reflexive** | (a, a) ∈ R for **every** a ∈ A | a self-loop on **every** node |\n| **Symmetric** | (a, b) ∈ R ⟹ (b, a) ∈ R | every arrow has a partner going back; drawable without arrowheads |\n| **Antisymmetric** | (a, b) ∈ R and a ≠ b ⟹ (b, a) ∉ R | no two-way arrow between **distinct** nodes |\n| **Transitive** | (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R ⟹ (a, c) ∈ R | every two-step path has a direct shortcut |\n\n**Professor's examples**, with P = the set of all persons:\n- symmetric: \"a is the spouse of b\"\n- antisymmetric: \"a is the father of b\"\n- transitive: \"a is an ancestor of b\"\n- **neither symmetric nor antisymmetric**: \"a is the **brother** of b\" — because a can be b's brother while b is his **sister**, so (b, a) is not in the relation (fails symmetry), yet other pairs of brothers do go both ways (fails antisymmetry). This \"why\" was asked as a live question in class, so expect it on a quiz.\n\n**Reflexive vs irreflexive (slide 10, the in-class question).** Diagram B has a self-loop only on c.\n- Not **reflexive** — (a,a), (b,b), (d,d) are missing.\n- Not **irreflexive** either — irreflexivity requires **no** self-loops at all, and (c, c) is there.\n- So a relation can fail both. They are not opposites.\n\n⚠️ **Slide 9 erratum.** Its NOTE reads \"whenever there is an arrow between two nodes, there are arrows between those nodes in both directions.\" That is the definition of **symmetric**, not reflexive. Reflexivity says nothing whatsoever about pairs of *distinct* nodes; it only demands the self-loops. The note presumably describes the particular figure on slide 10A, which happens to be both.\n\n⚠️ **Slide 11 erratum.** \"A symmetric relation without pairs of the form (a, b)…\" should read **(a, a)** — that is, no self-loops. Such a relation is drawn as an **undirected graph**: arrowheads are dropped because every edge is understood to go both ways.\n\n## Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)\n\n> **Equivalence relation** = reflexive **and** symmetric **and** transitive.\n\nWritten a ∼ b, read \"a and b are the same in some sense\". The three laws, for all a, b, c:\n\n1. **Reflexive:** a ∼ a\n2. **Symmetric:** a ∼ b ⟹ b ∼ a\n3. **Transitive:** a ∼ b and b ∼ c ⟹ a ∼ c\n\n**Example (slide 18) — this one is about strings, so it is directly relevant to the rest of the course.** On the set of all finite strings, define s ∼ t iff |s| = |t| (same length).\n- Reflexive: |s| = |s| ✓\n- Symmetric: |s| = |t| ⟹ |t| = |s| ✓\n- Transitive: |s| = |t| and |t| = |u| ⟹ |s| = |u| ✓\n\n**Equivalence classes (slide 19).** The clusters an equivalence relation cuts a set into. Notation **[a] = {b : (a, b) ∈ R}** — the class containing a.\n\n> Example: a ∼ b iff a and b have the same parents. The equivalence classes are **the groups of siblings**.\n\nThe classes of an equivalence relation always **partition** the set: non-empty, pairwise disjoint, and together they cover everything. (Partitions were slide 24 of Lecture 1 — this is why they were introduced.)\n\n⚠️ **Slide 17 wording.** \"An equivalence relation on a set R\" — R is being used both for the set and for the relation. Read the set as S.\n\n## Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)\n\n> **Finite:** A is finite iff there is some n ∈ ℕ and a **bijection** f : {0, 1, …, n−1} → A.\n\nA **bijection** is both:\n- **injective** (one-to-one) — no two inputs share an output, so no element of A is hit twice;\n- **surjective** (onto) — every element of A is hit at least once.\n\nThen **|A| = n**, the **cardinality**: A has n elements.\n\n⚠️ **Slide 20 erratum.** Its surjectivity bullet (\"every element of A is mapped to some domain element, in this case, N\") has the direction backwards. Surjective onto A means every element of **A** is the **image** of some element of {0, …, n−1}. The arrow runs {0,…,n−1} → A, not the other way.\n\n**Equinumerous (slides 22, 24).** A and B are equinumerous iff there is a bijection f : A → B. Because a bijection always has an inverse bijection f⁻¹ : B → A, **equinumerosity is symmetric** — and in fact it is an equivalence relation on sets, which is the point of covering relations first.\n\n- Finite example: A = {1,2,3}, B = {a,b,c}, f(1)=a, f(2)=b, f(3)=c. Equinumerous.\n\n**Countable** = finite **or** countably infinite.\n\n> **Countably infinite:** equinumerous with ℕ.\n\n**The example to remember (slide 23).** A = ℕ and B = {2, 4, 6, 8, …}. The map **f(n) = 2n** is a bijection, so ℕ and the even numbers have **the same cardinality** — even though the evens \"look smaller\" and are a proper subset. For infinite sets \"same size\" is not a count; it is **the existence of a bijection**. This is the whole trick, and the diagonalisation proof later in the course (that some languages are not decidable) is built on it.\n\n⚠️ **Slide 21 erratum, worth knowing for a quiz.** It says \"A set is also countable if it is countably infinite, e.g. {1, 2, …, n} for some natural number n.\" The example is **finite**, not countably infinite — it contradicts the sentence it illustrates. A correct example of countably infinite is ℕ itself, or the evens, or ℤ, or ℚ.\n\n⚠️ **Slide 22 wording.** \"countably finite\" is not standard terminology. A finite set is just **finite**.\n\n**Infinite (slide 25):** A is infinite iff A is not finite. ℕ = {0, 1, 2, …} is the standard example.\n\n## Defining a set by a property (slide 26)\n\nIf A is a set and P is a property, then **B = {x : x ∈ A and x has property P}** is a set.\n\n> I = {1, 3, 9}, G = {3, 9}: G = {x : x ∈ I and x is greater than 2}.\n\nThis is the set-builder notation from Lecture 1, restated because every later definition (\"the set of strings accepted by M\") uses it.\n\n## Functions (slides 27–32)\n\n> **Function:** a binary relation R on A and B such that for **each** a ∈ A there is **exactly one** pair (a, b) ∈ R with first component a.\n\n\"Exactly one\" does two jobs at once: **at least one** (every input is handled — the relation is total on A) and **at most one** (no input has two answers).\n\n**The cities-and-states example (slide 28)**, with C = US cities and S = US states:\n- f = {(x, y) : x ∈ C, y ∈ S, x is a city in state y} **is** a function — each city lies in exactly one state.\n- g = {(x, y) : x ∈ S, y ∈ C, x is the state for city y} is **not** a function — a state contains many cities, so one input has several outputs.\n\n**Notation (slide 29).** f : A → B, read \"f maps A into B\". A is the **domain**, B is the **codomain**.\n\n**Image (slide 32).** For f = {(a,3), …}, f(a) = 3. Here **3 is the image of a** under f, **a is the argument**, and 3 is the corresponding **value**. The professor's aside is a good anchor: this is exactly a function in a programming language — one input, one return value, every time.\n\n### In-class exercise (slides 30–31) — and the one answer to be careful with\n\nGiven f = {(a, 3), (c, 2), (b, 5), (d, 3)}:\n\n| Asked | Professor's answer | Correct answer |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------|\n| Domain | {a, c, b, d} | {a, c, b, d} ✓ |\n| Codomain | **{3, 2, 5, 3}** | see below |\n| Range | {3, 2, 5} | {3, 2, 5} ✓ |\n\n⚠️ **Slide 31 erratum, and the most likely trap on a quiz.** Two problems with \"{3, 2, 5, 3}\":\n\n1. A codomain is a **set**, so it cannot contain 3 twice. Written as a set, {3, 2, 5, 3} = {2, 3, 5}, which is just the range.\n2. More fundamentally, the codomain is **not recoverable from the pairs**. It is part of the declaration f : A → B. The pairs alone tell you the domain and the range; B could be {2,3,5}, or ℕ, or ℝ. If B is never stated, the honest answer is \"the codomain is whichever B was declared; the range is {2, 3, 5}\".\n\n**How to handle this in the quiz:** if the professor asks this exact exercise, give his answer and add one clause — \"codomain {2, 3, 5} as declared here, range {2, 3, 5}\". Writing a set with a duplicate costs nothing but looks careless. The distinction you must be able to state is **range ⊆ codomain, with equality exactly when f is surjective**.\n\n## Where this is heading\n\nRelations and functions, countability, and the bijection definition of \"same size\" are the three tools the course needs before defining a machine. The announced next step is **deterministic finite automata**, whose transition function δ : Q × Σ → Q is precisely a function in the sense of slide 27.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"In set theory, what *is* a relation?","a":"A set — specifically a set of ordered pairs (or tuples); the pairs for which the relation holds."},{"q":"Why must relation elements be ordered pairs rather than sets?","a":"Because {4,7} = {7,4}, so an unordered set cannot record that 4 < 7 but 7 ≮ 4."},{"q":"Define a binary relation on sets A and B.","a":"Any set R with R ⊆ A × B."},{"q":"Define the domain and range of R ⊆ A × B.","a":"Domain = {a ∈ A : ∃b, (a,b) ∈ R}; Range = {b ∈ B : ∃a, (a,b) ∈ R}."},{"q":"For R = {(a,3),(c,2),(b,5),(d,3)}, give the domain and the range.","a":"Domain {a,c,b,d}; range {3,2,5} — the repeated 3 is listed once, a range is a set."},{"q":"What is an n-ary relation?","a":"Any R ⊆ A₁ × A₂ × … × Aₙ; when all Aᵢ are the same A, an n-ary relation on A."},{"q":"How is a relation R ⊆ A × A drawn as a directed graph?","a":"One node per element of A, and an arrow a → b exactly when (a,b) ∈ R."},{"q":"Define reflexive, and what it looks like in the graph.","a":"(a,a) ∈ R for every a ∈ A; a self-loop on every node."},{"q":"Define symmetric.","a":"(a,b) ∈ R implies (b,a) ∈ R — every arrow has its reverse."},{"q":"Define antisymmetric.","a":"If (a,b) ∈ R and a ≠ b, then (b,a) ∉ R — no two-way arrow between distinct elements."},{"q":"Define transitive.","a":"(a,b) ∈ R and (b,c) ∈ R imply (a,c) ∈ R."},{"q":"Can a relation be neither symmetric nor antisymmetric? Give the professor's example.","a":"Yes — \"a is the brother of b\": b may be a sister so (b,a) fails symmetry, while brother–brother pairs go both ways so antisymmetry fails too."},{"q":"A graph has a self-loop on c only. Is the relation reflexive? Irreflexive?","a":"Neither — reflexive needs a loop on every node; irreflexive needs no loops at all, and (c,c) exists."},{"q":"What three properties make an equivalence relation?","a":"Reflexive, symmetric, transitive."},{"q":"Show that \"same length\" on strings is an equivalence relation.","a":"|s|=|s| (reflexive); |s|=|t| ⟹ |t|=|s| (symmetric); |s|=|t| and |t|=|u| ⟹ |s|=|u| (transitive)."},{"q":"What is an equivalence class, and how is it written?","a":"A cluster of mutually equivalent elements; [a] = {b : (a,b) ∈ R}."},{"q":"What are the equivalence classes of \"a and b have the same parents\"?","a":"The groups of siblings."},{"q":"Define a finite set formally.","a":"A is finite iff there is n ∈ ℕ and a bijection f : {0,1,…,n−1} → A; then |A| = n."},{"q":"Injective vs surjective vs bijective.","a":"Injective = no two inputs share an output; surjective = every codomain element is hit; bijective = both."},{"q":"When are two sets equinumerous, and why is equinumerosity symmetric?","a":"When a bijection f : A → B exists; f⁻¹ : B → A is then also a bijection."},{"q":"What does \"countable\" mean?","a":"Finite, or countably infinite (equinumerous with ℕ)."},{"q":"Show ℕ and the even numbers have the same cardinality.","a":"f(n) = 2n is a bijection ℕ → {2,4,6,…} — so they are equinumerous despite one being a proper subset."},{"q":"For infinite sets, what does \"same size\" actually mean?","a":"Not a count — the existence of a bijection between them."},{"q":"Define an infinite set.","a":"A set that is not finite; ℕ is the standard example."},{"q":"When is a binary relation R ⊆ A × B a function?","a":"When for each a ∈ A there is exactly one pair (a,b) ∈ R — every input handled, none handled twice."},{"q":"Why is \"x is a city in state y\" a function but \"x is the state for city y\" not?","a":"Each city lies in exactly one state; a state has many cities, so the reverse gives one input several outputs."},{"q":"In f : A → B, name A and B.","a":"A is the domain, B is the codomain."},{"q":"For f(a) = 3, name the three parts.","a":"a is the argument, 3 is the value, and 3 is the image of a under f."},{"q":"What is the relationship between range and codomain?","a":"Range ⊆ codomain, with equality exactly when f is surjective."},{"q":"Why can the codomain not be read off the list of pairs of f?","a":"The pairs determine only the domain and the range; the codomain is whatever B was declared in f : A → B."}],"figs":[{"n":2,"src":"slides/L2s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/L2s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/L2s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/L2s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/L2s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/L2s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/L2s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/L2s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/L2s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/L2s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/L2s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/L2s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/L2s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/L2s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/L2s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/L2s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/L2s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/L2s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/L2s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/L2s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/L2s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/L2s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/L2s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/L2s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/L2s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/L2s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/L2s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/L2s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/L2s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/L2s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/L2s032.jpg"}]},{"id":"03","title":"Proof Techniques","slides":"Lecture 2, slides 33–60","md":"# 03 — Proof Techniques (Lecture 2, slides 33–60)\n\n_Week 2. This is the part of Lecture 2 that gets examined as **\"prove that…\"** rather than \"define…\". Every later result in the course — the pumping lemma, undecidability of the halting problem, NP-completeness — is one of these seven techniques applied to automata. Quiz 1 (week 5) material._\n\n## Theorem, proof, axiom, hypothesis (slide 33)\n\n- A **theorem** is a statement that is true.\n- A **proof** is a sequence of mathematical statements forming an argument that the theorem is true.\n- The statements allowed in a proof are **axioms**, the **hypotheses** of the theorem, and **previously proved theorems**.\n\nThe professor's own footnotes:\n- **Axioms** — fundamental rules accepted as true without proof; the starting point for reasoning.\n- **Hypotheses** — proposed explanations that still require testing or confirmation.\n\nSlide 34 is the honest caveat: there is **no perfect method** for finding a proof, only a toolbox of strategies. Finding the proof is the creative part; writing it is mechanical.\n\n## The seven techniques (slide 35)\n\n| # | Technique | One-line shape |\n|---|-----------|----------------|\n| 1 | **Direct proof** | Argue straight from the hypothesis to the conclusion |\n| 2 | **Constructive proof** | Prove something exists by **building** it |\n| 3 | **Nonconstructive proof** | Prove something exists **without** exhibiting it |\n| 4 | **Pigeon-hole principle** | More objects than boxes ⟹ some box holds two |\n| 5 | **Proof by contradiction** | Assume the negation, derive something false |\n| 6 | **Proof by induction** | Base case, then n ⟹ n+1 |\n| 7 | **Proof by deduction** | Chain of logical steps — *in this course, the same as direct proof* |\n\n⚠️ Note the course's own convention (slide 51): **deduction and direct proof are treated as the same thing**. When slide 58 asks \"which technique proved De Morgan's first law?\", the expected answer is \"proof by deduction, i.e. a direct proof\".\n\n---\n\n## 1. Direct proofs (slides 36–37)\n\nApproach the theorem head-on with persuasive argument.\n\n**Example A — odd squares.**\n> **Theorem.** If n is an odd positive integer, then n² is odd.\n>\n> **Proof.** An odd positive integer can be written n = 2k + 1 for some integer k ≥ 0. Then\n> n² = (2k + 1)² = 4k² + 4k + 1 = **2(2k² + 2k) + 1**.\n> Since 2(2k² + 2k) is even and \"even plus one is odd\", n² is odd. ∎\n\nMemorise the move: **write the odd number as 2k+1, expand, and factor a 2 out of everything but the +1.** It reappears on slide 46 and it is the standard quiz question.\n\n**Example B — the Handshaking Lemma.**\n> **Theorem.** Let G = (V, E) be a graph. Then **Σ_{v∈V} deg(v) = 2|E|** — the sum of all vertex degrees is even.\n>\n> **Proof.** Each edge has exactly two ends, so adding up the degrees counts every edge **twice**. ∎\n\nThe professor's instance: the path v₁ – v₂ – v₃ – v₄ (4 vertices, 3 edges). Degrees 1, 2, 2, 1 → sum 6 = 2 × 3 = 2|E|. ✓\n\n⚠️ **Slide 37 notation.** \"G = (4,3)\" is loose shorthand for |V| = 4 and |E| = 3; a graph is properly written G = (V, E) with V and E as sets.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constructive proofs (slides 38–40)\n\nNot only show the object exists — **give a recipe that produces it**.\n\nTemplate:\n> Theorem: there exists an object with property P.\n> Proof: here is the object […]; and here is why it has P […].\n\n**Example — a rational strictly between any two rationals.**\n> **Theorem.** Given distinct rationals a < b, there is a rational r with a < r < b.\n>\n> **Proof.** Construct the midpoint **r = (a + b)/2**. Sums and halves of rationals are rational, so r ∈ ℚ. From a < b: 2a < a + b and a + b < 2b, hence a < r < b. ∎\n\nIt is constructive because the proof **hands you the number**, not merely the assurance that one exists.\n\n**Definitions the professor asked for in class (slide 40):**\n- **Rational number** — any number writable as a simple fraction with integer numerator and denominator and a **non-zero denominator**.\n- **Irrational number** — a real number that is **not** rational; its decimal expansion never terminates and never repeats. Examples: √2, π, e.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Nonconstructive proofs (slides 41–42)\n\nShow something exists **without producing an example**.\n\n**Example A — irrational powers (slide 41).**\n> **Theorem.** There exist irrational x, y such that xʸ is rational.\n>\n> **Proof.** Consider **√2^√2**. We do not know whether it is rational.\n> - **Case 1 — √2^√2 is rational.** Take x = y = √2. Both are irrational and xʸ is rational. Done.\n> - **Case 2 — √2^√2 is irrational.** Take x = √2^√2 and y = √2. Both are irrational, and\n>   xʸ = (√2^√2)^√2 = √2^(√2·√2) = √2² = **2**, which is rational. Done.\n>\n> One of the two cases must hold, so such a pair exists — but the proof never tells us **which** pair. ∎\n\n⚠️ **Slide 41 errata — the slide is garbled and this proof is a likely quiz question, so learn the version above.** The slide labels both cases \"Case 1\", and the first case's conclusion reads \"We cannot conclude that aᵇ is rational\", which is the opposite of what Case 1 establishes. The logic that works is the two-case split written out above.\n\n**Example B — shared hair counts (slide 42).**\n> **Theorem.** In a large enough city, two people have exactly the same number of hairs on their head.\n>\n> **Proof.** A human head has at most about 500 000 hairs, certainly fewer than 1 000 000. So the possible counts are 0, 1, 2, …, 1 000 000 — that is **1 000 001 possibilities**. In a city of **1 000 002 people** or more, the pigeonhole principle forces two people to share a count. ∎\n\nNonconstructive because it proves the pair exists without identifying the two people.\n\n⚠️ **Slide 42 typo:** the upper bound is printed \"1,000,0000\" with an extra zero. It should be 1 000 000, which is what makes the 1 000 001 count correct.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The pigeon-hole principle (slides 43–44)\n\n> **Statement.** If A and B are sets with **|A| > |B|**, then there is **no one-to-one function** from A to B.\n>\n> Equivalently: if **n + 1 or more objects** are placed into **n boxes**, at least one box holds **two or more** objects.\n\nBoth phrasings say the same thing; the second is how you use it, the first is why it is true (an injection would need a private box for each object).\n\n**Example (slide 44).**\n> **Theorem.** Among any 5 distinct integers, two leave the same remainder when divided by 4.\n>\n> **Proof.** The remainders mod 4 are 0, 1, 2, 3 — **4 holes**. With **5 pigeons** (the integers), two must land in the same hole. ∎\n\nThe exam skill is naming the pigeons and the holes correctly. Pigeons are the objects you have many of; holes are the finitely many possible outcomes. Later in the course the pumping lemma is exactly this argument: a long string visits more positions than the DFA has states, so some state repeats.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Proof by contradiction (slides 45–47)\n\n> **Principle.** It is sufficient to show that \"**¬S ⟹ false**\" in order to prove S true.\n\nProcedure: assume the statement is **false**, then derive something impossible.\n\n**Toy illustration (slide 45).** To prove S: \"1 + 1 = 2\", assume S is false; all that is needed is to derive a contradiction such as 1 + 1 = 3.\n\n**Example A — even squares (slide 46).**\n> **Theorem.** Let n be a positive integer. If n² is even, then n is even.\n>\n> **Proof.** Assume n² is even but n is **odd**. By the direct proof on slide 36, n odd implies n² is odd. So n² is both even and odd — a contradiction. Hence n is even. ∎\n\nNote how it **reuses an earlier theorem**; that is exactly what slide 33 licenses. (Strictly, this argument is a proof by *contraposition* — it proves \"n odd ⟹ n² odd\", which is the contrapositive. The course presents it as contradiction; answer as the professor framed it, but know the distinction.)\n\n**Example B — √2 is irrational (slide 47). The classic, and the single most likely \"prove this\" question.**\n> **Theorem.** √2 is irrational — it cannot be written as a fraction m/n of integers.\n>\n> **Proof.**\n> 1. Assume the opposite: **√2 = m/n** for integers m, n in **lowest terms** (no common factor > 1).\n> 2. Square both sides: 2 = m²/n², so **m² = 2n²**.\n> 3. Then m² is even, hence **m is even** (by the theorem just proved). Write **m = 2k**.\n> 4. Substitute: (2k)² = 2n² ⟹ 4k² = 2n² ⟹ **n² = 2k²**.\n> 5. So n² is even, hence **n is even** too.\n> 6. But then m and n are both even — contradicting that m/n was in lowest terms.\n>\n> The assumption is therefore false, and √2 is irrational. ∎\n\nObserve the dependency chain: slide 36 (direct) → slide 46 (contradiction) → slide 47 (contradiction). Steps 3 and 5 **both** rely on the earlier result. If a quiz asks you to prove √2 irrational, state that lemma rather than waving at it.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Proof by induction (slides 48–50)\n\n> Prove a **base case**, then show truth passes from **n to n + 1**.\n\nThe professor's three steps:\n1. Establish a **base case** — the statement P(k) holds for some starting integer k.\n2. Formulate the **induction hypothesis** — temporarily assume P(k) is true.\n3. Show that P(k) ⟹ P(k + 1), and therefore that P holds for the whole sequence.\n\n**Example A — the sum of the first n integers (slide 49).**\n> **Theorem.** P(n): 1 + 2 + ⋯ + n = **n(n + 1)/2**.\n>\n> **Base case.** n = 1: the left side is 1, the right side is 1·2/2 = 1. ✓\n>\n> **Induction hypothesis.** P(k) holds: 1 + 2 + ⋯ + k = k(k + 1)/2.\n>\n> **Step, P(k + 1).**\n> 1 + ⋯ + k + (k + 1) = k(k+1)/2 + (k + 1)\n> = [k(k + 1) + 2(k + 1)] / 2\n> = **(k + 1)(k + 2) / 2**\n>\n> which is the formula with n = k + 1. ∎\n\n**Example B — the Handshaking Lemma again, by induction on edges (slide 50).**\n> **Theorem.** For a graph G = (V, E) with m edges, **Σ_{v∈V} deg(v) = 2m**.\n>\n> **Base case, m = 0.** No edges, so every degree is 0 and Σ deg(v) = 0 = 2·0. ✓\n>\n> **Induction hypothesis.** The claim holds for every graph with m edges.\n>\n> **Step, m + 1 edges.** Let {a, b} be an arbitrary edge of G and let G′ be G with that edge removed, so G′ has m edges. By the hypothesis, Σ_{v∈G′} deg(v) = 2m. Putting the edge back raises deg(a) and deg(b) by one each:\n> Σ_{v∈G} deg(v) = Σ_{v∈G′} deg(v) + 2 = 2m + 2 = **2(m + 1)**. ∎\n\n⚠️ **Slides 49–50 label the steps wrongly, and induction structure is exactly what gets marked.** Both slides print the theorem under the heading \"Theorem (base case)\" and then print the genuine base case under \"Induction hypothesis\". On slide 49 the base case n = 1 is never checked at all. The correct three parts are the ones written out above: **theorem statement, base case, induction hypothesis, inductive step.** In a quiz, label them that way — an induction proof with no base case is not a proof, since it would equally \"prove\" a false statement.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Proof by deduction (slides 51–52)\n\n> **Course convention (slide 51): proof by deduction = direct proof.**\n\nThe recipe:\n1. Start from known facts — axioms, definitions, previously proved results.\n2. Apply valid rules of logic: transitivity, case analysis, rewriting via definitions, algebraic manipulation, set inclusions.\n3. Every line follows from earlier lines — **no leaps, no guessing**.\n\n> Proof by deduction assumes \"If P, then Q\" and concludes that since Q follows from assuming P, the implication **P ⟹ Q** is proved.\n\n**Example — transitivity of subset (slide 52).**\n> **Theorem.** If A ⊆ B and B ⊆ C, then A ⊆ C.\n>\n> **Proof.**\n> 1. Let x ∈ A. Since A ⊆ B, x ∈ B.\n> 2. Since B ⊆ C, x ∈ C.\n> 3. Every x in A is therefore in C, so A ⊆ C. ∎\n\nThree lines, each forced by the one before. This is the model answer format for any \"prove this set identity\" question.\n\n---\n\n## Set theory laws (slide 53)\n\nFor all sets A, B, C:\n\n| Law | Statements |\n|-----|-----------|\n| **Idempotency** | A ∪ A = A;  A ∩ A = A |\n| **Commutativity** | A ∪ B = B ∪ A;  A ∩ B = B ∩ A |\n| **Associativity** | (A ∪ B) ∪ C = A ∪ (B ∪ C);  (A ∩ B) ∩ C = A ∩ (B ∩ C) |\n| **Distributivity** | (A ∪ B) ∩ C = (A ∩ C) ∪ (B ∩ C);  (A ∩ B) ∪ C = (A ∪ C) ∩ (B ∪ C) |\n| **Absorption** | (A ∪ B) ∩ A = A;  (A ∩ B) ∪ A = A |\n| **De Morgan's laws** | A − (B ∪ C) = (A − B) ∩ (A − C);  A − (B ∩ C) = (A − B) ∪ (A − C) |\n\n⚠️ Note the form of De Morgan used here: **set difference**, not complement. The familiar (B ∪ C)ᶜ = Bᶜ ∩ Cᶜ is the special case where A is the universe.\n\nThe pattern worth memorising: **the operation flips.** Difference distributed over a union gives an intersection, and over an intersection gives a union.\n\n## Proving De Morgan's first law (slides 54–56)\n\n> **Claim.** A − (B ∪ C) = (A − B) ∩ (A − C)\n\nLet L = A − (B ∪ C) and R = (A − B) ∩ (A − C). Prove **L = R** by double inclusion, **L ⊆ R and R ⊆ L** — the standard technique from Lecture 1 for proving two sets equal.\n\n**(a) L ⊆ R.** Let x ∈ L. Then x ∈ A, and x ∉ B ∪ C, so x ∉ B **and** x ∉ C. Hence x ∈ A − B and x ∈ A − C, so x ∈ R.\n\n**(b) R ⊆ L.** Let x ∈ R. Then x is in both A − B and A − C, so x ∈ A, x ∉ B and x ∉ C. Hence x ∉ B ∪ C, so x ∈ A − (B ∪ C) = L.\n\nBoth inclusions hold, so **L = R**. ∎\n\n⚠️ **Slide 54 typo:** it writes \"L < R and R < L\". The symbol is **⊆**, set inclusion, not \"less than\".\n\n**Numerical check (slide 56).** A = {1,2,3,4}, B = {2,5}, C = {3,6}.\n\n| Side | Working | Result |\n|------|---------|--------|\n| Left | B ∪ C = {2,3,5,6}; A − (B ∪ C) = {1,2,3,4} − {2,3,5,6} | **{1, 4}** |\n| Right | A − B = {1,3,4}; A − C = {1,2,4}; intersect | **{1, 4}** |\n\nL = R. ✓ A numerical check is **not** a proof — it confirms one instance. The slides give both because the arithmetic is what makes the abstract argument readable.\n\n**Which technique was used (slides 57–58)?** The professor's answer: **proof by deduction, that is, a direct proof.** Each line follows from the definitions of −, ∪ and ∩ with no assumption of the negation and no induction.\n\n**Set as homework (slide 59):** prove the second law, **A − (B ∩ C) = (A − B) ∪ (A − C)**, the same way. Sketch: x ∈ LHS means x ∈ A and x ∉ B ∩ C, i.e. x ∈ A and (x ∉ B **or** x ∉ C), which is exactly x ∈ A − B or x ∈ A − C. Both directions follow because every step is an \"iff\".\n\n## Sources the professor credits (slide 60)\n\nMaheshwari & Smid (2024); Sipser (2012); Lewis & Papadimitriou, *Elements of the Theory of Computation*, 2nd ed. (1998); Hasan, Raqibul, IUS lecture notes (2024).\n\nBoth main books are already digested in `study/book/` and `study/book-sipser/`. The proof techniques map onto **M&S chapter 1** and **Sipser chapter 0**, so drill `b1` and `s0` alongside this chapter.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What is a theorem, and what is a proof?","a":"A theorem is a true statement; a proof is a sequence of statements forming an argument that it is true."},{"q":"Which three kinds of statement may appear in a proof?","a":"Axioms, the theorem's hypotheses, and previously proved theorems."},{"q":"What is an axiom?","a":"A fundamental rule accepted as true without proof — the starting point for reasoning."},{"q":"Name the seven proof techniques from slide 35.","a":"Direct, constructive, nonconstructive, pigeon-hole, contradiction, induction, deduction."},{"q":"In this course, how do deduction and direct proof relate?","a":"They are treated as the same technique (slide 51)."},{"q":"Prove directly: if n is an odd positive integer then n² is odd.","a":"n = 2k+1 ⟹ n² = 4k²+4k+1 = 2(2k²+2k)+1, which is even plus one, hence odd."},{"q":"State the Handshaking Lemma.","a":"For a graph G = (V,E), Σ_{v∈V} deg(v) = 2|E| — the degree sum is even."},{"q":"Why is the degree sum twice the edge count?","a":"Every edge has two ends, so it is counted once at each endpoint."},{"q":"Check the Handshaking Lemma on the path v1–v2–v3–v4.","a":"Degrees 1,2,2,1 sum to 6 = 2 × 3 edges. ✓"},{"q":"What makes a proof constructive?","a":"It exhibits the object and shows it has the property, rather than only proving existence."},{"q":"Constructively find a rational between rationals a < b.","a":"r = (a+b)/2 — rational since sums and halves of rationals are rational, and a < r < b."},{"q":"Define a rational and an irrational number.","a":"Rational = a fraction of integers with non-zero denominator; irrational = a real number that is not rational (√2, π, e)."},{"q":"What does a nonconstructive proof establish, and what does it withhold?","a":"That an object exists, without exhibiting a specific example."},{"q":"Prove there are irrational x, y with xʸ rational.","a":"If √2^√2 is rational take x=y=√2; otherwise take x=√2^√2, y=√2, giving (√2^√2)^√2 = √2² = 2. One case must hold, but we never learn which."},{"q":"Why is the hair-count theorem nonconstructive?","a":"It proves two people share a count without identifying them."},{"q":"State the pigeon-hole principle, both ways.","a":"If |A| > |B| there is no one-to-one function A → B; equivalently, n+1 objects in n boxes force some box to hold two."},{"q":"Show that among 5 integers two share a remainder mod 4.","a":"Remainders 0,1,2,3 are 4 holes; 5 integers are 5 pigeons, so two share a hole."},{"q":"What is the principle behind proof by contradiction?","a":"Showing \"¬S ⟹ false\" suffices to prove S — assume the negation and derive an impossibility."},{"q":"Prove by contradiction: if n² is even then n is even.","a":"Assume n² even and n odd; the direct proof gives n² odd, so n² is both even and odd — contradiction."},{"q":"Prove √2 is irrational.","a":"Assume √2 = m/n in lowest terms; then m² = 2n², so m is even, m = 2k; substituting gives n² = 2k², so n is even too — contradicting lowest terms."},{"q":"In the √2 proof, which earlier result is used twice?","a":"\"If n² is even then n is even\" — once for m, once for n."},{"q":"What are the parts of a proof by induction?","a":"The theorem statement, a verified base case, the induction hypothesis P(k), and the step P(k) ⟹ P(k+1)."},{"q":"Why is the base case indispensable?","a":"Without it the induction step alone would equally \"prove\" false statements — nothing anchors the chain."},{"q":"Prove 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n+1)/2 by induction.","a":"Base n=1: 1 = 1·2/2. Step: k(k+1)/2 + (k+1) = [k(k+1)+2(k+1)]/2 = (k+1)(k+2)/2."},{"q":"Prove the degree-sum formula by induction on edges.","a":"Base m=0: all degrees 0. Step: remove an edge {a,b} to get G′ with sum 2m; restoring it adds 1 to deg(a) and deg(b), giving 2m+2 = 2(m+1)."},{"q":"What are the three rules of a deductive proof?","a":"Start from known facts; apply only valid logical rules; every line follows from earlier lines, with no leaps."},{"q":"Prove that A ⊆ B and B ⊆ C imply A ⊆ C.","a":"Let x ∈ A; then x ∈ B since A ⊆ B; then x ∈ C since B ⊆ C; so every element of A is in C."},{"q":"State De Morgan's laws in the set-difference form.","a":"A − (B ∪ C) = (A−B) ∩ (A−C) and A − (B ∩ C) = (A−B) ∪ (A−C)."},{"q":"State idempotency, absorption and distributivity.","a":"A∪A = A, A∩A = A; (A∪B)∩A = A, (A∩B)∪A = A; (A∪B)∩C = (A∩C)∪(B∩C)."},{"q":"How do you prove two sets are equal?","a":"Double inclusion — show L ⊆ R and R ⊆ L."},{"q":"Prove A − (B ∪ C) ⊆ (A−B) ∩ (A−C).","a":"x ∈ LHS means x ∈ A and x ∉ B∪C, so x ∉ B and x ∉ C; hence x is in both A−B and A−C."},{"q":"Verify De Morgan 1 with A={1,2,3,4}, B={2,5}, C={3,6}.","a":"LHS: A − {2,3,5,6} = {1,4}. RHS: {1,3,4} ∩ {1,2,4} = {1,4}. Equal."},{"q":"Is a numerical check a proof?","a":"No — it confirms a single instance; the general argument is still required."},{"q":"Which technique proves De Morgan's first law?","a":"Proof by deduction, i.e. a direct proof."},{"q":"Sketch the proof of A − (B ∩ C) = (A−B) ∪ (A−C).","a":"x ∈ LHS iff x ∈ A and not (x ∈ B and x ∈ C), iff x ∈ A and (x ∉ B or x ∉ C), iff x ∈ A−B or x ∈ A−C."}],"figs":[{"n":33,"src":"slides/L2s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/L2s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/L2s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/L2s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/L2s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/L2s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/L2s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/L2s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/L2s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/L2s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/L2s043.jpg"},{"n":44,"src":"slides/L2s044.jpg"},{"n":45,"src":"slides/L2s045.jpg"},{"n":46,"src":"slides/L2s046.jpg"},{"n":47,"src":"slides/L2s047.jpg"},{"n":48,"src":"slides/L2s048.jpg"},{"n":49,"src":"slides/L2s049.jpg"},{"n":50,"src":"slides/L2s050.jpg"},{"n":51,"src":"slides/L2s051.jpg"},{"n":52,"src":"slides/L2s052.jpg"},{"n":53,"src":"slides/L2s053.jpg"},{"n":54,"src":"slides/L2s054.jpg"},{"n":55,"src":"slides/L2s055.jpg"},{"n":56,"src":"slides/L2s056.jpg"},{"n":57,"src":"slides/L2s057.jpg"},{"n":58,"src":"slides/L2s058.jpg"},{"n":59,"src":"slides/L2s059.jpg"},{"n":60,"src":"slides/L2s060.jpg"}]},{"id":"04","title":"NFAs & NFA → DFA","slides":"Lecture 4, slides 1–44 + practice diagrams","md":"# 04 — Nondeterministic Finite Automata (Lecture 4, slides 1–44)\n\n_Week 4 (\"last lecture material for the previous week\", received 25.10.2026). Lecture 3 (DFAs) is still missing from the folder; Tutorial 3 covers it (chapter **Tutorials 2–4**). **This whole lecture is In-Class Quiz 1 material**: the prep sheet's Q1, Q9–Q10 and Q11 (NFA → DFA, new this year) are exactly this deck. The deck follows Maheshwari & Smid §2.2–2.5 and Sipser §1.2 almost word for word, so `book/B2` and `book-sipser/S1` are the long versions._\n\n**Checked by code.** Every machine and claim on the slides was re-run in Python: the slide-4 NFA on every binary string up to length 12, all of its computations on 010 and 010110 (slides 6–7), the run of 01100 (slide 13), the ε-closures (slide 24), and the complete subset construction of slides 29–40 (all 8 rows, the lettered version, the pruning and the final DFA against the NFA on every string up to length 12). **Every worked example is correct.** The errors in this deck are in the surrounding text, not in the examples. They are listed in the errata table at the end and marked ⚠️ inline.\n\n## Determinism vs nondeterminism (slides 2–3, 9)\n\n- **Deterministic:** in a given state, reading a given symbol, the next state is fixed. One computation path.\n- **Nondeterministic:** several next states may be possible. The computation is a **tree** of paths (slide 9: one chain vs a branching tree with \"reject\" leaves and one \"accept\" leaf).\n- Nondeterminism is a **generalisation** of determinism: every DFA is also an NFA.\n\n**The three differences (slide 3).** An NFA may have:\n\n| | What the NFA may do | What a DFA must do |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | several arrows on the same symbol out of one state (or none) | **exactly one** arrow per symbol out of every state |\n| 2 | arrows labelled **ε** (a \"free\" move that reads nothing) | arrows labelled with symbols of Σ only |\n| 3 | paths that **hang** (no arrow for the next symbol), as long as some path accepts | never hang: δ is total |\n\n⚠️ **Slide 3, point 1, is the course's answer to the missing-arrow question (prep Q1).** It says a DFA state \"always has exactly one exiting transition arrow for each symbol\". So a state with **no** arrow on some symbol breaks the DFA rule just as two arrows do. Point 3 and slide 4 (\"it cannot continue, aka it hangs\") present hanging as an NFA-only behaviour. The prep-sheet answers were updated to lead with this (see `quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md`, Q1).\n\n## The running example N (slides 4–7, 12–13, 24)\n\nΣ = {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q4}.\n- q1 loops on 0, 1; q1 –1→ q2\n- q2 –0→ q3 and q2 –ε→ q3\n- q3 –1→ q4\n- q4 loops on 0, 1\n\nTransition table (slide 12), δ : Q × Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q):\n\n| | 0 | 1 | ε |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| → q1 | {q1} | {q1, q2} | ∅ |\n| q2 | {q3} | ∅ | {q3} |\n| q3 | ∅ | {q4} | ∅ |\n| ∗ q4 | {q4} | {q4} | ∅ |\n\n**L(N) = strings that contain 11 or 101** (verified on all 8 190 strings up to length 12). Reading: q1 guesses where the pattern starts; q2 → q3 is either the 0 of 101 or a free ε-skip, which gives 11.\n\n**Slide 4's three facts:** in q1 on a 1 there are two options (stay, or go to q2); in q2 the machine can move to q3 without reading anything; in q3 on a 0 there is no arrow, so that copy hangs.\n\n**How it computes (slide 5).** On a choice, the machine splits into copies that run in parallel. A copy with no arrow for the next symbol dies. **The NFA accepts if at least one copy is in an accept state when the input ends.**\n\n**Slide 6: 010110 is accepted.** The tree has two accepting branches:\n- q1 –0→ q1 –1→ q2 –0→ q3 –1→ q4 –1→ q4 –0→ q4\n- q1 –0→ q1 –1→ q1 –0→ q1 –1→ q2 –ε→ q3 –1→ q4 –0→ q4\n\n**Slide 7: 010 is rejected.** Exactly three computations exist (confirmed by enumerating them):\n- q1 –0→ q1 –1→ q1 –0→ q1 (ends in q1, not accepting)\n- q1 –0→ q1 –1→ q2 –0→ q3 (ends in q3, not accepting)\n- q1 –0→ q1 –1→ q2 –ε→ q3, then the 0 has no arrow: hang\n\nNone ends in q4, so N rejects 010. This is the right shape for a quiz answer: list **every** branch and show none accepts.\n\n## Formal definition (slides 10–11)\n\nAn **NFA** is a 5-tuple **M = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F)**:\n- Q — a finite set of **states**\n- Σ — a finite **alphabet**\n- **δ : Q × Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q)** — the **transition function**\n- q ∈ Q — the **start state**\n- F ⊆ Q — the **accept states**\n\nwhere **Σ_ε = Σ ∪ {ε}** and **𝒫(Q) = {R : R ⊆ Q}** is the power set.\n\nCompare with a DFA: δ : Q × Σ → Q. Two changes: the input may be ε, and the output is a **set** of states (possibly ∅).\n\n## When does an NFA accept? (slides 13–15)\n\nM accepts w if\n- **w = ε and q ∈ F**, or\n- there is an m ≥ 1 and a way to write **w = y₁y₂⋯yₘ with each yᵢ ∈ Σ_ε** (so ε's may be inserted anywhere), and a sequence of states **r₀, r₁, …, rₘ** with\n  - r₀ = q,\n  - rᵢ₊₁ ∈ δ(rᵢ, yᵢ₊₁) for i = 0, …, m − 1,\n  - rₘ ∈ F.\n\nOtherwise M rejects w. **The language of M is L(M) = {w ∈ Σ∗ : M accepts w}.**\n\n**Slide 13's worked example, re-checked.** w = 01100 is written as 0 · 1 · ε · 1 · 0 · 0 = y₁⋯y₆ (an ε inserted after \"01\"). States: r₀ = q1, r₁ = q1, r₂ = q2, r₃ = q3, r₄ = q4, r₅ = q4, r₆ = q4. Every step is in δ: δ(q1,0) ∋ q1, δ(q1,1) ∋ q2, δ(q2,ε) ∋ q3, δ(q3,1) ∋ q4, δ(q4,0) ∋ q4 twice. r₆ = q4 ∈ F, so M accepts. Note m = 6 although |w| = 5: **m counts the ε-steps too.**\n\n**Informal version (slide 8):** w is accepted iff some path (i) starts in the start state, (ii) does not hang before the whole string is read, and (iii) ends in an accept state.\n\n## Slide 16 · DFA or NFA?\n\nq0 loops on 0, 1; q0 –1→ q1; q1 –0,1→ q2; q2 accepting (no arrows out). **Answer: NFA**, because from q0 on a 1 the machine can stay in q0 or go to q1. (A second reason the slide doesn't give: q2 has no outgoing arrows, so δ(q2, 0) = δ(q2, 1) = ∅.) The language is **strings whose second symbol from the end is 1** (verified). Converted in practice question P2 below.\n\n## Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)\n\n**Theorem (slide 28).** A language A is regular **iff** some NFA accepts A. (Regular means \"accepted by some DFA\". Every DFA is an NFA, so the work is the other direction: converting any NFA into a DFA.)\n\n**Proof idea (slide 18).** After reading part of the input, the DFA's state is **the set of all states the NFA could be in**.\n\n**Step 1, no ε-arrows (slides 19–20).** For N = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F) build M = (Q′, Σ, δ′, q′, F′):\n- Q′ = 𝒫(Q), so |Q′| = 2^|Q|\n- q′ = {q}\n- F′ = {R ∈ Q′ : R ∩ F ≠ ∅}\n- δ′(R, a) = ⋃ over r ∈ R of δ(r, a)\n\n**Step 2, with ε-arrows (slides 21–27).** A computation alternates \"zero or more ε-moves, read one real symbol, zero or more ε-moves, …\" (slide 21). The DFA does \"read a symbol, then all ε-moves\" in **one** step (slide 22). That needs the ε-closure.\n\n**ε-closure (slides 22–24).** C_ε(r) = the set of states reachable from r by **zero or more** ε-arrows. It always contains r. For a set, C_ε(S) = ⋃ over r ∈ S of C_ε(r). Slide 23 also writes it EClos(q); the prep sheet and our solutions write E(q). **All three names mean the same thing.**\n\nSlide 24, on N: C_ε(q1) = {q1}, **C_ε(q2) = {q2, q3}**, C_ε(q3) = {q3}, C_ε(q4) = {q4}, and C_ε({q1, q2}) = {q1, q2, q3}. ✓ (code)\n\n**The general construction (slide 28)** — memorise this box:\n- **Q′ = 𝒫(Q)**\n- **q′ = C_ε({q})** (not just {q})\n- **F′ = {R ∈ Q′ : R ∩ F ≠ ∅}**\n- **δ′(R, a) = ⋃ over r ∈ R of C_ε(δ(r, a))**\n\nThe prep solutions write the same rule as δ′(R, a) = E(⋃ δ(r, a)). These are equal because closing each piece and then taking the union gives the same set as taking the union and then closing.\n\n**Why the DFA's states are sets (slides 25, 36).** A DFA is in exactly **one** state at a time. \"{1,2,3}\" is the **name** of one DFA state; it records the three NFA states the NFA could be in. It could equally be called Y or Λ (slides 34–36 rename them S…Y).\n\n## The worked conversion (slides 29–40)\n\nN = ({1,2,3}, {a,b}, δ, 1, {2}):\n\n| | a | b | ε |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| → 1 | {3} | ∅ | {2} |\n| ∗ 2 | {1} | ∅ | ∅ |\n| 3 | {2} | {2, 3} | ∅ |\n\n**Slide 30's question \"What is Q′?\"** (left blank on the slide): Q′ = 𝒫({1,2,3}) = {∅, {1}, {2}, {3}, {1,2}, {1,3}, {2,3}, {1,2,3}}, eight states.\n- q′ = C_ε({1}) = **{1, 2}** (the ε-arrow 1 → 2)\n- F′ = the sets containing 2 = {{2}, {1,2}, {2,3}, {1,2,3}}\n\n**Full table (slides 31–32), every row verified by code:**\n\n| R | δ′(R, a) | δ′(R, b) | letter (slide 35) | reachable from {1,2}? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ∅ | ∅ | ∅ | ∅ | yes |\n| {1} | {3} | ∅ | S | no |\n| ∗ {2} | {1,2} | ∅ | T | no |\n| {3} | {2} | {2,3} | U | no |\n| → ∗ {1,2} | {1,2,3} | ∅ | V | yes |\n| {1,3} | {2,3} | {2,3} | W | no |\n| ∗ {2,3} | {1,2} | {2,3} | X | yes |\n| ∗ {1,2,3} | {1,2,3} | {2,3} | Y | yes |\n\nTwo rows worked out by hand:\n- δ′({2}, a) = C_ε(δ(2, a)) = C_ε({1}) = {1, 2}. The ε-arrow out of 1 pulls 2 back in.\n- δ′({1,3}, b) = C_ε(δ(1, b)) ∪ C_ε(δ(3, b)) = C_ε(∅) ∪ C_ε({2,3}) = {2, 3}.\n\n**Pruning (slides 38–39).** {1} and {1,3} have no incoming arrows. {3}'s only incoming arrow comes from {1}, and {2}'s only one from {3}. So none of the four can be reached from {1,2}; remove them. **The general rule is reachability from q′**, not \"has an incoming arrow\": {2} and {3} do have incoming arrows and are still unreachable.\n\n**Final DFA (slide 39): 4 states, and ∅ is kept.**\n- {1,2} (start, accept): a → {1,2,3}, b → ∅\n- {1,2,3} (accept): a → itself, b → {2,3}\n- {2,3} (accept): a → {1,2}, b → itself\n- ∅ (dead): a, b → itself\n\n**What language is it?** The slides never say. Every state except ∅ accepts, and the only way into ∅ is a b read in {1,2}. You are in {1,2} at the start and right after \"…ba\". So **L(N) = strings over {a,b} that do not start with b and do not contain bab** (verified to length 12). Accepted: ε, a, ab, aba, abb. Rejected: b, ba, abab and aabab (both contain bab).\n\n## Slides 41–42 · \"Some easy questions\" (Tutorial 4's \"slide 42\")\n\nDFA (Sipser Exercise 1.1, M₂): start q1, F = {q1, q4}.\n- q1: a → q1, b → q2\n- q2: a → q3, b → q4\n- q3: a → q2, b → q1\n- q4: a → q3, b → q4\n\nAnswers (all checked):\n- Start state **q1**; accept states **{q1, q4}**.\n- Run on aabb: **q1, q1, q1, q2, q4** (5 states for 4 symbols). The slide writes this as transitions \"q1, q1→q1, q1→q1, q1→q2, q2→q4\"; on the quiz write the plain state sequence.\n- aabb: **accepted** (ends in q4 ∈ F).\n- ε: **accepted**, because the start state q1 is an accept state.\n\nMr. Hadzo's Tutorial 4 Part 2 has more questions of exactly this form.\n\n## What Monday's quiz (26.10, 11:00) can ask from this lecture\n\nLikely, in this order:\n1. **DFA or NFA, with reasons** (prep Q1, Q9; slide 16). Name the three signs: two arrows on a symbol, a missing arrow, an ε-arrow. Give the δ signature.\n2. **Formal 5-tuple of an NFA / ε-NFA**, with δ as a table including the ε column (prep Q5, Q10, Q11a; slide 12).\n3. **ε-closures** of every state (prep Q11c; slide 24).\n4. **The full NFA → DFA conversion**: start state C_ε(q), table over all 2^|Q| subsets, F′, diagram before pruning, after pruning (prep Q11; slides 29–39).\n5. **Accept or reject a string**, by listing the NFA's computations (slides 6–7) or running the DFA.\n6. \"Easy questions\" on a DFA diagram (slides 41–42).\n\n## Practice questions (quiz style, with solutions — all verified by code)\n\n**P1 · Computations.** For the slide-4 NFA N, list every computation on input 11 and decide.\n_Solution._ Paths: q1 –1→ q1 –1→ q1 (ends in q1); q1 –1→ q1 –1→ q2 (ends in q2); q1 –1→ q1 –1→ q2 –ε→ q3 (ends in q3); q1 –1→ q2 –ε→ q3 –1→ q4 (**ends in q4: accept**); q1 –1→ q2, then the second 1 has no arrow from q2: hang. One path accepts, so **11 ∈ L(N)**.\n\n**P2 · Convert the slide-16 NFA** (q0 loops 0,1; q0 –1→ q1; q1 –0,1→ q2; F = {q2}) to a DFA, the lecture's way.\n_Solution._ No ε-arrows, so q′ = {q0}. All 8 rows:\n\n| R | 0 | 1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| ∅ | ∅ | ∅ |\n| → {q0} | {q0} | {q0,q1} |\n| {q1} | {q2} | {q2} |\n| ∗ {q2} | ∅ | ∅ |\n| {q0,q1} | {q0,q2} | {q0,q1,q2} |\n| ∗ {q0,q2} | {q0} | {q0,q1} |\n| ∗ {q1,q2} | {q2} | {q2} |\n| ∗ {q0,q1,q2} | {q0,q2} | {q0,q1,q2} |\n\nAfter pruning, 4 states remain: {q0}, {q0,q1}, {q0,q2}∗, {q0,q1,q2}∗. ∅ is unreachable here, so there is no dead state. The DFA remembers the last two symbols. Diagram: Figures tab, \"L4 practice P2\".\n\n**P3 · Convert the slide-4 NFA N** (it has an ε-arrow, 16 subsets).\n_Solution._ C_ε(q2) = {q2,q3}, all other closures are singletons. q′ = C_ε(q1) = {q1}. Only the reachable rows (6 of 16):\n\n| R | 0 | 1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| → {q1} | {q1} | {q1,q2,q3} |\n| {q1,q2,q3} | {q1,q3} | {q1,q2,q3,q4} |\n| {q1,q3} | {q1} | {q1,q2,q3,q4} |\n| ∗ {q1,q2,q3,q4} | {q1,q3,q4} | {q1,q2,q3,q4} |\n| ∗ {q1,q3,q4} | {q1,q4} | {q1,q2,q3,q4} |\n| ∗ {q1,q4} | {q1,q4} | {q1,q2,q3,q4} |\n\nCheck row 1: δ′({q1}, 1) = C_ε({q1, q2}) = {q1} ∪ {q2, q3}. **The closure is taken after the move**, which is why q3 appears. Test: 0110 ends in {q1,q3,q4} (accept); 1001 ends in {q1,q2,q3} (reject). On a quiz that says \"list all subsets\", write all 16 rows; the other 10 are unreachable. Diagram: Figures tab, \"L4 practice P3\".\n\n**P4 · A backward ε-arrow.** NFA: p loops on a; p –a→ q; q –b→ r; r –ε→ p; start p; F = {r}. Give the closures, the DFA and the language.\n_Solution._ C_ε(p) = {p}, C_ε(q) = {q}, **C_ε(r) = {r, p}** (the ε-arrow leaves r, so it enlarges r's closure, not p's). q′ = {p}.\n- {p}: a → {p,q}, b → ∅\n- {p,q}: a → {p,q}, b → C_ε({r}) = {p,r}\n- {p,r} (accept): a → {p,q}, b → ∅\n- ∅: dead\n\nL = **(a⁺b)⁺**: one or more blocks, each some a's followed by one b. aab and abab are accepted; abb and ba are rejected. Diagrams: Figures tab, \"L4 practice P4\".\n\n**P5 · True or false?**\n- (a) Every DFA is an NFA. **True**: δ(r, a) = {the one next state}, and δ(r, ε) = ∅.\n- (b) An NFA without ε-arrows is \"not a true NFA\". **False** (slide 20's wording, see errata). Multiple choices or missing arrows already make it nondeterministic.\n- (c) The subset-construction DFA of an n-state NFA can need 2ⁿ states. **True** in the worst case; Q′ = 𝒫(Q).\n- (d) The DFA state {1,2,3} means the DFA is in three states at once. **False** (slide 36). It is one state named after a set.\n- (e) An NFA rejects w if some computation on w hangs. **False**. It rejects only if **no** computation ends in an accept state.\n\n## ⚠️ Lecture 4 slide errata\n\nThis deck is much cleaner than Lecture 2: **every worked example is right**. The problems are in the explanatory text.\n\n| Slide | What it says | What is correct |\n|---|---|---|\n| 20 | \"this NFA is not a true NFA, because it does not allow ε\" | **Wrong.** An NFA without ε-arrows is still an NFA: several arrows on one symbol (or none) already make it nondeterministic. The no-ε case is just the simpler half of the proof. |\n| 26 | \"the special case we proved (**with** ε-transitions)\" | The special case was **without** ε-transitions (slide 18 says so). Also \"e-transitions\" means ε-transitions. |\n| 3 | \"There 3 major differences …\"; \"a dead-end or **hang state**\" | \"There are 3\". Hanging is not a state: it means that copy of the machine has no arrow to follow and dies. |\n| 13 | Uses the y₁⋯yₘ / r₀⋯rₘ acceptance definition, and calls the machine M | That definition only appears on slide 15. Slide 5 called the same machine N1. Same machine, same meaning. |\n| 17 | \"To a causal observer\" | \"casual\" |\n| 7 | \"lead to an the accept state\" | \"lead to the accept state\" (the content is right: exactly 3 computations, none accepts) |\n| 30 | \"Question: What is Q′?\" with no answer; the top text is cut off by the inset picture | Q′ = 𝒫({1,2,3}), all 8 subsets. The cut sentence reads \"…show that N and M accept the same language. Let the DFA be specified by\" |\n| 35 | Legend's last row reads \"{1,2,:\" (cropped) | {1,2,3} = Y |\n| 38 | Prunes by \"no incoming edges\" | That only finds {1} and {1,3}; the slide then chains to {3} and {2}. The rule to state on a quiz is **\"not reachable from the start state\"**. |\n| 42 | Run on aabb given as \"q1, q1→q1, q1→q1, q1→q2, q2→q4\" | The answer is correct but mixes states and transitions. Write the sequence of states: q1, q1, q1, q2, q4. |\n\n## Sources (slide 44)\n\nMaheshwari & Smid (2024); Sipser (2012); Lewis & Papadimitriou (1998); Hasan, IUS lecture notes (2024). The equivalence proof is M&S §2.5 almost verbatim; the running example N is Sipser's N₁ (Fig. 1.27), and the DFA on slide 41 is Sipser Exercise 1.1's M₂.\n","lesson":"# Lesson 04 — NFAs and the subset construction, the engineer's way\n\n_Quiz 1 is Monday 26.10 at 11:00. This lesson is the 20-minute version of chapter 04. Everything here was checked by running the machines in code._\n\n## An NFA is a DFA whose δ returns a set\n\nThink of the types:\n\n- DFA: `delta(state, symbol) → State` — total, exactly one answer.\n- NFA: `delta(state, symbol_or_eps) → Set[State]` — the answer may be empty, a singleton or several states, and the input may be ε.\n\nSo δ : Q × Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q). That one signature explains all three differences on slide 3:\n- **a set with two or more states** = several arrows on one symbol,\n- **the empty set** = a missing arrow, so that branch hangs,\n- **the ε input** = a free move that reads nothing.\n\n⚠️ **Trick (prep Q1).** A machine with one arrow per symbol everywhere *except* a state with no arrows is still not a DFA by slide 3's rule (\"exactly one exiting arrow for each symbol\"). Say \"NFA, because δ is not total\", then add that a dead state fixes it.\n\n## Running an NFA = breadth-first search over sets\n\nSimulating an NFA is the loop you would write anyway:\n\n```\ncurrent = closure({start})\nfor c in w:\n    current = closure(union of delta(r, c) for r in current)\naccept iff current ∩ F is non-empty\n```\n\nThe subset construction is **this loop, tabulated in advance**: every value `current` can take becomes one DFA state. The DFA is in one state at a time; that state is *named* by a set. Slide 36: {1,2,3} could just as well be called Y.\n\n## ε-closure = graph reachability on ε-edges only\n\nC_ε(r) = everything you reach from r following only ε-arrows, **including r itself** (zero moves count).\n\n⚠️ **Trick.** An ε-arrow from x to y enlarges **x's** closure, not y's. In practice P4 (r –ε→ p), C_ε(r) = {r, p} but C_ε(p) = {p}.\n\n⚠️ **Trick.** Close **after every move** and **at the start**. Slide 24's NFA: δ′({q1}, 1) = C_ε({q1, q2}) = {q1, q2, q3}. Forgetting the closure gives {q1, q2}, and every row after it is wrong.\n\n## The four lines to write on the quiz\n\n- Q′ = 𝒫(Q) (2^|Q| states, ∅ included)\n- q′ = C_ε({q})\n- F′ = {R ∈ Q′ : R ∩ F ≠ ∅}\n- δ′(R, a) = ⋃ over r ∈ R of C_ε(δ(r, a))\n\nThen the lecture's layout (slides 31–39), which the prep sheet's Q11 asks for:\n- table with **all** subsets (before pruning),\n- diagram of all of them,\n- remove those **not reachable from q′**,\n- redraw, **keeping ∅ as the dead state** if it is reachable (slide 39 keeps it).\n\n## Rejecting needs every branch\n\nAn NFA accepts if **some** branch ends in an accept state. To show it **rejects**, you must show **every** branch fails: list them, as slide 7 does for 010 (three computations, none ends in q4). One failing branch proves nothing.\n\n## Self-test (answers in chapter 04)\n\n- Slide 16's NFA has 3 states. How many DFA states before pruning? After?  (8; 4)\n- What is the start state of the DFA for slides 29–40, and why is it not {1}?  ({1,2}: the ε-arrow 1 → 2)\n- True or false: an NFA with no ε-arrows is not a real NFA.  (False; slide 20 is wrong)\n","cards":[{"q":"What are the three differences between a DFA and an NFA (slide 3)?","a":"An NFA state may have several arrows (or none) on a symbol; an NFA may have ε-arrows; an NFA path may hang, which is fine if some other path accepts. A DFA has exactly one arrow per symbol from every state and no ε-arrows."},{"q":"Give the 5-tuple definition of an NFA.","a":"M = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F): Q finite set of states, Σ finite alphabet, δ : Q × Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q) the transition function, q ∈ Q the start state, F ⊆ Q the accept states."},{"q":"What are Σ_ε and 𝒫(Q)?","a":"Σ_ε = Σ ∪ {ε}; 𝒫(Q) = {R : R ⊆ Q}, the power set of Q."},{"q":"How does an NFA compute on a choice, and when does it accept?","a":"It splits into copies that follow every option in parallel; a copy with no arrow to follow dies. It accepts if at least one copy is in an accept state when the input ends."},{"q":"What does it mean for an NFA path to \"hang\"?","a":"The current state has no arrow for the next input symbol (and no ε-move helps), so that computation dies before reading the whole input. Example: q3 reading 0 in the slide-4 NFA."},{"q":"Formally, when does NFA M accept w?","a":"If w = ε and q ∈ F, or if w = y₁⋯yₘ with each yᵢ ∈ Σ_ε and there are states r₀,…,rₘ with r₀ = q, rᵢ₊₁ ∈ δ(rᵢ, yᵢ₊₁), and rₘ ∈ F."},{"q":"Slide 13 runs 01100 through N in m = 6 steps. Why 6, not 5?","a":"w is written 0·1·ε·1·0·0: the ε-step q2 → q3 counts as a step yᵢ = ε."},{"q":"What language does the slide-4 NFA (q1 loops; q1–1→q2; q2–0,ε→q3; q3–1→q4; q4 loops) accept?","a":"Binary strings containing 11 or 101."},{"q":"List the computations of the slide-4 NFA on 010 and decide.","a":"q1q1q1q1 (ends q1); q1q1q2q3 (ends q3); q1q1q2 –ε→ q3 then hang on 0. None ends in q4: rejected."},{"q":"Slide 16 (q0 loops 0,1; q0–1→q1; q1–0,1→q2 ✓): DFA or NFA, and why?","a":"NFA: from q0 on 1 it can stay in q0 or go to q1. Also q2 has no outgoing arrows. It accepts strings whose second symbol from the end is 1."},{"q":"State the DFA–NFA equivalence theorem.","a":"A language A is regular iff there is an NFA that accepts A."},{"q":"Subset construction without ε-arrows: Q′, q′, F′, δ′?","a":"Q′ = 𝒫(Q); q′ = {q}; F′ = {R : R ∩ F ≠ ∅}; δ′(R,a) = ⋃ over r∈R of δ(r,a)."},{"q":"Subset construction with ε-arrows: what changes?","a":"q′ = C_ε({q}) and δ′(R,a) = ⋃ over r∈R of C_ε(δ(r,a)). Q′ and F′ stay the same."},{"q":"Define the ε-closure C_ε(r).","a":"The set of states reachable from r by zero or more ε-arrows; it always contains r. C_ε(S) = ⋃ over r∈S of C_ε(r). Also written EClos(r) or E(r)."},{"q":"ε-closures of the slide-4 NFA?","a":"C_ε(q1) = {q1}, C_ε(q2) = {q2,q3}, C_ε(q3) = {q3}, C_ε(q4) = {q4}; C_ε({q1,q2}) = {q1,q2,q3}."},{"q":"Why does the DFA's start state have to be C_ε({q}) rather than {q}?","a":"Before reading the first symbol the NFA can already make ε-moves, so it may be in any state of C_ε(q)."},{"q":"If an NFA has n states, how many states does the subset-construction DFA have before pruning?","a":"2ⁿ, one per subset (|𝒫(Q)| = 2^|Q|), including ∅."},{"q":"Does the DFA state {1,2,3} mean the DFA is in three states?","a":"No. A DFA is always in exactly one state; {1,2,3} is that state's name (it could be called Y). It records which NFA states are possible."},{"q":"Slides 29–40 NFA (1–a→3, 1–ε→2, 2–a→1, 3–a→2, 3–b→{2,3}, F={2}): start state and accept states of the DFA?","a":"q′ = C_ε({1}) = {1,2}. F′ = {{2}, {1,2}, {2,3}, {1,2,3}}."},{"q":"Same NFA: δ′({2}, a) and δ′({1,3}, b)?","a":"δ′({2},a) = C_ε({1}) = {1,2}. δ′({1,3},b) = C_ε(∅) ∪ C_ε({2,3}) = {2,3}."},{"q":"Same NFA: which DFA states are removed by pruning, and what is left?","a":"{1}, {2}, {3}, {1,3} are unreachable from {1,2}. Left: {1,2}, {1,2,3}, {2,3} (all accepting) and ∅ (dead)."},{"q":"What is the general pruning rule?","a":"Remove every state that cannot be reached from the start state. \"No incoming arrow\" is not enough: {2} and {3} have incoming arrows but are still unreachable."},{"q":"What language does the slides 29–40 NFA accept?","a":"Strings over {a,b} that do not start with b and do not contain bab (the only way into ∅ is reading b in {1,2})."},{"q":"Slide 41 DFA (Sipser M₂): run on aabb, and is ε accepted?","a":"q1, q1, q1, q2, q4: accepted. ε is accepted because the start state q1 is an accept state."},{"q":"Slide 20 says an NFA without ε is \"not a true NFA\". Right or wrong?","a":"Wrong. Several arrows on one symbol or missing arrows already make it nondeterministic; the no-ε case is just the easier half of the proof."},{"q":"Slide 26 says \"the special case we proved (with ε-transitions)\". What should it say?","a":"\"without ε-transitions\": the first, simpler case of the proof assumed N has no ε-arrows."},{"q":"NFA p loops a, p–a→q, q–b→r, r–ε→p, F = {r}. What is C_ε(r), and what is the language?","a":"C_ε(r) = {r, p}, because the ε-arrow leaves r. L = (a⁺b)⁺. Pruned DFA: {p}, {p,q}, {p,r}✓, ∅."},{"q":"True or false: every DFA is an NFA.","a":"True. Set δ_N(r,a) = {δ(r,a)} and δ_N(r,ε) = ∅."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/L4s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/L4s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/L4s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/L4s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/L4s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/L4s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/L4s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/L4s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/L4s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/L4s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/L4s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/L4s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/L4s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/L4s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/L4s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/L4s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/L4s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/L4s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/L4s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/L4s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/L4s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/L4s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/L4s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/L4s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/L4s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/L4s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/L4s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/L4s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/L4s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/L4s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/L4s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/L4s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/L4s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/L4s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/L4s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/L4s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/L4s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/L4s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/L4s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/L4s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/L4s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/L4s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/L4s043.jpg"},{"n":44,"src":"slides/L4s044.jpg"},{"n":"Practice P2: DFA for the slide-16 NFA (second symbol from the end is 1), reachable states","src":"slides/L4d_p2_slide16_dfa.png"},{"n":"Practice P3: DFA for the slide-4 NFA (contains 11 or 101), 6 reachable of 16 subsets","src":"slides/L4d_p3_slide4_dfa.png"},{"n":"Practice P4: ε-NFA with a backward ε-arrow, L = (a⁺b)⁺","src":"slides/L4d_p4_enfa.png"},{"n":"Practice P4: its DFA after pruning; Cε(r) = {p, r}","src":"slides/L4d_p4_dfa.png"}]},{"id":"05","title":"Regular Expressions & Closure","slides":"Lecture 5, slides 1–43","md":"# 05 — Regular Operations, Closure and Regular Expressions (Lecture 5)\n\n_Lecture 5, \"Regular Expressions and Regular Languages\", 43 slides, Dr. Kazeem Oladejo, October 2026. Received 02.11.2026. It was given on the day of In-Class Quiz 1 (slide 3: \"Week 5 **Today**\"; slide 42: \"Quiz @11am!\"). Every slide was read from its image, and every regex, identity and NFA on the slides was checked by brute force over all strings up to length 8. **No mathematical errors**; five cosmetic ones are listed at the end._\n\n**For the In-Tutorial Quiz 1 (week 7: Regular Expressions + Pumping Lemma), go to `quizzes/TQ1-prep.md`.** This chapter is the lecture itself.\n\n---\n\n## Recap and the three regular operations (slides 4–8)\n\nA language A is **regular** if some finite automaton M has L(M) = A (slide 4).\n\nFor languages A and B (slide 5):\n- **Union** A ∪ B = {x : x ∈ A or x ∈ B}\n- **Concatenation** A ∘ B = AB = {xy : x ∈ A and y ∈ B}\n- **Star** A* = {x₁x₂…x_k : k ≥ 0 and each xᵢ ∈ A}. Since k = 0 is allowed, **ε ∈ A* always**, even when A = ∅.\n\nFormally (slide 8): A⁰ = {ε}, Aᵏ = A·Aᵏ⁻¹ for k ≥ 1, and A* = ⋃_{k≥0} Aᵏ.\n\n### The class exercise (slides 6–7): A = {0, 01}, B = {1, 10}\n- A ∪ B = {0, 01, 1, 10}\n- AB = {01, 010, 011, 0110}: 0·1, 0·10, 01·1, 01·10\n- A* = {ε, 0, 01, 00, 001, 010, 0101, 000, 0001, 00101, …}\n- \"AB is finite, but A* is countably infinite\" (the slide says \"infinitely countable\").\n\n⚠ **How the slide lists A\\*.** It goes by the number of pieces: A⁰ = {ε}, A¹ = {0, 01}, A² = {00, 001, 010, 0101}, then the start of A³ (000, 0001, 00101). That is **not** shortlex order. Shortlex, the order Tutorial 3 calls \"lexicographic enumeration\", gives ε, 0, 00, 01, 000, 001, 010, 0000, 0001, 0010. Either order is fine if you **say which one you use**. Also note that 011 ∉ A* and 1 ∉ A*: every 1 must come straight after the 0 of its \"01\" piece.\n\n**Trap: count duplicates.** For A = {a, ab} and B = {b, ε}, AB = {ab, a, abb}: 3 strings, not 4, because a·b and ab·ε are both ab.\n\n## Closure (slides 9–18)\n\nA collection is **closed** under an operation if applying the operation to members always gives a member (slide 9: ℕ is closed under ×, not under ÷, since 1/2 ∉ ℕ).\n\n**Theorem (slides 10, 13, 16).** If A and B are regular languages over Σ, then A ∪ B, AB and A* are regular. Each proof builds an **NFA** from NFAs M₁ = (Q₁, Σ, δ₁, q₁, F₁) and M₂ = (Q₂, Σ, δ₂, q₂, F₂), with Q₁ ∩ Q₂ = ∅.\n\n| | **Union** (slides 11–12) | **Concatenation** (slides 14–15) | **Star** (slides 17–18) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| States | {q₀} ∪ Q₁ ∪ Q₂, q₀ **new** | Q₁ ∪ Q₂, **no new state** | {q₀} ∪ Q₁, q₀ **new** |\n| Start | q₀ | q₁ (the slide renames it q₀) | q₀ |\n| Accept | F₁ ∪ F₂ | **F₂ only** (F₁ stops accepting) | **{q₀} ∪ F₁** (q₀ accepts, so ε ∈ A*) |\n| New ε-arrows | q₀ →ε→ q₁ and q₀ →ε→ q₂ | every state of F₁ →ε→ q₂ | q₀ →ε→ q₁, and every state of F₁ →ε→ q₁ |\n\nδ for the union (slide 11), with the other two following the same pattern:\n\n```\n          ⎧ δ₁(r, a)    if r ∈ Q₁\nδ(r, a) = ⎨ δ₂(r, a)    if r ∈ Q₂\n          ⎪ {q₁, q₂}    if r = q₀ and a = ε\n          ⎩ ∅           if r = q₀ and a ≠ ε\n```\n\nConcatenation (slide 14): δ(r, a) = δ₁(r, a) inside Q₁, except δ₁(r, ε) ∪ {q₂} when r ∈ F₁ and a = ε; δ₂ inside Q₂.\nStar (slide 17): δ₁ inside Q₁, except δ₁(r, ε) ∪ {q₁} when r ∈ F₁ and a = ε; δ(q₀, ε) = {q₁}, δ(q₀, a) = ∅ for a ≠ ε.\n\n⚠ **Classic trap (star).** Why a **new** accepting start state, instead of just making q₁ accepting? If q₁ has incoming arrows, making it accepting lets the machine stop at q₁ in the middle of a pass and accept strings outside A* (M&S Exercise 2.7). The new state q₀ has no incoming arrows, so it can only accept ε.\n\n## Regular expressions (slides 19–31)\n\n### Examples over Σ = {0, 1} (slides 19–25)\n\n| Language | Regular expression | Slide |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1st symbol anything, 2nd symbol 0, then only 1s: {00, 001, 0011, …, 10, 101, 1011, …} | (0 ∪ 1)01* | 19 |\n| exactly two 0s | 1*01*01* | 20 |\n| at least two 0s | (0 ∪ 1)*0(0 ∪ 1)*0(0 ∪ 1)* | 21 |\n| 1011 is a substring (asked on slide 22) | (0 ∪ 1)*1011(0 ∪ 1)* | 23 |\n| even length (asked on slide 24) | ((0 ∪ 1)(0 ∪ 1))* | 25 |\n\nPattern: \"**contains** X\" = (0∪1)\\*X(0∪1)\\*; \"**exactly k** of a symbol\" = put that symbol k times, separated by stars of the other symbol; \"**at least k**\" = the same with (0∪1)\\* in the gaps; \"**length condition**\" = star of a block of that length.\n\n### Formal definition (slide 26) and what each one means (slide 28)\nLet Σ be a non-empty alphabet.\n1. ε is a regular expression, and it describes {ε}.\n2. ∅ is a regular expression, and it describes ∅.\n3. Each a ∈ Σ is a regular expression, and it describes {a}.\n4. If R₁ and R₂ are regular expressions, then R₁ ∪ R₂ is one, describing L₁ ∪ L₂.\n5. If R₁ and R₂ are regular expressions, then R₁R₂ is one, describing L₁L₂.\n6. If R is a regular expression, then R* is one, describing L*.\n\nRules 1–3 are the \"building blocks\"; rules 4–6 combine them. Slide 27 builds (0 ∪ 1)\\*101(0 ∪ 1)\\* rule by rule: 0 and 1 (rule 3) → 0 ∪ 1 (rule 4) → (0 ∪ 1)\\* (rule 6) → 10, 101 (rule 5) → (0∪1)\\*101 → (0∪1)\\*101(0∪1)\\* (rule 5).\n\n⚠ **ε vs ∅.** ε describes a language with **one** string (the empty one); ∅ describes the language with **no** strings.\n\n### Language examples (slides 29–31)\n- (0 ∪ ε)(1 ∪ ε) describes {01, 0, 1, ε}.\n- (0 ∪ ε)1\\* describes {0, 01, 011, …} ∪ {ε, 1, 11, …}, which is also described by 01\\* ∪ 1\\*.\n- **1\\*∅ = ∅** (slide 30 proof): L(1\\*∅) = {xy : x ∈ L(1\\*), y ∈ ∅}. There is no y in ∅, so the set is empty. **Concatenating with ∅ kills everything.**\n- **∅\\* = {ε}**: the star always contains ε (k = 0).\n- **Equality** (slide 31): R₁ = R₂ means L(R₁) = L(R₂). So (0 ∪ ε)1\\* = 01\\* ∪ 1\\* even though they are different expressions.\n\n### The 16 identities (slides 32–33) — R₁, R₂, R₃ are regular expressions\n| # | Identity | In words |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | R₁∅ = ∅R₁ = ∅ | ∅ annihilates concatenation |\n| 2 | R₁ε = εR₁ = R₁ | ε is the identity for concatenation |\n| 3 | R₁ ∪ ∅ = ∅ ∪ R₁ = R₁ | ∅ is the identity for union |\n| 4 | R₁ ∪ R₁ = R₁ | union is idempotent |\n| 5 | R₁ ∪ R₂ = R₂ ∪ R₁ | union is commutative |\n| 6 | R₁(R₂ ∪ R₃) = R₁R₂ ∪ R₁R₃ | left distributive |\n| 7 | (R₁ ∪ R₂)R₃ = R₁R₃ ∪ R₂R₃ | right distributive |\n| 8 | R₁(R₂R₃) = (R₁R₂)R₃ | concatenation is associative |\n| 9 | ∅\\* = ε | |\n| 10 | ε\\* = ε | |\n| 11 | (ε ∪ R₁)\\* = R₁\\* | |\n| 12 | (ε ∪ R₁)(ε ∪ R₁)\\* = R₁\\* | |\n| 13 | R₁\\*(ε ∪ R₁) = (ε ∪ R₁)R₁\\* = R₁\\* | |\n| 14 | R₁\\*R₂ ∪ R₂ = R₁\\*R₂ | R₂ is already in R₁\\*R₂ (take zero copies of R₁) |\n| 15 | R₁(R₂R₁)\\* = (R₁R₂)\\*R₁ | \"shifting\": a(ba)\\* = (ab)\\*a |\n| 16 | (R₁ ∪ R₂)\\* = (R₁\\*R₂)\\*R₁\\* = (R₂\\*R₁)\\*R₂\\* | |\n\n**Not identities** (the traps): concatenation is **not commutative** (ab ≠ ba); (R₁ ∪ R₂)\\* ≠ R₁\\* ∪ R₂\\* (ab is in the left one only); (R₁R₂)\\* ≠ R₁\\*R₂\\* (a is in the right one only).\n\n**Slide 34 worked example.** (0 ∪ ε)1\\* = 01\\* ∪ ε1\\* (identity 7) = 01\\* ∪ 1\\* (identity 2). On a quiz, **cite the identity number or name for every step**, the same way you name the laws in MATH204.\n\n## Regular expressions = regular languages (slides 35–39)\n\n**Theorem (slide 35).** A language L is regular **if and only if** some regular expression describes L.\n⚠ The slide cites M&S §2.8.1 for the whole proof. §2.8.1 proves only regex → regular language (by induction on the rules). The other direction, regular language → regex, is **§2.8.2**: write one language equation per DFA state and solve it with the lemma \"L = BL ∪ C and ε ∉ B ⟹ L = B\\*C\" (see `book/B2`).\n\n### The worked example: an NFA for (ab ∪ a)\\* over {a, b} (slides 36–39)\nBuild it the way the expression is built: a, b → ab → ab ∪ a → (ab ∪ a)\\*.\n1. **M₁ for a**: q₀ –a→ q₁, with q₁ accepting. **M₂ for b**: r₀ –b→ r₁, with r₁ accepting.\n2. **M₃ = ab** (concatenation): p₁ –a→ p₂ –ε→ p₃ –b→ p₄. Only p₄ accepts (p₂ no longer does).\n3. **M₄ = ab ∪ a** (union): a new start state s, with s –ε→ p₁ (the ab branch) and s –ε→ q₁ (a fresh copy of the a machine, q₁ –a→ q₂). It accepts at p₄ and q₂.\n4. **M₅ = (ab ∪ a)\\*** (star): a new **accepting** start state n, with n –ε→ s, plus ε-arrows from both accept states back: p₄ –ε→ s and q₂ –ε→ s. It accepts at n, p₄ and q₂. That is 8 states in total, and a simulation confirms it accepts exactly (ab ∪ a)\\*.\n\nOn a quiz, draw each stage. The marker wants to see the three constructions, not just a final machine. A smaller hand-made NFA is also correct (for example, one state that is start and accepting, looping on a, plus a 2-state detour for ab), but say that you built it directly.\n\n## Closure under complement and intersection (slides 40–41)\n\nBoth are \"accepted a priori\" (stated without proof):\n- **Complement:** if A is regular, so is Ā = {w ∈ Σ\\* : w ∉ A}.\n- **Intersection:** if A₁ and A₂ are regular, so is A₁ ∩ A₂.\n\nThe proofs, which are not on the slides but may be asked as \"explain\":\n- **Complement:** take a **DFA** for A and swap its accept and non-accept states. This does **not** work on an NFA, because a string can have an accepting path and a rejecting path at the same time.\n- **Intersection:** use the product DFA (run both machines at once; accept states = pairs where both accept), or use De Morgan's law: A₁ ∩ A₂ = complement of (Ā₁ ∪ Ā₂).\n\n**Why they matter for Question 2:** closure gives a second way to prove a language is **not** regular. Example: {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m ≠ n}. Its complement intersected with 0\\*1\\* is {0ⁿ1ⁿ}, so if it were regular, {0ⁿ1ⁿ} would be too. See TQ1-prep §2.5.\n\nRegular languages are closed under: **∪, ∘, \\*, complement, ∩** (and reversal, difference).\n\n## ⚠ Lecture 5 slide errata (all cosmetic, none changes an answer)\n| Slide | What it says | Correct |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 | \"Carlton University\" | Carleton University |\n| 5 | \"operations on can perform\" | operations one can perform |\n| 7 | \"A\\* is infinitely countable\"; \"first 10 elements\" | countably infinite; the listing is by number of pieces, not shortlex (see above) |\n| 11 | union proof never says Q₁ ∩ Q₂ = ∅ | assume the state sets are disjoint (slide 13 does) |\n| 19 | \"end with zero or more 1s\" | everything after the 2nd symbol is a 1 |\n| 30 | \"{1ᵏ \\| k ≥ 0})includes ε)\" | unbalanced parentheses |\n| 35 | proof \"in section 2.8.1\" | §2.8.1 is regex → language only; the converse is §2.8.2 |\n| 43 | \"Papdimitriou\" | Papadimitriou |\n","lesson":"# Lesson 05 — Regular expressions, the engineer's way\n\n_The 20-minute version of chapter 05 (Lecture 5). For the week-7 tutorial quiz, do `quizzes/TQ1-prep.md` after this._\n\n## A regex is a tiny expression tree with three operators\n\nThink of the grammar:\n\n```\nR ::= ε | ∅ | a          (leaves, one per symbol)\n    | R ∪ R               (union: either branch)\n    | R R                 (concatenation: one then the other)\n    | R*                  (star: zero or more repetitions)\n```\n\nThat is slide 26 in five lines. Every regex is built by these rules, so every proof about regexes (\"each one describes a regular language\") is an induction over this tree, and every NFA built from a regex is assembled bottom-up along the same tree (slides 36–39).\n\nPrecedence, tightest first: **star, then concatenation, then union**. So ab\\* ∪ c means (a(b\\*)) ∪ c.\n\n## The two values people mix up\n\n- ε: the language {ε}. One string, of length 0. It is the identity for concatenation: Rε = R.\n- ∅: the language with **no** strings. It kills concatenation (R∅ = ∅) and disappears in a union (R ∪ ∅ = R).\n- ∅\\* = ε, because the star always contains the empty concatenation.\n\n## Building regexes: think in blocks, never \"subtract\"\n\nA regex cannot say \"not\". To describe \"no 11\", decide which **blocks** can be glued together safely:\n- blocks 0 and 10 can follow each other forever without making 11,\n- and one final 1 may hang at the end.\n\nSo the answer is (0 ∪ 10)\\*(ε ∪ 1). The same thinking gives \"no 001\" = (1 ∪ 01)\\*0\\* and \"no 100\" = 0\\*(1 ∪ 10)\\*.\n\nThen **test it like code**: one string that must match, and one that must not. Every regex on the slides and the tutorial sheet was tested this way, against every string up to length 12. Two of the tutorial sheet's English descriptions failed (see TQ1-prep §1.2).\n\n## Closure = the three NFA constructions are functions on machines\n\n```\nunion(M1, M2)  = new start --ε--> M1.start, M2.start ; accept = F1 ∪ F2\nconcat(M1, M2) = start M1 ; each f in F1 --ε--> M2.start ; accept = F2\nstar(M)        = new ACCEPTING start --ε--> M.start ; each f in F --ε--> M.start\n```\n\nThey take NFAs and return NFAs, so the regular languages are closed under all three. Complement needs a **DFA** (swap the accept states), and intersection is the product machine.\n\n## Where the pumping lemma fits\n\nA regex or a DFA has only finitely many states of memory. Any language that needs **unbounded counting** ({0ⁿ1ⁿ}, {ww}) cannot be described. The pumping lemma turns that intuition into a proof: see TQ1-prep §2.\n","cards":[{"q":"Define union, concatenation and star of languages A and B (slide 5).","a":"A ∪ B = {x : x ∈ A or x ∈ B}; AB = {xy : x ∈ A, y ∈ B}; A\\* = {x₁…x_k : k ≥ 0, each xᵢ ∈ A}. Because k = 0 is allowed, ε ∈ A\\* always."},{"q":"A = {0, 01}, B = {1, 10}. Give A ∪ B and AB.","a":"A ∪ B = {0, 01, 1, 10}; AB = {01, 010, 011, 0110}."},{"q":"For A = {0, 01}, list A⁰, A¹, A². Is 011 in A\\*?","a":"A⁰ = {ε}, A¹ = {0, 01}, A² = {00, 001, 010, 0101}. 011 ∉ A\\*: a 1 can only come right after the 0 of a \"01\" piece, so A\\* has no 11."},{"q":"A = {a, ab}, B = {b, ε}. How many strings are in AB?","a":"Three: {a, ab, abb}. a·b and ab·ε are both ab, so count each string once."},{"q":"What does \"closed under an operation\" mean? Give the slide's example.","a":"Applying the operation to members always gives a member. ℕ is closed under multiplication but not division (1, 2 ∈ ℕ but 1/2 ∉ ℕ)."},{"q":"NFA construction for A ∪ B (slide 11)?","a":"New start state q₀ with ε-arrows to q₁ and q₂; states {q₀} ∪ Q₁ ∪ Q₂; F = F₁ ∪ F₂; δ(q₀, a) = ∅ for a ≠ ε."},{"q":"NFA construction for AB (slide 14)?","a":"No new state. Start q₁, F = F₂ only, and an ε-arrow from every state of F₁ to q₂. The accept states of M₁ stop accepting."},{"q":"NFA construction for A\\* (slide 17), and why the new start state?","a":"New start state q₀, which is accepting (so ε ∈ A\\*), with q₀ –ε→ q₁ and every state of F₁ –ε→ q₁; F = {q₀} ∪ F₁. Making the old q₁ accepting instead could accept extra strings that pass back through q₁."},{"q":"The six rules that define regular expressions (slide 26)?","a":"ε, ∅ and every a ∈ Σ are regular expressions (building blocks). If R₁ and R₂ are, so are R₁ ∪ R₂ and R₁R₂; if R is, so is R\\*."},{"q":"What languages do ε, ∅, 1\\*∅ and ∅\\* describe?","a":"{ε}; ∅; ∅ (concatenating with ∅ gives ∅, since there is no y in ∅); {ε} (the star always contains ε)."},{"q":"Regex for {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w contains exactly two 0s}, and for at least two 0s (slides 20–21)?","a":"1\\*01\\*01\\*; (0∪1)\\*0(0∪1)\\*0(0∪1)\\*."},{"q":"Regex for \"1011 is a substring\" and for \"even length\" (slides 23, 25)?","a":"(0∪1)\\*1011(0∪1)\\*; ((0∪1)(0∪1))\\*."},{"q":"What does (0 ∪ 1)01\\* describe (slide 19)?","a":"Strings whose 2nd symbol is 0 and whose remaining symbols are all 1: {00, 001, 0011, …, 10, 101, …}."},{"q":"What does R₁ = R₂ mean for regular expressions (slide 31)?","a":"They describe the same language, L(R₁) = L(R₂). Example: (0 ∪ ε)1\\* = 01\\* ∪ 1\\*."},{"q":"Prove (0 ∪ ε)1\\* = 01\\* ∪ 1\\* with identities (slide 34).","a":"(0 ∪ ε)1\\* = 01\\* ∪ ε1\\* (identity 7, distributivity) = 01\\* ∪ 1\\* (identity 2, ε is the concatenation identity)."},{"q":"Identities 1–3 (slide 32)?","a":"R∅ = ∅R = ∅; Rε = εR = R; R ∪ ∅ = ∅ ∪ R = R."},{"q":"Identities 9–11 (slide 33)?","a":"∅\\* = ε; ε\\* = ε; (ε ∪ R)\\* = R\\*."},{"q":"Identities 15 and 16?","a":"R₁(R₂R₁)\\* = (R₁R₂)\\*R₁ (e.g. a(ba)\\* = (ab)\\*a); (R₁ ∪ R₂)\\* = (R₁\\*R₂)\\*R₁\\* = (R₂\\*R₁)\\*R₂\\*."},{"q":"True or false: (a ∪ b)\\* = a\\* ∪ b\\*; (ab)\\* = a\\*b\\*.","a":"Both false. ab is in (a ∪ b)\\* but not in a\\* ∪ b\\*; a is in a\\*b\\* but not in (ab)\\*."},{"q":"State the theorem of slide 35 and where its two directions are proved.","a":"L is regular ⟺ some regular expression describes L. Regex → regular is M&S §2.8.1 (induction, NFA constructions); regular → regex is §2.8.2 (language equations, L = BL ∪ C ⟹ L = B\\*C)."},{"q":"Build an NFA for (ab ∪ a)\\* the lecture's way (slides 36–39).","a":"M₁ for a and M₂ for b; concatenate to M₃ (p₁ –a→ p₂ –ε→ p₃ –b→ p₄); union with a fresh a-machine via a new start s; star with a new accepting start n, n –ε→ s, and ε from each accept state back to s. 8 states, accepting n, p₄, q₂."},{"q":"How do you prove closure under complement, and why must it be a DFA?","a":"Swap the accept and non-accept states of a DFA for A. With an NFA, a string can have both an accepting and a rejecting path, so it could be accepted before and after the swap."},{"q":"Two ways to prove closure under intersection?","a":"The product DFA (pairs of states, accepting when both components accept), or De Morgan: A₁ ∩ A₂ = complement of (Ā₁ ∪ Ā₂), using closure under complement and union."},{"q":"Which operations are regular languages closed under (Lecture 5)?","a":"Union, concatenation, star (slides 10–18), complement (40) and intersection (41)."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/L5s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/L5s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/L5s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/L5s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/L5s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/L5s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/L5s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/L5s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/L5s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/L5s010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/L5s011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/L5s012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/L5s013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/L5s014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/L5s015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/L5s016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/L5s017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/L5s018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/L5s019.jpg"},{"n":20,"src":"slides/L5s020.jpg"},{"n":21,"src":"slides/L5s021.jpg"},{"n":22,"src":"slides/L5s022.jpg"},{"n":23,"src":"slides/L5s023.jpg"},{"n":24,"src":"slides/L5s024.jpg"},{"n":25,"src":"slides/L5s025.jpg"},{"n":26,"src":"slides/L5s026.jpg"},{"n":27,"src":"slides/L5s027.jpg"},{"n":28,"src":"slides/L5s028.jpg"},{"n":29,"src":"slides/L5s029.jpg"},{"n":30,"src":"slides/L5s030.jpg"},{"n":31,"src":"slides/L5s031.jpg"},{"n":32,"src":"slides/L5s032.jpg"},{"n":33,"src":"slides/L5s033.jpg"},{"n":34,"src":"slides/L5s034.jpg"},{"n":35,"src":"slides/L5s035.jpg"},{"n":36,"src":"slides/L5s036.jpg"},{"n":37,"src":"slides/L5s037.jpg"},{"n":38,"src":"slides/L5s038.jpg"},{"n":39,"src":"slides/L5s039.jpg"},{"n":40,"src":"slides/L5s040.jpg"},{"n":41,"src":"slides/L5s041.jpg"},{"n":42,"src":"slides/L5s042.jpg"},{"n":43,"src":"slides/L5s043.jpg"}]},{"id":"06","title":"The Pumping Lemma (Tutorial 6)","slides":"Tutorial 6 slides 1–10 + notes (6 pp)","md":"# 06 — The Pumping Lemma (Tutorial 6: slides + notes)\n\n_Ingested 09.11.2026 from `tutorials/Tutorial6/` (`The Pumping Lemma.pdf`, 10 slides, and `CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx`, 6 pages; the notes call it \"Lecture 6\"). **This is the official course version of the pumping lemma.** It uses the same statement and the same 5-line template as `quizzes/TQ1-prep.md` §2, so nothing there changes. **Every proof below was checked by computer for p = 1…10 over every legal split, and there are no errors in either file.** In-Tutorial Assignment 1 is **Tue 10.11.2026 at 10:00 (45 min)**. It has one question on regular languages and one on the pumping lemma._\n\n## The statement (slide 6, notes §1.3)\n\n**Pumping lemma.** Let A be a regular language. Then there is an integer p ≥ 1, the pumping length, such that every s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p can be written as s = xyz, where\n1. |y| ≥ 1 (y ≠ ε),\n2. |xy| ≤ p,\n3. xyⁱz ∈ A for every i ≥ 0.\n\nyⁱ means i copies of y: i = 0 deletes y, and i = 2 doubles it.\n\n**Why it is true (slides 2–5).** A DFA with p states reads the first p symbols and visits p + 1 states, so one state r repeats (pigeonhole). x leads to r, y loops from r back to r, and z leads on to accept. The loop can be skipped or repeated.\n\n## The game (slide 7) — who picks what\n\n| Step | Who | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Opponent | p (any p ≥ 1; you never give it a value) |\n| 2 | **You** | s ∈ L with \\|s\\| ≥ p, written using p |\n| 3 | Opponent | the split xyz with \\|y\\| ≥ 1, \\|xy\\| ≤ p (handle **every** split) |\n| 4 | **You** | i, so that xyⁱz ∉ L |\n\n**Main trick:** choose s so that its first p symbols are all the same letter. Then |xy| ≤ p forces y = aᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p, and there is only one case.\n\n## The five lines (slide 10 — copy this layout)\n\n**Assume.** L is regular, with pumping length p. **Choose.** s ∈ L, |s| ≥ p, in terms of p. **Split.** Use |xy| ≤ p to say y = aᵏ, 1 ≤ k ≤ p. **Pump.** Pick i and show xyⁱz ∉ L. **So.** Contradiction, so L is not regular. ∎\n\nSlide 10 also gives three tips: the lemma cannot prove a language **is** regular; you pick s and i, never p or the split; if you are stuck, try i = 0 and i = 2 first.\n\n## Worked examples (notes §2, slides 8–9)\n\n| Language | s | i | Why xyⁱz ∉ L |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| aⁿb²ⁿ | aᵖb²ᵖ | 2 | a^{p+k}b^{2p}: 2(p + k) ≠ 2p |\n| aᵐbⁿ, m > n | a^{p+1}bᵖ | **0** | a^{p+1−k}bᵖ has at most p a's. Pumping up stays in L. |\n| palindromes | aᵖbaᵖ | 2 | a^{p+k}baᵖ: the b is no longer in the middle. s = aᵖ fails, because every pump is still a palindrome. |\n| a^N, N a square | a^{p²} | 2 | p² < p² + k ≤ p² + p < (p + 1)² |\n\n## Tutorial tasks (notes §3)\n\n- **Task 1. aᵐbⁿ, m ≤ n.** s = aᵖbᵖ, i = **2** (a^{p+k}bᵖ). Pumping down keeps m ≤ n, so i = 0 never works. This is the mirror image of the m > n example.\n- **Task 2. {wcw : w ∈ {a,b}\\*}.** s = aᵖcaᵖ, i = 0: a^{p−k}caᵖ. There is one c, but the two sides differ.\n- **Task 3. #a(w) = 2·#b(w).**\n  - (a) s = a^{2p}bᵖ, i = 0: 2p − k a's against p b's.\n  - (b) Closure: L ∩ a\\*b\\* = {a^{2n}bⁿ}. If L were regular, so would this be, and pumping a^{2p}bᵖ with i = 2 shows it is not. Intersecting with a\\*b\\* removes the mixed orders.\n- **Task 4. Regular or not?**\n  - (a) {aⁿbⁿ : n ≤ 100}: **regular**, because it is finite (101 strings).\n  - (b) Both #a and #b even: **regular** (4-state product DFA).\n  - (c) {a^{2ⁿ}}: **not regular**. s = a^{2ᵖ}, i = 2, and 2ᵖ < 2ᵖ + k < 2ᵖ⁺¹ because p < 2ᵖ.\n- **Task 5. Find the mistake.** The student proved \"a\\*b\\* is not regular\" by choosing x = a^{p−1}, y = ab, z = b^{p−1}. There are two errors:\n  - the student chose the split, which is the opponent's move;\n  - the split is illegal anyway, since |xy| = p + 1 > p.\n\n  a\\*b\\* is regular: every legal y = aᵏ pumps to a string that stays in a\\*b\\*.\n\n## Common mistakes (notes, boxed)\n\n- Setting p to a number.\n- Choosing the split yourself.\n- Using an s that is not in L, or is shorter than p.\n- Trying only i = 2.\n- Concluding \"so L is regular\" when you found no contradiction.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"State the pumping lemma (Tutorial 6 wording).","a":"If A is regular, there is p ≥ 1 such that every s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p can be written s = xyz with |y| ≥ 1, |xy| ≤ p, and xyⁱz ∈ A for every i ≥ 0."},{"q":"Why must a long accepted string repeat a state?","a":"Reading p symbols visits p + 1 states, but there are only p states (pigeonhole). The repeat is a loop, and y is read around it."},{"q":"In the pumping game, which two moves are yours?","a":"The string s (in terms of p) and the pump i. The opponent picks p and the split."},{"q":"What is the \"main trick\" for choosing s?","a":"Make its first p symbols the same letter, so |xy| ≤ p forces y = aᵏ (1 ≤ k ≤ p): one case covers every split."},{"q":"Give the five lines of every proof.","a":"Assume (regular, p) → Choose s → Split (y = aᵏ) → Pump (pick i, xyⁱz ∉ L) → So (contradiction, not regular)."},{"q":"Which s and i for {aⁿb²ⁿ}?","a":"s = aᵖb²ᵖ, i = 2: a^{p+k}b^{2p} would need 2(p + k) = 2p, so k = 0."},{"q":"Which s and i for {aᵐbⁿ : m > n}?","a":"s = a^{p+1}bᵖ, i = 0: at most p a's remain, so m > n fails. i = 2 keeps the string in L."},{"q":"Why does s = aᵖ fail for palindromes, and what works?","a":"Every pumped aⁿ is still a palindrome. Use s = aᵖbaᵖ, i = 2."},{"q":"Prove {a^N : N a perfect square} is not regular.","a":"s = a^{p²}, i = 2: p² < p² + k ≤ p² + p < (p + 1)², so the length is not a square."},{"q":"Which i for {aᵐbⁿ : m ≤ n} with s = aᵖbᵖ, and why not the other?","a":"i = 2 (too many a's). i = 0 removes a's and stays in L."},{"q":"Prove {wcw : w ∈ {a,b}\\*} is not regular.","a":"s = aᵖcaᵖ, y = aᵏ in the left block, i = 0: a^{p−k}caᵖ, and the two sides differ."},{"q":"Give the closure proof that {w : #a = 2·#b} is not regular.","a":"L ∩ a\\*b\\* = {a^{2n}bⁿ}. Regular languages are closed under ∩, and pumping a^{2p}bᵖ with i = 2 shows this intersection is not regular, so L is not regular."},{"q":"Is {aⁿbⁿ : 0 ≤ n ≤ 100} regular?","a":"Yes. It is finite (101 strings), and every finite language is regular."},{"q":"Is {a^{2ⁿ}} regular?","a":"No. s = a^{2ᵖ}, i = 2: 2ᵖ < 2ᵖ + k ≤ 2ᵖ + p < 2ᵖ⁺¹, so the length is not a power of 2."},{"q":"A student proves a\\*b\\* is not regular using x = a^{p−1}, y = ab, z = b^{p−1}. What is wrong?","a":"The student chose the split (the opponent's move), and the split is illegal (|xy| = p + 1). a\\*b\\* is regular anyway."},{"q":"Can the pumping lemma show a language IS regular?","a":"No. Some non-regular languages pass the test. Build a DFA/NFA/regex or use closure."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/T6s001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/T6s002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/T6s003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/T6s004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/T6s005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/T6s006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/T6s007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/T6s008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/T6s009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/T6s010.jpg"}]},{"id":"mr","title":"★ Midterm Review — 5 parts × 20%","slides":"Professor's review document (19 pp) · proofs: direct, constructive, contradiction only","md":"# ★ Midterm Review — the professor's review document, digested\n\n_Ingested 09.11.2026 from `midterm-review/CS313_Midterm_Review.docx` (19 pages; the site's Slides tab shows every page). **Every answer in it was checked by computer, and there are no errors:**_\n- _22 regexes and equivalences, over all strings up to length 10–12;_\n- _the five automata: the aba DFA, the mod-3 DFA, the N₃ and N₅ conversions, and the ends-with-011 DFA;_\n- _the set answers, the 66 / 32,768 count, and all pumping proofs for p = 1…10._\n\n_The midterm is in **week 8** (from Mon 16.11.2026; exact date TBA)._\n\n## Scope — what is on the midterm\n\n| Part | Topic | Weight | Study from |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Set theory | 20% | `01-sets-and-operations.md` + Part 1 below |\n| 2 | Proof techniques | 20% | **only direct, constructive and contradiction** (class vote, professor's message 09.11.2026) |\n| 3 | DFA and NFA | 20% | `04-…`, `quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md`, `quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md` |\n| 4 | Regular expressions | 20% | `05-regular-expressions.md`, `quizzes/TQ1-prep.md` §1 |\n| 5 | Pumping lemma | 20% | `06-pumping-lemma.md`, `quizzes/TQ1-prep.md` §2. **About 60% worked examples, about 40% \"show that\" in general form** |\n\n**Proof techniques are cut to three.** The review document still teaches six techniques; the class voted to keep three.\n- **Kept:** direct, constructive, contradiction.\n- **Out of Part 2:** nonconstructive (counting), pigeonhole and induction.\n\nTwo caveats:\n- Pigeonhole still appears **inside Part 5**: the proof of the pumping lemma itself (review Question 5.2) uses it. Know that one proof.\n- Every pumping-lemma proof is a proof by contradiction.\n\n**Not on the midterm:** context-free languages. The deck map's \"Midterm\" label on B3 and S2 is out of date.\n\n## Part 1 — Set theory (key results)\n\n- |A × B| = |A|·|B|, |P(A)| = 2^|A|, |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|, De Morgan: (A ∪ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ.\n- **Membership vs subset traps (Q1.1),** for A = {1, 2}:\n  - 1 ∈ A ✓;\n  - {1} ∈ A ✗;\n  - {1} ⊆ A ✓;\n  - ∅ ⊆ A ✓;\n  - ∅ ∈ A ✗;\n  - ∅ ∈ {∅} ✓;\n  - {∅} = ∅ ✗;\n  - {1} ∈ P(A) ✓.\n- P(∅) = {∅} (1 element). P({∅}) = {∅, {∅}} (2 elements).\n- A = {1,2,3}, B = {2,3,4}: |A × B| = 9, |P(A)| = 8, |P(A × B)| = 2⁹ = 512. (2,2) ∈ A × B; (4,1) ∉ A × B.\n- 40 students, 25 Java, 18 Python, 7 both: 36 study at least one, **4 neither**, **18 only Java**.\n- Venn regions:\n  - exactly one of two sets is (A − B) ∪ (B − A) = (A ∪ B) − (A ∩ B);\n  - A ∩ B outside C is (A ∩ B) − C = A ∩ B ∩ Cᶜ.\n- {x ∈ ℤ : x² < 10} = {−3, …, 3}; {2n + 1 : n ∈ {0,…,3}} = {1,3,5,7}; {1,4,9,16,25} = {n² : n ∈ ℤ, 1 ≤ n ≤ 5}.\n- **Element-chasing proof (Q1.7):** x ∈ A − (B ∪ C) ⇔ x ∈ A ∧ x ∉ B ∧ x ∉ C ⇔ (x ∈ A ∧ x ∉ B) ∧ (x ∈ A ∧ x ∉ C) ⇔ x ∈ (A − B) ∩ (A − C). Give a reason on every line.\n- Sets of strings: A = {a, ab}, B = {ε, b}. AB = {a, ab, abb}, which is **3 strings, not 4**, because ab is made twice.\n\n## Part 2 — Proof techniques (the three that count)\n\n| Technique | How it starts | Review's example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Direct** | Assume P; reason forward to Q. | L regular ⇒ Σ\\* − L regular. Swap F with Q − F **in a DFA**; in an NFA, swapping fails. |\n| **Constructive** | \"Here is the object…\", then check it works. | For every n ≥ 1, the circle DFA Mₙ: δ(qᵢ, a) = q_{(i+1) mod n}, F = {q₀}, accepts the lengths divisible by n. |\n| **Contradiction** | \"Suppose, for a contradiction, that …\" | Every DFA for {aᵏ : 3 \\| k} has ≥ 3 states. ε forces q₀ to accept; a forces q₁ ≠ q₀ to reject; aa forces δ(q₁, a) = q₁. Then aaa is rejected, which is impossible. |\n\n**Why swapping fails for an NFA (know this):**\n- Take q₀ accepting with an a-loop, and q₀ –a→ q₁ with q₁ not accepting. This accepts all of a\\*.\n- After the swap, it still accepts a through q₁, although the complement is ∅.\n\n### Extra practice, one per technique (written in the same style; each checked)\n\n**P2.1 (direct).** If A ⊆ B, then P(A) ⊆ P(B).\n*Proof.* Let X ∈ P(A). Then X ⊆ A. Since A ⊆ B, every element of X is in B, so X ⊆ B, that is, X ∈ P(B). ∎\n\n**P2.2 (direct).** If L₁ and L₂ are regular, then L₁ ∩ L₂ is regular.\n*Proof.* Take DFAs M₁ = (Q₁, Σ, δ₁, q₁, F₁) and M₂ = (Q₂, Σ, δ₂, q₂, F₂). Let M = (Q₁ × Q₂, Σ, δ, (q₁, q₂), F₁ × F₂) with δ((r, s), a) = (δ₁(r, a), δ₂(s, a)). On any w, M is in the pair of states the two machines would be in. So M accepts w ⇔ both accept ⇔ w ∈ L₁ ∩ L₂. ∎\n\n**P2.3 (constructive).** For every string w = w₁⋯wₙ over Σ there is a DFA with n + 2 states that accepts exactly {w}.\n*Proof.*\n- **States:** q₀, …, qₙ and a trap t.\n- **Moves:** δ(q_{i−1}, wᵢ) = qᵢ for i = 1…n. Every other move goes to t, and t loops on every symbol.\n- **Start and accept:** start q₀, F = {qₙ}.\n\nWhy it works:\n- Reading w follows the chain to qₙ, which accepts.\n- Any other string either leaves the chain into t, stops early in some qᵢ with i < n, or continues past qₙ into t.\n\nSo only w is accepted. ∎\n\n**P2.4 (contradiction).** Every DFA for L = {w ∈ {a,b}\\* : w ends with ab} has at least 3 states. (No DFA with at most 2 states recognizes L; checked exhaustively.)\n*Proof.* Suppose a DFA M with at most 2 states recognizes L, with start state q₀.\n1. ε ∉ L, so q₀ rejects.\n2. ab ∈ L, so M has a second state q₁, and q₁ accepts.\n3. If δ(q₀, a) = q₁, then M accepts a, but a ∉ L. So δ(q₀, a) = q₀.\n4. Then ab ends in δ(q₀, b), which must be q₁, because ab ∈ L.\n5. But then M accepts b, and b ∉ L.\n\nContradiction, so every DFA for L has at least 3 states. ∎\n\n**P2.5 (contradiction, the classic).** {0ⁿ1ⁿ} is not regular: see the pumping lemma (`06-pumping-lemma.md`).\n\n## Part 3 — DFA and NFA (key results)\n\n- **Contains aba:** 4 states, q₀ –a→ q₁ –b→ q₂ –a→ q₃.\n  - Careful arrows: q₁ –a→ q₁, and q₂ –b→ **q₀**, because abb has no useful suffix.\n  - babab is accepted; abbab is rejected.\n- **Binary multiples of 3:** remainder states r₀, r₁, r₂, with r → (2r + c) mod 3. r₀ is both the start and the only accept state.\n  - 1001 = 9 ✓, 1011 = 11 ✗.\n  - 110 ✓, 1100 ✓, and 10110 ends in r₁, since 22 mod 3 = 1.\n- **NFA for \"contains aa or bb\", converted:** 5 DFA states. {q₀,q₁,q₃} and {q₀,q₂,q₃} accept; they could be merged, but the conversion does not have to do this.\n- **N₅ with an ε-arrow:**\n  - **start = C_ε(1) = {1,2}**;\n  - δ′({3}, b) = C_ε({1}) = **{1,2}**. Forgetting this closure is the most common mistake;\n  - ∅ is a trap;\n  - abba is accepted, aab is rejected.\n- **True/false:**\n  - every DFA is an NFA ✓;\n  - at most 2ⁿ states ✓;\n  - two arrows with the same symbol in a DFA ✗;\n  - an accepting start state accepts ε ✓;\n  - \"every path must accept\" ✗;\n  - ∅ can be a DFA state ✓.\n- **Ends with 011:** the NFA has 4 states, and the DFA has only **4** reachable states: {q₀}, {q₀,q₁}, {q₀,q₂}, {q₀,q₃}.\n\n## Part 4 — Regular expressions (key results)\n\n| Language | Regex |\n|---|---|\n| starts a, ends b | a(a ∪ b)\\*b |\n| at least two a's | b\\*ab\\*a(a ∪ b)\\* |\n| exactly one b | a\\*ba\\* |\n| even length | ((a ∪ b)(a ∪ b))\\* |\n| no aa | (b ∪ ab)\\*(ε ∪ a) |\n| does not end with ab | ε ∪ a ∪ b ∪ (a ∪ b)\\*(aa ∪ ba ∪ bb) |\n| #1's a multiple of 3 | 0\\*(10\\*10\\*10\\*)\\* |\n\n| Regex | In words |\n|---|---|\n| (a ∪ b)\\*abb | ends with abb |\n| a\\*(ba\\*ba\\*)\\* | even number of b's |\n| (a ∪ ba)\\*(ε ∪ b) | no bb |\n| ((a ∪ b)³)\\* | length a multiple of 3 |\n| (a ∪ b)\\*a(a ∪ b)(a ∪ b) | third symbol from the end is a |\n| (ε ∪ a)(ε ∪ b) | {ε, a, b, ab} |\n| (ab ∪ b)\\*a | ends with a, no aa |\n\n**Equalities:**\n- (a ∪ b)\\* = (a\\*b\\*)\\* ✓\n- a\\*b\\* ≠ (ab)\\*, witness aab\n- a(ba)\\* = (ab)\\*a ✓\n- (a ∪ b)\\*a(a ∪ b)\\* = b\\*a(a ∪ b)\\* ✓\n- a\\* ∪ b\\* ≠ (a ∪ b)\\*, witness ab\n\n**Automata ↔ regex:**\n- **Example 4.1:** start a, then trap on b, then a 1↔2 cycle. It accepts the strings that **start with a and have even length**: a(a ∪ b)((a ∪ b)(a ∪ b))\\*.\n- **(a ∪ ab)\\*:** a 2-state NFA. q₀ is the start and accepts, with an a-loop on q₀, q₀ –a→ q₁ and q₁ –b→ q₀. Every b comes right after an a.\n- **State elimination on the mod-3 DFA:**\n  1. Remove r₂: r₁ gets the loop 01\\*0.\n  2. Remove r₁: r₀ gets the loop 0 ∪ 1(01\\*0)\\*1.\n  3. Result: **(0 ∪ 1(01\\*0)\\*1)\\***.\n\n## Part 5 — Pumping lemma (how the question is built)\n\n- **About 60% worked examples:** aⁿb²ⁿ (i = 2), aᵐbⁿ with m > n (a^{p+1}bᵖ, i = 0), palindromes (aᵖbaᵖ, i = 2). Write all five lines.\n- **About 40% \"show that\" in general form.**\n  - **Q5.1:** for every k ≥ 1, {aⁿb^{kn}} is not regular. Fix an arbitrary k and use s = aᵖb^{kp}, i = 2. Then k(p + j) = kp would need kj = 0, but k, j ≥ 1. Never give k a value.\n  - **Q5.2:** prove the lemma itself. Take a DFA with p states and let r₀…r_p be the states it visits on the first p symbols. By pigeonhole, r_j = r_l with j < l ≤ p. Set x = s₁⋯s_j, y = s_{j+1}⋯s_l, z = the rest. Then |y| = l − j ≥ 1 and |xy| = l ≤ p, and y is a loop, so xyⁱz ends in the same accept state.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What are the five midterm parts and their weights?","a":"Sets, proof techniques, DFA/NFA, regular expressions, pumping lemma: 20% each."},{"q":"Which proof techniques are on the midterm?","a":"Only direct, constructive and contradiction (class vote). Nonconstructive, pigeonhole and induction are out of Part 2, but pigeonhole is still inside the proof of the pumping lemma."},{"q":"How is the pumping-lemma part split?","a":"About 60% worked examples and about 40% \"show that\" questions in general form."},{"q":"A = {1,2}: true or false? {1} ∈ A, {1} ⊆ A, ∅ ∈ A, {1} ∈ P(A).","a":"False, true, false, true."},{"q":"P(∅) and P({∅})?","a":"{∅} (1 element) and {∅, {∅}} (2 elements)."},{"q":"40 students: 25 Java, 18 Python, 7 both. How many study neither, and how many only Java?","a":"40 − (25 + 18 − 7) = 4 neither; 25 − 7 = 18 only Java."},{"q":"A = {a, ab}, B = {ε, b}. What is AB?","a":"{a, ab, abb}: 3 strings, because ab arises twice."},{"q":"Prove A − (B ∪ C) = (A − B) ∩ (A − C).","a":"Element chasing: x ∈ A ∧ x ∉ B ∧ x ∉ C ⇔ (x ∈ A ∧ x ∉ B) ∧ (x ∈ A ∧ x ∉ C), with a reason on each line."},{"q":"Direct proof: L regular ⇒ Σ\\* − L regular.","a":"Take a DFA for L and replace F by Q − F. Same run, so the new machine accepts w ⇔ w ∉ L."},{"q":"Why does swapping the accept states not complement an NFA?","a":"One string can have an accepting path and a rejecting path. Example: q₀ accepting with an a-loop and q₀ –a→ q₁; after the swap it still accepts a."},{"q":"Constructive proof: a DFA with n states accepting aᵏ exactly when n | k.","a":"States q₀…q_{n−1} in a circle, δ(qᵢ, a) = q_{(i+1) mod n}, start = accept = q₀."},{"q":"Contradiction: why does {aᵏ : 3 | k} need 3 states?","a":"ε forces q₀ to accept; a forces a rejecting q₁; aa forces q₁ to loop on a; then aaa is rejected, which is impossible."},{"q":"Contradiction: why does \"ends with ab\" need 3 states?","a":"q₀ rejects (ε); ab needs an accepting q₁; δ(q₀, a) = q₀ (else a would be accepted); so δ(q₀, b) = q₁, and then b is accepted, which is impossible."},{"q":"In the aba DFA, where does q₂ (input ends ab) go on b?","a":"Back to q₀: abb has no suffix that starts aba."},{"q":"N₅ (ε: 1→2). What are the DFA start state and δ′({3}, b)?","a":"{1,2} for both, because you take the ε-closure after the move."},{"q":"How many DFA states does the ends-with-011 NFA give?","a":"4: {q₀}, {q₀,q₁}, {q₀,q₂}, {q₀,q₃}."},{"q":"Give a regex for \"no two adjacent a's\".","a":"(b ∪ ab)\\*(ε ∪ a)."},{"q":"Give a regex for \"does not end with ab\".","a":"ε ∪ a ∪ b ∪ (a ∪ b)\\*(aa ∪ ba ∪ bb)."},{"q":"Which is NOT equal: a\\* ∪ b\\* vs (a ∪ b)\\*, or a(ba)\\* vs (ab)\\*a?","a":"The first pair (witness ab). The second pair is equal."},{"q":"What does state elimination on the mod-3 DFA give?","a":"(0 ∪ 1(01\\*0)\\*1)\\*."},{"q":"\"Show that {aⁿb^{kn}} is not regular for every k ≥ 1\": what is the key move?","a":"Fix an arbitrary k; s = aᵖb^{kp}, y = aʲ, i = 2; k(p + j) ≠ kp because kj ≥ 1."},{"q":"Prove the pumping lemma in three sentences.","a":"A DFA with p states visits p + 1 states on the first p symbols, so r_j = r_l for some j < l ≤ p. Let y be the input read between them: |y| ≥ 1 and |xy| ≤ p. y is a loop, so xyⁱz is accepted for every i."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/MRp001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/MRp002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/MRp003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/MRp004.jpg"},{"n":5,"src":"slides/MRp005.jpg"},{"n":6,"src":"slides/MRp006.jpg"},{"n":7,"src":"slides/MRp007.jpg"},{"n":8,"src":"slides/MRp008.jpg"},{"n":9,"src":"slides/MRp009.jpg"},{"n":10,"src":"slides/MRp010.jpg"},{"n":11,"src":"slides/MRp011.jpg"},{"n":12,"src":"slides/MRp012.jpg"},{"n":13,"src":"slides/MRp013.jpg"},{"n":14,"src":"slides/MRp014.jpg"},{"n":15,"src":"slides/MRp015.jpg"},{"n":16,"src":"slides/MRp016.jpg"},{"n":17,"src":"slides/MRp017.jpg"},{"n":18,"src":"slides/MRp018.jpg"},{"n":19,"src":"slides/MRp019.jpg"}]},{"id":"tq","title":"★ Tutorial Quiz 1 Prep — Regex + Pumping Lemma","slides":"Tutorial_RegEx sheet (4 pp, 2 answers corrected) + M&S §2.9","md":"# ★ In-Tutorial Quiz 1 — Regular Expressions + Pumping Lemma (week 7, 10%)\n\n> **Confirmed 09.11.2026:** In-Tutorial Assignment 1 is on **Tuesday 10 November 2026 at 10:00**, in the tutorial, and lasts **45 minutes**. Do not be late. It has **2 questions, one per topic: (1) regular languages / regular expressions, and (2) the pumping lemma.** That is about 20 minutes per question, plus 5 to check. \"Regular languages\" may also include regex ↔ automaton and closure, so revise §1.4 and §2.5 too. **Tutorial 6 (pumping lemma, 09.11)** uses exactly the statement and 5-line template below; its new tasks are digested in `06-pumping-lemma.md`, and Mock Quiz B (§3b) is built from it.\n\n_Built 02.11.2026. The professor announced **2 questions: (1) Regular Expressions, (2) Pumping Lemma**. Sources: Lecture 5 (`05-regular-expressions.md`), the tutorial sheet `tutorials/Tutorial5/Tutorial_RegEx.pdf` (4 scanned pages, exercises from Elaine Rich's textbook, the course's third book), and M&S §2.9 for the pumping lemma, which Lecture 5 does not cover. **Every answer on this page was checked by computer**: each regex against its language over all strings up to length 10–12, and each pumping proof over every legal split for p = 1…10._\n\n**Two errors on the tutorial sheet are corrected below (§1.2):** the FSM M description (\"do not contain ba\" should be **ab**) and item 3b (\"starts and ends with a\" should be **starts with a and ends with ba**).\n\n---\n\n## 0. What to expect and how to split the time\n\n| | Question 1 — Regular expressions | Question 2 — Pumping lemma |\n|---|---|---|\n| Likely forms | write a regex for a described language; describe a regex in English; simplify with identities; true/false with proof; regex ↔ automaton; regular operations on small sets | prove a given language is not regular; possibly state the lemma, or say which i/s to choose |\n| Where it comes from | Lecture 5 slides 19–39 + the tutorial sheet (the tutorial is the closest model) | M&S Thm 2.9.1 and its examples; the classics {0ⁿ1ⁿ}, {ww}, {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m > n} |\n| What earns the marks | a correct regex **plus a one-line check** (test it on a string that must be in and one that must not) | the full 5-step template, with **every** split handled through \\|xy\\| ≤ p |\n\nNotation used by the tutorial sheet (Rich's book): ∪ for union, sometimes written + or v; **a⁺ = aa\\*** (\"one or more\"); \"FSM\"/\"DFSM\" = finite state machine, i.e. a DFA (missing arrows go to an undrawn dead state).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Question 1 — Regular expressions\n\n### 1.1 Recipes (the patterns behind every tutorial answer)\n\n| The language says… | Build | Example over {0,1} |\n|---|---|---|\n| contains X | (0∪1)\\* X (0∪1)\\* | contains 001: (0∪1)\\*001(0∪1)\\* |\n| starts with X / ends with X | X(0∪1)\\* / (0∪1)\\*X | ends with 01: (0∪1)\\*01 |\n| exactly k copies of a symbol | that symbol k times, with stars of the others between | exactly two 1s: 0\\*10\\*10\\* |\n| at least k | the same, with (0∪1)\\* in the gaps | at least two 0s: (0∪1)\\*0(0∪1)\\*0(0∪1)\\* |\n| at most one 0 | 1\\*(0 ∪ ε)1\\* | |\n| length condition | the star of a block of that length | multiple of 3: ((0∪1)(0∪1)(0∪1))\\*; odd: (0∪1)((0∪1)(0∪1))\\* |\n| even number of some symbol | 1\\*(01\\*01\\*)\\* (even number of 0s) | |\n| every 0 is immediately followed by 1 | build from the allowed blocks: (1 ∪ 01)\\* | |\n| **does NOT contain X** | the hardest kind: **think about which blocks are safe**, never \"subtract\" | no 11: (0 ∪ 10)\\*(ε ∪ 1); no 001: (1 ∪ 01)\\*0\\*; no 100: 0\\*(1 ∪ 10)\\* |\n\n**Always check your regex with two strings:** one that must be in the language, and one that must not. On a quiz, write the check in one line. It catches most mistakes, and it shows the marker you understand.\n\n### 1.2 The tutorial sheet, solved (every item, with the two corrections)\n\n**Writing regular expressions**\n\n| Language | Sheet's answer | Check |\n|---|---|---|\n| strings of 0s and 1s starting with 0 and ending with 1 | 0(0 ∪ 1)\\*1 | ✓ |\n| {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w has 001 as a substring} | (0 ∪ 1)\\*001(0 ∪ 1)\\* | ✓ |\n| {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w does **not** have 001 as a substring} | (1 ∪ 01)\\*0\\* | ✓ Once 00 appears, a later 1 would make 001, so: blocks of 1 and 01, then only 0s. |\n| {w ∈ {a,b}\\* : w has bba as a substring} | (a ∪ b)\\*bba(a ∪ b)\\* | ✓ |\n| {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : none of the prefixes of w ends in 0} | 1\\* | ✓ No prefix ends in 0 ⟺ w contains no 0. |\n| {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w has 100 as a substring} | (0 ∪ 1)\\*100(0 ∪ 1)\\* | ✓ |\n| {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w does **not** have 100 as a substring} | 0\\*(1 ∪ 10)\\* | ✓ After the first 1, a 0 must be followed by a 1 or end the string. |\n| {w ∈ {a,b}\\* : exactly one occurrence of aaa} | (ab ∪ aab ∪ b)\\* aaa (ba ∪ baa ∪ b)\\* | ✓ (aaaa contains two overlapping occurrences, so it is correctly excluded) |\n| {w ∈ {a,b}\\* : the first a is immediately followed by two b's} | b\\*(ε ∪ abb(a ∪ b)\\*) | ✓ Strings with no a at all are included. Its FSM: T0 (start, accepting) loops on b; T0 –a→ T1 –b→ T2 –b→ T3 (accepting), and T3 loops on a, b. |\n\n**Describing regular expressions in English**\n\n| Expression | Sheet's description | Check |\n|---|---|---|\n| a\\*(ba)a\\*(ba ∪ a\\*)\\*a\\* | strings over {a,b} with at least one b, where every b is immediately followed by at least one a | ✓ |\n| (a ∪ aab)(a ∪ b)\\*(ba ∪ bba) | \"length at least three, both start and end with a\" | ⚠ **WRONG.** The expression always ends in **ba** (ba or bba), so aaa is not in it, even though aaa fits the description. **Correct: strings that start with a and end with ba**, i.e. a(a ∪ b)\\*ba (length ≥ 3 follows automatically). Checked up to length 12. |\n\n**Simplifying**\n\n| Expression | Simpler | Why (cite the identities) |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a ∪ b)\\*(a ∪ ε)b\\* | (a ∪ b)\\* | (a ∪ b)\\* already contains everything; the extra parts add nothing, and the right side ⊆ (a ∪ b)\\* |\n| (∅\\* ∪ b)b\\* | b\\* | ∅\\* = ε (identity 9), and (ε ∪ b)b\\* = b\\* (identity 13) |\n| (a ∪ b)\\*a\\* ∪ b | (a ∪ b)\\* | (a ∪ b)\\*a\\* = (a ∪ b)\\*, and b is already in it |\n| ((a ∪ b)\\*)\\* | (a ∪ b)\\* | starring twice changes nothing |\n| ((a ∪ b)⁺)\\* | (a ∪ b)\\* | (R⁺)\\* = R\\* (the star puts ε back) |\n| ((a ∪ b) ∪ (ab))\\* | (a ∪ b)\\* | ab is already a string over {a, b}, so it adds nothing; the language is regular |\n\n**Is it a regular expression?** E = a⁺aⁿbⁿ: **not a regular expression** (an exponent n tied to another n is not one of the six rules), and the language {aᵐbⁿ : m > n} is **not regular** (pumping lemma: pump DOWN, see PL3 in §2).\n\n**True or false — prove it**\n\n| Statement | Answer | Proof |\n|---|---|---|\n| (ab)\\*a = a(ba)\\* | **True** | Identity 15 with R₁ = a, R₂ = b: a(ba)\\* = (ab)\\*a. Both are a, aba, ababa, … |\n| (a ∪ b)\\*b(a ∪ b)\\* = a\\*b(a ∪ b)\\* | **True** | Both are \"contains at least one b\". The right side splits at the **first** b. |\n| (a ∪ b)\\*b(a ∪ b)\\* ∪ (a ∪ b)\\*a(a ∪ b)\\* = (a ∪ b)\\* | **False** | ε is in the right side but not in the left side (the left side needs at least one symbol). The left side equals (a ∪ b)⁺. One counterexample string is the whole proof. |\n\n**Cardinality**\n- L = a\\*b\\* − {ab}: **countably infinite**. An infinite set minus one element is still countably infinite.\n- L(a\\*b\\* ∪ a): **countably infinite**. L(a) ⊆ L(a\\*b\\*), so L = L(a\\*b\\*). Every infinite language over a finite alphabet is countably infinite.\n\n**Automata ↔ regular expressions**\n- **The q0/q1 DFA** (q0: start, accepting, loops on a; q0 –b→ q1; q1 loops on a; q1 –b→ q0). It accepts the strings with an **even number of b's**. Regex: **(a ∪ ba\\*b)\\***: from q0, either read a and stay, or read b, any number of a's in q1, then b back to q0.\n  - Is ε accepted? **Yes**, because the start state is accepting. (The sheet gives no answer; this is ours.)\n  - Is abbba accepted? **No**: q0 –a→ q0 –b→ q1 –b→ q0 –b→ q1 –a→ q1 ends in the non-accepting q1 (3 b's, odd). (Ours as well.)\n- **FSM M** (S0: start, accepting, loops on b; S0 –a→ S1; S1 accepting, loops on a; S1 –b→ S2; S2 is a dead state looping on a, b).\n  - Regex: **b\\*a\\***. (a ∪ b)a\\*b\\* is not a solution: it matches ab, which M rejects.\n  - In English: all strings where **all the b's come before all the a's**.\n  - ⚠ The sheet adds \"(or do not contain the substring **ba**)\". That is **wrong**: M rejects ab (no ba in it) and accepts ba. **Correct: \"do not contain the substring ab\".**\n- **Smallest DFSM for (ba ∪ bbab)\\***: U0 (start, the only accept state) –b→ U1; U1 –a→ U0; U1 –b→ U2; U2 –a→ U3; U3 –b→ U0. Every missing arrow goes to a dead state. Drawing that 5th dead state makes the machine a complete DFA, so draw it unless the question says FSM/DFSM in Rich's style.\n\n### 1.3 Practice — write the regex (answers verified over all strings ≤ 11)\n| # | Language over {0,1} | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| W1 | ends with 01 | (0∪1)\\*01 |\n| W2 | at most one 0 | 1\\*(0 ∪ ε)1\\* |\n| W3 | non-empty, first symbol = last symbol | 0 ∪ 1 ∪ 0(0∪1)\\*0 ∪ 1(0∪1)\\*1 |\n| W4 | length is a multiple of 3 | ((0∪1)(0∪1)(0∪1))\\* |\n| W5 | does not contain 11 | (0 ∪ 10)\\*(ε ∪ 1) |\n| W6 | every 0 is immediately followed by a 1 | (1 ∪ 01)\\* |\n| W7 | an even number of 0s | 1\\*(01\\*01\\*)\\* |\n| W8 | odd length | (0∪1)((0∪1)(0∪1))\\* |\n| W9 | starts with 1 and has even length | 1(0∪1)((0∪1)(0∪1))\\* |\n| W10 | contains at least one 0 and at least one 1 | (0∪1)\\*(01 ∪ 10)(0∪1)\\* |\n| W11 | exactly two 1s | 0\\*10\\*10\\* |\n| W12 | no two consecutive 0s | (0 ∪ ε)(1 ∪ 10)\\* |\n| W13 | over {a,b}: length at most 2 | (a ∪ b ∪ ε)(a ∪ b ∪ ε) |\n\n**Describe in English:** (0∪1)\\*00(0∪1)\\* = contains 00 · 0\\*10\\*10\\* = exactly two 1s · (a∪b)\\*a(a∪b)(a∪b) = the third symbol from the end is a · (0 ∪ ε)(1 ∪ 10)\\* = no two consecutive 0s.\n\n**True or false (verified):**\n- (a ∪ ε)\\* = a\\* **T** (identity 11)\n- (a\\*b\\*)\\* = (a ∪ b)\\* **T**\n- a\\*a = aa\\* **T**\n- b\\*(ab\\*)\\* = (a ∪ b)\\* **T** (identity 16)\n- (a ∪ b)\\* = a\\* ∪ b\\* **F** (ab)\n- (ab)\\* = a\\*b\\* **F** (a)\n- (a ∪ ab)\\* = (a ∪ b)\\* **F** (b)\n- a(ba)\\*b = (ab)\\*ab **T**\n\n**Regular operations on finite sets:** A = {a, ab}, B = {b, ε}.\n- AB = {a, ab, abb}: **3** strings, because a·b = ab·ε.\n- A² = {aa, aab, aba, abab}.\n- A ∪ B = {a, ab, b, ε}.\n\n### 1.4 Regex → NFA (if the question says \"construct an NFA\")\nUse Lecture 5's three constructions in the order the regex is built, drawing each stage (slides 36–39 do this for (ab ∪ a)\\*). The three rules:\n- **Union:** a new start state with ε-arrows to both machines.\n- **Concatenation:** ε-arrows from the first machine's accept states to the second machine's start; the first machine's accept states stop accepting.\n- **Star:** a new **accepting** start state with an ε-arrow to the old start, plus ε-arrows from the accept states back to the old start.\n\n---\n## 2. Question 2 — the Pumping Lemma\n\nLecture 5 does not cover the pumping lemma (it ends with closure under complement and intersection), so the statement below is the course textbook's, **M&S Theorem 2.9.1**. Lecture 5 slide 35 cites M&S §2.8.1 for the proof before it, so this is the notation the course uses. Sipser's Theorem 1.70 is the same statement, with conditions numbered 3–2–1 instead of 1–2–3.\n\n### 2.1 The statement — write it exactly, with every quantifier\n\n**Pumping Lemma (M&S Thm 2.9.1).** Let A be a regular language. Then there exists an integer p ≥ 1, called the **pumping length**, such that every string s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p can be written as s = xyz, such that\n1. y ≠ ε (that is, |y| ≥ 1),\n2. |xy| ≤ p, and\n3. for all i ≥ 0, xyⁱz ∈ A.\n\n**Quantifier order: ∃p ∀s ∃x,y,z ∀i.** Marks are lost for getting any of these wrong.\n\n**Why it is true (say this if asked \"explain\"):** take a DFA for A with p states. Reading the first p symbols of s visits p + 1 states, so by the **pigeonhole principle** some state repeats within the first p symbols. y is the piece of input read around that loop, so y ≠ ε and |xy| ≤ p. The loop can be run 0, 1, 2, … times, so xyⁱz is accepted for every i.\n\n**What it can and cannot do:** it can only prove that a language is **not** regular. Satisfying the pumping conditions does **not** prove a language is regular, because the converse of the lemma is false (M&S Exercise 2.30).\n\n### 2.2 The game — who chooses what\n\n| The opponent chooses (you must handle EVERY choice) | You choose (one good choice is enough) |\n|---|---|\n| the pumping length p | the string s ∈ A with \\|s\\| ≥ p, written in terms of p |\n| the split s = xyz (any split with y ≠ ε, \\|xy\\| ≤ p) | the value of i (usually 2, sometimes 0) |\n\nTwo classic mistakes cost the most: **(1) picking a number for p** (\"let p = 3\"), and **(2) picking one particular split** (\"let x = 0, y = 0\") instead of arguing about every split. The condition |xy| ≤ p is what lets you argue about every split at once: if s starts with p copies of the same symbol, y must lie inside them.\n\n### 2.3 The 5-step template (copy this layout on the quiz)\n\n1. **Assume** A is regular. Let p ≥ 1 be the pumping length given by the pumping lemma.\n2. **Choose** s = … (in terms of p). Then s ∈ A because …, and |s| = … ≥ p.\n3. By the pumping lemma, s = xyz with y ≠ ε, |xy| ≤ p, and xyⁱz ∈ A for all i ≥ 0. Since |xy| ≤ p and the first p symbols of s are all …, **x and y consist only of …**. So y = …ᵏ for some k with 1 ≤ k ≤ p.\n4. **Take i = …**: xyⁱz = … . This string is **not in A** because … .\n5. This **contradicts** condition 3 of the pumping lemma. Therefore A is **not regular**. ∎\n\n### 2.4 Worked proofs — from the standard to the tricky (every claim checked by computer for p = 1…12, over every legal split)\n\n**PL1. A = {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0}** (the one you must be able to write in your sleep)\n1. Assume A is regular; let p be the pumping length.\n2. Choose s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ. s ∈ A, and |s| = 2p ≥ p.\n3. s = xyz with y ≠ ε, |xy| ≤ p. The first p symbols of s are 0s, so y = 0ᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p.\n4. Take i = 2: xy²z = 0^{p+k}1ᵖ. It has p + k > p zeros but only p ones, so it is not in A.\n5. This contradicts condition 3, so A is not regular. ∎\n\n**PL2. A = {w ∈ {0,1}* : w has as many 0s as 1s}.** Same s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ, same argument: y = 0ᵏ, and xy²z has p + k zeros and p ones. ⚠ **The choice of s matters:** s = (01)ᵖ fails, because the opponent can take y = 01, and then every xyⁱz is still balanced.\n\n**PL3. A = {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m > n ≥ 0} — you must pump DOWN.**\n2. Choose s = 0^{p+1}1ᵖ. s ∈ A (p + 1 > p), and |s| = 2p + 1 ≥ p.\n3. |xy| ≤ p, so y = 0ᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p.\n4. Take **i = 0**: xz = 0^{p+1−k}1ᵖ. Since k ≥ 1, the number of 0s is p + 1 − k ≤ p, which is not greater than p, so xz ∉ A.\n5. Contradiction, so A is not regular. ∎\n*Why not i = 2?* xy²z = 0^{p+1+k}1ᵖ still has more 0s than 1s, so it stays in A and gives no contradiction.\n\n**PL4. A = {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m < n}.** s = 0ᵖ1^{p+1}, y = 0ᵏ, i = 2: 0^{p+k}1^{p+1} has p + k ≥ p + 1 zeros, so m < n fails. Not regular. (Here pumping **up** works: you add 0s until they catch up.)\n\n**PL5. A = {ww : w ∈ {0,1}*}.** s = 0ᵖ1 0ᵖ1 (it is ww with w = 0ᵖ1). y = 0ᵏ lies in the first block; xy²z = 0^{p+k}1 0ᵖ1. Suppose it were uu. It contains exactly two 1s, so each u contains exactly one 1; u is the second half, so it ends with the string's last symbol, 1. So u = 0ʲ1 and uu = 0ʲ1 0ʲ1, which needs j = p + k and j = p at once: impossible, because k ≥ 1. So xy²z ∉ A, and A is not regular. ⚠ s = 0^{2p} **fails**: if y has even length, every pumped string is still of the form ww.\n\n**PL6. Palindromes, A = {w ∈ {0,1}* : w = wᴿ}.** s = 0ᵖ1 0ᵖ. y = 0ᵏ in the first block; xy²z = 0^{p+k}1 0ᵖ, whose reverse is 0ᵖ1 0^{p+k}, which is different. Not regular.\n\n**PL7. A = {aⁿbᵐaⁿ : n, m ≥ 0}** (M&S exercise 2.20.3). s = aᵖbaᵖ. y = aᵏ in the first block; xy²z = a^{p+k}baᵖ has unequal outer blocks. Not regular.\n\n**PL8. A = {aⁿb²ⁿ : n ≥ 0}.** s = aᵖb^{2p}. y = aᵏ; xy²z = a^{p+k}b^{2p}, and 2(p + k) ≠ 2p. Not regular.\n\n**PL9. A = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 0}.** s = aᵖbᵖcᵖ. y = aᵏ; xy²z = a^{p+k}bᵖcᵖ ∉ A. Not regular.\n\n**PL10. A = {1^{n²} : n ≥ 0} — a length argument (unary language).**\n2. s = 1^{p²}, |s| = p² ≥ p.\n3. y = 1ᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p (any split; the alphabet has one symbol).\n4. i = 2: |xy²z| = p² + k, and p² < p² + k ≤ p² + p < p² + 2p + 1 = (p + 1)². So the length lies strictly between two consecutive squares and is not a square: xy²z ∉ A.\n5. Contradiction, so A is not regular. ∎\n\n**PL11. A = {0^{2ⁿ} : n ≥ 0} (lengths that are powers of 2).** s = 0^{2ᵖ}. y = 0ᵏ, 1 ≤ k ≤ p. i = 2: length 2ᵖ + k, with 2ᵖ < 2ᵖ + k ≤ 2ᵖ + p < 2ᵖ⁺¹ (since p < 2ᵖ). It lies strictly between two consecutive powers of 2, so xy²z ∉ A.\n\n**PL12. A = {1ⁿ : n is prime}.** s = 1ⁿ with n ≥ max(p, 2) prime. y = 1ᵏ, 1 ≤ k ≤ p. Take **i = n + 1**: length n + nk = n(1 + k), a product of two integers ≥ 2, so not prime. Not regular. (The value of i can depend on n; it does not have to be 0 or 2.)\n\n### 2.5 Closure shortcut (accepted as a proof, and sometimes much easier)\n\n**A = {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m ≠ n}.** Pumping is awkward here. Instead: suppose A is regular. Lecture 5 (slides 40–41) gives closure under complement and intersection, so Ā ∩ L(0*1*) would be regular. But Ā ∩ 0*1* = {0ⁿ1ⁿ}, which is not regular (PL1). Contradiction.\n\n### 2.6 Is it regular at all? — check before you start pumping\n\n| Language | Regular? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| {aⁿbᵐ : n, m ≥ 0} | **Yes** | a*b*. No link between n and m. |\n| {aⁿbⁿ : 0 ≤ n ≤ 100} | **Yes** | It is finite, and every finite language is regular. |\n| {aⁿ : n is even} | **Yes** | (aa)* |\n| {w : #01(w) = #10(w)} | **Yes** | It is ε plus the strings that start and end with the same symbol (M&S example 7). |\n| {w : #a(w) ≡ #b(w) (mod 3)} | **Yes** | A DFA can count mod 3. |\n| {aⁿbⁿ}, {ww}, palindromes, {1^{n²}}, {1^{prime}} | **No** | PL1, PL5, PL6, PL10, PL12 |\n\nRule of thumb: a language is non-regular when recognising it needs **unbounded counting or matching** (comparing two counts, or remembering a whole first half). A finite automaton has only p states of memory.\n\n### 2.7 Pumping-lemma checklist (the mistakes graders look for)\n- [ ] Statement written with **∃p ∀s ∃xyz ∀i** and all three conditions.\n- [ ] p is **not** given a number.\n- [ ] s is written in terms of p, **is in A**, and has |s| ≥ p.\n- [ ] You argue about **every** split, using |xy| ≤ p to pin down what y is.\n- [ ] One specific i, with the computed string xyⁱz written out.\n- [ ] Say exactly why xyⁱz ∉ A (count the symbols).\n- [ ] End with \"contradiction, therefore A is not regular\".\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mock In-Tutorial Quiz (2 questions — do it on paper, then check)\n\n**Question 1 (Regular expressions).** Σ = {a, b} unless stated otherwise.\n\n(a) Write a regular expression for {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w does not contain 11}.\n\n(b) Describe in English the language of (a ∪ b)\\*a(a ∪ b)(a ∪ b).\n\n(c) Simplify (a ∪ ε)\\*b ∪ b, naming the identity used at each step.\n\n(d) True or false: (a\\*b\\*)\\* = (a ∪ b)\\*. Justify.\n\n(e) Let A = {a, ab} and B = {b, ε}. Give AB and A ∪ B.\n\n**Question 2 (Pumping lemma).** Prove that A = {aⁱbʲ : 0 ≤ i ≤ j} is not regular.\n\n### Answers\n**1(a)** (0 ∪ 10)\\*(ε ∪ 1). Every 1 that is not last must be followed by a 0, so build from the blocks 0 and 10, then allow one final 1. Check: 0101 ✓ is in; 0110 ✗ is not matched.\n\n**1(b)** All strings of length ≥ 3 whose **third symbol from the end is a**.\n\n**1(c)** (a ∪ ε)\\*b ∪ b = a\\*b ∪ b (identity 11: (ε ∪ R)\\* = R\\*, with union commutative, identity 5) = a\\*b (identity 14: R₁\\*R₂ ∪ R₂ = R₁\\*R₂).\n\n**1(d)** **True.** ⊆: every string in (a\\*b\\*)\\* is made of a's and b's. ⊇: any string over {a, b} is a sequence of single symbols, and each symbol a = a¹b⁰ and b = a⁰b¹ is in a\\*b\\*, so the whole string is in (a\\*b\\*)\\*.\n\n**1(e)** AB = {a, ab, abb} (ab arises twice, as a·b and ab·ε, and is listed once). A ∪ B = {ε, a, b, ab}.\n\n**2.**\n1. Assume A is regular, and let p ≥ 1 be its pumping length.\n2. Choose s = aᵖbᵖ. s ∈ A because p ≤ p, and |s| = 2p ≥ p.\n3. By the pumping lemma, s = xyz with y ≠ ε, |xy| ≤ p, and xyⁱz ∈ A for all i ≥ 0. The first p symbols of s are all a, and |xy| ≤ p, so y = aᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p.\n4. Take i = 2: xy²z = a^{p+k}bᵖ. Since k ≥ 1, p + k > p, so the number of a's exceeds the number of b's and xy²z ∉ A.\n5. This contradicts condition 3. Therefore A is not regular. ∎\n(Pumping down, i = 0, would give a^{p−k}bᵖ, which is still in A. That is why i = 2 is the right choice here.)\n\n**Extra pumping drills** (all verified):\n- {0ⁿ1 0ⁿ : n ≥ 0}: take s = 0ᵖ10ᵖ and i = 2.\n- {aⁿb²ⁿ}: see PL8.\n- {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m > n}: pump **down** (PL3).\n- {w : #0 = #1}: take s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ, **not** (01)ᵖ.\n\n### 3b. Mock Quiz B (built 09.11 from Tutorial 6 and the midterm review; every answer checked by computer)\n\n**Question 1 (Regular languages).** Σ = {a, b}.\n\n(a) Write a regular expression for the strings with an even number of a's.\n\n(b) Describe in words the language of (a ∪ b)\\*b(a ∪ b).\n\n(c) Is {w : #a(w) is a multiple of 3} regular? Justify.\n\n(d) Draw an NFA for (a ∪ ba)\\* and describe its language.\n\n**Question 2 (Pumping lemma).**\n\n(a) Prove that L = {aᵐbⁿ : m ≥ 2n} is not regular.\n\n(b) Show that for every integer k ≥ 1, L_k = {a^{n+k}bⁿ : n ≥ 0} is not regular.\n\n**Answers.**\n\n**1(a)** b\\*(ab\\*ab\\*)\\*. Each block adds exactly two a's. Check: abba is in; ab is not.\n\n**1(b)** Strings of length ≥ 2 whose second-to-last symbol is b.\n\n**1(c)** Yes. A 3-state DFA counts the a's mod 3, or use the regex b\\*(ab\\*ab\\*ab\\*)\\*. Counting **mod** a fixed number needs only finitely many states. The pumping lemma would be the wrong tool here.\n\n**1(d)** Two states:\n- q₀ is the start and also accepting;\n- q₀ has an a-loop;\n- q₀ –b→ q₁ and q₁ –a→ q₀.\n\nLanguage: no bb, and the string does not end with b. In other words, every b is immediately followed by a.\n\n**2(a)**\n1. **Assume** L is regular, with pumping length p.\n2. **Choose** s = a^{2p}bᵖ ∈ L (2p ≥ 2p), with |s| = 3p ≥ p.\n3. **Split:** the first p symbols are a's and |xy| ≤ p, so y = aᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p.\n4. **Pump** with **i = 0**: xz = a^{2p−k}bᵖ, and 2p − k < 2p, so xz ∉ L.\n\n   Pumping up (i = 2) only adds a's and stays in L, so this question needs i = 0.\n5. **So:** this is a contradiction, and L is not regular. ∎\n\n**2(b)** Fix an arbitrary k ≥ 1.\n1. **Assume** L_k is regular, with pumping length p.\n2. **Choose** s = a^{p+k}bᵖ ∈ L_k (take n = p), with |s| ≥ p.\n3. **Split:** y = aʲ with 1 ≤ j ≤ p.\n4. **Pump** with **i = 0**: xz = a^{p+k−j}bᵖ. To be in L_k it would need p + k − j = p + k, that is, j = 0. But j ≥ 1, so xz ∉ L_k.\n\n   i = 2 works too here: the string gets j too many a's.\n5. **So:** this is a contradiction, and since k was arbitrary, L_k is not regular for every k ≥ 1. ∎\n\n**From Tutorial 6, also drill these:**\n- {wcw}: s = aᵖcaᵖ, i = 0.\n- #a = 2·#b: s = a^{2p}bᵖ, i = 0, or the closure proof with ∩ a\\*b\\*.\n- {a^{2ⁿ}}: s = a^{2ᵖ}, i = 2.\n- **Find the mistake:** the student chose the split.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Last hour before the quiz\n1. Write the pumping lemma from memory: ∃p ≥ 1 ∀s ∈ A, |s| ≥ p, ∃x, y, z with s = xyz, y ≠ ε, |xy| ≤ p, ∀i ≥ 0: xyⁱz ∈ A.\n2. Write PL1 ({0ⁿ1ⁿ}) and PL3 ({0ᵐ1ⁿ : m > n}, pump down) completely, without looking.\n3. Cover the answers in §1.2 and redo the two \"does not contain\" regexes, (1 ∪ 01)\\*0\\* and 0\\*(1 ∪ 10)\\*.\n4. Say out loud the 16 identities' key four: R∅ = ∅, Rε = R, ∅\\* = ε, (ε ∪ R)\\* = R\\*.\n5. Remember the two tutorial corrections (FSM M: no **ab**; 3b: ends in **ba**).\n6. Do Mock Quiz B (§3b) on paper in 40 minutes. For each pumping question, decide first whether i = 0 or i = 2 breaks the language.\n7. Flick through Tutorial 6's slides 7, 9 and 10: the game, choosing s and i, and the five steps.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What two questions are on In-Tutorial Quiz 1 (week 7)?","a":"(1) Regular expressions and (2) the pumping lemma (announced by the professor)."},{"q":"State the pumping lemma for regular languages exactly (M&S Thm 2.9.1).","a":"If A is regular, there exists an integer p ≥ 1 such that every s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p can be written s = xyz with (1) y ≠ ε, (2) |xy| ≤ p, (3) xyⁱz ∈ A for all i ≥ 0."},{"q":"In a pumping proof, what do you choose and what must you handle for every possibility?","a":"You choose s (in terms of p) and i. You must handle every p and every split xyz satisfying y ≠ ε and |xy| ≤ p."},{"q":"Give the 5-step pumping template.","a":"(1) Assume A regular, p the pumping length; (2) pick s ∈ A, |s| ≥ p; (3) use |xy| ≤ p to say what y is, y = aᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p; (4) pick i and show xyⁱz ∉ A; (5) contradiction, so A is not regular."},{"q":"Prove {0ⁿ1ⁿ} is not regular in one line.","a":"s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ; |xy| ≤ p forces y = 0ᵏ, k ≥ 1; xy²z = 0^{p+k}1ᵖ ∉ A. Contradiction."},{"q":"For {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m > n}, which s and which i?","a":"s = 0^{p+1}1ᵖ and i = 0 (pump down): xz = 0^{p+1−k}1ᵖ has at most p zeros, so m > n fails. i = 2 stays in A."},{"q":"Why does s = (01)ᵖ fail for {w : #0 = #1}?","a":"The opponent may choose y = 01; pumping it keeps the counts equal. Use s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ."},{"q":"Why does s = 0^{2p} fail for {ww}? What works?","a":"If y has even length, every pumped string is still ww. Use s = 0ᵖ1 0ᵖ1."},{"q":"Prove {1^{n²}} is not regular.","a":"s = 1^{p²}, y = 1ᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p; |xy²z| = p² + k lies strictly between p² and (p+1)² = p² + 2p + 1, so it is not a square."},{"q":"Can the pumping lemma prove a language IS regular?","a":"No. The converse is false; some non-regular languages satisfy the pumping conditions. To show regular, give a DFA, NFA or regex."},{"q":"Prove {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m ≠ n} is not regular without pumping.","a":"If it were regular, its complement ∩ 0\\*1\\* = {0ⁿ1ⁿ} would be regular (closure under complement and intersection, Lecture 5 slides 40–41). Contradiction."},{"q":"Is {w : #01(w) = #10(w)} regular?","a":"Yes. It is ε plus the strings whose first and last symbols are equal: ε ∪ 0 ∪ 1 ∪ 0(0∪1)\\*0 ∪ 1(0∪1)\\*1."},{"q":"Regex for {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w does not have 001 as a substring}?","a":"(1 ∪ 01)\\*0\\*. Once 00 appears, no 1 may follow."},{"q":"Regex for {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w does not have 100 as a substring}?","a":"0\\*(1 ∪ 10)\\*."},{"q":"Regex for {w ∈ {0,1}\\* : w does not contain 11}?","a":"(0 ∪ 10)\\*(ε ∪ 1)."},{"q":"FSM M accepts b\\*a\\*. Describe L(M), and what is wrong on the tutorial sheet?","a":"All b's come before all a's, i.e. no substring **ab**. The sheet's \"(or do not contain ba)\" is wrong: M accepts ba and rejects ab."},{"q":"Describe (a ∪ aab)(a ∪ b)\\*(ba ∪ bba).","a":"Strings that start with a and end with ba (= a(a ∪ b)\\*ba). The sheet's \"length ≥ 3, starts and ends with a\" is wrong: aaa is not generated."},{"q":"Simplify (∅\\* ∪ b)b\\*.","a":"b\\*: ∅\\* = ε (identity 9), and (ε ∪ b)b\\* = b\\* (identity 13)."},{"q":"True or false: (a ∪ b)\\*b(a ∪ b)\\* ∪ (a ∪ b)\\*a(a ∪ b)\\* = (a ∪ b)\\*.","a":"False. ε is on the right only; the left side is (a ∪ b)⁺."},{"q":"Regex for the 2-state DFA accepting strings with an even number of b's? Is ε accepted?","a":"(a ∪ ba\\*b)\\*. Yes, ε is accepted, because the start state accepts."},{"q":"What is a⁺, and what is (R⁺)\\*?","a":"a⁺ = aa\\* (one or more). (R⁺)\\* = R\\*."},{"q":"Is a⁺aⁿbⁿ a regular expression? Is its language regular?","a":"No: a counter n shared between two places is not one of the six rules. The language {aᵐbⁿ : m > n} is not regular (pump down)."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/T5p001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/T5p002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/T5p003.jpg"},{"n":4,"src":"slides/T5p004.jpg"}]},{"id":"q1","title":"★ Quiz 1 Prep — solved","slides":"Professor's prep sheet (2 pp) + solution diagrams","md":"# In-Class Quiz 1 — the professor's prep questions, solved\n\n**▶ Click-to-answer practice: [open the Quiz Trainer](quiz.html)** — 231 A–D, Yes/No and True/False questions in 5 chapters (L3 DFA 64, L4 NFA 65 — every automaton checked by code — plus L1 sets, L2 relations, L2 proofs), with per-topic analysis and a study plan.\n\n**Source:** `quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf` (shared by the professor, 25.10.2026, without answers).\n**Quiz logistics (her email):** the quiz is in the **3rd hour** of class and **starts promptly at 11:00** — arrive early.\n**Her notes:** Q11 (NFA → DFA conversion) is **new this year**. She recommends Mr. Hadzo's tutorial materials (Teams → Tutorial). Those are now ingested in the chapter **Tutorials 2–4**; Tutorial 2 already contains Q2 and Q6 with answers that match ours.\n**Her warning:** \"There should be no assumed relationship between these practice questions and actual test questions\" — so master the *methods*, not these particular answers.\n\nEvery answer below was **checked by code**: the automata were simulated on all strings up to length 8–9, and the subset construction was run mechanically. The diagrams for Q7, Q8 and Q11 are in this chapter's **Figures** tab, after the two question pages.\n\n---\n\n## Q1–5 · The automaton M\n\nM (as drawn): start q0, accept q1.\n- q0 —a→ q1, q0 —b→ q2\n- q1 —a→ q0, q1 —b→ q1 (self-loop)\n- q2: **no outgoing arrows**\n\n### Q1 · DFA or NFA?\n\n⚠️ **The course's own materials disagree on this one. Read this before the quiz.**\n\n**Update 25.10, Lecture 4 (the professor's own slides) takes the strict view.** Slide 3 says a DFA state \"always has exactly one exiting transition arrow for each symbol in the alphabet\", and slides 3–4 present hanging (no arrow for the next symbol) as something only an NFA does. So **lead with \"NFA\"** and keep the one-line DFSM caveat below. (Chapter 04 has the details.)\n\n- **The strict view: NFA.** A DFA's δ : Q × Σ → Q is *total*, so every state needs exactly one arrow for every symbol. q2 has no a- or b-arrow, so δ(q2, a) and δ(q2, b) are undefined. Mr. Hadzo's Tutorial 3 says \"an empty cell … means δ is undefined there, so the machine is not yet a DFA\". His Tutorial 4 (Q2.2) counts \"some arrows are also missing\" as enough, on its own, to make an automaton an NFA.\n- **The convention view: DFA.** Tutorial 2 contains *this exact machine*, as \"Which of the following will not be accepted by the following **DFSM**?\". It calls M deterministic, under the convention that omitted arrows lead to an unseen dead state (q2 already behaves like one).\n\n**What to write** (covers both readings in one answer): *\"M is deterministic in the sense that no state has two arrows on the same symbol and there are no ε-transitions. However, q2 has no transitions on a or b, so δ is not a total function Q × Σ → Q. By the formal definition, M is therefore not (yet) a DFA but an NFA. Adding a dead state, or a, b self-loops on q2, makes it a DFA that accepts the same language.\"*\n\n**Better still:** ask the professor which answer she expects. Her email invited questions before the quiz. Without an answer from her, \"NFA\" is the answer that matches Lecture 4.\n\n### Q2 · Which string is NOT accepted?\n\n| String | Run | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. ababaabaa** | q0 –a→ q1 –b→ q1 –a→ q0 –**b**→ q2 (stuck) | **rejected** |\n| B. abbbaa | q0 –a→ q1 –bbb→ q1 –a→ q0 –a→ q1 | accepted |\n| C. abbbaabb | q0 –a→ q1 –bbb→ q1 –a→ q0 –a→ q1 –bb→ q1 | accepted |\n| D. abbaabbaa | q0 –a→ q1 –bb→ q1 –a→ q0 –a→ q1 –bb→ q1 –a→ q0 –a→ q1 | accepted |\n\n**Answer: A.** A b read in q0 sends M to q2, and nothing leaves q2.\n\n### Q3 · The language L(M)\n\nEach visit to q0 must be left by **a** (b kills the run). From q1, any number of b's keep you there and an a returns you to q0. So a run is: loops of the form *a b∗ a* back to q0, then a final *a b∗* that ends in q1.\n\n**L(M) = (ab∗a)∗ ab∗**\n\nIn words: **the strings over {a, b} with an odd number of a's, in which every b is preceded by an odd number of a's.** (Checked by code to be identical to the regular expression for all strings up to length 9.)\n\n### Q4 · Transition table\n\n| State | a | b |\n|---|---|---|\n| → q0 | q1 | q2 |\n| ∗ q1 | q0 | q1 |\n| q2 | ∅ (none) | ∅ (none) |\n\n(→ = start, ∗ = accept. Written as an NFA table, the entries are sets: {q1}, {q2}, {q0}, {q1}, ∅, ∅.)\n\n### Q5 · Formal definition\n\nM = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) where\n- Q = {q0, q1, q2}\n- Σ = {a, b}\n- δ is the table in Q4; as an NFA, δ : Q × Σ → 𝒫(Q), with δ(q0,a) = {q1}, δ(q0,b) = {q2}, δ(q1,a) = {q0}, δ(q1,b) = {q1}, δ(q2,a) = δ(q2,b) = ∅\n- start state q₀ = q0\n- F = {q1}\n\n---\n\n## Q6 · First twelve strings of L = {w ∈ {1,2,3}∗ : |w| even}\n\nIn a **lexicographic enumeration** the strings are listed shortest first, and in dictionary order within each length. (This is \"shortlex\" order. Pure dictionary order could never finish listing even the length-2 strings of an infinite language before 11, 1111, 111111, …)\n\n**ε, 11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32, 33, 1111, 1112**\n\n- **ε counts:** |ε| = 0 is even.\n- There are 3² = 9 strings of length 2.\n- Length 4 starts with 1111, 1112.\n- No odd lengths appear.\n\n---\n\n## Q7 · DFA for strings starting with \"ab\" (Σ = {a, b})\n\n| State | a | b | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| → q0 | q1 | qd | nothing read yet |\n| q1 | qd | q2 | read \"a\" |\n| ∗ q2 | q2 | q2 | read \"ab\": accept whatever follows |\n| qd | qd | qd | dead state: wrong start |\n\nL = ab(a ∪ b)∗. **Don't forget qd:** without it this would be an NFA (see Q1). Diagram: Figures tab.\n\n---\n\n## Q8 · NFA for L = {w ∈ {a,b,c}∗ : ∃x, y ∈ {a,b,c}∗ (w = x abcabb y)}\n\nL = all strings that **contain the substring abcabb**. The NFA uses non-determinism to *guess* where the substring starts:\n\n```\n  (loop a,b,c)                                                      (loop a,b,c)\n→ (q0) --a--> (q1) --b--> (q2) --c--> (q3) --a--> (q4) --b--> (q5) --b--> ((q6))\n```\n\n- q0 loops on a, b, c (reading x) and, on an a, may also guess \"abcabb starts here\" and go to q1.\n- q1 … q6 spell out a b c a b b.\n- q6 is accepting and loops on a, b, c (reading y).\n- 7 states; no transitions are needed for wrong letters inside the chain (missing arrows are fine in an NFA).\n\nFormally: N = ({q0,…,q6}, {a,b,c}, δ, q0, {q6}).\n- δ(q0,a) = {q0,q1}, δ(q0,b) = δ(q0,c) = {q0}\n- δ(q1,b) = {q2}, δ(q2,c) = {q3}, δ(q3,a) = {q4}, δ(q4,b) = {q5}, δ(q5,b) = {q6}\n- δ(q6,x) = {q6} for every x\n- every other entry is ∅\n\n---\n\n## Q9–10 · Automaton A (from the table)\n\n| State | 0 | 1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| → q0 | {q0, q1} | {q2} |\n| q1 | {q0} | {q1} |\n| ^ q2 (accept) | ∅ | {q0, q1} |\n\n### Q9 · DFA or NFA? (≥ 2 arguments)\n\n**NFA.** Three reasons:\n1. **More than one next state:** δ(q0, 0) = {q0, q1} and δ(q2, 1) = {q0, q1}. A DFA allows exactly one next state per (state, symbol) pair.\n2. **No next state:** δ(q2, 0) = ∅. A DFA's δ is total, so every pair must have a defined next state.\n3. **The type of δ:** the table's entries are *sets of states*, so δ : Q × Σ → 𝒫(Q) (the NFA signature), not δ : Q × Σ → Q (the DFA signature).\n\n(There is no ε column, so it is an NFA without ε-moves. That doesn't change the answer.)\n\n### Q10 · Formal definition of the transition function\n\nδ : Q × Σ → 𝒫(Q), with Q = {q0, q1, q2} and Σ = {0, 1}:\n\n- δ(q0, 0) = {q0, q1}\n- δ(q0, 1) = {q2}\n- δ(q1, 0) = {q0}\n- δ(q1, 1) = {q1}\n- δ(q2, 0) = ∅\n- δ(q2, 1) = {q0, q1}\n\n(Full machine, if asked: A = (Q, Σ, δ, q0, F) with F = {q2}.)\n\n---\n\n## Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)\n\nThe ε-NFA N, as drawn:\n- q0 loops on a; q0 —a, ε→ q1\n- q1 —b→ q2\n- q2 loops on b\n- start q0, accept q2\n\n**L(N) = a∗b⁺**: some a's, then at least one b. For example, b ∈ L via the ε-move.\n\n### a. Formal definition of the ε-NFA\n\nN = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) with\n- Q = {q0, q1, q2}\n- Σ = {a, b}\n- δ : Q × Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q), where Σ_ε = Σ ∪ {ε}, given in table (b)\n- q₀ = q0\n- F = {q2}\n\n### b. Transition table of the ε-NFA\n\n| State | a | b | ε |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| → q0 | {q0, q1} | ∅ | {q1} |\n| q1 | ∅ | {q2} | ∅ |\n| ∗ q2 | ∅ | {q2} | ∅ |\n\n### c. ε-closure of every state\n\nE(q) = q plus everything reachable from q by ε-arrows alone. (Lecture 4 writes this C_ε(q), and slide 23 also EClos(q). Same thing; on the quiz, use the lecture's C_ε.)\n- **E(q0) = {q0, q1}**\n- **E(q1) = {q1}**\n- **E(q2) = {q2}**\n\n### d. Start state of the DFA\n\n**E(q0) = {q0, q1}**. It is *not* {q0}: the start state must include everything the NFA can reach without reading input.\n\n### e. Transition table of the DFA (subset construction)\n\nFor a subset R and symbol x: δ′(R, x) = E( ⋃ over r ∈ R of δ(r, x) ). Lecture 4 (slide 28) writes the same rule as δ′(R, x) = ⋃ over r ∈ R of C_ε(δ(r, x)); the two give identical sets. All 2³ = 8 subsets, in the same row order the lecture uses (slide 32):\n\n| DFA state | a | b |\n|---|---|---|\n| ∅ | ∅ | ∅ |\n| {q0} | {q0, q1} | ∅ |\n| {q1} | ∅ | {q2} |\n| ∗ {q2} | ∅ | {q2} |\n| → {q0, q1} | {q0, q1} | {q2} |\n| ∗ {q0, q2} | {q0, q1} | {q2} |\n| ∗ {q1, q2} | ∅ | {q2} |\n| ∗ {q0, q1, q2} | {q0, q1} | {q2} |\n\nWorked example: from {q0,q1} on a, δ(q0,a) ∪ δ(q1,a) = {q0,q1} ∪ ∅ = {q0,q1}, and E({q0,q1}) = {q0,q1}. On b it is ∅ ∪ {q2} = {q2}, and E({q2}) = {q2}.\n\n### f. Accept states of the DFA\n\nEvery subset that contains an NFA accept state (q2): **{q2}, {q0,q2}, {q1,q2}, {q0,q1,q2}**.\n\n### g. DFA before pruning\n\nAll 8 states and arrows from table (e). See the Figures tab (\"Q11g\").\n\n### h. DFA after pruning\n\nKeep only the states reachable from the start {q0,q1}:\n- {q0,q1} —a→ {q0,q1}, —b→ {q2}\n- {q2} —b→ {q2}, —a→ ∅\n- ∅ —a,b→ ∅\n\n**3 states: {q0,q1} (start), {q2} (accept), ∅ (dead).** The removed states, {q0}, {q1}, {q0,q2}, {q1,q2} and {q0,q1,q2}, have no path from the start.\n\n| DFA state | a | b |\n|---|---|---|\n| → {q0,q1} | {q0,q1} | {q2} |\n| ∗ {q2} | ∅ | {q2} |\n| ∅ | ∅ | ∅ |\n\n**Keep ∅.** Lecture 4 settles this: its pruned DFA (slide 39) keeps ∅ with its a, b self-loop and removes only the unreachable non-empty sets. (Some instructors delete ∅ and leave its arrows out; don't, here. Label it as the dead state.) When you justify the pruning, say \"not reachable from the start state {q0,q1}\", which is the rule; slide 38's \"no incoming edges\" is only a first check.\n\n### i. A string both accept: \"aab\"\n\n- **DFA:** {q0,q1} –a→ {q0,q1} –a→ {q0,q1} –b→ **{q2}**. That is an accept state, so the DFA accepts.\n- **NFA:** q0 –a→ q0 –a→ q0 –ε→ q1 –b→ **q2**. This path ends in the accept state, so the NFA accepts. (Another accepting path: q0 –a→ q0 –a→ q1 –b→ q2.)\n\n### j. A string both reject: \"aba\"\n\n- **DFA:** {q0,q1} –a→ {q0,q1} –b→ {q2} –a→ **∅**. Not an accept state, so the DFA rejects.\n- **NFA:** after \"ab\" every live branch is in q2, and q2 has no a-arrow, so every branch dies. No path ends in q2, so the NFA rejects. (The empty string ε is another easy reject: the DFA stays in {q0,q1}, which is not accepting.)\n\n---\n\n## Method summary — what to write on the quiz\n\n1. **DFA or NFA?** Check the three NFA signs: more than one arrow on a symbol from some state; a missing arrow; an ε-arrow. Name the δ signature: DFA δ : Q×Σ → Q, NFA δ : Q×Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q).\n2. **Formal definition** = the 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F). Spell out each part, and give δ as a table or as a list of equations.\n3. **Subset construction:** ε-closures first. Start = E(q₀). Then δ′(R,x) = E(⋃ δ(r,x)). The accept states are the subsets containing an NFA accept state. Prune what is unreachable from the start.\n4. **Lexicographic enumeration** = shortest first, then dictionary order; ε comes first if it is in the language.\n5. **Substring language** (x pattern y): self-loop at the start, a chain spelling the pattern, self-loop at the accept state.\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"M has a state with no outgoing arrows (and no state has two arrows on one symbol). DFA or NFA?","a":"Strictly an NFA, because a DFA's δ : Q×Σ → Q must be total (Lecture 4 slide 3: \"exactly one exiting transition arrow for each symbol\"; Tutorials 3–4). But Tutorial 2 calls this exact machine a DFSM, treating missing arrows as going to a hidden dead state. Write both: deterministic, but δ is partial, so not yet a DFA; adding a dead state makes it one."},{"q":"Prep Q2: which of ababaabaa, abbbaa, abbbaabb, abbaabbaa does M reject?","a":"ababaabaa. After \"aba\" M is back in q0, and the next b sends it to q2, which has no way out."},{"q":"Language of M (q0–a→q1, q0–b→q2, q1–a→q0, q1–b→q1, accept q1)?","a":"(ab∗a)∗ab∗: an odd number of a's, and every b is preceded by an odd number of a's."},{"q":"First twelve strings of {w ∈ {1,2,3}∗ : |w| even} in lexicographic (shortlex) order?","a":"ε, 11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32, 33, 1111, 1112."},{"q":"Minimal DFA idea for \"strings starting with ab\" over {a,b}?","a":"Four states: q0 –a→ q1 –b→ q2 (accept, loops on a,b). A wrong symbol from q0 or q1 goes to a dead state, which loops on a,b."},{"q":"NFA for strings containing abcabb?","a":"Seven states. q0 loops on a,b,c; the chain q0–a→q1–b→q2–c→q3–a→q4–b→q5–b→q6 spells the pattern; q6 is accepting and loops on a,b,c."},{"q":"Three reasons a transition table shows an NFA?","a":"(1) a cell holds more than one state, e.g. {q0,q1}; (2) a cell is ∅; (3) entries are sets, so δ : Q×Σ → 𝒫(Q). (An ε column is a fourth.)"},{"q":"Signature of δ for a DFA, an NFA and an ε-NFA?","a":"DFA: Q×Σ → Q. NFA: Q×Σ → 𝒫(Q). ε-NFA: Q×Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q), with Σ_ε = Σ ∪ {ε}."},{"q":"What is the ε-closure E(q)?","a":"The set of states reachable from q by zero or more ε-arrows, which always includes q itself."},{"q":"In the subset construction, what is the DFA start state, and why is it not just {q₀}?","a":"E(q₀), because the NFA is already in every ε-reachable state before reading any input."},{"q":"Subset-construction rule for δ′(R, x)?","a":"δ′(R,x) = E( ⋃ over r∈R of δ(r,x) ): move on x from every state in R, then take the ε-closure."},{"q":"Which DFA states are accepting after the subset construction?","a":"Every subset containing at least one NFA accept state."},{"q":"Prep Q11 (q0 loops on a; q0 –a,ε→ q1; q1 –b→ q2; q2 loops on b; accept q2): what is the pruned DFA?","a":"Start {q0,q1} (on a stays, on b → {q2}); {q2} accepting (on b stays, on a → ∅); ∅ dead. Language a∗b⁺."},{"q":"What does \"pruning\" a subset-construction DFA mean?","a":"Deleting the subset states that cannot be reached from the start state. They never affect which strings are accepted. Lecture 4 keeps ∅ if it is reachable (it is the dead state)."},{"q":"How does an NFA reject a string?","a":"It rejects only if **no** computation branch ends in an accept state after reading the whole input. Branches that die on a missing arrow just disappear."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/Q1p001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/Q1p002.jpg"},{"n":"diagram 1","src":"slides/Q1d_q7_starts_with_ab.png"},{"n":"diagram 2","src":"slides/Q1d_q8_nfa_abcabb.png"},{"n":"diagram 3","src":"slides/Q1d_q11_enfa.png"},{"n":"diagram 4","src":"slides/Q1d_q11g_dfa_full.png"},{"n":"diagram 5","src":"slides/Q1d_q11h_dfa_pruned.png"}]},{"id":"t2","title":"Tutorials 2–4 (Mr. Hadzo) — Quiz 1 practice","slides":"Tutorial 2 PDF (3 pp) + Tutorial 3–4 diagrams","md":"# Mr. Hadzo's tutorials (Teams → Tutorial): Tutorials 2, 3 and 4\n\n**Files:** `tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf` (Tutorial 2, 3 pages, answers only), `tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx` (Tutorial 3: DFAs, δ, δ∗, closure, for Lecture 3) and `tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx` (Tutorial 4: the conversion, written \"like the quiz question\"). The professor recommended these for Quiz 1. Received 25.10.2026.\n\n**Checked by code:** every worked solution in Tutorials 3 and 4, and every machine and enumeration in Tutorial 2, was re-run. All of them are correct. (The checks: the subset construction on all five NFAs; the language of each DFA against its description for every string up to length 8–9; the shortlex lists; and C∗, CD, DC, C².)\n\n**Lecture 4 is now ingested** (25.10.2026, chapter **04 — Nondeterministic Finite Automata**). Tutorial 4's \"slide 42\" is Lecture 4's \"Some easy questions\" slide (start state, accept states, run on aabb, ε), and Tutorial 4's recipe is Lecture 4's slide-28 construction word for word. ⚠️ **Lecture 3 (DFAs) is still not in the course folder**; Tutorial 3 says it \"goes with Lecture 3\" and stands in for it until the deck arrives.\n\n---\n\n## What the tutorials settle about the quiz\n\n**1 · \"Lexicographic enumeration\" means shortlex.** Tutorial 3 §1.3 says \"The lecture calls this a lexicographic enumeration\": shorter strings first, then dictionary order. Tutorial 2 even contains prep Q6 word for word, with the answer **ε, 11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32, 33, 1111, 1112**, which matches our answer.\n**2 · Prep Q2 appears in Tutorial 2** (question 5) with **A (ababaabaa)** marked \"correct\", which matches our answer.\n**3 · Missing arrows: DFA or NFA? The course's own material disagrees.**\n   - Tutorial 2 calls **the very machine M from prep Q1–5 a \"DFSM\"** (deterministic finite state machine), even though q2 has no outgoing arrows. That is the drawing convention in which omitted arrows go to an invisible dead state (E. Rich's book, which the course quotes, uses the term DFSM).\n   - Tutorials 3 and 4 take the strict view. Tutorial 3 §3.3: \"An empty cell does not mean reject. It means δ is undefined there, so the machine is not yet a DFA. Add a trap state.\" Tutorial 4 Q2.2 lists \"some arrows are also missing\" as one of three reasons E is an NFA, and says \"any one of these reasons is enough\".\n   - **Lecture 4 sides with the strict view** (slide 3: a DFA state \"always has exactly one exiting transition arrow for each symbol\"; slides 3–4: hanging on a missing arrow is NFA behaviour). So answer **NFA** first.\n   - **What to write for prep Q1 / any similar quiz question:** give both halves in one answer. *\"M is deterministic: no state has two arrows on the same symbol and there are no ε-arrows. But q2 has no a- or b-arrow, so δ is not total. Strictly by the 5-tuple definition it is not yet a DFA (it is an NFA). Adding a dead state, or self-loops on q2, makes it a DFA with the same language.\"* **Best of all: ask the professor before the quiz.** She invited questions in her email.\n**4 · The conversion: two accepted layouts.**\n   - **Lecture way** (now confirmed by Lecture 4, slides 29–39): list all 2^|Q| subsets, fill every row, then prune the ones not reachable from q′. The lecture's pruned DFA **keeps ∅** as the dead state. The prep sheet's Q11 asks for \"before pruning\" and \"after pruning\", so **use this layout on the quiz**.\n   - **Tutorial 4's shortcut:** start from q′ and add rows only for sets that appear. This gives the same final DFA directly.\n**5 · Tutorial 4's hand-in checklist.**\n   - The start state is the ε-closure of the NFA's start state, not just the start state.\n   - Take the ε-closure after every move.\n   - Every set gets its own row, including ∅ if it appears (∅ is a normal state: a trap that loops on every symbol).\n   - Every set containing an NFA accept state is accepting.\n   - The diagram matches the table: one arrow per symbol out of every state.\n\n---\n\n## Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)\n\n**Recipe (§1.2):** (1) transition table, with an ε column if needed, and ∅ for missing arrows; (2) the ε-closure C_ε(r) of every state; (3) q′ = C_ε({q}); (4) fill rows: δ′(R, a) = ⋃ over r∈R of C_ε(δ(r, a)); (5) F′ = {R : R ∩ F ≠ ∅}; (6) draw the DFA, with ∅ as a trap state.\n\nWorked solutions (all verified):\n\n| NFA | Start q′ | Reachable DFA states | Language |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| §1.3: q0 loops a, q0 –ε→ q1, q1 –b→ {q1,q2}, q2 loops a; F={q2} | {q0,q1} | {q0,q1}, {q1,q2}✓, {q2}✓, ∅ | a∗b⁺a∗ (some a's, at least one b, some a's) |\n| 3.1 N₁: q0 loops 0,1; q0 –1→ q1; F={q1} | {q0} | {q0}, {q0,q1}✓ | strings ending in 1 (0101 accept, 0110 reject) |\n| 3.2 N₂: as N₁ plus q1 –0,1→ q2; F={q2} | {q0} | {q0}, {q0,q1}, {q0,q2}✓, {q0,q1,q2}✓ | second symbol from the end is 1 (0110 accept, 1001 reject) |\n| 3.3 N₃: q0 –ε→ q1, q2; q1 loops 0; q2 loops 1; F={q1,q2} | {q0,q1,q2}✓ | {q0,q1,q2}✓, {q1}✓, {q2}✓, ∅ | only 0s or only 1s, including ε (000 ✓, 01 ✗, ε ✓) |\n| 3.4 N₄: 1 loops a, 1 –a→ 2, 2 –b→ 3, 3 loops a, 3 –ε→ 2; F={3} | {1} | {1}, {1,2}, {2,3}✓, ∅ | starts with a and contains a b (aab ✓, ba ✗) |\n\n(✓ = accepting.) Note 3.4: C_ε(3) = {2,3}, so δ′({1,2}, b) = C_ε(∅) ∪ C_ε({3}) = {2,3}. **An ε-arrow pointing backwards changes the closure of the state it leaves, not of the state it enters.**\n\n**Warm-up 2.2 (automaton E):** q0 –a→ {q0,q1}, q1 –b→ q2, q2 –ε→ q0, F = {q2}. C_ε(q2) = {q0,q2}. After *a*: {q0,q1}. After *ab*: {q0,q2}. So ab is accepted; aba and ε are rejected.\n\n---\n\n## Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)\n\n- **5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F):** δ : Q×Σ → Q is total (a table with |Q|·|Σ| cells, no empty cells). There is exactly one start state. F may be ∅ (then L = ∅) or Q (then L = Σ∗). q₀ ∈ F ⇔ ε is accepted.\n- **Valid-δ rules (§3.3):** one row per state and one column per symbol; every cell holds exactly one state; every state named is in Q. Arrow labels are single symbols, so a label \"ab\" is illegal. Unreachable states, F = ∅ and a start state that is also accepting are all allowed.\n- **Number of transition functions:** |Q|^(|Q|·|Σ|). With 2 states over {a, b}: 2⁴ = 16.\n- **Extended transition function δ∗ : Q × Σ∗ → Q:** δ∗(r, ε) = r and δ∗(r, va) = δ(δ∗(r, v), a). Facts: δ∗ agrees with δ on single symbols; δ∗(r, xy) = δ∗(δ∗(r, x), y); M accepts w ⇔ δ∗(q₀, w) ∈ F.\n- **D₃ (binary multiples of 3):** states r₀, r₁, r₂ = the remainder. δ(rᵢ, b) = r_(2i+b mod 3). Start and accept state r₀. Proving it correct is an induction on |w| using δ∗.\n- **Regular operations:** union, concatenation AB (not commutative), and star A∗ = ⋃ Aᵏ with A⁰ = {ε}.\n  - With A = {a, aa}: A² = {aa, aaa, aaaa}, which has 3 strings, not 4.\n  - ∅∗ = {ε} and {ε}∗ = {ε}.\n  - A∅ = ∅ and A{ε} = A.\n- **Product construction:** Q₁×Q₂. Start (q₁, q₂). δ((r₁, r₂), a) = (δ₁(r₁, a), δ₂(r₂, a)). The choice of F decides the operation:\n\n| Operation | Accepting pairs F |\n|---|---|\n| Union | F₁×Q₂ ∪ Q₁×F₂ |\n| Intersection | F₁×F₂ |\n| Difference A − B | F₁×(Q₂−F₂) |\n| Symmetric difference | exactly one of the two accepts |\n\n- **Complement:** swap F and Q − F. **This only works on a complete DFA**, so add the trap state first. (Example: D₂ \"starts with ab\" with no trap state would wrongly reject b.)\n- **Closure works in one direction only.** If A ∪ B is regular, A and B need not be regular: A ∪ Σ∗ = Σ∗. A subset of a regular language need not be regular.\n\n---\n\n## Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out\n\n- L = {Austin, Houston, Dallas}: |L∗| is **countably infinite**. Σ = {a,b,c}: Σ∗ is **countably infinite**.\n- {w ∈ {a,b}∗ : every a is immediately followed by a b}, first six in shortlex: **ε, b, ab, bb, abb, bab**. (The language is (b ∪ ab)∗.)\n- {w ∈ {1,2,3}∗ : |w| even}: **ε, 11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32, 33, 1111, 1112**. This is prep Q6.\n\nTrue or false: the tutorial gives the verdict; the reasons are added here.\n\n| | Statement | Verdict | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| a | (L⁺)⁺ = L⁺ | True | Concatenating ≥1 blocks of ≥1 strings of L gives exactly ≥1 strings of L. |\n| b | (L∗)⁺ = (L⁺)∗ | True | Both equal L∗. |\n| c | L∗ = L⁺ ∪ ∅ | False | L⁺ ∪ ∅ = L⁺. With L = {a}, ε ∈ L∗ but ε ∉ L⁺. |\n| d | (L₁L₂L₃)∗ = L₁∗L₂∗L₃∗ | False | L₁={a}, L₂={b}, L₃={c}: \"a\" is on the right but not the left. |\n| e | L₁∗ ∪ L₂∗ = (L₁∗ ∪ L₂∗)∗ | False | L₁={a}, L₂={b}: \"ab\" is on the right only. |\n| f | L∗L = L⁺ | True | ⋃ₖ≥₀ LᵏL = ⋃ₖ≥₁ Lᵏ. |\n| g | L₁∗(L₂ ∪ L₃)⁺ = L₁∗L₂⁺ ∪ L₁∗L₃⁺ | False | L₂={b}, L₃={c}: \"bc\" is on the left only. |\n| h | ∅L∗ = ∅ | True | Nothing to concatenate with. |\n| i | L∅ = L∗ | False | L∅ = ∅, but ε ∈ L∗. |\n| j | L₁ − L₂ = L₂ − L₁ | False | L₁={a}, L₂=∅. |\n| k | ((L₁L₂) ∪ (L₁L₃))∗ = (L₁(L₂ ∪ L₃))∗ | True | Concatenation distributes over union. |\n| l | ((L ∪ {ε})∗(L ∪ {ε})∗)∗ = L∗ | True | (L ∪ {ε})∗ = L∗, L∗L∗ = L∗, (L∗)∗ = L∗. |\n| m | {ε} ∪ L⁺ = L⁺ | False | Any L that does not contain ε. |\n\nDFSMs drawn in Tutorial 2 (all correct):\n- **Not ending in a**, over {a,b,c}: 1 (start, accept; loops b,c) –a→ 2 (loops a) –b,c→ 1.\n- **Not ending in bab:** 4 states, state 4 = \"just read bab\" is the only rejecting one.\n- **At most one b:** q0 ✓ (loops a) –b→ q1 ✓ (loops a) –b→ d (dead, loops a,b).\n- **Contains 101:** q0 –1→ q1 –0→ q2 –1→ q3 ✓ (loops). Back-arrows: q0 loops 0; q1 loops 1; q2 –0→ q0.\n- **Contains 001:** s0 –0→ s1 –0→ s2 –1→ s3 ✓ (loops). s0 loops 1; s1 –1→ s0; s2 loops 0.\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What does the course mean by a \"lexicographic enumeration\" of a language?","a":"Shortlex order: shorter strings first, dictionary order within a length, ε first if it is in L (Tutorial 3 §1.3: \"the lecture calls this a lexicographic enumeration\"). Plain dictionary order never gets past a, aa, aaa, …"},{"q":"First six strings of {w ∈ {a,b}∗ : every a is immediately followed by b} in lexicographic order?","a":"ε, b, ab, bb, abb, bab."},{"q":"First six strings of {w ∈ {a,b}∗ : #a(w) = #b(w)} in shortlex order?","a":"ε, ab, ba, aabb, abab, abba."},{"q":"Does an empty cell in a DFA table mean \"reject\"?","a":"No. δ is undefined there, so the machine is not yet a DFA. Add a trap state that loops on every symbol (Tutorial 3)."},{"q":"How many transition functions δ : Q×Σ → Q exist?","a":"|Q|^(|Q|·|Σ|). For 2 states over {a,b}: 2⁴ = 16."},{"q":"Recursive definition of δ∗?","a":"δ∗(r, ε) = r; δ∗(r, va) = δ(δ∗(r, v), a) for v ∈ Σ∗, a ∈ Σ."},{"q":"When does a DFA accept ε?","a":"Exactly when q₀ ∈ F, since δ∗(q₀, ε) = q₀."},{"q":"δ for the DFA accepting binary multiples of 3?","a":"States r₀, r₁, r₂ (the remainder); δ(rᵢ, b) = r_((2i+b) mod 3); start and accept r₀."},{"q":"A = {a, aa}. What is A², and why does it have only 3 strings?","a":"{aa, aaa, aaaa}. a·aa and aa·a are the same string."},{"q":"∅∗ = ? and {ε}∗ = ?","a":"Both are {ε}."},{"q":"Product construction for A ∪ B: states, start, δ, F?","a":"Q₁×Q₂; (q₁, q₂); δ((r₁,r₂),a) = (δ₁(r₁,a), δ₂(r₂,a)); F = (F₁×Q₂) ∪ (Q₁×F₂)."},{"q":"Which F gives intersection, and which gives difference A − B, in the product machine?","a":"F₁×F₂; F₁×(Q₂−F₂)."},{"q":"Why must a DFA be complete before you complement it by swapping F?","a":"A missing arrow rejects silently, and still rejects after the swap. Example: \"starts with ab\" without a trap state would wrongly reject b in the complement."},{"q":"True or false: if A ∪ B is regular, then A and B are regular.","a":"False. Take B = Σ∗: A ∪ Σ∗ = Σ∗ is regular for any A."},{"q":"In the NFA→DFA conversion, what is the start state, and what is δ′(R, a)?","a":"q′ = C_ε({q₀}); δ′(R, a) = ⋃ over r∈R of C_ε(δ(r, a))."},{"q":"Tutorial 4, N₄: 1 loops a, 1 –a→ 2, 2 –b→ 3, 3 loops a, 3 –ε→ 2, F = {3}. What is C_ε(3), and what is the DFA?","a":"C_ε(3) = {2,3}. DFA: {1} –a→ {1,2}, {1} –b→ ∅, {1,2} –a→ {1,2}, {1,2} –b→ {2,3}✓, {2,3} –a,b→ {2,3}, ∅ trap. Language: starts with a and contains a b."},{"q":"NFA N₂ (q0 loops 0,1; q0 –1→ q1 –0,1→ q2 ✓): what does it accept, and how many DFA states does it need?","a":"Strings whose second symbol from the end is 1. The NFA has 3 states; the DFA needs 4 ({q0}, {q0,q1}, {q0,q2}✓, {q0,q1,q2}✓), because it must remember the last two symbols."},{"q":"N₃ has ε-arrows from q0 to q1 and q2, q1 loops 0, q2 loops 1, F = {q1,q2}. Does it accept ε, 000 and 01?","a":"ε yes (the start {q0,q1,q2} is accepting), 000 yes, 01 no (reaches ∅). L = only 0s or only 1s."},{"q":"True or false: (L₁L₂L₃)∗ = L₁∗L₂∗L₃∗ for all languages.","a":"False. With L₁={a}, L₂={b}, L₃={c}, \"a\" ∈ L₁∗L₂∗L₃∗ but \"a\" ∉ (abc)∗."},{"q":"True or false: L∗L = L⁺.","a":"True: ⋃ over k≥0 of LᵏL = ⋃ over k≥1 of Lᵏ = L⁺."}],"figs":[{"n":1,"src":"slides/T2p001.jpg"},{"n":2,"src":"slides/T2p002.jpg"},{"n":3,"src":"slides/T2p003.jpg"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: NFA N: q0 loops on a, epsilon-arrow from q0 to q1, q1 loops on b and goes to q2 on …","src":"slides/T4_01.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: DFA M with states {q0,q1} (start), {q1,q2}, {q2} and the empty set; {q1,q2} and {q2…","src":"slides/T4_02.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: DFA D with states q1 (start), q2, q3 (accepting).","src":"slides/T4_03.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: Automaton E: q0 loops on a and goes to q1 on a; q1 goes to q2 on b; epsilon-arrow f…","src":"slides/T4_04.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: NFA N1: q0 loops on 0 and 1, goes to q1 on 1; q1 accepting.","src":"slides/T4_05.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: DFA M1 with states {q0} (start) and {q0,q1} (accepting).","src":"slides/T4_06.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: NFA N2: q0 loops on 0 and 1, goes to q1 on 1; q1 goes to q2 on 0 and 1; q2 acceptin…","src":"slides/T4_07.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: DFA M2 with states {q0} (start), {q0,q1}, {q0,q2} and {q0,q1,q2}; the last two acce…","src":"slides/T4_08.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: NFA N3: epsilon-arrows from q0 to q1 and to q2; q1 loops on 0; q2 loops on 1; q1 an…","src":"slides/T4_09.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: DFA M3 with states {q0,q1,q2} (start), {q1}, {q2} and the empty set; all except the…","src":"slides/T4_10.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: NFA N4: state 1 loops on a and goes to 2 on a; 2 goes to 3 on b; 3 loops on a; epsi…","src":"slides/T4_11.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 4: DFA M4 with states {1} (start), {1,2}, {2,3} (accepting) and the empty set.","src":"slides/T4_12.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: Nested circles: Regular languages (FSMs) inside Context-Free languages (PDAs) insid…","src":"slides/T3_13.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of D1: states s0, s1, s2; s2 accepting; accepts strings ending with a…","src":"slides/T3_14.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of D2: p0 to p1 on a, p1 to p2 on b, p2 accepting with a loop; trap s…","src":"slides/T3_15.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram for at least two b&#39;s: c0, c1, c2; c2 accepting.","src":"slides/T3_16.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram for strings that start and end with the same symbol.","src":"slides/T3_17.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of D3 with states r0, r1, r2; r0 is start and accepting.","src":"slides/T3_18.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of M3 with states q0 to q3; q3 accepting.","src":"slides/T3_19.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of M4: a-arrows go around 0,1,2,3; b-arrows join 0 with 2 and 1 with …","src":"slides/T3_20.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of M1 with states E (start, accepting) and O.","src":"slides/T3_21.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of M2 with states N (start) and Y (accepting).","src":"slides/T3_22.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: Product machine M with four states (E,N), (E,Y), (O,N), (O,Y); all except (O,N) acc…","src":"slides/T3_23.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of M5: s to y on b, s to t on a; y accepting; y and t loop on a, b.","src":"slides/T3_24.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: State diagram of M6: e (start, accepting) and o, swapping on a and b.","src":"slides/T3_25.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: Reachable part of the product of M5 and M6 with five states; (y,e) accepting.","src":"slides/T3_26.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: Drawing of M8: u0 (start, accepting) to u1 (accepting) on a; no other arrows.","src":"slides/T3_27.png"},{"n":"Tutorial 3: Completed machine after the swap: u0, u1, and accepting trap state t.","src":"slides/T3_28.png"}]},{"id":"b1","title":"Book 1 · Intro & Proof Techniques","slides":"M&S ch. 1 · weeks 1–2","md":"# B1 — Introduction & Proof Techniques (Maheshwari–Smid, Chapter 1)\n\n> **Aligned with course weeks 1–2** — Lecture 1 covered §1.2 sets; the professor announced proof techniques (§1.3) as next class, so this chapter IS the immediate lecture material.\n\nSource: Maheshwari & Smid, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation* (free Carleton edition), Chapter 1 \"Introduction\", PDF pages 9–28.\n\n---\n\n## §1.1 Purpose and motivation\n\nThe Theory of Computation asks:\n- What are the mathematical properties of computer hardware and software?\n- What is a computation? What is an algorithm? Can we define these rigorously?\n- What are the limitations of computers? Can \"everything\" be computed? (**Answer: no.**)\n\n**Purpose:** Develop formal mathematical models of computation that reflect real-world computers. The field started with mathematicians and logicians in the **1930s** (Gödel, Turing, Church) asking whether all mathematical problems can be solved systematically — this research led to real computers.\n\nThree areas (know the \"central question\" of each — classic quiz item):\n\n| Area | Central question | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Complexity Theory** | Classify problems by degree of \"difficulty\"; prove that seemingly hard problems really are hard. | Easy: sorting, searching a directory, shortest route. Hard: timetable scheduling, factoring a 300-digit integer, VLSI chip layout. |\n| **Computability Theory** | Classify problems as **solvable or unsolvable**. | \"Is an arbitrary mathematical statement true or false?\" — unsolvable (Gödel/Turing/Church, 1930s). |\n| **Automata Theory** | Do different computation models have the **same power**, or can one solve more problems than another? | Finite automata (text processing, compilers, hardware); context-free grammars (programming languages, AI); Turing machines (abstract model of a real computer). |\n\nCourse order: Automata Theory first, then Computability Theory (Complexity is a later course).\n\n---\n\n## §1.2 Mathematical preliminaries — ALL definitions\n\n### Sets\n- **Set**: a collection of well-defined objects.\n- **N** = {1, 2, 3, …} (natural numbers — note: this book **starts N at 1**).\n- **Z** = {…, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, …} (integers).\n- **Q** = {m/n : m ∈ Z, n ∈ Z, n ≠ 0} (rationals). **R** = real numbers.\n- **Subset**: A ⊆ B if every element of A is also an element of B. Always A ⊆ A and ∅ ⊆ A.\n- **Power set**: P(B) = {A : A ⊆ B}, the set of all subsets of B. Note ∅ ∈ P(B) and B ∈ P(B). (If |B| = n then |P(B)| = 2ⁿ.)\n- **Union**: A ∪ B = {x : x ∈ A or x ∈ B}.\n- **Intersection**: A ∩ B = {x : x ∈ A and x ∈ B}.\n- **Difference**: A \\ B = {x : x ∈ A and x ∉ B}.\n- **Cartesian product**: A × B = {(x, y) : x ∈ A and y ∈ B}.\n- **Complement**: Ā = {x : x ∉ A}.\n\n### Relations and functions\n- **Binary relation** on sets A and B: any subset of A × B.\n- **Function** f : A → B: a binary relation R ⊆ A × B such that for each a ∈ A there is **exactly one** ordered pair in R whose first component is a. Write f(a) = b (\"f maps a to b\", \"the image of a under f is b\").\n  - **Domain** = A. **Range** = {b ∈ B : there is an a ∈ A with f(a) = b}.\n- **One-to-one (injective)**: distinct a ≠ a′ in A implies f(a) ≠ f(a′).\n- **Onto (surjective)**: for each b ∈ B there exists a ∈ A with f(a) = b (range = B).\n- **Bijection**: both injective and surjective.\n- **Equivalence relation**: R ⊆ A × A satisfying all three:\n  1. **Reflexive**: (a, a) ∈ R for every a ∈ A.\n  2. **Symmetric**: (a, b) ∈ R ⇒ (b, a) ∈ R.\n  3. **Transitive**: (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R ⇒ (a, c) ∈ R.\n\n### Graphs\n- **Graph** G = (V, E): V a set of **vertices**; E a set of **edges**, each edge a pair of distinct vertices. (Named examples: K₅ complete graph on 5 vertices, K₃,₃ complete bipartite graph, Petersen graph.)\n- **Degree** deg(v): number of edges incident on v.\n- **Path**: sequence of vertices connected by edges. **Cycle**: path that starts and ends at the same vertex. **Simple path**: no repeated vertices.\n- **Connected**: there is a path between every pair of vertices.\n- **3-regular**: every vertex has degree 3 (used in §1.3 examples).\n- **Kₙ**: complete graph on n vertices — every pair of distinct vertices joined by an edge.\n- **Complement Ḡ** of G on vertex set V: the edges of Kₙ that are **not** in G.\n- **Planar**: can be drawn/embedded in the plane so no two edges intersect except at endpoints. An embedding has vertices, edges, and **faces** (including the unbounded face).\n\n### Strings and languages (memorize — these recur all semester)\n- **Alphabet**: a **finite set** whose elements are called **symbols**. Examples: Σ = {0, 1}, Σ = {a, b, …, z}.\n- **String over Σ**: a **finite sequence of symbols**, each symbol an element of Σ.\n- **Length** |w|: the number of symbols in w.\n- **Empty string ϵ**: the string of length zero. (E.g. over Σ = {0,1}: 10, 1000, 0, 101, ϵ have lengths 2, 4, 1, 3, 0.)\n- **Language**: a **set of strings**. (That's the whole definition — can be finite or infinite.)\n- **Σ\\*** : the set of **all** strings over Σ, including ϵ. (Introduced formally slightly later in the book, but used from the start.) Every language over Σ is a subset of Σ\\*; note Σ\\* is infinite (countably) even though Σ is finite, and ∅, {ϵ}, and Σ\\* itself are all languages — **∅ ≠ {ϵ}**.\n\n### Boolean logic\n- Boolean values: 1 = true, 0 = false.\n- Operations: **¬** NOT, **∧** AND, **∨** OR, **⊕** XOR, **↔** equivalence, **→** implication.\n- Key truth-table facts: ¬0 = 1; a ∧ b = 1 only if both 1; a ∨ b = 0 only if both 0; a ⊕ b = 1 iff a ≠ b; a ↔ b = 1 iff a = b; **implication: 0 → anything = 1, and 1 → 0 = 0** (only false when true implies false — a favorite trick question).\n\n---\n\n## §1.3 Proof techniques\n\nA **theorem** is a statement that is true. A **proof** is a sequence of mathematical statements forming an argument that a theorem is true; it may use axioms, the theorem's hypotheses, and previously proved theorems.\n\nGeneral tips (from Pólya's *How to Solve It*): (1) fully understand the statement first; (2) \"A iff B\" = two proofs, A ⇒ B and B ⇒ A; \"set A = set B\" = prove A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A; (3) work small cases first; (4) write the proof down to catch mistakes; (5) be patient and precise.\n\n### 1.3.1 Direct proof\n**Template:** Start from the hypothesis, apply definitions and algebra, arrive at the conclusion.\n\n- **Theorem 1.3.1** — If n is an odd positive integer, then n² is odd.\n  *Proof idea:* Write n = 2k + 1. Then n² = 4k² + 4k + 1 = 2(2k² + 2k) + 1, which is even plus one, hence odd.\n- **Theorem 1.3.2 / 1.3.3 (Handshake)** — For any graph G = (V, E): Σ_{v∈V} deg(v) = 2|E| (in particular, the sum of degrees is even).\n  *Proof idea:* Each edge is incident on exactly two distinct vertices, so each edge contributes exactly 2 to the sum.\n\n### 1.3.2 Constructive proof\n**Template:** To prove \"there exists an object with property P\": exhibit the object explicitly, then verify it has property P. Shows existence **by building the object**.\n\n- **Theorem 1.3.5** — For every even integer n ≥ 4, there exists a 3-regular graph with n vertices.\n  *Proof idea:* Take V = {0, …, n−1}; edges = the cycle {i, i+1} (0 ≤ i ≤ n−2) plus {n−1, 0}, plus the \"diameters\" {i, i + n/2} for 0 ≤ i ≤ n/2 − 1. Each vertex gets degree 2 (cycle) + 1 (diameter) = 3.\n\n### 1.3.3 Nonconstructive proof\n**Template:** Show an object exists **without producing it** — e.g., by case analysis where every case implies existence.\n\n- **Theorem 1.3.6** — There exist irrational x, y with x^y rational.\n  *Proof idea:* Consider √2^√2. Case 1: it's rational — take x = y = √2. Case 2: it's irrational — take x = √2^√2, y = √2; then x^y = √2² = 2 ∈ Q. Either way such a pair exists, but the proof never tells us which pair works.\n\n### 1.3.4 Proof by contradiction\n**Template:** To prove statement S: assume ¬S, derive a contradiction (something false, like 1 + 1 = 3). This works because ¬S ⇒ false is contrapositively true ⇒ S, i.e., S.\n\n- **Theorem 1.3.8** — If n² is even, then n is even.\n  *Proof idea:* Assume n² even but n odd. By Theorem 1.3.1, n odd ⇒ n² odd — contradiction.\n- **Theorem 1.3.9** — **√2 is irrational.**\n  *Proof idea:* Assume √2 = m/n with gcd(m, n) = 1. Squaring: 2n² = m², so m² even, so m even (Thm 1.3.8), write m = 2k. Then 2n² = 4k² ⇒ n² = 2k² ⇒ n² even ⇒ n even. Both m and n even contradicts gcd(m, n) = 1.\n\n### 1.3.5 Pigeonhole principle\n**Statement:** If n + 1 or more objects are placed into n boxes, then at least one box contains two or more objects. Equivalently: if |A| > |B|, there is no one-to-one function from A to B.\n\n- **Theorem 1.3.10 (Erdős–Szekeres)** — Every sequence of n² + 1 **distinct** reals contains a monotone (increasing or decreasing) subsequence of length n + 1.\n  *Proof idea:* For each i let (incᵢ, decᵢ) = lengths of the longest increasing/decreasing subsequences starting at aᵢ. Assume all ≤ n: the n² + 1 pairs land in only n² boxes {1..n}×{1..n}, so some i < j share (incᵢ, decᵢ) = (inc_j, dec_j). But aᵢ < a_j forces incᵢ ≥ 1 + inc_j, and aᵢ > a_j forces decᵢ ≥ 1 + dec_j — contradiction either way.\n  *Worked example in book:* (20, 10, 9, 7, 11, 2, 21, 1, 20, 31), 10 = 3² + 1 numbers, has increasing subsequence (10, 11, 21, 31) of length 4 = 3 + 1.\n\n### 1.3.6 Proof by induction — TEMPLATE (memorize the two named steps)\nTo prove statement P(n) for all positive integers n:\n1. **Basis:** Prove P(1) is true.\n2. **Induction step:** Prove for all n ≥ 1: if P(n) is true, then P(n + 1) is true. The assumption \"P(n) is true\" is the **induction hypothesis**.\n\n- **Theorem 1.3.11** — 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2.\n  *Proof idea:* Basis n = 1: both sides equal 1. Step: (1 + … + n) + (n + 1) = n(n+1)/2 + (n+1) = (n+1)(n+2)/2.\n  *Alternative (Gauss trick):* write S forwards and backwards, add: 2S = n(n + 1).\n- **Theorem 1.3.12** — For every n ≥ 1, a − b is a factor of aⁿ − bⁿ.\n  *Proof idea:* Basis: a − b divides a − b. Step: aⁿ⁺¹ − bⁿ⁺¹ = aⁿ(a − b) + (aⁿ − bⁿ)b; first term divisible by a − b directly, second by the induction hypothesis. (Direct alternative: factor aⁿ − bⁿ = (a − b)(aⁿ⁻¹ + aⁿ⁻²b + … + bⁿ⁻¹).)\n- **Theorem 1.3.13** — Handshake theorem again, by induction on the number m of edges. Basis m = 0: sum of degrees = 0. Step: remove one edge {a, b} → sum drops by exactly 2; apply hypothesis to the smaller graph: 2m + 2 = 2(m + 1).\n\n### 1.3.7 More examples (mixed techniques)\n- **Theorem 1.3.14** — No 3-regular graph exists on an odd number n ≥ 5 of vertices.\n  *Proof idea:* (Contradiction + handshake.) If one existed, Σ deg(v) = 3n and also = 2m, so m = 3n/2; since gcd(2, 3) = 1, n must be even — contradiction.\n- **Theorem 1.3.15** — For n ≥ 2 and any graph G on n vertices: **G is connected or Ḡ is connected.**\n  *Proof idea:* Induction on n. Basis n = 2 (one edge → G connected; no edge → Ḡ connected). Step: 3 cases on some vertex v — deg 0 (Ḡ connected via v), deg n (G connected via v), else delete v, apply hypothesis to G′, and reattach v using an edge it must have in whichever of G′/Ḡ′ is connected.\n  Rephrased as **Theorem 1.3.16**: 2-color the edges of Kₙ red/blue; then the red graph or the blue graph is connected (baby Ramsey-style statement).\n- **Theorem 1.3.17 (Euler, general form)** — For an embedding of a planar graph with v vertices, e edges, f faces (counting the unbounded face), and c connected components: **v − e + f = c + 1**.\n  *Proof idea:* Induction on e. Basis e = 0: f = 1, c = v, formula holds. Step, adding one edge: either it joins two components (c down 1, e up 1 — balanced) or joins vertices in the same component (it splits a face: f up 1, e up 1 — balanced).\n- **Theorem 1.3.18 (Euler, usual form)** — Connected planar graph: **v − e + f = 2** (the c = 1 case).\n\n### Which technique for which claim shape? (exam cheat-sheet)\n| Claim shape | Technique |\n|---|---|\n| \"If A then B\" with usable algebraic hypothesis | **Direct** (rewrite A, compute to B) |\n| \"There exists an object with property P\" | **Constructive** (build it) — or nonconstructive case analysis if you can't build it |\n| \"X is irrational / there is no … / X is impossible\" | **Contradiction** (assume it exists/is rational, derive absurdity) |\n| \"For all n ≥ n₀, P(n)\" (sums, divisibility, graph statements parameterized by size) | **Induction** on n (basis + induction step) |\n| \"Among any n+1 things from n classes, two collide/share\" | **Pigeonhole** (often wrapped inside a contradiction) |\n| \"A iff B\" | Two proofs: A ⇒ B and B ⇒ A |\n| \"Set A = set B\" | Prove A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A |\n\n---\n\n## Key exercises (§1.4 — quiz questions often come from these)\n\n1. **Ex 1.1** — Every integer n ≥ 2 is a product of primes. *Hint:* strong induction; if n is prime, done; else n = ab with 2 ≤ a, b < n, apply the hypothesis to both factors.\n2. **Ex 1.2** — For every prime p, √p is irrational. *Hint:* mirror the √2 proof: √p = m/n, gcd 1 ⇒ pn² = m² ⇒ p | m (p prime!) ⇒ m = pk ⇒ p | n — contradiction.\n3. **Ex 1.3** — If n is not a perfect square, √n is irrational. *Hint:* contradiction; use a prime appearing to an odd power in n's factorization (or minimal denominator argument).\n4. **Ex 1.4** — 3 divides n⁴ − 4n² for all n ≥ 1. *Hint:* induction; expand (n+1)⁴ − 4(n+1)² minus (n⁴ − 4n²) and show the difference is divisible by 3 (it's 4n³ + 6n² − 4n − 3, and 4n³ − 4n = 4n(n−1)(n+1) has 3 consecutive integers).\n5. **Ex 1.5** — Σᵢ₌₁ⁿ 1/i² < 2 − 1/n for n ≥ 2. *Hint:* induction; in the step it suffices that 1/(n+1)² < 1/n − 1/(n+1) = 1/(n(n+1)).\n6. **Ex 1.6** — 9 divides n³ + (n+1)³ + (n+2)³ for n ≥ 0. *Hint:* induction; the step's difference is (n+3)³ − n³ = 9n² + 27n + 27 = 9(n² + 3n + 3).\n7. **Ex 1.7** — Any n + 1 numbers from {1, …, 2n} contain two consecutive numbers. *Hint:* pigeonhole with the n boxes {1,2}, {3,4}, …, {2n−1, 2n}.\n8. **Ex 1.8** — Any n + 1 numbers from {1, …, 2n} contain two where one divides the other. *Hint:* write each as 2ᵏ·q with q odd; only n possible odd parts q ⇒ two numbers share q ⇒ the smaller divides the larger.\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What are the three areas of the Theory of Computation and the central question of each?","a":"Complexity theory — classify problems by difficulty and prove hard problems are really hard; Computability theory — classify problems as solvable or unsolvable; Automata theory — do different computation models have the same power?"},{"q":"Define an alphabet.","a":"A finite set whose elements are called symbols, e.g. Σ = {0, 1}."},{"q":"Define a string over an alphabet Σ, and |w|.","a":"A finite sequence of symbols, each from Σ; |w| is the number of symbols in w (its length)."},{"q":"What is ϵ and what is |ϵ|?","a":"The empty string, the string of length zero: |ϵ| = 0."},{"q":"Define a language. What is Σ\\*?","a":"A language is a set of strings. Σ\\* is the set of all (finite) strings over Σ, including ϵ; every language over Σ is a subset of Σ\\*."},{"q":"Is ∅ the same language as {ϵ}?","a":"No — ∅ contains no strings; {ϵ} contains exactly one string, the empty string."},{"q":"Define the power set P(B) and give two elements it always contains.","a":"P(B) = {A : A ⊆ B}, the set of all subsets of B; it always contains ∅ and B itself."},{"q":"When is a binary relation R ⊆ A × B a function f : A → B?","a":"When for each a ∈ A there is exactly one ordered pair in R whose first component is a."},{"q":"Define injective, surjective, and bijection.","a":"Injective (one-to-one): distinct inputs have distinct images, a ≠ a′ ⇒ f(a) ≠ f(a′). Surjective (onto): every b ∈ B is f(a) for some a (range = B). Bijection: both."},{"q":"What three properties make R ⊆ A × A an equivalence relation?","a":"Reflexive ((a,a) ∈ R for all a), symmetric ((a,b) ∈ R ⇒ (b,a) ∈ R), transitive ((a,b), (b,c) ∈ R ⇒ (a,c) ∈ R)."},{"q":"State the handshake theorem (Theorem 1.3.3) and its one-line proof.","a":"Σ_{v∈V} deg(v) = 2|E|; each edge is incident on exactly two vertices, so it contributes 2 to the sum."},{"q":"Which proof technique proves \"there exists an object with property P\" by exhibiting the object, and which proves existence without exhibiting it?","a":"Constructive proof exhibits and verifies the object; nonconstructive proof (e.g. the √2^√2 case analysis for irrational x, y with x^y rational) shows existence without producing an example."},{"q":"Give the template of a proof by contradiction and say why it is valid.","a":"To prove S, assume ¬S and derive something false; this establishes ¬S ⇒ false, whose contrapositive is true ⇒ S, i.e. S is true."},{"q":"Sketch the proof that √2 is irrational.","a":"Assume √2 = m/n with gcd(m, n) = 1; then 2n² = m² ⇒ m² even ⇒ m even ⇒ m = 2k ⇒ n² = 2k² ⇒ n even; m and n both even contradicts gcd(m, n) = 1."},{"q":"State the pigeonhole principle (both formulations).","a":"If n + 1 or more objects are placed into n boxes, some box holds at least two; equivalently, if |A| > |B| there is no one-to-one function from A to B."},{"q":"What are the two named steps of a proof by induction, and what is the induction hypothesis?","a":"Basis: prove P(1). Induction step: for arbitrary n ≥ 1, assume P(n) (the induction hypothesis) and prove P(n + 1)."},{"q":"Which technique fits: (a) \"for all n, 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n+1)/2\", (b) \"√p is irrational\", (c) \"among any n+1 numbers from {1..2n} two are consecutive\"?","a":"(a) induction on n, (b) contradiction, (c) pigeonhole (boxes {1,2}, {3,4}, …, {2n−1,2n})."},{"q":"How do you prove \"A if and only if B\", and how do you prove \"set A = set B\"?","a":"Iff: prove both implications A ⇒ B and B ⇒ A. Set equality: prove both inclusions A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A."},{"q":"State Euler's formula for a connected planar graph and the general version with c components.","a":"Connected: v − e + f = 2 (f counts the unbounded face); general: v − e + f = c + 1. Proved by induction on the number of edges."},{"q":"Why is there no 3-regular graph on an odd number n ≥ 5 of vertices?","a":"By contradiction with the handshake theorem: 3n = Σ deg(v) = 2m would force m = 3n/2, so n must be even."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"b2","title":"Book 2 · Finite Automata & Regular Langs","slides":"M&S ch. 2 · weeks 3–6","md":"# B2 — Finite Automata and Regular Languages (Maheshwari & Smid, Chapter 2)\n\n**Aligned with course weeks 3–6** — DFA announced as upcoming in Lecture 1; In-Class Quiz 1 (week 5) will likely reach DFA/NFA; In-Tutorial Quiz 1 (week 7) likely regex/pumping lemma.\n\nSource: Maheshwari & Smid, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*, Chapter 2 (PDF pages 29–98, book pages 21–90).\n\n---\n\n## 2.1 Motivating example: the toll gate\n\nA gate opens once the driver has paid at least 25 cents, using coins of 5, 10, 25 cents (no change returned). The controller only needs to remember **which of six states it is in**:\n\n- q0 = 0 cents collected, q1 = 5, q2 = 10, q3 = 15, q4 = 20, q5 = \"25 or more\" (gate opens).\n\nStart state q0; accept state q5 (drawn with a double circle). Reading coin sequence (10, 5, 5, 10): q0 → q2 → q3 → q4 → q5.\n\n**Takeaway (exam favorite):** a finite automaton needs only ⌈log 6⌉ = 3 bits of memory — constant memory, independent of input length. This is the informal definition of \"regular\": processable with a fixed finite amount of memory. As a formal FA: Q = {q0,…,q5}, Σ = {5, 10, 25}, start q0, F = {q5}.\n\n## 2.2 Deterministic finite automata (DFA)\n\n**Definition 2.2.1 (verbatim).** A finite automaton is a 5-tuple M = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F), where\n\n1. Q is a finite set, whose elements are called *states*,\n2. Σ is a finite set, called the *alphabet*; the elements of Σ are called *symbols*,\n3. δ : Q × Σ → Q is a function, called the *transition function*,\n4. q is an element of Q; it is called the *start state*,\n5. F is a subset of Q; the elements of F are called *accept states*.\n\nδ is the \"program\" of M: in state r reading symbol a, M switches to state δ(r, a) (possibly r itself). **Deterministic** = for every (state, symbol) pair there is exactly one next state.\n\n**Definition 2.2.2 (acceptance via a state sequence).** Let w = w1w2…wn be a string over Σ. Define states r0, r1, …, rn by r0 = q and r_{i+1} = δ(r_i, w_{i+1}) for i = 0, …, n−1. If r_n ∈ F then M **accepts** w; if r_n ∉ F then M **rejects** w. For w = ϵ (n = 0) the sequence is just r0 = q, so **ϵ is accepted iff the start state is in F**.\n\n**Definition 2.2.3.** L(M) = {w : w is a string over Σ and M accepts w} — the *language accepted by M*.\n\n**Definition 2.2.4.** A language A is called **regular** if there exists a finite automaton M such that A = L(M).\n\n**Extended transition function** δ : Q × Σ* → Q, defined recursively:\n\n- δ(r, w) = r if w = ϵ,\n- δ(r, w) = δ(δ(r, v), a) if w = va with v a string and a ∈ Σ.\n\nMeaning: δ(r, w) is the state M reaches starting from r after reading all of w. Equivalently: **L(M) = {w ∈ Σ* : δ(q, w) ∈ F}**.\n\n### Worked machine example 1 (Section 2.2, opening example)\n\n**Language:** L(M) = {w : w contains at least one 1 and ends with an even number of 0s} over {0,1}.\n**Machine:** Q = {q1, q2, q3}, start q1, F = {q2}. Transitions: δ(q1,0)=q1, δ(q1,1)=q2, δ(q2,0)=q3, δ(q2,1)=q2, δ(q3,0)=q2, δ(q3,1)=q2. Intuition: q1 = \"no 1 seen yet\"; q2 = \"seen a 1, even number of 0s since the last 1\"; q3 = \"odd number of 0s since the last 1\". Trace: 1101 → q1,q2,q2,q3,q2 ∈ F, accepted; 0101010 ends in q3, rejected.\n\n### Worked machine example 2 (Section 2.2.1) — parity\n\n**Language:** A = {w : w is a binary string containing an odd number of 1s}.\n**Machine:** Q = {qe, qo} (even/odd number of 1s so far), Σ = {0,1}, start qe, F = {qo}. δ: on 0 stay put; on 1 flip qe ↔ qo.\n**Key design lesson:** the naive idea \"one state per count i\" needs infinitely many states — illegal. A DFA can only remember a *finite* abstraction of the input (here: parity). This \"what must the machine remember?\" question is THE way to design DFAs.\n\n### Worked machine example 3 (Section 2.2.2) — substring 101\n\n**Language:** A = {w : w contains 101 as a substring}.\n**Machine:** Q = {q, q1, q10, q101}, start q, F = {q101}. States remember the longest useful suffix seen: q = no progress, q1 = last symbol was 1, q10 = last two were 10, q101 = 101 already seen (trap accept state).\nδ: (q,0)=q, (q,1)=q1; (q1,0)=q10, (q1,1)=q1; (q10,0)=q, (q10,1)=q101; (q101,0)=(q101,1)=q101.\n\n### Worked machine example 4 (Section 2.2.3) — third symbol from the right\n\n**Language:** A = {w ∈ {0,1}* : w has a 1 in the third position from the right}.\n**Machine:** 8 states q_ijk, i,j,k ∈ {0,1}, remembering the last three symbols read (padded with 0s early on). Start q000; accept states {q100, q110, q101, q111} (those with i = 1). Reading symbol b in state q_ijk moves to q_jkb. Shows a DFA can handle \"position from the right\" — but needs 2³ states; compare the 4-state NFA below.\n\n## 2.3 Regular operations\n\nFor languages A, B over the same alphabet:\n\n1. **Union:** A ∪ B = {w : w ∈ A or w ∈ B}.\n2. **Concatenation:** AB = {ww′ : w ∈ A and w′ ∈ B}.\n3. **Star:** A* = {u1u2…uk : k ≥ 0 and ui ∈ A for all i = 1,…,k}. Note k = 0 is allowed, so **ϵ ∈ A\\* always**.\n\nExample: A = {0, 01}, B = {1, 10} gives A ∪ B = {0, 01, 1, 10}, AB = {01, 010, 011, 0110}. Equivalent star definition: A⁰ = {ϵ}, Aᵏ = AA^{k−1}, and A* = ⋃_{k≥0} Aᵏ.\n\n**Theorem 2.3.1 (union, product construction — DFA proof).** If A and B are regular then A ∪ B is regular.\n*Proof idea:* An FA reads the input only once, so it cannot run M1 then M2. Instead run them **simultaneously**: build M with Q = Q1 × Q2, start (q1, q2), δ((r1,r2), a) = (δ1(r1,a), δ2(r2,a)), and F = {(r1,r2) : r1 ∈ F1 or r2 ∈ F2} = (F1×Q2) ∪ (Q1×F2). Correctness: the extended function satisfies δ((q1,q2),w) = (δ1(q1,w), δ2(q2,w)). (With \"and\" instead of \"or\", i.e. F = F1×F2, the same construction proves closure under intersection.)\n\nConcatenation and star are much harder with DFAs (the machine would have to guess where to split the input during a single scan) — this motivates NFAs.\n\n## 2.4 Nondeterministic finite automata (NFA)\n\nThree new features vs. a DFA: (i) a state may have **several** outgoing edges with the same label (choice); (ii) **ϵ-transitions**: switch state without reading a symbol; (iii) a state may have **no** edge for some symbol — the computation **hangs** (dies).\n\nNotation: Σ_ϵ = Σ ∪ {ϵ}; P(Q) = power set of Q = {R : R ⊆ Q}.\n\n**Definition 2.4.1 (verbatim).** A nondeterministic finite automaton (NFA) is a 5-tuple M = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F), where\n\n1. Q is a finite set, whose elements are called *states*,\n2. Σ is a finite set, called the *alphabet*; the elements of Σ are called *symbols*,\n3. δ : Q × Σ_ϵ → P(Q) is a function, called the *transition function*,\n4. q is an element of Q; it is called the *start state*,\n5. F is a subset of Q; the elements of F are called *accept states*.\n\n**The only difference from the DFA 5-tuple is item 3:** δ maps to a *set* of states (possibly ∅) and also handles ϵ. If δ(r,a) = ∅ the computation hangs.\n\n**Definition 2.4.2 (NFA acceptance).** M accepts w if either w = ϵ and q ∈ F, or there exists m ≥ 1 such that w = y1y2…ym with each yi ∈ Σ_ϵ, and there exists a sequence of states r0, r1, …, rm with r0 = q, r_{i+1} ∈ δ(r_i, y_{i+1}) for i = 0,…,m−1, and r_m ∈ F. Otherwise M rejects w.\n**In words: accept iff at least ONE computation path (i) starts at the start state, (ii) does not hang before the whole string is read, and (iii) ends in an accept state.** Reject = every path fails.\n\n### Worked NFA example 1 (Section 2.4.1)\n\n**Language:** binary strings containing 101 or 11 as a substring. Q = {q1,q2,q3,q4}, start q1, F = {q4}. δ table: δ(q1,0)={q1}, δ(q1,1)={q1,q2}; δ(q2,0)={q3}, δ(q2,ϵ)={q3}; δ(q3,1)={q4}; δ(q4,0)=δ(q4,1)={q4}; all other entries ∅. On input 010110 there are seven possible computations; exactly two end in q4, so 010110 is accepted. On 010 all three computations fail, so 010 is rejected.\n\n### Worked NFA example 2 (Section 2.4.2) — guess and verify\n\n**Language:** A = {w : w has a 1 in the third position from the right}. Four states q1→q2→q3→q4: stay in q1 on 0,1; on reading a 1 the NFA may **guess** \"this is the third symbol from the right\" and move to q2; then q2→q3→q4 on any symbols verify exactly two symbols remain (extra symbols make it hang in q4... there is no transition out of q4). Compare: the DFA for the same language needed 8 states. **Guess-and-verify is the canonical NFA design pattern.**\n\n### Worked NFA example 3 (Section 2.4.3) — union by guessing\n\n**Language:** A = {0^k : k ≡ 0 mod 2 or k ≡ 0 mod 3} over Σ = {0}. The NFA has a start state with two ϵ-transitions: one into a 2-state cycle (checks length even), one into a 3-state cycle (checks length divisible by 3). At the start it guesses which condition to verify.\n\n## 2.5 Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (subset construction)\n\nDFA → NFA is trivial: set δ′(r,a) = {δ(r,a)} for a ≠ ϵ and δ′(r,ϵ) = ∅; same state diagram, same language.\n\n**Theorem 2.5.1.** Let N = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F) be an NFA. There exists a DFA M such that L(M) = L(N).\n\n*Proof idea:* Build a DFA that runs **all computations of N simultaneously**; its state after reading a prefix is exactly the set of states N could be in after that prefix.\n\n**ϵ-closure:** C_ϵ(r) = set of all states reachable from r by zero or more ϵ-transitions; C_ϵ(R) = ⋃_{r∈R} C_ϵ(r).\n\n**The subset construction (memorize):** N = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F) becomes M = (Q′, Σ, δ′, q′, F′) where\n\n- Q′ = P(Q) (so |Q′| = 2^|Q|),\n- q′ = C_ϵ({q}),\n- F′ = {R ∈ Q′ : R ∩ F ≠ ∅} (accept iff the set contains at least one accept state of N),\n- δ′(R, a) = ⋃_{r∈R} C_ϵ(δ(r, a)) — read one real symbol, then absorb any ϵ-moves.\n\n(Without ϵ-transitions this simplifies to q′ = {q} and δ′(R,a) = ⋃_{r∈R} δ(r,a).)\n\n**Theorem 2.5.2.** A language A is regular **if and only if** there exists an NFA that accepts A.\n\n### Worked example (Section 2.5.1) — full conversion\n\nNFA N: Q = {1,2,3}, Σ = {a,b}, start 1, F = {2}; δ(1,a)={3}, δ(1,ϵ)={2}; δ(2,a)={1}; δ(3,a)={2}, δ(3,b)={2,3}; all else ∅.\nConversion: q′ = C_ϵ({1}) = {1,2}; F′ = {{2},{1,2},{2,3},{1,2,3}}. Key transitions: δ′({1,2},a) = C_ϵ({3}) ∪ C_ϵ({1}) = {1,2,3}, δ′({1,2},b) = ∅; δ′({1,2,3},a) = {1,2,3}, δ′({1,2,3},b) = {2,3}; δ′({2,3},a) = {1,2}, δ′({2,3},b) = {2,3}; δ′(∅,a) = δ′(∅,b) = ∅.\n**Practical tip:** of the 8 subsets, only {1,2}, {1,2,3}, {2,3}, ∅ are reachable from the start state — build only reachable states (do a BFS from q′) and you avoid the exponential blow-up on quizzes.\n\n## 2.6 Closure under the regular operations (NFA constructions — memorize the pictures)\n\n**Theorem 2.6.1 (union, NFA version).** Given NFAs M1, M2 (with disjoint state sets), build M: new start state q0 with ϵ-transitions to both q1 and q2; Q = {q0} ∪ Q1 ∪ Q2; F = F1 ∪ F2; all old transitions kept. M guesses which machine will accept.\n\n**Theorem 2.6.2 (concatenation).** Build M: Q = Q1 ∪ Q2; start state q0 = q1 (start of M1); F = F2 (accept states of M2 only); keep all old transitions and **add an ϵ-transition from every accept state of M1 to the start state q2 of M2** (formally δ(r,ϵ) = δ1(r,ϵ) ∪ {q2} for r ∈ F1). M nondeterministically guesses where to split w ∈ A1A2.\n\n**Theorem 2.6.3 (star).** Build M: new start state q0 with Q = {q0} ∪ Q1; F = {q0} ∪ F1 (q0 must accept since ϵ ∈ A*); ϵ-transition from q0 to q1, and **ϵ-transitions from every state of F1 back to q1**. Warning (Exercise 2.7): you cannot just make the old start state accepting — extra strings could then be accepted (paths may return through q1 mid-word).\n\n**Theorem 2.6.4 (stated without proof).** Regular languages are also closed under **complement** (A̅ = Σ* \\ A) and **intersection**. (Proof recipe for complement: take a DFA — must be a DFA, not an NFA! — and swap accept/non-accept states. Intersection: product construction with F = F1 × F2, or De Morgan.)\n\n## 2.7 Regular expressions\n\n**Definition 2.7.1 (syntax, inductive).** Let Σ be a non-empty alphabet.\n1. ϵ is a regular expression.\n2. ∅ is a regular expression.\n3. For each a ∈ Σ, a is a regular expression.\n4. If R1, R2 are regular expressions, then R1 ∪ R2 is a regular expression.\n5. If R1, R2 are regular expressions, then R1R2 is a regular expression.\n6. If R is a regular expression, then R* is a regular expression.\n\n**Definition 2.7.2 (semantics).** ϵ describes {ϵ}; ∅ describes ∅; a describes {a}; R1 ∪ R2 describes L1 ∪ L2; R1R2 describes L1L2; R* describes L*. We write R1 = R2 (Def. 2.7.3) when they describe the same language.\n\nStandard examples over {0,1} (all quiz-worthy):\n- exactly two 0s: `1*01*01*`\n- at least two 0s: `(0∪1)*0(0∪1)*0(0∪1)*`\n- contains 1011: `(0∪1)*1011(0∪1)*`\n- even length: `((0∪1)(0∪1))*` ; odd length: `(0∪1)((0∪1)(0∪1))*`\n- first symbol = last symbol: `0(0∪1)*0 ∪ 1(0∪1)*1 ∪ 0 ∪ 1`\n- Watch the corner cases: **1\\*∅ = ∅** (concatenating with ∅ kills everything), but **∅\\* = {ϵ}**.\n\n**Theorem 2.7.4 (algebraic identities, most-used ones):** R∅ = ∅R = ∅; Rϵ = ϵR = R; R ∪ ∅ = R; R ∪ R = R; union is commutative; concatenation distributes over union both ways; ∅* = ϵ; ϵ* = ϵ; (ϵ ∪ R)* = R*; R*(ϵ ∪ R) = R*; R1(R2R1)* = (R1R2)*R1; (R1 ∪ R2)* = (R1\\*R2)\\*R1\\*. Sample use: (0 ∪ ϵ)1* = 01* ∪ ϵ1* = 01* ∪ 1*.\n\n## 2.8 Equivalence of regular expressions and regular languages\n\n**Theorem 2.8.1.** L is regular **iff** there exists a regular expression describing L. Two directions:\n\n### Direction 1 (Section 2.8.1): regex → NFA\n*Proof idea:* Induction on the structure of R. Base cases are tiny NFAs: for ϵ, a single accepting start state with no transitions; for ∅, a single non-accepting state; for a ∈ Σ, two states q1 →(a)→ q2 with q2 accepting. Induction step: R1 ∪ R2, R1R2, R* handled by the closure constructions of Theorems 2.6.1/2.6.2/2.6.3.\n**Worked example:** for (ab ∪ a)* build NFAs for a and b; concatenate to get ab (ϵ-link); union with a (new start, two ϵ-edges); apply star (new accepting start, ϵ-edges back). The book's M5 has 9 states — build it by mechanically applying the three constructions.\n\n### Direction 2 (Section 2.8.2): DFA → regex, via language equations\n(This book uses **recurrence/equation solving**, not GNFA state elimination.)\n\n**Lemma 2.8.2 (Arden-style).** Let B, C, L be languages with ϵ ∉ B and L = BL ∪ C. Then **L = B\\*C**.\n*Proof idea:* B*C ⊆ L by induction on the number of B-blocks; L ⊆ B*C by induction on string length — every u ∈ L is either in C or peels off a nonempty b ∈ B, and ϵ ∉ B guarantees the peeling strictly shortens the string.\n\n**The conversion.** For a DFA M and each state r, let L_r = strings that lead from r to an accept state (so L(M) = L_q for start state q). These satisfy:\n- L_r = ⋃_{a∈Σ} a·L_{δ(r,a)}  if r ∉ F   (2.2)\n- L_r = ϵ ∪ ⋃_{a∈Σ} a·L_{δ(r,a)}  if r ∈ F   (2.3)\n\nOne equation per state; substitute and repeatedly apply Lemma 2.8.2 to eliminate unknowns until L_q is a pure regular expression. Lemma 2.8.3 (induction on the number of unknowns) guarantees the system is always solvable.\n\n**Worked example (book, Sec. 2.8.2):** DFA with Q = {q0,q1,q2}, start q0, F = {q2}. Equations: L_{q0} = a·L_{q0} ∪ b·L_{q2}; L_{q1} = a·L_{q0} ∪ b·L_{q1}; L_{q2} = ϵ ∪ a·L_{q1} ∪ b·L_{q0}. Substituting L_{q2} into the first: L_{q0} = (a ∪ bb)L_{q0} ∪ ba·L_{q1} ∪ b. From the second equation, Lemma 2.8.2 gives L_{q1} = b\\*a·L_{q0}. Substituting back: L_{q0} = (a ∪ bb ∪ bab\\*a)L_{q0} ∪ b, so finally **L(M) = (a ∪ bb ∪ bab\\*a)\\*b**.\n\n## 2.9 The pumping lemma and nonregular languages\n\nIntuition: a regular language is decided with constant memory, so long strings must revisit a state — an infinite regular language must contain highly repetitive infinite subsets.\n\n**Theorem 2.9.1 (Pumping Lemma for Regular Languages) — EXACT statement, all quantifiers:**\nLet A be a regular language. Then there **exists** an integer p ≥ 1, called the *pumping length*, such that the following holds: **Every** string s in A, with |s| ≥ p, can be written as s = xyz, such that\n1. y ≠ ϵ (i.e., |y| ≥ 1),\n2. |xy| ≤ p, and\n3. **for all** i ≥ 0, x yⁱ z ∈ A.\n\nQuantifier structure: ∃p ∀s (s∈A, |s|≥p) ∃x,y,z (s=xyz, conditions 1–2) ∀i≥0 : xyⁱz ∈ A. Note i = 0 (pumping **down**) is allowed and sometimes essential.\n\n*Proof idea (4 lines):* Take a DFA for A with p states. A string s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p visits p+1 states within its first p symbols, so by the **pigeonhole principle** some state repeats: r_j = r_ℓ, j < ℓ ≤ p+1. Let x = part before the first visit, y = the loop (nonempty, |xy| ≤ p), z = the rest. The loop can be traversed 0, 1, 2, … times, so xyⁱz is accepted for all i ≥ 0.\n\n### Proof recipe for nonregularity (use in every quiz answer)\n\n1. Assume A is regular. Let p be the pumping length given by the pumping lemma (you do NOT get to choose p).\n2. **Choose a specific string s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p** — this is YOUR only real choice; pick s so that condition |xy| ≤ p forces y into a known region.\n3. The lemma writes s = xyz with y ≠ ϵ, |xy| ≤ p. Argue what y must look like (usually: y consists of only the first symbol type).\n4. **Choose one value of i** (usually i = 2 or i = 0) and show xyⁱz ∉ A.\n5. Contradiction with condition 3; hence A is not regular.\n\n### Worked nonregular examples\n\n1. **A = {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0}.** Take s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ. Since |xy| ≤ p, y is all 0s; y ≠ ϵ means it has ≥ 1 zero. Then xyyz has more 0s than 1s, ∉ A. Contradiction.\n2. **A = {w : #0s = #1s}.** Same string s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ, same argument: xy²z has more 0s than 1s.\n3. **A = {ww : w ∈ {0,1}*}.** Take s = 0ᵖ10ᵖ1. y is all 0s from the first block, so xy²z ∉ A. **Choosing s = 0^{2p} FAILS** (y of even length still pumps inside A) — string choice matters.\n4. **A = {0^m 1^n : m > n ≥ 0}.** Take s = 0^{p+1}1ᵖ and **pump down**: i = 0 gives xz with at most p zeros and exactly p ones, so m > n fails. (Here i = 2 would not give a contradiction.)\n5. **A = {1^{n²} : n ≥ 0}.** Take s = 1^{p²}. Then |xy²z| = p² + |y| with 1 ≤ |y| ≤ p, so p² < |xy²z| ≤ p² + p < (p+1)² — strictly between consecutive squares, hence not a square.\n6. **A = {1ⁿ : n prime}.** Take s = 1ⁿ, n ≥ p prime, y = 1ᵏ, k ≥ 1. Pump with i = n+1: length becomes n + nk = n(1+k), composite since n ≥ 2 and 1+k ≥ 2. Contradiction.\n7. **Cautionary tale (7th example): looks nonregular but IS regular.** A = {w : #occurrences of 01 = #occurrences of 10}. Between two 01s there is exactly one 10 (and vice versa), so the counts differ by at most 1; in fact A = ϵ ∪ strings whose first and last symbols are equal = `ϵ ∪ 0 ∪ 1 ∪ 0(0∪1)*0 ∪ 1(0∪1)*1`. A 5-state DFA also works. **Moral: \"counting\" languages are not automatically nonregular.**\n8. **(Hard, 8th example)** {w : w is the binary representation of a prime} is not regular — pump s = binary rep of a prime N > 2ᵖ with i = N; Fermat's Little Theorem shows N divides the pumped number. Know that this example exists; the details are beyond quiz level.\n\nAlso note (Exercise 2.30): the **converse of the pumping lemma is false** — some nonregular languages satisfy the pumping conditions. The pumping lemma can only prove NONregularity, never regularity.\n\n## 2.10 Higman's Theorem (brief)\n\nx is a **subsequence** of y if x is obtained by deleting zero or more symbols from y. SUBSEQ(L) = {x : x is a subsequence of some y ∈ L}.\n\n**Theorem 2.10.1 (Higman, 1952).** For any finite alphabet Σ and **any** language L ⊆ Σ* (regular or not, even undecidable!), SUBSEQ(L) is regular.\n\n*Proof idea:* Uses **Dickson's Theorem** (1913): any subset of ℕⁿ has finitely many minimal elements under coordinate-wise domination (induction on n). For Σ = {0,1}: strings with at most n (0,1)-alternations split into blocks 1ᵃ0ᵇ1ᶜ… ; map blocks to vectors in ℕⁿ, where \"subsequence\" = \"dominated by\". The complement of SUBSEQ(L) is determined by finitely many minimal forbidden strings, each contributing a regular expression; finite unions/intersections/complements of regular languages are regular.\n\n---\n\n## Key exercises\n\n1. **(Ex. 2.1.4, 2.1.7)** Build DFAs for: {w : w contains the substring 1011} and {w : w begins with 1 and ends with 0}. *Hint:* for substrings, states = longest prefix of 1011 matched so far (5 states, like the 101 example); for begin/end, remember (started with 1?, last symbol) — plus a dead state for strings starting with 0.\n2. **(Ex. 2.2.1)** Give a 3-state NFA for {w : w ends with 10}. *Hint:* guess-and-verify — loop on 0,1 in the start state, then guess the final 10: q1 →1→ q2 →0→ q3 (accept).\n3. **(Ex. 2.5 / 2.6)** Convert an NFA with ϵ-transitions to a DFA. *Hint:* first compute C_ϵ(r) for every state; start state is C_ϵ({q}); then build only the subsets reachable via δ′(R,a) = ⋃_{r∈R} C_ϵ(δ(r,a)); a subset accepts iff it meets F.\n4. **(Ex. 2.9)** Does swapping accept and non-accept states of an NFA accept the complement? *Hint:* No for NFAs — a string can have both an accepting and a rejecting path, so it may be accepted by both M and N. Yes for DFAs — exactly one computation per string. This is why complementation goes through DFAs.\n5. **(Ex. 2.10)** A* = ⋃_{k=0}^∞ Aᵏ and regular languages are closed under union and concatenation. Why doesn't that already prove closure under star? *Hint:* closure under union only applies to the union of *finitely many* languages; this union is infinite.\n6. **(Ex. 2.13.1, 2.13.6)** Give regexes for {w : at least three 1s} and {w : every odd position of w is 1}. *Hint:* `0*10*10*1(0∪1)*` for the first; for the second, think of w as pairs 1x with a possible trailing 1: `(1(0∪1))*(1 ∪ ϵ)`.\n7. **(Ex. 2.15–2.17)** Convert a 3-state DFA to a regular expression. *Hint:* write the equations (2.2)/(2.3), one per state (add ϵ for accept states); eliminate states one at a time with Lemma 2.8.2 (L = BL ∪ C, ϵ ∉ B ⟹ L = B*C); solve for L_start.\n8. **(Ex. 2.20.3, 2.21)** Prove {aⁿbᵐaⁿ : n,m ≥ 0} is not regular; prove {aᵐbⁿ : m ≠ n} is not regular. *Hint:* first one, pump s = aᵖbaᵖ (y is all leading a's). Second one, the pumping lemma is awkward directly — instead use closure: if A were regular then so would be A̅ ∩ a\\*b\\* = {aⁿbⁿ}, contradiction (complement and intersection preserve regularity).\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"State the formal definition of a finite automaton (DFA) as a 5-tuple.","a":"M = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F): Q a finite set of states; Σ a finite alphabet of symbols; δ : Q × Σ → Q the transition function; q ∈ Q the start state; F ⊆ Q the set of accept states."},{"q":"How does the NFA 5-tuple differ from the DFA 5-tuple?","a":"Only the transition function: NFA has δ : Q × Σ_ϵ → P(Q) (Σ_ϵ = Σ ∪ {ϵ}), mapping to a set of zero or more states, allowing choice, ϵ-transitions, and hanging (δ(r,a) = ∅). The other four components are identical."},{"q":"Define the extended transition function δ : Q × Σ* → Q of a DFA.","a":"δ(r, ϵ) = r; δ(r, va) = δ(δ(r, v), a) for a ∈ Σ. It gives the state reached from r after reading all of w, and L(M) = {w : δ(q, w) ∈ F}."},{"q":"When is a language A called regular?","a":"If there exists a finite automaton M such that A = L(M). (Equivalently, by Theorems 2.5.2 and 2.8.1: iff some NFA accepts A, iff some regular expression describes A.)"},{"q":"When does a DFA accept the empty string ϵ?","a":"Iff the start state q is an accept state (q ∈ F) — with n = 0 the state sequence is just r0 = q."},{"q":"When does an NFA accept a string w?","a":"If w can be written as y1…ym (yi ∈ Σ_ϵ) and there is a state sequence r0 = q, r_{i+1} ∈ δ(r_i, y_{i+1}), r_m ∈ F — i.e., at least one computation path reads all of w without hanging and ends in an accept state. Reject means every path fails."},{"q":"Define the three regular operations.","a":"Union A ∪ B = {w : w ∈ A or w ∈ B}; concatenation AB = {ww′ : w ∈ A, w′ ∈ B}; star A* = {u1…uk : k ≥ 0, each ui ∈ A}. Since k = 0 is allowed, ϵ ∈ A* always."},{"q":"Give the product construction proving regular languages are closed under union (DFA version).","a":"Q = Q1 × Q2, start (q1,q2), δ((r1,r2),a) = (δ1(r1,a), δ2(r2,a)), F = (F1×Q2) ∪ (Q1×F2). Runs both machines simultaneously; for intersection use F = F1 × F2 instead."},{"q":"Describe the subset construction converting an NFA N = (Q,Σ,δ,q,F) with ϵ-transitions to a DFA M.","a":"M = (Q′,Σ,δ′,q′,F′) with Q′ = P(Q), q′ = C_ϵ({q}), F′ = {R : R ∩ F ≠ ∅}, and δ′(R,a) = ⋃_{r∈R} C_ϵ(δ(r,a))."},{"q":"What is the ϵ-closure C_ϵ(r) and where does it appear in the subset construction?","a":"The set of all states reachable from r by zero or more ϵ-transitions. It defines the DFA's start state C_ϵ({q}) and is applied after each symbol: δ′(R,a) = ⋃_{r∈R} C_ϵ(δ(r,a))."},{"q":"How many states can the subset-construction DFA have, and how do you keep the number small in practice?","a":"Up to 2^|Q|, since Q′ = P(Q). In practice construct only subsets reachable from the start state (BFS following δ′); unreachable subsets can be discarded without changing the language."},{"q":"NFA construction for concatenation A1A2?","a":"Take NFAs M1, M2 with disjoint states; start state = start of M1; accept states = F2 only; add an ϵ-transition from every state of F1 to the start state of M2. The machine guesses where to split the input."},{"q":"NFA construction for star A*, and why is a NEW start state needed?","a":"Add a new start state q0 that is accepting (so ϵ is accepted), with an ϵ-edge to q1, and add ϵ-edges from every state of F1 back to q1. Simply making the old start state accepting is invalid (Exercise 2.7): the machine could then accept extra strings via paths that pass through q1 mid-computation."},{"q":"Why does complementing a DFA's accept states give the complement language, but doing the same to an NFA fail?","a":"A DFA has exactly one computation per string, so \"ends in F\" and \"ends in Q∖F\" are complementary events. An NFA string can have accepting AND non-accepting paths, so it can be accepted both before and after the swap."},{"q":"Give the inductive definition of regular expressions over Σ.","a":"Base: ϵ, ∅, and a for each a ∈ Σ are regular expressions. Induction: if R1, R2, R are regular expressions, so are R1 ∪ R2, R1R2, and R*. Semantics: {ϵ}, ∅, {a}, L1 ∪ L2, L1L2, L*."},{"q":"What languages do 1\\*∅ and ∅\\* describe?","a":"1\\*∅ describes ∅ (concatenation with ∅ yields ∅: R∅ = ∅), while ∅\\* describes {ϵ} (k = 0 concatenations always give ϵ)."},{"q":"State Lemma 2.8.2 (the equation-solving lemma) used to convert DFAs to regular expressions.","a":"If ϵ ∉ B and L = BL ∪ C, then L = B*C. Repeatedly applied to the system L_r = ⋃_a a·L_{δ(r,a)} (with an extra ∪ ϵ when r ∈ F), it expresses L(M) = L_q as a regular expression."},{"q":"State the pumping lemma for regular languages exactly, with all quantifiers.","a":"If A is regular, there EXISTS p ≥ 1 such that EVERY s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p can be written s = xyz with (1) y ≠ ϵ, (2) |xy| ≤ p, and (3) FOR ALL i ≥ 0, xyⁱz ∈ A. (∃p ∀s ∃xyz ∀i.)"},{"q":"Give the 5-step recipe for proving a language A nonregular with the pumping lemma, and sketch it for {0ⁿ1ⁿ}.","a":"(1) assume A regular, get pumping length p; (2) pick a specific s ∈ A, |s| ≥ p; (3) for any split s = xyz with y ≠ ϵ, |xy| ≤ p, determine what y must be; (4) pick one i (often 2 or 0) with xyⁱz ∉ A; (5) contradiction. For {0ⁿ1ⁿ}: s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ forces y to be all 0s, and xy²z has more 0s than 1s."},{"q":"Why does the pumping-lemma proof work — what principle produces the loop — and can the lemma prove a language regular?","a":"A DFA with p states reading a string of length ≥ p visits p+1 states among r1,…,r_{p+1}, so by the pigeonhole principle some state repeats; the substring y between the two visits is a loop that can be traversed i times. No: the converse fails (Ex. 2.30) — the lemma only proves nonregularity. (Bonus: Higman — SUBSEQ(L) is regular for EVERY language L.)"}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"b3","title":"Book 3 · Context-Free Languages","slides":"M&S ch. 3 · weeks 7–9","md":"# B3 — Context-Free Languages (Maheshwari & Smid, Chapter 3)\n\n**Aligned with course weeks 7–9 (estimate)** — likely straddles the midterm (25%, TBA ~week 8); expect grammar-writing and CNF-conversion exam tasks.\n\nSource: *Introduction to Theory of Computation*, Maheshwari & Smid, Chapter 3 (PDF pages 99–144, book pages 91–136). Class contains all regular languages plus nonregular ones like {0^n 1^n : n ≥ 0}; two equivalent formalisms: context-free grammars (CFGs) and nondeterministic pushdown automata (PDAs).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Context-free grammars (§3.1)\n\n**Definition 3.1.1 (CFG, 4-tuple — memorize verbatim).** A context-free grammar is a 4-tuple G = (V, Σ, R, S), where\n\n1. V is a finite set, whose elements are called **variables**,\n2. Σ is a finite set, whose elements are called **terminals**,\n3. V ∩ Σ = ∅,\n4. S is an element of V; it is called the **start variable**,\n5. R is a finite set, whose elements are called **rules**. Each rule has the form A → w, where A ∈ V and w ∈ (V ∪ Σ)*.\n\n(The book lists 5 numbered conditions inside the 4-tuple — the disjointness condition V ∩ Σ = ∅ is part of the definition; don't drop it on an exam.)\n\n**Derivations.**\n- **One step** (Def 3.1.2): if A → w is a rule and u, v ∈ (V ∪ Σ)*, then uAv ⇒ uwv (\"uwv can be derived in one step from uAv\").\n- **Zero or more steps** (Def 3.1.3): u ⇒* v iff u = v, or there is a chain u = u₁ ⇒ u₂ ⇒ … ⇒ u_k = v (k ≥ 2). I.e., v is obtained from u by applying rules zero or more times.\n- **Language of G** (Def 3.1.4): L(G) = {w ∈ Σ* : S ⇒* w} — all **terminal-only** strings derivable from the start variable.\n- **Context-free language** (Def 3.1.5): L is context-free iff some CFG G has L(G) = L.\n\n**Book's opening example.** V = {S, A, B}, Σ = {a, b}, rules S → AB, A → a | aA, B → b | bB. Language: {aᵐbⁿ : m ≥ 1, n ≥ 1}. Sample derivation: S ⇒ AB ⇒ aAB ⇒ … ⇒ aaaabb. Derivations can be drawn as **parse trees** (root = S, leaves left-to-right spell the string).\n\nNotation: `A → w₁ | w₂` abbreviates two rules (\"|\" = \"or\").\n\n---\n\n## 2. Worked grammar examples (§3.2) — know these cold\n\n| Language | Grammar (start variable first) | Design idea |\n|---|---|---|\n| Properly nested parentheses (a = \"(\", b = \")\") | S → ϵ \\| aSb \\| SS | Empty, or wrap a balanced string in ( ), or concatenate two balanced strings |\n| {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0} (nonregular!) | S₁ → ϵ \\| 0S₁1 | Add matching 0 and 1 simultaneously on the outside |\n| {1ⁿ0ⁿ : n ≥ 0} | S₂ → ϵ \\| 1S₂0 | Symmetric |\n| {0ⁿ1ⁿ} ∪ {1ⁿ0ⁿ} | S → S₁ \\| S₂ plus the two grammars above | **Union trick**: new start variable choosing between sub-grammars |\n| Complement of {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0} | S → S₁ \\| S₂ \\| S₃; S₁ → 1 \\| S₁1 \\| 0S₁1; S₂ → 0 \\| 0S₂ \\| 0S₂1; S₃ → X10X; X → ϵ \\| 0X \\| 1X | w ∈ complement iff w = 0ᵐ1ⁿ with m < n (S₁), or 0ᵐ1ⁿ with n < m (S₂), or w contains substring 10 (S₃; X generates all of {0,1}*) |\n| Addition-verifier {aⁿbᵐcⁿ⁺ᵐ : n ≥ 0, m ≥ 0} | A → aAc \\| B; B → ϵ \\| bBc (start variable A) | Each a pairs with a c on the outside; then B sits between as and cs and pairs each b with a c. Book first builds S → ϵ\\|A, A → ϵ\\|aAc\\|B, B → ϵ\\|bBc, then simplifies |\n| Palindrome-style {ww^R : w ∈ {a,b}*} (§3.8.2) | S → ϵ \\| aSa \\| bSb | Matching outer symbols; contrast: {ww} is NOT context-free |\n\n**Reusable design patterns:** (1) matched outer pairs → A → xAy; (2) union → S → S₁|S₂; (3) concatenation of independent parts → S → S₁S₂; (4) \"anything\" → X → ϵ|0X|1X; (5) a variable as a \"pointer\" marking the position where symbols must be inserted (the B in the addition grammar).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Regular ⟹ context-free (§3.3)\n\n**Theorem 3.3.1.** Let Σ be an alphabet and let L ⊆ Σ* be a regular language. Then L is a context-free language.\n\n**Construction (from a DFA M = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F)).** Build G = (V, Σ, R, S) with:\n- V = Q (variables = states),\n- S = q (start variable = start state),\n- for every transition δ(A, a) = B, a rule **A → aB**,\n- for every accept state A ∈ F, a rule **A → ϵ**.\n\n**Invariant / proof idea:** M is in state A after reading w₁…w_i ⟺ S ⇒* w₁…w_i A. A derivation simulates the state sequence r₀, r₁, …, r_n; it can terminate in a terminal-only string exactly when the current variable is an accept state (rule A → ϵ). So S ⇒* w iff M accepts w. (Book proves L ⊆ L(G) in detail; converse left as exercise.)\n\n**Worked example (§3.3.1).** L = {w ∈ {0,1}* : 101 is a substring of w}, DFA states S, A, B, C (C accepting sink). Resulting grammar:\n- S → 0S | 1A\n- A → 0B | 1A\n- B → 0S | 1C\n- C → 0C | 1C | ϵ\n\nE.g. S ⇒* 010011011 mirrors the DFA's visit sequence S,S,A,B,S,A,A,B,C,C; 10011 is not derivable because it strands the derivation at non-accepting A (no A → ϵ rule).\n\nSince {0ⁿ1ⁿ} is context-free but not regular, **regular ⊊ context-free** (proper containment).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Chomsky normal form (§3.4)\n\n**Definition 3.4.1.** G = (V, Σ, R, S) is in **Chomsky normal form (CNF)** if every rule has one of these three forms:\n\n1. A → BC, where A, B, C ∈ V, **B ≠ S and C ≠ S** (start variable never on a right-hand side),\n2. A → a, where A ∈ V and a ∈ Σ,\n3. S → ϵ, where S is the start variable (the only ϵ-rule allowed; present iff ϵ ∈ L(G)).\n\n**Theorem 3.4.2.** Every context-free language has a CFG in Chomsky normal form.\n\n**Conversion — 5 steps IN THIS ORDER (exam favorite):**\n\n1. **New start variable.** Add fresh S₁ with rule S₁ → S. Guarantees the start variable never appears on any right-hand side.\n2. **Eliminate ϵ-rules** (A → ϵ, A ≠ start). Remove A → ϵ; for every rule with A on the right, add versions with A deleted: B → A gives B → ϵ (unless already deleted); B → uAv gives B → uv; B → uAvAw gives B → uvw, B → uAvw, B → uvAw (every subset of occurrences); never re-add an ϵ-rule already eliminated. Repeat until no ϵ-rules remain (S → ϵ may remain).\n3. **Eliminate unit-rules** (A → B, both variables). Remove A → B; for every rule B → u add A → u, unless that unit-rule was already eliminated. Repeat.\n4. **Break long right-hand sides.** A → u₁u₂…u_k with k ≥ 3 becomes the chain A → u₁A₁, A₁ → u₂A₂, …, A_{k−3} → u_{k−2}A_{k−2}, A_{k−2} → u_{k−1}u_k, with fresh variables A₁,…,A_{k−2}.\n5. **Lift terminals out of length-2 rules.** In A → u₁u₂ where not both are variables, replace each terminal a by a fresh variable U with rule U → a (e.g. A → aB becomes A → UB, U → a; A → ab becomes A → U₁U₂, U₁ → a, U₂ → b; A → aa needs only one new variable).\n\nEach step preserves the language: L(G₅) = L(G₄) = … = L(G).\n\n**Order matters:** step 2 can create unit-rules (so it precedes step 3); step 3 can create long rules; the bookkeeping \"unless already deleted\" prevents infinite loops.\n\n**Worked example (§3.4.1).** Start: A → BAB | B | ϵ, B → 00 | ϵ (start A). After all five steps the CNF grammar is:\n- S → ϵ | BB | AB | BA | BA₁ | A₃A₃\n- A → BB | AB | BA | BA₂ | A₄A₄\n- B → A₅A₅\n- A₁ → AB, A₂ → AB\n- A₃ → 0, A₄ → 0, A₅ → 0\n\n(Intermediate checkpoints worth remembering: after step 2 the grammar is S → A|ϵ, A → BAB|B|BB|AB|BA|A, B → 00; after step 3 unit-rules A → A, S → A, S → B, A → B are gone and S, A each get the alternative 00.)\n\n**Useful CNF fact (Lemma 3.8.2):** in a CNF grammar, a parse tree of height ℓ yields a string of length ≤ 2^{ℓ−1}. (Used to prove the pumping lemma; also: rules never shorten strings.)\n\n---\n\n## 5. Pushdown automata (§3.5)\n\nThis book's PDA model is slightly nonstandard — learn ITS conventions:\n\n**Definition 3.5.1 (deterministic PDA, 5-tuple).** M = (Σ, Γ, Q, δ, q), where\n\n1. Σ is the **tape alphabet** (input alphabet); the blank symbol □ ∉ Σ,\n2. Γ is the **stack alphabet**, containing the special bottom symbol **$**,\n3. Q is the finite set of **states**,\n4. q ∈ Q is the **start state**,\n5. δ : Q × (Σ ∪ {□}) × Γ → Q × {N, R} × Γ* is the **transition function**.\n\n**One computation step.** δ(r, a, A) = (r′, σ, w), written as the instruction **raA → r′σw**, means: in state r, reading tape symbol a, with A on top of the stack — switch to state r′; the tape head moves right if σ = R, stays put if σ = N; the top stack symbol A is **replaced** by the string w ∈ Γ*:\n- w = ϵ: A is **popped**;\n- w = B₁B₂…B_k: A is replaced and **B_k becomes the new top**.\n\n**Start configuration:** state q, head on the leftmost input symbol, stack containing only $.\n\n**Termination:** the computation terminates exactly when the **stack becomes empty** (never empties ⇒ runs forever).\n\n**Acceptance:** M accepts input a₁…a_n iff (1) it terminates, and (2) at termination the head is on the cell just right of a_n (the blank □). Otherwise it rejects — including by looping forever. L(M) = {w ∈ Σ* : M accepts w}.\n\n**Nondeterministic PDA:** δ : Q × (Σ ∪ {□}) × Γ → P_f(Q × {N, R} × Γ*) (finite sets of options). Accepts iff **some** computation accepts; rejects iff **every** computation rejects.\n\n**Key contrast with DFA:** a PDA = finite control + unbounded LIFO stack. The stack gives unbounded memory (counting, matching), but access is last-in-first-out only. Unlike finite automata, **nondeterministic PDAs are strictly more powerful than deterministic PDAs** (Remark 3.6.1 — e.g. \"b in the middle\" needs a nondeterministic guess).\n\n---\n\n## 6. PDA examples (§3.6)\n\n**(a) Properly nested parentheses (deterministic, one state q).** Σ = {a, b} (a=\"(\", b=\")\"), Γ = {$, S}. Push S per a, pop per b; accept iff the count never goes negative and ends at zero. Instructions:\n- qa$ → qR$S, qaS → qRSS (push S for each a)\n- qbS → qRϵ (pop for each b)\n- qb$ → qNϵ (too many b's: empty the stack early ⇒ terminate mid-input ⇒ reject)\n- q□$ → qNϵ (input done, balance zero ⇒ empty stack ⇒ **accept**)\n- q□S → qNS (input done but a's outnumber b's ⇒ loop forever ⇒ reject)\n\n**(b) {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0} (deterministic, states q₀, q₁).** In q₀ push S per 0; on first 1 switch to q₁ and start popping; any 0 after a 1, or a count mismatch, leads to a \"loop forever\" instruction (reject). Key instructions: q₀0$ → q₀R$S; q₀0S → q₀RSS; q₀1S → q₁Rϵ; q₁1S → q₁Rϵ; q₀□$ → q₀Nϵ (ϵ accepted); q₁□$ → q₁Nϵ (accept); everything else loops (e.g. q₁0S → q₁NS, q₁1$ → q₁N$, q₁□S → q₁NS).\n\n**(c) Odd-length strings with b in the middle (NONdeterministic, states q, q′).** L = {vbw : |v| = |w|}. In q, push S per symbol read; on reading a b, **guess** it is the middle and switch to q′ (both options exist: qb$ → q′R$ and qb$ → qR$S; likewise qbS → q′RS / qbS → qRSS). In q′, pop one S per symbol; accept via q′□$ → q′Nϵ. Stack height tracks (symbols read left of middle) − (symbols read right of middle). No deterministic PDA accepts this language.\n\n**(d) {ww^R} (sketch, §3.8.2):** state q pushes the first half, nondeterministically switch to q′, which matches each symbol against the stack top and pops. Again inherently nondeterministic.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Equivalence of PDAs and CFGs (§3.7)\n\n**Theorem 3.7.1.** A language A ⊆ Σ* is context-free **if and only if** there exists a nondeterministic pushdown automaton that accepts A.\n\nThe book proves CFG ⟹ PDA only.\n\n**Construction (CFG in CNF ⟶ one-state NPDA).** Take G in CNF with start variable $ (renamed so the start variable doubles as the stack-bottom symbol). Build M = (Σ, V, {q}, δ, q): tape alphabet = terminals, **stack alphabet = variables**, one state q. Instructions:\n- rule A → BC ⟹ instructions **qaA → qNCB** for every a ∈ Σ (replace top A by CB, so B is on top; head does not move),\n- rule A → a ⟹ instruction **qaA → qRϵ** (top variable A \"produces\" the terminal being read: pop and move right),\n- rule $ → ϵ (if present) ⟹ instruction **q□$ → qNϵ**.\n\n**Invariant (Claim 3.7.2, proof idea).** The PDA simulates a **leftmost derivation**: the sentential form a₁…a_{i−1}A_kA_{k−1}…A₁ corresponds exactly to the configuration \"head on cell i, stack (top to bottom) A_k, …, A₁\". Terminals already produced are consumed input; the pending variables sit on the stack, leftmost variable on top. Derivation completes (i = n+1, k = 0) iff the stack empties just after the input — i.e., $ ⇒* w iff M accepts w. Induction on derivation steps.\n\n**Theorem 3.7.3 (nice corollary).** Every context-free language is accepted by a nondeterministic PDA with **only one state**.\n\n---\n\n## 8. The pumping lemma for context-free languages (§3.8)\n\n**Theorem 3.8.1 (Pumping Lemma for Context-Free Languages — EXACT statement).**\nLet L be a context-free language. Then there exists an integer p ≥ 1, called the pumping length, such that the following holds: Every string s in L, with |s| ≥ p, can be written as s = uvxyz, such that\n\n1. |vy| ≥ 1 (i.e., v and y are not both empty),\n2. |vxy| ≤ p, and\n3. u vⁱ x yⁱ z ∈ L, for all i ≥ 0.\n\n**vs. the regular pumping lemma:** regular splits s = xyz and pumps **one** window y (with |xy| ≤ p); the CFL version splits s = uvxyz and pumps **two** windows v and y **in lockstep** (same exponent i), with the constraint |vxy| ≤ p on the combined window. Every regular language also satisfies the CFL lemma (take v or y empty) — consistent with regular ⊆ context-free.\n\n**Proof idea.** Take G in CNF with r variables; set p = 2^r. If |s| ≥ p, then by Lemma 3.8.2 (CNF parse tree of height ℓ yields |s| ≤ 2^{ℓ−1}) the parse tree has height ℓ ≥ r + 1. Among the last r + 1 variables A_{ℓ−1−r}, …, A_{ℓ−1} on a longest root-to-leaf path, the **pigeonhole principle** gives a repeat A_j = A_k. The two occurrences cut s into uvxyz with S ⇒* uA_jz, A_j ⇒* vA_jy, A_j ⇒* x. Then S ⇒* u vⁱ A_j yⁱ z ⇒* u vⁱ x yⁱ z (property 3); the subtree at A_j has height ℓ − j ≤ r + 1 so |vxy| ≤ 2^{ℓ−j−1} ≤ 2^r = p (property 2); the first rule A_j → BC in A_j ⇒* vA_ky plus \"CNF rules never shorten strings\" gives |vA_ky| ≥ 2, hence |vy| ≥ 1 (property 1).\n\n**Non-CFL proof template:** assume L is context-free with pumping length p; pick a specific s ∈ L, |s| ≥ p (choice matters!); for **every** possible location of vxy (case analysis — you don't get to choose the split), find some i with u vⁱ x yⁱ z ∉ L; contradiction.\n\n### Worked non-context-free examples (§3.8.2)\n\n**Example 1: A = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 0} is not context-free.** Take s = aᵖbᵖcᵖ. Since |vxy| ≤ p, vxy cannot touch both a's and c's. Case 1 (vxy has no c): pumping up (i = 2) increases a's or b's but leaves exactly p c's ⇒ out of A. Case 2 (vxy has no a): symmetric — a's stay at p while b's or c's grow. Case 3 (vxy contains an a and a c) is impossible since it would force |vxy| > p. Contradiction in all cases. **Moral:** the two pumping windows can only synchronize TWO blocks, never three.\n\n**Example 2: {ww^R} is CF but B = {ww : w ∈ {a,b}*} is NOT.**\n- ww^R: grammar S → ϵ | aSa | bSb (or the guess-the-middle NPDA). Matching works outside-in.\n- ww: **string choice lesson** — s = aᵖbaᵖb FAILS (the split u = a^{p−1}, v = a, x = b, y = a, z = a^{p−1}b pumps within B, no contradiction). Correct choice: **s = aᵖbᵖaᵖbᵖ**. Case 1 (vxy straddles the midpoint): vxy lies inside the middle bᵖaᵖ block; pumping down to uxz either shorts the first b-block or inflates the first a-block relative to the second half ⇒ uxz ∉ B. Case 2 (vxy in left half): pumping (down or up) shifts the midpoint so the two halves disagree — none of uxz, uv²xy²z, … lie in B. Case 3 (right half): symmetric. Not context-free.\n\n**Example 3: A = {aᵐbⁿc^{mn} : m, n ≥ 0} (multiplication) is not context-free.** (CFGs can verify addition — {aⁿbᵐcⁿ⁺ᵐ} §3.2.4 — but not multiplication.) Take s = aᵖbᵖc^{p²}. Case 1 (v, y contain only c's): pumping up changes the c-count away from p² with a, b counts fixed at p. Case 2 (v, y contain no c): pumping up makes (#a)(#b) ≥ p(p+1) > p² = #c. Case 3 (v has a b, y has a c; no a's possible since |vxy| ≤ p): write vy = bʲcᵏ, j, k ≥ 1; pumping down, membership forces p(p − j) = p² − k, i.e. k = jp, so |vxy| ≥ j + jp ≥ 1 + p > p — contradicts |vxy| ≤ p. Not context-free.\n\n### Closure facts lurking in the exercises (know for exams)\n- CFLs are closed under **union, concatenation, star** (Ex 3.4: combine grammars with S → S₁|S₂, S → S₁S₂, S → ϵ|SS₁).\n- CFLs are **NOT closed under intersection**: {aᵐbⁿcⁿ} ∩ {aᵐbᵐcⁿ} = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} (Ex 3.5); by De Morgan, **not closed under complement** either.\n- CFL ∩ regular IS context-free; CFL \\ regular is context-free (Ex 3.6).\n\n---\n\n## Key exercises\n\n1. **(Ex 3.1)** Write CFGs over Σ = {0,1} for: {0^{2n}1ⁿ : n ≥ 0}; strings with at least three 1s; odd-length strings with middle symbol 0; palindromes; strings starting and ending with the same symbol; with different symbols. *Hint:* for 0^{2n}1ⁿ use S → ϵ | 00S1; for the middle-0 language grow symmetrically, S → 0 | 0S0 | 0S1 | 1S0 | 1S1; palindromes: S → ϵ | 0 | 1 | 0S0 | 1S1.\n2. **(Ex 3.2)** Prove that S → 0S|1A|ϵ, A → 0B|1S, B → 0A|1B generates exactly binary representations of multiples of 3 (plus ϵ). *Hint:* invariant — S, A, B remember remainder 0, 1, 2 of the number read so far, mod 3; reading bit b sends remainder r to 2r + b mod 3. Mirrors the regular⟹CF construction.\n3. **(Ex 3.3)** For S → aB|bA, A → a|aS|BAA, B → b|bS|ABB: show ababba ∈ L(G), then prove L(G) = nonempty strings with #a = #b. *Hint:* strengthen the induction: S derives balanced strings, A derives strings with one extra a, B one extra b; induct on string length, splitting on the first symbol.\n4. **(Ex 3.4)** Prove CFLs are closed under union, concatenation, star. *Hint:* rename so the two grammars share no variables; add S → S₁|S₂ (union), S → S₁S₂ (concatenation), S → ϵ|SS₁ (star).\n5. **(Ex 3.5)** Give grammars for A = {aᵐbⁿcⁿ} and B = {aᵐbᵐcⁿ}; conclude CFLs are not closed under intersection or complement. *Hint:* A: S → XY, X → ϵ|aX, Y → ϵ|bYc. A ∩ B = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ}, known non-CF; then complement via De Morgan: A ∩ B = complement(complement A ∪ complement B).\n6. **(Ex 3.8)** Build PDAs for {0^{2n}1ⁿ}, {0ⁿ1ᵐ0ⁿ : n,m ≥ 1}, {w : more 1s than 0s}, {ww^R}, palindromes. *Hint:* for 0^{2n}1ⁿ push one S per PAIR of 0s (two states alternate) or pop two per 1; for ww^R and palindromes, nondeterministically guess the middle like §3.6.3; for \"more 1s than 0s\" let the stack hold the surplus of whichever symbol currently leads, with the state or a distinct stack symbol recording which one leads.\n7. **(Ex 3.9)** L = {aᵐbⁿ : 0 ≤ m ≤ n ≤ 2m}: give both a CFG and an NPDA. *Hint:* each a is later matched by one OR two b's: S → ϵ | aSb | aSbb. PDA: on each a nondeterministically push one or two counters; pop one per b.\n8. **(Ex 3.10, selected)** Prove not context-free: {aⁿ b a^{2n} b a^{3n}}, {aⁿbⁿaⁿbⁿ}, {aᵐbⁿcᵏ : k = max(m,n)}, {w#x : w a substring of x}, {1ⁿ : n prime}. *Hint:* pump s built from p-sized blocks (e.g. aᵖbᵖaᵖbᵖ for the second; aᵖ b a^{2p} b a^{3p} for the first — |vxy| ≤ p means at most two of the three a-blocks are touched, and even then ratios 1:2:3 break). For primes: pump i so the length factors (adapt the regular-language argument, pumping both v and y together).\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Give the 4-tuple definition of a context-free grammar, with all conditions.","a":"G = (V, Σ, R, S): V a finite set of variables; Σ a finite set of terminals with V ∩ Σ = ∅; S ∈ V the start variable; R a finite set of rules, each of the form A → w with A ∈ V and w ∈ (V ∪ Σ)*."},{"q":"Define uAv ⇒ uwv and u ⇒* v.","a":"uAv ⇒ uwv (one step) iff A → w is a rule and u, v ∈ (V ∪ Σ)*; u ⇒* v iff u = v or there is a finite chain u = u₁ ⇒ u₂ ⇒ … ⇒ u_k = v (rules applied zero or more times)."},{"q":"Define L(G) and \"context-free language\".","a":"L(G) = {w ∈ Σ* : S ⇒* w} — terminal strings derivable from the start variable. L is context-free iff L = L(G) for some CFG G."},{"q":"Grammar for {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0}? Why is it notable?","a":"S → ϵ | 0S1. It is context-free but not regular, so regular languages are a proper subclass of context-free languages."},{"q":"Grammar for properly nested parentheses (a = \"(\", b = \")\")?","a":"S → ϵ | aSb | SS — empty, or a balanced string wrapped in parentheses, or two balanced strings concatenated."},{"q":"Grammar for the addition language {aⁿbᵐcⁿ⁺ᵐ : n, m ≥ 0}?","a":"Start A: A → aAc | B, B → ϵ | bBc. Each a pairs with an outer c; B marks the insertion point between a's and c's and pairs each b with a c."},{"q":"State Theorem 3.3.1 and its construction (DFA → CFG).","a":"Every regular language is context-free. From DFA M = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F): variables = states, start variable = start state, rule A → aB for each transition δ(A, a) = B, and rule A → ϵ for each accept state A."},{"q":"What invariant drives the DFA → CFG proof?","a":"M is in state A after reading w₁…w_i ⟺ S ⇒* w₁…w_i A; the derivation can end (drop the variable via A → ϵ) exactly when A is accepting."},{"q":"Define Chomsky normal form (three rule forms).","a":"Every rule is A → BC (B, C variables, neither the start variable), A → a (a a terminal), or S → ϵ (start variable only). S → ϵ is in R iff ϵ ∈ L(G)."},{"q":"List the five CNF-conversion steps in order.","a":"(1) add new start variable S₁ with S₁ → S; (2) eliminate ϵ-rules A → ϵ (A ≠ start), patching every rule containing A; (3) eliminate unit-rules A → B, copying B's rules to A; (4) split right-hand sides of length ≥ 3 into a chain of length-2 rules with new variables; (5) replace terminals in length-2 right-hand sides by new variables U → a. Language preserved at every step."},{"q":"In CNF conversion, why does ϵ-elimination come before unit-rule elimination, and what prevents infinite loops?","a":"Removing A → ϵ can create new unit-rules (e.g. B → A stays, B → uAv may shrink to a unit); never re-add a rule already eliminated — that bookkeeping guarantees termination."},{"q":"Give the 5-tuple definition of a (deterministic) pushdown automaton in this book.","a":"M = (Σ, Γ, Q, δ, q): tape alphabet Σ (blank □ ∉ Σ); stack alphabet Γ containing $; finite state set Q; start state q ∈ Q; transition function δ : Q × (Σ ∪ {□}) × Γ → Q × {N, R} × Γ*."},{"q":"What does the instruction raA → r′σw mean, and how does the stack change?","a":"In state r, reading a, with A on top: go to state r′; move the head right (σ = R) or stay (σ = N); replace top symbol A by w ∈ Γ* — if w = ϵ, A is popped; if w = B₁…B_k, then B_k becomes the new top."},{"q":"When does this book's PDA terminate, and when does it accept?","a":"It terminates exactly when the stack becomes empty. It accepts iff it terminates AND at that moment the head is on the blank cell just past the input. It rejects otherwise — including by looping forever."},{"q":"PDA vs DFA — what is the essential difference, and are deterministic and nondeterministic PDAs equivalent?","a":"A PDA is a finite-state machine plus an unbounded LIFO stack, so it can count/match unboundedly (e.g. 0ⁿ1ⁿ). Unlike finite automata, NPDAs are strictly more powerful than deterministic PDAs (e.g. \"odd length with b in the middle\" needs a nondeterministic guess of the middle)."},{"q":"State the PDA–CFG equivalence theorem and sketch the CFG → PDA construction.","a":"A language is context-free iff some NPDA accepts it (Thm 3.7.1). Given G in CNF with start variable $: one state q, stack alphabet = variables; A → BC gives qaA → qNCB (all a ∈ Σ); A → a gives qaA → qRϵ; $ → ϵ gives q□$ → qNϵ. The stack holds the pending variables of a leftmost derivation (leftmost on top); every CFL is even accepted by a one-state NPDA (Thm 3.7.3)."},{"q":"State the pumping lemma for context-free languages exactly.","a":"If L is context-free, there is a pumping length p ≥ 1 such that every s ∈ L with |s| ≥ p can be written s = uvxyz with (1) |vy| ≥ 1, (2) |vxy| ≤ p, and (3) u vⁱ x yⁱ z ∈ L for all i ≥ 0."},{"q":"How does the CFL pumping lemma differ from the regular one?","a":"Regular: s = xyz, one pumped window y, |xy| ≤ p. CFL: s = uvxyz, TWO windows v and y pumped in lockstep (same exponent), with |vxy| ≤ p bounding the combined window; two synchronized windows can match two blocks but never three."},{"q":"Sketch why {aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 0} is not context-free.","a":"Pump s = aᵖbᵖcᵖ. Since |vxy| ≤ p, vxy misses the c's or misses the a's (touching both a's and c's would force |vxy| > p); pumping up then changes at most two of the three symbol counts, breaking equality. Contradiction in every case."},{"q":"{ww^R} vs {ww}: which is context-free, and what string works for pumping {ww}?","a":"{ww^R} is context-free (S → ϵ | aSa | bSb; outside-in matching). {ww} is not: pump s = aᵖbᵖaᵖbᵖ (the naive aᵖbaᵖb fails!) — wherever vxy sits, pumping misaligns the two halves."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"b4","title":"Book 4 · Turing Machines","slides":"M&S ch. 4 · weeks 10–11","md":"# B4 — Turing Machines and the Church-Turing Thesis\n\n**Aligned with course weeks 10–11 (estimate)** — post-midterm block.\n\n*Source: Maheshwari & Smid, Chapter 4 (PDF pp. 145–164; book pp. 137–156).*\n\nWhy this chapter exists: DFAs/NFAs and pushdown automata are not powerful enough for languages like A = {a^m b^n c^{mn} : m ≥ 0, n ≥ 0} (shown non-context-free in Sec. 3.8.2). The Turing machine is a simple model of a real computer that handles all context-free languages **and** languages like A. The claim that every problem solvable on a real computer is solvable by a Turing machine is the Church-Turing Thesis. (Chapter 5 then studies the *limits* of TMs.)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Informal picture (k-tape machine)\n\n1. There are **k tapes** (fixed k ≥ 1), each divided into cells and **infinite both to the left and to the right**. Each cell stores a symbol of a finite **tape alphabet Γ**, which contains the **blank symbol ␣** (the book writes it as 2/□). A cell containing ␣ is empty.\n2. Each tape has a **tape head** that moves one cell per move, can read the scanned cell, and can replace its symbol.\n3. A **state control** is in one of finitely many states Q, including three special states: a **start state**, an **accept state**, and a **reject state**.\n\nOne computation step, given current state r and the k scanned symbols: (a) switch to a state r′ ∈ Q (possibly = r), (b) each head writes a symbol of Γ into its current cell (possibly the same symbol), (c) each head moves one cell left, one cell right, or stays put.\n\n## 2. Formal definition (Definition 4.1.1) — MEMORIZE VERBATIM\n\n> A **deterministic Turing machine** is a 7-tuple\n>\n> **M = (Σ, Γ, Q, δ, q, q_accept, q_reject)**, where\n>\n> 1. **Σ** is a finite set, called the **input alphabet**; the blank symbol ␣ is **not** contained in Σ,\n> 2. **Γ** is a finite set, called the **tape alphabet**; this alphabet contains the blank symbol ␣, and **Σ ⊆ Γ**,\n> 3. **Q** is a finite set, whose elements are called **states**,\n> 4. **q** is an element of Q; it is called the **start state**,\n> 5. **q_accept** is an element of Q; it is called the **accept state**,\n> 6. **q_reject** is an element of Q; it is called the **reject state**,\n> 7. **δ** is the **transition function**:\n>    **δ : Q × Γ^k → Q × Γ^k × {L, R, N}^k.**\n\nδ is the \"program\" of the machine. A transition\n\nδ(r, a₁, …, a_k) = (r′, a′₁, …, a′_k, σ₁, …, σ_k)\n\nmeans: if the machine is in state r and head i reads aᵢ (1 ≤ i ≤ k), then it switches to state r′, head i replaces aᵢ by a′ᵢ, and head i moves according to σᵢ (L = one cell left, R = one cell right, N = no move). The book writes such a step as the **instruction**\n\n`r a₁a₂…a_k → r′ a′₁a′₂…a′_k σ₁σ₂…σ_k`.\n\n**Exam traps:** ␣ ∈ Γ but ␣ ∉ Σ; Σ ⊆ Γ; the head option N (\"stay\") exists in this book's definition; the tape is two-way infinite.\n\n## 3. Configurations, computation, acceptance\n\n**Start configuration:** the input w ∈ Σ\\* is on the **first** tape, with that tape's head on the **leftmost symbol** of w; all other k−1 tapes are entirely blank; the machine is in start state q.\n\nA **configuration** = (current state, contents of all tapes, positions of all heads). Each δ-step transforms one configuration into the next; the computation is the resulting sequence of configurations from the start configuration.\n\n**Termination:** the computation terminates **the moment the machine enters q_accept or q_reject**. If it never enters either, the computation does not terminate (it \"loops\").\n\n**Acceptance:** M **accepts** w if the computation on w terminates in q_accept; M **rejects** w if it terminates in q_reject. **L(M)** = the language accepted by M = the set of all strings in Σ\\* accepted by M.\n\n**Key observation (book, verbatim in content):** w ∉ L(M) if and only if, on input w, either\n- the computation of M terminates in q_reject, **or**\n- the computation of M does not terminate.\n\n**Recognizer vs. decider (the accept/loop distinction):**\n- M *recognizes* (accepts) L if L(M) = L — strings not in L may be rejected **or** may cause M to run forever.\n- M *decides* L if L(M) = L **and** M halts (in q_accept or q_reject) on **every** input. A decider never loops.\n- Every decider is a recognizer; the converse fails (Chapter 5). \"Three possible outcomes per input: accept, reject, loop\" — deciders only have the first two.\n\n## 4. Worked examples — strategies as step recipes\n\n### 4.1 Palindromes over {a,b}, one tape (Sec. 4.2.1)\nLanguage: {w ∈ {a,b}\\* : w is a palindrome}. Σ = {a,b}, Γ = {a,b,␣}. 8 states: q₀, q_a, q_b, q′_a, q′_b, q_L, q_accept, q_reject.\n\nRecipe (match ends, shrink inward):\n1. In q₀ read the leftmost symbol, **delete it** (write ␣) and **remember it in the state** (q_a if it was a, q_b if b); move right.\n2. Walk right over a's and b's until the first ␣ past the string, then step left onto the rightmost symbol (states q′_a / q′_b).\n3. If the rightmost symbol matches the remembered one: delete it, enter q_L, walk left to the new leftmost symbol, re-enter q₀, repeat. If it does not match: **reject**.\n4. **Accept** as soon as the string on the tape is empty (q₀ reads ␣, or q′_a/q′_b read ␣ — odd-length middle symbol).\n\n### 4.2 Palindromes, two tapes (Sec. 4.2.2)\nSame language; only 5 states (q₀, q₁, q₂, q_accept, q_reject) — the two-tape machine is *easier to design*.\n\nRecipe:\n1. **Copy** w from tape 1 to tape 2 (state q₀, both heads moving right).\n2. Move head 1 back to the leftmost symbol of w; head 2 stays at the rightmost symbol (state q₁).\n3. Move head 1 rightwards and head 2 leftwards **simultaneously**, comparing symbols at each step (state q₂). Any mismatch → reject; both heads reach blanks → accept.\n\n### 4.3 {aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 0}, one tape, extra symbol d (Sec. 4.2.3)\nNot context-free (Sec. 3.8.2), yet TM-acceptable. Σ = {a,b,c}, Γ = {a,b,c,d,␣}.\n\nRecipe, two stages:\n- **Stage 1 (format check):** walk left-to-right checking w ∈ a\\*b\\*c\\* (states q_a, q_b, q_c); reject on out-of-order symbols; then walk back to the leftmost symbol (q_L).\n- **Stage 2 (counting):** repeat — walk left to right, replace the **leftmost a** by d, then the **leftmost b** by d, then the **leftmost c** by d, then walk back left (states q′_a, q′_b, q′_c, q′_L). Accept when, searching for an a, only d's followed by ␣ remain; reject if the counts don't match (e.g., a b is found where an a was expected).\n- **Why Stage 1 is necessary:** without it, the malformed string **aabcbc** would be accepted (Stage 2 alone only counts, it doesn't check order).\n\n### 4.4 {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} with tape alphabet exactly {a,b,c,␣} (Sec. 4.2.4)\nSame language, no extra symbol d. Recipe, repeated until the string is empty:\n- **Stage 1:** walk left to right; delete the leftmost a, delete the leftmost b, and delete the **rightmost** c.\n- **Stage 2:** shift the remaining substring of b's and c's **one position to the left** (to close the gap left by the deleted b), then walk back to the leftmost symbol.\n- Accept when the string becomes empty (with a special state q′₁ handling the boundary case input a^i b c). Point of the example: extra tape symbols are a convenience, not a necessity.\n\n### 4.5 {a^m b^n c^{mn} : m ≥ 0, n ≥ 0}, one tape (Sec. 4.2.5, sketch)\nΣ = {a,b,c}, Γ = {a,b,c,$,␣}. Key insight: a^m b^n c^k is in the language iff **for every a, the string contains n c's** (mn = m·n).\n\nRecipe:\n- **Stage 1:** check w ∈ a\\*b\\*c\\* (reject otherwise). **Stage 2:** walk back to the leftmost symbol.\n- **Stage 3:** delete the leftmost a (write ␣); then **zigzag** between b's and c's: each round replace the leftmost b by $ and the rightmost c by ␣; if some b has no matching c, reject. This transforms a^m b^n c^k into a^{m−1} $^n c^{k−n} — one a \"consumes\" n c's.\n- **Stage 4:** restore every $ back to b; walk to the leftmost a. Now the tape holds a^{m−1} b^n c^{k−n}, which is in the language iff the original was.\n- Repeat Stages 3–4 until no a's remain; then accept iff no c's remain.\n\n## 5. Multi-tape TMs and equivalence (Sec. 4.3)\n\n**Theorem 4.3.1.** *Let k ≥ 1 be an integer. Any k-tape Turing machine can be converted to an equivalent one-tape Turing machine.*\n\n\"Equivalent\" means, for **all** w ∈ Σ\\*: M accepts w ⇔ N accepts w; M rejects w ⇔ N rejects w; and M does not terminate on w ⇔ N does not terminate on w. So multi-tape machines are *more convenient* but **not more powerful**.\n\n**Proof idea (simulation, sketched for k = 2).** Build a one-tape machine N with tape alphabet\n\nΓ ∪ { ẋ : x ∈ Γ } ∪ { # }\n\n— every symbol of Γ, plus a **dotted copy** ẋ of each symbol, plus a separator **#**.\n\nEncoding: N stores the contents of both of M's tapes on its single tape, separated (and bounded) by three #'s, e.g. tape contents 1001 and aaba become `# 1 0 ˙0 1 # a ˙a b a #`. The **dotted symbol** in each segment marks where M's head sits on that tape.\n\nSimulating **one** step of M:\n1. N remembers M's current state (in its own state); its head starts at the leftmost #.\n2. N scans right, finds the **first dotted symbol** (head position on M's tape 1), remembers it, keeps scanning to the **second dotted symbol** (head position on tape 2), and remembers that too. Now N knows both symbols M reads.\n3. Standing at the second dotted symbol, N applies M's transition to the tape-2 segment: writes the new symbol and moves the dot to model the head move.\n4. N walks left back to the first dotted symbol and updates the tape-1 segment the same way.\n5. If a segment must grow (M's head moves past its segment boundary), N **shifts part of the tape** to make room.\n6. N records M's new state and returns to the leftmost # for the next simulated step.\n\nN accepts/rejects exactly when M does, so L(N) = L(M) and halting behavior is preserved.\n\n## 6. The Church-Turing Thesis (Sec. 4.4)\n\n**Theorem 4.4.1 (equivalence of models).** The following computation models are equivalent — any one of them can be converted to any other one:\n1. one-tape Turing machines; 2. k-tape Turing machines, for any k ≥ 1; 3. non-deterministic Turing machines; 4. Java programs; 5. C++ programs; 6. Lisp programs.\n\nWhichever model you pick to define \"algorithm\", you get the same notion.\n\n**Church-Turing Thesis (exact formulation in the book):**\n\n> **Every computational process that is intuitively considered to be an algorithm can be converted to a Turing machine.**\n\nIn other words: we *define* an algorithm to be a Turing machine.\n\n**What it claims / does not claim:**\n- It is a **thesis, not a theorem** — it links an informal, intuitive notion (\"algorithm\") to a formal one (TM), so it **cannot be mathematically proved**. It could in principle be *disproved*: exhibit an intuitively algorithmic process that no Turing machine can carry out. No such process has ever been found; every rigorous definition proposed (Turing machines 1936, λ-calculus 1936, later C/Java/Lisp/Pascal-based notions) turned out equivalent.\n- It says **nothing about efficiency** — only about what is computable at all, not how fast.\n- It does **not** say TMs can solve everything: Chapter 5 shows problems no TM (hence, by the thesis, no computer) can solve.\n\n**Historical anchor (know for context):** Hilbert's 10th problem (1900): is there a finite process deciding whether an arbitrary polynomial with integer coefficients has integral roots? Matiyasevich (1970) proved **no such algorithm exists** — a proof that only makes sense once \"algorithm\" is formally defined, which is exactly what the thesis settles.\n\n---\n\n## Key exercises\n\n1. **(Book Ex. 4.1)** Construct a one-tape TM accepting {0^{2n} 1^n : n ≥ 0}.\n   *Hint:* Each round, cross off **two** 0's and **one** 1 (or cross off one 1 for every two 0's). Reject if symbols run out unevenly or a 0 appears after a 1. Accept on an empty (all-crossed) tape. Model your states on the aⁿbⁿcⁿ machine of Sec. 4.2.3.\n\n2. **(Book Ex. 4.3)** Describe informally a one-tape TM for A = {w ∈ {a,b,c}\\* : w has more b's than a's **and** more c's than a's}.\n   *Hint:* Repeatedly cancel one a against one b **and** one c (mark them, e.g. with dots or a spare symbol). When no a's remain unmarked, accept iff at least one unmarked b and one unmarked c survived… careful: check both conditions independently — two passes (a-vs-b, then a-vs-c on a restored tape) is cleanest.\n\n3. **(Book Ex. 4.4)** Build a one-tape TM computing x ↦ x+1 on binary representations (head starts and ends on the leftmost bit; machine halts in a final state q₁).\n   *Hint:* Walk to the **rightmost** bit, then move left turning 1's into 0's until you hit a 0 (make it 1) or fall off the left end (write a leading 1 — the 111…1 carry case). Then return to the leftmost symbol.\n\n4. **(Book Ex. 4.5)** Build a **two-tape** TM computing x + y in binary (x on tape 1, y on tape 2, heads on rightmost bits; sum replaces y).\n   *Hint:* Standard ripple-carry addition: move both heads leftwards in lockstep, adding bit pairs; keep the carry **in the state** (two \"modes\": carry-0 and carry-1). Handle unequal lengths by treating ␣ as 0.\n\n5. **(Book Ex. 4.8)** Describe a **three-tape** TM computing x·y in binary.\n   *Hint:* Multiplication = repeated addition. Use the decrement machine (Ex. 4.7, x ↦ x−1) on tape 2's counter and the addition machine (Ex. 4.5) to add x into tape 3, y times. Composing previously built TMs is the intended lesson.\n\n6. **(Book Ex. 4.9)** Build a one-tape TM over Γ = {1, ␣} that transforms 1ⁿ into 1ⁿ␣1ⁿ (a copying machine), halting with the head on the middle ␣.\n   *Hint:* You cannot mark symbols (no extra tape letters!), so copy by **deleting** a 1 on the left end and **appending** a 1 at the far right end, repeating; track the shrinking/growing boundary using the blank gap between the two blocks. Count carefully where the middle ␣ ends up.\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Give the 7-tuple defining a deterministic Turing machine.","a":"M = (Σ, Γ, Q, δ, q, q_accept, q_reject): Σ = input alphabet (␣ ∉ Σ); Γ = tape alphabet (␣ ∈ Γ, Σ ⊆ Γ); Q = finite set of states; q ∈ Q start state; q_accept ∈ Q accept state; q_reject ∈ Q reject state; δ the transition function."},{"q":"What is the type (signature) of the transition function of a k-tape deterministic TM, and what do L, R, N mean?","a":"δ : Q × Γ^k → Q × Γ^k × {L, R, N}^k. Per step: new state, one written symbol per tape, and per head a move Left one cell, Right one cell, or No move."},{"q":"Two required relationships between Σ, Γ, and the blank symbol ␣?","a":"␣ ∈ Γ but ␣ ∉ Σ, and Σ ⊆ Γ. (So the input never contains blanks, and any input symbol may appear on the tape.)"},{"q":"Describe the start configuration of a k-tape TM on input w.","a":"w is on the first tape with that head on w's leftmost symbol; the other k−1 tapes are all blank; the machine is in the start state q."},{"q":"When does a TM computation terminate, and what are the three possible behaviors on an input?","a":"It terminates the moment the machine enters q_accept or q_reject. On any input the machine either accepts (halts in q_accept), rejects (halts in q_reject), or loops forever (never enters either)."},{"q":"Per the book, w ∉ L(M) if and only if…?","a":"…on input w, the computation of M terminates in q_reject, **or** the computation of M does not terminate."},{"q":"Decider vs. recognizer — what's the difference?","a":"A recognizer for L accepts exactly the strings of L but may loop on strings outside L; a decider accepts exactly L **and halts on every input** (always accept or reject, never loop). Every decider is a recognizer, not conversely."},{"q":"One-tape palindrome TM (Sec. 4.2.1): what is the strategy?","a":"Read and delete the leftmost symbol, remembering it in the state (q_a/q_b); walk right to the rightmost symbol; if it matches, delete it and walk back left to repeat; if not, reject; accept when the tape is empty."},{"q":"Two-tape palindrome TM (Sec. 4.2.2): strategy, and why is it simpler?","a":"Copy w to tape 2; return head 1 to w's left end (head 2 stays at the right end); then move head 1 right and head 2 left simultaneously, comparing symbols; mismatch → reject, blanks reached → accept. Only 5 states vs. 8 — multi-tape machines are easier to design."},{"q":"Strategy of the one-tape TM for {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} with extra symbol d, and why is the a\\*b\\*c\\* pre-check (Stage 1) necessary?","a":"Stage 1 verifies w ∈ a\\*b\\*c\\*; Stage 2 repeatedly replaces the leftmost a, leftmost b, leftmost c by d and returns left; accept when all symbols are d. Without Stage 1, the out-of-order string aabcbc would be wrongly accepted."},{"q":"How does the Sec. 4.2.5 TM accept {a^m b^n c^{mn}}?","a":"After checking a\\*b\\*c\\* format: delete one a, then zigzag replacing each leftmost b by $ and each rightmost c by ␣ (so one a consumes n c's, giving a^{m−1}$^n c^{k−n}); restore $'s to b's; repeat until no a's remain; accept iff no c's remain (reject earlier if c's run out)."},{"q":"State Theorem 4.3.1.","a":"For every integer k ≥ 1, any k-tape Turing machine can be converted to an equivalent one-tape Turing machine — equivalent meaning same accept/reject/non-termination behavior on every input. Multi-tape adds convenience, not power."},{"q":"In the k=2 → 1 tape simulation, what tape alphabet does the one-tape machine N use and why?","a":"Γ ∪ {ẋ : x ∈ Γ} ∪ {#}: both tape contents are stored on one tape in segments separated by #'s, and the dotted symbol in each segment marks where that tape's head is."},{"q":"Outline how N simulates one step of the two-tape machine M.","a":"From the leftmost #, N scans right to find both dotted symbols (learning what M reads on each tape); it applies M's transition at the second dot, walks back and applies it at the first dot (moving each dot to model head moves, shifting tape content if a segment must grow); it remembers M's new state and returns to the leftmost #."},{"q":"State the Church-Turing Thesis as formulated in the book.","a":"\"Every computational process that is intuitively considered to be an algorithm can be converted to a Turing machine.\" I.e., we define an algorithm to be a Turing machine."},{"q":"Why can't the Church-Turing Thesis be proved, and how could it in principle be disproved?","a":"It equates an informal notion (intuitive \"algorithm\") with a formal one (TM), so no mathematical proof is possible; it would be disproved by exhibiting an intuitively algorithmic process that no TM can perform — none has ever been found."},{"q":"Which computation models does Theorem 4.4.1 declare equivalent?","a":"One-tape TMs; k-tape TMs (any k ≥ 1); non-deterministic TMs; Java programs; C++ programs; Lisp programs — any one convertible to any other."},{"q":"What is Hilbert's 10th problem and what did Matiyasevich prove (1970)?","a":"Whether a finite process (algorithm) exists that decides if a given polynomial with integer coefficients has integral roots; Matiyasevich proved no such algorithm exists — a result that presupposes the formal TM definition of \"algorithm\"."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"b5","title":"Book 5 · Decidability","slides":"M&S ch. 5 · weeks 12–13","md":"# B5 — Decidable and Undecidable Languages (Maheshwari–Smid, Ch. 5)\n\n**Aligned with course weeks 12–13 (estimate)** — In-Class Quiz 2 (week 12) territory.\n\nBig picture: Chapter 4 showed TMs capture \"everything intuitively computable\". Chapter 5 shows the limits: not everything is computable — in fact \"most\" languages are not even enumerable.\n\nEncoding convention: pairs (C, w) — a computation model C plus an input string w — are encoded as a binary string, written ⟨C, w⟩.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Decidability\n\n**Definition 5.1.1 (decidable).** Let Σ be an alphabet, A ⊆ Σ*. A is **decidable** if there exists a Turing machine M such that for every string w ∈ Σ*:\n1. if w ∈ A, then M's computation on input w **terminates in the accept state**;\n2. if w ∉ A, then M's computation on input w **terminates in the reject state**.\n\nEquivalently: an algorithm that (i) terminates on *every* input, and (ii) correctly answers \"w ∈ A?\". A language that is not decidable is **undecidable**.\n\n### Decidable examples (the ⟨machine, string⟩ family)\n\n**Theorem 5.1.2.** A_DFA = {⟨M, w⟩ : M is a DFA that accepts w} is decidable.\n*Proof sketch:* On input u, check u has the format ⟨M, w⟩ (reject if not); construct M and w; **simulate** M on w. A DFA always finishes after |w| steps, so the simulation terminates; accept iff M accepts.\n\n**Theorem 5.1.3.** A_NFA = {⟨M, w⟩ : M is an NFA that accepts w} is decidable.\n*Proof sketch:* An NFA's computation on w is not unique, so first **convert** the NFA to an equivalent DFA using the subset construction (Section 2.5 — this construction is algorithmic), then proceed as for A_DFA.\n\n**Theorem 5.1.4.** A_CFG = {⟨G, w⟩ : G is a context-free grammar with w ∈ L(G)} is decidable.\n*Proof sketch:* Naively trying \"all derivations\" fails: with ε-rules or shortening steps, there is no obvious bound on how long an intermediate string can get. Trick: convert G to **Chomsky normal form** G′ (algorithmic, Section 3.4). If |w| = n and w ∈ L(G′), then *every* derivation of w in G′ takes **exactly 2n − 1 steps**. So try the finitely many derivations of 2n − 1 steps; accept iff one yields w.\n\n**Theorem 5.1.5.** Every context-free language is decidable. *(Take a CNF grammar for A; decide membership as above.)*\n\n### Theorem 5.1.6: A_TM is undecidable\n\nA_TM = {⟨M, w⟩ : M is a Turing machine that accepts the string w}.\n\nMeaning: no algorithm can decide, in finite time, whether an arbitrary algorithm M accepts an arbitrary input w.\n\n*Proof (self-reference / diagonalization):*\n1. Suppose H decides A_TM: H always terminates; accepts ⟨M, w⟩ iff M accepts w, rejects otherwise (M rejects w or loops).\n2. Build TM **D**: on input ⟨M⟩, run H on ⟨M, ⟨M⟩⟩ and **do the opposite**: if H accepts → D rejects; if H rejects → D accepts. D terminates on every input since H does.\n3. So: M accepts ⟨M⟩ ⟹ D rejects ⟨M⟩; M rejects/loops on ⟨M⟩ ⟹ D accepts ⟨M⟩.\n4. Feed D its own description (M := D): D accepts ⟨D⟩ ⟺ D rejects ⟨D⟩. Contradiction — H cannot exist. ∎\n\n### Theorem 5.1.7: the Halting problem is undecidable\n\nHalt = {⟨P, w⟩ : P is a Java program that terminates on the input string w}. (The book states it with Java programs; ⟨M, w⟩-with-TMs version is used later interchangeably.)\n\n*Proof:*\n1. Suppose Java program H decides Halt: H(P, w) = true if P(w) terminates, false otherwise; H always terminates.\n2. Build program **Q**(⟨P⟩): `while H(P, ⟨P⟩) = true do (nothing useful) endwhile`. So Q(⟨P⟩) terminates ⟺ H(P, ⟨P⟩) = false ⟺ P(⟨P⟩) does **not** terminate.\n3. Run Q on its own code: Q(⟨Q⟩) terminates ⟺ Q(⟨Q⟩) does not terminate. Contradiction. ∎\n\n---\n\n## 2. Countable and uncountable sets\n\n**Bijection:** f : A → B is a bijection if it is one-to-one (injective: a ≠ a′ ⟹ f(a) ≠ f(a′)) and onto (surjective: every b ∈ B is f(a) for some a). N = {1, 2, 3, …}.\n\n**Definition 5.2.1 (same size).** Sets A and B have the **same size** if there exists a bijection f : A → B.\n\n**Definition 5.2.2 (countable).** A is **countable** if A is finite, or A and N have the same size. An infinite countable A can be listed A = {f(1), f(2), f(3), …} with every element appearing exactly once — i.e., its elements can be numbered by positive integers.\n\n**Theorem 5.2.3.** Z, N × N, and Q are countable. (For Z: list 0, 1, −1, 2, −2, 3, −3, …; formally f(n) = n/2 if n even, −(n−1)/2 if n odd is a bijection N → Z.)\n\n### Theorem 5.2.4 (Cantor): R is not countable\n\n*Proof (THE diagonalization argument — memorize this shape):*\n1. Let A = {x ∈ R : 0 ≤ x < 1}; showing A uncountable suffices (A ⊆ R).\n2. Suppose a bijection f : N → A exists; write each f(n) in decimal: f(n) = 0.d_{n1} d_{n2} d_{n3}…\n3. Define x = 0.d₁d₂d₃… by the **diagonal-flip rule**: dₙ = 4 if d_{nn} ≠ 4, and dₙ = 5 if d_{nn} = 4.\n4. Then x ∈ A, so x = f(n) for some n. Compare n-th digits: f(n)'s is d_{nn}, x's is dₙ, and equality forces d_{nn} = dₙ — but the definition guarantees d_{nn} ≠ dₙ. Contradiction. ∎\nKey idea: x is built to differ from f(n) in the n-th digit, so x escapes the entire list.\n\n### Theorem 5.2.5: A and P(A) never have the same size\n\nP(A) = {B : B ⊆ A} is the power set. (It's \"at least as large\": a ↦ {a} is one-to-one.)\n\n*Proof sketch:* Suppose bijection g : A → P(A) exists. Define B = {a ∈ A : a ∉ g(a)}. Since B ∈ P(A), B = g(a) for some a. If a ∈ B, then a ∈ g(a), so by B's definition a ∉ B — contradiction. If a ∉ B, then a ∉ g(a), so a ∈ B — contradiction. So g doesn't exist. ∎ (Same diagonal move, set-flavored.)\n\n### Halting problem revisited (Section 5.2.1) — same proof, diagonal dress\n\nThe set of all Java programs is **countable**: each ⟨P⟩ is a finite binary string, at most 2ⁿ programs have descriptions of length n, so list by length 0, 1, 2, … — every program occurs exactly once. Fix such a list P₁, P₂, P₃, …\n\nSuppose H decides Halt. Build D: on input ⟨Pₙ⟩, run H on ⟨Pₙ, ⟨Pₙ⟩⟩; if true → loop forever; if false → terminate (return true). Then D terminates on ⟨Pₙ⟩ ⟺ Pₙ does not terminate on ⟨Pₙ⟩ — D's behavior differs from Pₙ's on the diagonal input ⟨Pₙ⟩, for every n. But D is a Java program, so D = Pₙ for some n, giving: D terminates on ⟨D⟩ ⟺ D does not terminate on ⟨D⟩. Contradiction. (Remark 5.2.6: D was built to differ from every row of the list, yet must be in the list — exactly Cantor's x.)\n\n### The diagonalization recipe (exam template)\n\n1. Assume the objects in question form a countable list O₁, O₂, O₃, … (or assume a decider H exists).\n2. Build a \"diagonal\" object D whose behavior/value on the n-th test point is **flipped** relative to Oₙ's (via H's answer if H is assumed).\n3. Argue D itself is an object of the listed kind, so D = Oₙ for some n.\n4. Evaluate D at its own index/description: get \"X ⟺ not X\". Contradiction; the list/decider cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Rice's Theorem\n\nLet T = {⟨M⟩ : M is a Turing machine with input alphabet {0,1}}.\n\n**Theorem 5.3.1 (Rice) — EXACT statement.** Let P be a subset of T such that\n1. P ≠ ∅, i.e., there exists a Turing machine M such that ⟨M⟩ ∈ P;\n2. P is a **proper** subset of T, i.e., there exists a Turing machine N such that ⟨N⟩ ∉ P; and\n3. for any two Turing machines M₁ and M₂ with L(M₁) = L(M₂): either both ⟨M₁⟩ and ⟨M₂⟩ are in P, or neither is.\n\nThen the language P is **undecidable**.\n\n**What counts as a non-trivial property:** think of P as \"all TMs satisfying some property\". Conditions 1–2 say the property is **non-trivial** (some TM has it, some TM doesn't). Condition 3 says it is a **property of the language only**: whether M satisfies it depends solely on L(M), not on M's states/structure/running time.\n\n**Example applications (all undecidable by Rice):**\n- P₁ = {⟨M⟩ : ε ∈ L(M)} — non-empty (a TM accepting everything), proper (a TM accepting nothing), depends only on L(M). ✓\n- P₂ = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) = {1011, 001100}} ✓\n- P₃ = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) is a regular language} ✓\n- Exercise favorites: Empty = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) = ∅}; REG_TM.\n\n**Rice application pattern (exam):** (a) exhibit one TM in P; (b) exhibit one TM not in P; (c) argue membership depends only on L(M). Conclude undecidable. **Warning:** Rice does NOT apply to machine-behavior properties (\"M has 5 states\", \"M visits state q\", \"M halts within 100 steps\") — condition 3 fails; those need a direct reduction.\n\n### Proof of Rice's Theorem (reduction from Halt)\n\nStrategy: if P were decidable, Halt would be decidable — contradicting Theorem 5.1.7.\n\n1. Let M₁ be a TM that immediately rejects everything, so L(M₁) = ∅. **Assume ⟨M₁⟩ ∉ P** (other case at the end). By condition 1 choose M₂ with ⟨M₂⟩ ∈ P.\n2. For fixed (M, w), build TM **T_{Mw}(x)**: first run M on w (ignoring x); if that terminates, run M₂ on x and copy its accept/reject answer.\n3. Then: ⟨M, w⟩ ∈ Halt ⟹ T_{Mw} behaves like M₂ on every x ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = L(M₂) ⟹ ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ ∈ P (condition 3). And ⟨M, w⟩ ∉ Halt ⟹ T_{Mw} never gets past step 1 ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = ∅ = L(M₁) ⟹ ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ ∉ P.\n4. If H decides P, then H′(⟨M, w⟩) := \"construct ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ (just text manipulation — never run it!), run H on it, copy the answer\" decides Halt. Contradiction.\n5. If instead ⟨M₁⟩ ∈ P, run the same proof on the complement P̄ (which now misses ⟨M₁⟩), showing P̄ undecidable; since L decidable ⟺ L̄ decidable, P is undecidable too. ∎\n\n---\n\n## 4. Enumerability\n\n**Definition 5.4.1 (enumerable).** Let Σ be an alphabet, A ⊆ Σ*. A is **enumerable** if there exists a Turing machine M such that for every w ∈ Σ*:\n1. if w ∈ A, then M's computation on w **terminates in the accept state**;\n2. if w ∉ A, then M's computation on w **does not terminate in the accept state** — it either terminates in the reject state **or does not terminate**.\n\nSo membership gets a guaranteed finite-time \"yes\"; non-membership may never get an answer. (Other books: \"recursively enumerable\" / \"recognizable\".)\n\n**Theorem 5.4.2.** Every decidable language is enumerable. *(Immediate from the definitions: a decider satisfies the enumerability conditions.)*\n\n**Theorem 5.4.3.** Hilbert = {⟨p⟩ : p a polynomial with integer coefficients having an integral root} is enumerable (it is NOT decidable — Matiyasevich 1970).\n*Proof sketch:* Search Zⁿ (n = #variables) exhaustively in a systematic order: visit growing hypercube shells H₀, H₁, H₂, … where H_d = points with all |xᵢ| ≤ d and some |xⱼ| = d (each shell finite: (2d+1)ⁿ − (2d−1)ⁿ points for d ≥ 1). Accept when p evaluates to 0. Root exists ⟹ found in finite time; no root ⟹ runs forever. Exactly the enumerable pattern.\n\n**Theorem 5.4.4.** A_TM is enumerable.\n*Proof sketch:* On ⟨M, w⟩, **simulate** M on w. M accepts ⟹ accept; M rejects ⟹ reject; M loops ⟹ we loop. Yes-instances always accepted in finite time. (The simulator is the \"universal TM\" idea.)\n\n### Why \"enumerable\"? (Section 5.5)\n\n**Definition 5.5.1 (enumerator).** An enumerator for A is a TM E with a **print tape** and **print state**; entering the print state sends the current print-tape string to the printer and clears the tape. E starts on empty tapes, and: (3) every string in A is printed **at least once**; (4) no string outside A is ever printed. No order required; duplicates allowed; E never terminates if A is infinite.\n\n**Theorem 5.5.2.** A language is enumerable ⟺ it has an enumerator.\n*Proof sketch (⇐):* Given enumerator E and input w: run E; whenever E prints a string v, compare v = w; accept if equal. w ∈ A ⟹ eventually printed ⟹ accept; w ∉ A ⟹ run forever.\n*Proof sketch (⇒):* Given recognizer M, fix the canonical list s₁, s₂, s₃, … of all of Σ* (by length, e.g. ε, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10, 11, …). For n = 1, 2, 3, …: for i = 1..n, run M on sᵢ **for n steps**; print sᵢ if accepted within n steps. **The n-step cap is the point** — a plain \"run M on each string\" would get stuck the first time M loops (dovetailing). If w = sᵢ ∈ A is accepted in m steps, iteration n = max(m, i) prints it. ∎\n\nCountable vs enumerable (don't confuse!): *every* language A ⊆ Σ* is countable, so A = {s₁, s₂, …} always exists as a numbering; A is enumerable iff an **algorithm** can produce that listing; not enumerable iff no algorithm can.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Most languages are not enumerable (Section 5.6)\n\nTwo counting facts:\n\n**Lemma 5.6.1.** E = {A ⊆ {0,1}* : A enumerable} is **countable**.\n*Proof sketch:* Each enumerable A has a recognizer T_A, encodable as a unique binary string s_A; A ↦ s_A is a bijection from E onto S = {s_A}. S ⊆ {0,1}* has at most 2ⁿ strings of each length n, so list S by length — a numbering of S, hence of E. ∎\n\n**Lemma 5.6.2.** L = {A : A ⊆ {0,1}* is a language} is **not countable**.\n*Proof sketch (two steps):*\n(1) B = {infinite binary sequences} is uncountable, by diagonalization: assume f : N → B a bijection; define w with wₙ = 1 if the n-th bit of f(n) is 0, wₙ = 0 otherwise; w ∈ B so w = f(n) for some n, but w's n-th bit differs from f(n)'s n-th bit by construction — contradiction.\n(2) L and B have the same size: fix the canonical listing {0,1}* = {s₁, s₂, s₃, …}; map each language A to its **characteristic sequence** g(A) whose n-th bit is 1 iff sₙ ∈ A. g is a bijection L → B (different languages give different sequences; every sequence arises from some language). ∎\n\n**Theorem 5.6.3.** There exist languages that are not enumerable. *(E countable, L uncountable, E ⊆ L — so L \\ E ≠ ∅; in fact \"most\" languages are non-enumerable.)* Note: this argument proves existence without exhibiting a specific example.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Decidable vs enumerable vs co-enumerable (Section 5.7)\n\n**Theorem 5.7.1.** A is **decidable** ⟺ both A and its complement Ā are **enumerable**.\n*Proof sketch (⇒):* A decidable ⟹ A enumerable (Thm 5.4.2); Ā is also decidable (swap accept/reject) hence enumerable.\n*(⇐):* Take recognizers M₁ for A and M₂ for Ā. Build a **two-tape** TM M running M₁ and M₂ **in parallel** (simultaneously, one per tape) on input w: if M₁ accepts → accept; if M₂ accepts → reject. Every w lies in A or Ā, so one of the two simulations is guaranteed to accept in finite time — M always terminates and answers correctly. ∎\n\n**Theorem 5.7.2.** The complement of A_TM is **not enumerable**. *(A_TM is enumerable (5.4.4) but undecidable (5.1.6); if its complement were also enumerable, Thm 5.7.1 would make A_TM decidable.)*\n\n**Theorem 5.7.3.** The complement of Halt is **not enumerable**. *(Same argument: Halt is enumerable — simulate and accept upon termination, Exercise 5.12 — but undecidable.)*\n\nThe classification picture:\n- decidable ⟹ enumerable; converse false (A_TM, Halt).\n- enumerable ∧ co-enumerable ⟹ decidable (Thm 5.7.1).\n- A_TM, Halt: enumerable, not co-enumerable. Their complements: co-enumerable, not enumerable.\n- EQ_TM: neither.\n\n---\n\n## 7. A language with A and Ā both non-enumerable (Section 5.8)\n\nEQ_TM = {⟨M₁, M₂⟩ : M₁ and M₂ are Turing machines and L(M₁) = L(M₂)}.\n\n**Theorem 5.8.1.** Both EQ_TM and its complement are not enumerable.\n\nShared gadget: for fixed (M, w), define **T_{Mw}(x)**: run M on w (ignoring x); then accept. So:\n- ⟨M, w⟩ ∈ Halt ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = {0,1}* (everything accepted);\n- ⟨M, w⟩ ∉ Halt ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = ∅ (never get past the simulation).\n\n**EQ_TM not enumerable:** Suppose H recognizes EQ_TM (accepts when L(M₁) = L(M₂); rejects or loops otherwise). Build H′(⟨M, w⟩): let M₁ reject every input (L(M₁) = ∅); construct T_{Mw}; run H on ⟨M₁, T_{Mw}⟩ and copy its behavior. Then: ⟨M, w⟩ ∈ Halt̄ (M does not terminate on w) ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = ∅ = L(M₁) ⟹ H accepts ⟹ H′ accepts; ⟨M, w⟩ ∈ Halt ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = {0,1}* ≠ ∅ = L(M₁) ⟹ H rejects/loops ⟹ H′ rejects/loops. So H′ recognizes **Halt̄** — contradicting Theorem 5.7.3 (Halt̄ not enumerable). ∎\n\n**complement of EQ_TM not enumerable (symmetric):** Suppose H recognizes the complement (accepts when L(M₁) ≠ L(M₂)). Build H′(⟨M, w⟩): let M₁ **accept** every input (L(M₁) = {0,1}*); run H on ⟨M₁, T_{Mw}⟩, copy behavior. M not terminating on w ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = ∅ ≠ {0,1}* ⟹ H accepts ⟹ H′ accepts; M terminating ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = {0,1}* = L(M₁) ⟹ H rejects/loops ⟹ H′ rejects/loops. Again H′ recognizes Halt̄ — contradiction. ∎\n\nReduction template used throughout §5.3 and §5.8: **build T_{Mw} whose language flips between two fixed languages according to whether M halts on (or accepts) w; feed ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ to the assumed decider/recognizer; conclude Halt (or its complement) would be decidable/enumerable.** T_{Mw} is only *constructed* (its code written down), never run, by the outer machine.\n\n---\n\n## Key exercises\n\n1. **(Ex. 5.2)** Prove that F, the set of all functions f : N → N, is not countable.\n   *Hint:* Diagonalize. Assume a list f₁, f₂, f₃, …; define g(n) = fₙ(n) + 1. Then g ∈ F but g ≠ fₙ for every n (they differ at n).\n\n2. **(Ex. 5.3)** Prove that there exist functions f : N → N that are not computable.\n   *Hint:* Counting argument. The set of Turing machines (hence of computable functions) is countable — list encodings by length; F is uncountable by Ex. 5.2. Countable ⊊ uncountable.\n\n3. **(Ex. 5.7)** With M₁, M₂, M₃, … a list of all TMs and ⟨n⟩ the binary representation of n, prove A = {⟨n⟩ : Mₙ terminates on input ⟨n⟩ and rejects it} is undecidable.\n   *Hint:* A decider for A is itself a TM, so it equals M_k for some k. Ask what M_k does on input ⟨k⟩ — the decider must accept ⟨k⟩ iff M_k rejects ⟨k⟩. Direct diagonal contradiction, no reduction needed.\n\n4. **(Ex. 5.8 / 5.9)** Use Rice's Theorem to show Empty = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) = ∅} and REG_TM = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) regular} are undecidable; then derive UselessState and EQ_TM undecidability from Empty.\n   *Hint:* For Rice: check the three conditions (some TM has the property, some doesn't, and it depends only on L(M)). For EQ_TM: a decider for EQ_TM decides Empty by fixing M₂ with L(M₂) = ∅ and asking ⟨M, M₂⟩. For UselessState: L(M) = ∅ iff M's accept state is useless (careful: UselessState itself is a machine property, so Rice doesn't apply directly — reduce instead).\n\n5. **(Ex. 5.10)** Prove REG_TM undecidable *without* Rice, via the guided reduction: T_{Mw}(x) accepts if x = 0ⁿ1ⁿ, else simulates M on w and copies its answer.\n   *Hint:* M accepts w ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = {0,1}* (regular); M does not accept w ⟹ L(T_{Mw}) = {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n ≥ 0} (not regular). A decider R for REG_TM run on ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ thus decides A_TM.\n\n6. **(Ex. 5.12 & 5.13)** (a) Prove Halt is enumerable. (b) Prove that for L = {⟨0, M, w⟩ : ⟨M, w⟩ ∈ A_TM} ∪ {⟨1, M, w⟩ : ⟨M, w⟩ ∉ A_TM}, neither L nor L̄ is enumerable.\n   *Hint:* (a) simulate P on w; accept the moment it terminates. (b) The 0-part of L encodes A_TM and the 1-part encodes its complement. A recognizer for L would recognize both A_TM (query ⟨0, M, w⟩) and its complement (query ⟨1, M, w⟩), making A_TM decidable by Thm 5.7.1 — contradiction; L̄ is symmetric (its 0-part encodes the complement of A_TM, its 1-part encodes A_TM).\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Define: language A is decidable.","a":"There is a TM M such that for every w ∈ Σ*: w ∈ A ⟹ M on w terminates in the accept state; w ∉ A ⟹ M on w terminates in the reject state. (Always terminates, always answers correctly.)"},{"q":"Define: language A is enumerable.","a":"There is a TM M such that: w ∈ A ⟹ M on w terminates in the accept state; w ∉ A ⟹ M on w does not terminate in the accept state (it rejects or runs forever)."},{"q":"What is the exact difference between a decider and a recognizer (enumerable-witness) on strings NOT in A?","a":"A decider must terminate in the reject state; a recognizer may reject or may run forever — it just may never accept."},{"q":"Why is A_DFA decidable, and what extra step does A_NFA need?","a":"Simulate the DFA on w — always terminates after |w| steps. For A_NFA, first convert the NFA to an equivalent DFA via the (algorithmic) subset construction, then simulate."},{"q":"How do you decide A_CFG = {⟨G, w⟩ : w ∈ L(G)}?","a":"Convert G to Chomsky normal form G′; if |w| = n, every derivation of w in G′ has exactly 2n − 1 steps; check the finitely many derivations of that length. (Corollary: every context-free language is decidable.)"},{"q":"Define A_TM and state its status in the decidable/enumerable classification.","a":"A_TM = {⟨M, w⟩ : M is a TM that accepts w}. It is enumerable (simulate M on w) but undecidable; its complement is not enumerable."},{"q":"Sketch the proof that A_TM is undecidable.","a":"Assume decider H. Build D: on ⟨M⟩, run H on ⟨M, ⟨M⟩⟩ and flip the answer (H accepts → D rejects; H rejects → D accepts). Run D on ⟨D⟩: D accepts ⟨D⟩ ⟺ D rejects ⟨D⟩. Contradiction."},{"q":"Sketch the proof that Halt is undecidable.","a":"Assume H(P, w) decides termination. Define Q(⟨P⟩): loop while H(P, ⟨P⟩) = true. Then Q(⟨P⟩) terminates ⟺ P(⟨P⟩) doesn't. Setting P := Q: Q(⟨Q⟩) terminates ⟺ Q(⟨Q⟩) doesn't terminate. Contradiction."},{"q":"Define: A and B have the same size; A is countable.","a":"Same size: there exists a bijection f : A → B. Countable: A is finite, or A and N have the same size (elements can be numbered 1, 2, 3, …)."},{"q":"Give the 4-step diagonalization recipe.","a":"(1) Assume the objects form a list O₁, O₂, … (or a decider exists). (2) Build D that flips the n-th object's behavior at the n-th test point. (3) D is an object of the same kind, so D = Oₙ for some n. (4) Evaluate D at its own index: \"X ⟺ not X\" — contradiction."},{"q":"Sketch Cantor's proof that R is not countable.","a":"Suppose f : N → [0,1) is a bijection; write f(n) = 0.d_{n1}d_{n2}…; define x = 0.d₁d₂… with dₙ = 4 if d_{nn} ≠ 4, else 5. Then x ∈ [0,1), so x = f(n) for some n, but x's n-th digit differs from f(n)'s n-th digit by construction. Contradiction."},{"q":"State Rice's Theorem exactly (book version).","a":"Let P ⊆ T = {⟨M⟩ : M a TM with input alphabet {0,1}} such that (1) P ≠ ∅, (2) P is a proper subset of T, and (3) for any TMs M₁, M₂ with L(M₁) = L(M₂), either both encodings are in P or neither is. Then P is undecidable."},{"q":"What is the 3-check pattern for applying Rice's Theorem, and when does Rice NOT apply?","a":"Check: (a) some TM is in P, (b) some TM is not in P, (c) membership depends only on L(M). It does NOT apply to machine-structure/behavior properties (number of states, visiting a state, running time) — condition 3 fails; use a direct reduction there."},{"q":"In the proof of Rice's Theorem, what is T_{Mw} and what is L(T_{Mw}) in each case?","a":"T_{Mw}(x): run M on w; if it terminates, run M₂ (a machine with ⟨M₂⟩ ∈ P) on x and copy its answer. If M halts on w: L(T_{Mw}) = L(M₂), so ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ ∈ P. If not: L(T_{Mw}) = ∅ = L(M₁), so ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ ∉ P. A decider for P applied to ⟨T_{Mw}⟩ would decide Halt."},{"q":"State the theorem linking decidable, enumerable, and co-enumerable, with proof idea of the hard direction.","a":"A is decidable ⟺ both A and Ā are enumerable. (⇐): run recognizers M₁ (for A) and M₂ (for Ā) in parallel on two tapes; exactly one must accept in finite time; accept if M₁ does, reject if M₂ does. Consequences: complements of A_TM and Halt are not enumerable."},{"q":"What is an enumerator, and how do you build one from a recognizer M for A?","a":"A TM with a print tape/state that prints every string of A at least once and nothing else (any order, repeats allowed). Construction (dovetailing): fix listing s₁, s₂, … of Σ*; for n = 1, 2, …: run M on each of s₁,…,sₙ for n steps; print those accepted within n steps. The n-step cap prevents getting stuck on looping inputs."},{"q":"Sketch the counting proof that most languages are not enumerable, and the countable-vs-enumerable distinction.","a":"E (enumerable languages) is countable: map each A to a binary encoding s_A of its recognizer, list by length. L (all languages over {0,1}) is uncountable: languages ↔ infinite binary characteristic sequences (n-th bit = [sₙ ∈ A]), and those sequences are uncountable by diagonalization (flip n-th bit of f(n)). So E ⊊ L. Distinction: every language is countable (a numbering exists); enumerable means an algorithm can produce the numbering."},{"q":"Which language has both itself and its complement non-enumerable, and what gadget proves it?","a":"EQ_TM = {⟨M₁, M₂⟩ : L(M₁) = L(M₂)}. Gadget T_{Mw}(x): run M on w, then accept — so L(T_{Mw}) = {0,1}* if M halts on w, else ∅. Comparing T_{Mw} against a reject-all machine (for EQ_TM) or an accept-all machine (for the complement), a recognizer for either language would recognize the complement of Halt — impossible by Thm 5.7.3."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"b6","title":"Book 6 · Complexity & Course Summary","slides":"M&S ch. 6–7 · weeks 14–15","md":"# B6 — Complexity Theory (Maheshwari & Smid, Chapter 6) + Course Summary (Chapter 7)\n\n**Aligned with course weeks 14–15 (estimate)** — In-Tutorial Quiz 2 (week 14) and final-exam (35%) territory; the Summary section doubles as a final-exam revision map.\n\nSource: Maheshwari & Smid, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*, Ch. 6 (pp. 197–238, PDF pages 205–246) and Ch. 7 \"Summary\" (PDF pages 247–252).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Running time of algorithms (Section 6.1)\n\n**Running time.** For a Turing machine M and input string w:\n> tM(w) := the number of computation steps made by M on input w.\n\n**Definition 6.1.1.** Let T : N0 → N0. The Turing machine M **decides the language A in time T** (resp. **computes the function F in time T**) if tM(w) ≤ T(|w|) for **all** strings w in Σ*. So T(n) is a **worst-case upper bound** over all inputs of length n (n = |w| = input length).\n\n**Book's worked example — palindromes:**\n- One-tape TM (Section 4.2.1, zig-zag deleting end symbols): O(1 + 2 + ... + n) = **O(n²)**. (The book notes Ω(n²) is in fact a lower bound for one-tape TMs, without proof.)\n- Two-tape TM (Section 4.2.2): **O(n)**.\n\nSo the *model* affects the *speed*, even though all models decide the same languages.\n\n**Theorem 6.1.2 (model conversion costs).** If A is decided in time T on model M, it can be decided in time T′ on model N:\n\n| From (M) | To (N) | T′ |\n|---|---|---|\n| k-tape TM | one-tape TM | O(T²) |\n| one-tape TM | Java program | O(T²) |\n| Java program | k-tape TM | O(T⁴) |\n\nKey point: all conversions are **polynomial** blow-ups, so \"polynomial time\" is model-independent.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The class P (Section 6.2)\n\n**Definition 6.2.1.** Algorithm M decides language A (resp. computes function F) in **polynomial time** if there exists an integer k ≥ 1 such that the running time of M is O(nᵏ) for any input of length n.\n\n**Theorem 6.2.2.** Polynomial-time decidability does not depend on the model (Java program / k-tape TM / one-tape TM) — follows from Theorem 6.1.2.\n\n**The classes:**\n> **P** := {A : the language A is decidable in polynomial time}\n> **FP** := {F : the function F is computable in polynomial time}\n\n### Example problems the book puts in P\n1. **Pal** (palindromes over {a,b}) ∈ P — the O(n²) one-tape TM suffices.\n2. **FP examples:** F₁(x) = x+1, F₂(x,y) = x+y, F₃(x,y) = xy.\n3. **Every context-free language is in P** (Theorem 6.2.3, via dynamic programming — the naive CNF-derivation algorithm from Ch. 5 is *not* polynomial). Obviously every language in P is decidable.\n4. **2Color** := {⟨G⟩ : the graph G is 2-colorable} ∈ P.\n\n**2Color algorithm (know the idea).** Input: adjacency matrix, m vertices, input length n = m². Greedy BFS-style coloring: color vertex 1 red; repeatedly pick a colored-but-unmarked vertex i, examine all neighbors j: if f(j) = f(i) return NO; if j uncolored give it the opposite color; then mark i. If no colored-unmarked vertex exists, start a new connected component by coloring an arbitrary uncolored vertex red. Return YES when all m vertices are marked. Outer loop runs ≤ 2m times (each iteration either marks a vertex or seeds a component), each iteration O(m) ⇒ total **O(m²) = O(n)**.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The class NP (Section 6.3)\n\n### Motivating examples (solutions easy to *verify*, unknown whether easy to *find*)\n- **kColor** := {⟨G⟩ : G is k-colorable}. In P for k = 2; for k ≥ 3 membership in P is **unknown**.\n- **HC** := {⟨G⟩ : G contains a Hamilton cycle} (cycle visiting each vertex exactly once). Unknown whether in P.\n- **SOS** (sum of subset) := {⟨a₁,...,aₘ,b⟩ : ∃I ⊆ {1,...,m}, Σ_{i∈I} aᵢ = b}. Unknown whether in P.\n- **NPrim** := {⟨x⟩ : x ≥ 2 and x is not a prime number}. Shown to be in P only in **2002** (AKS) — but its NP membership is easy: a certificate is a factorization.\n\n**Observation 6.3.5.** These languages share: given a proposed \"solution\", we can verify its correctness in polynomial time, and a solution of length polynomial in the input always exists (for yes-instances).\n\n### The certificate/verifier definition — MEMORIZE\n\n**Definition 6.3.6.** A language A belongs to the class **NP** if there exist a **polynomial p** and a **language B ∈ P** such that for every string w:\n> w ∈ A ⟺ ∃s : |s| ≤ p(|w|) and ⟨w, s⟩ ∈ B.\n\nIn words: w ∈ A iff (1) there is a \"solution\" (certificate) s whose length is polynomial in |w|, and (2) correctness of s is verifiable in polynomial time. B is the **verification language**: B = {⟨w,s⟩ : s is a correct solution for w}.\n\n**What the certificates are:**\n- kColor: the coloring f₁,...,fₘ (check fᵢ ∈ {1..k}; every edge (i,j) has fᵢ ≠ fⱼ). Length O(m log k).\n- HC: the vertex sequence v₁,...,vₘ (check it is all of V; check consecutive pairs + wrap-around are edges).\n- SOS: the bit-vector c₁,...,cₘ ∈ {0,1} (check Σ cᵢaᵢ = b).\n- NPrim: the pair (a,b) with 2 ≤ a < x, 2 ≤ b < x, x = ab. The book proves formally NPrim ∈ NP with p(n) := n + 2 and B := {⟨x,a,b⟩ : x ≥ 2, 2 ≤ a < x, 2 ≤ b < x, x = ab}, using |⟨a,b⟩| = ⌊log a⌋+1 + ⌊log b⌋+1 ≤ log(ab) + 2 = log x + 2 ≤ |⟨x⟩| + 2.\n\n### Theorem 6.3.7: P ⊆ NP\nProof trick: for A ∈ P take p(n) := 0 and B := {⟨w, ε⟩ : w ∈ A}. The empty certificate works — in P you can *compute* the solution yourself; in NP you only need to *verify* a given one.\n\n### Theorem 6.3.8: NP ⊆ EXP\n**EXP** := {A : ∃ polynomial p such that A can be decided in time 2^{p(n)}}.\nProof: brute-force try **all** certificates s with |s| ≤ p(|w|) (at most 3^{p(n)} strings over {0,1,blank}); test ⟨w,s⟩ ∈ B in time q(n + p(n)). Total ≤ 3^{p(n)} · q(n+p(n)) ≤ 2^{p′(n)} for the polynomial p′(n) = 2p(n) + q(n+p(n)).\n(Illustration: trial division for NPrim takes ≥ x − 2 ≥ 2^{n−1} − 2 steps on primes — exponential in the *bit length* n.)\n\n### Summary of section 6.3 (book's own bullet list)\n- P ⊆ NP; whether P = NP is open (Clay $1M prize). Most people believe P ⊊ NP.\n- NP ⊆ EXP; whether NP = EXP is open.\n- Hence P ⊆ EXP, and here properness **is** known: P ⊊ EXP.\n- P = the efficiently decidable languages.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Non-deterministic algorithms (Section 6.4)\n\nNP = **N**on-deterministic **P**olynomial time. Deterministic: next step uniquely determined. Non-deterministic: one or more possibilities for the next step; the algorithm *chooses* one.\n\n**Book's example — non-deterministic algorithm for SOS:** s := 0; for i := 1 to m do `s := s | s := s + aᵢ` endfor; return YES iff s = b. The line \"s := s | s := s + aᵢ\" means *either* instruction may be executed. If the input is in SOS, the choices cᵢ give an accepting computation; if not, every computation returns NO. Runs in O(m) = O(n) time.\n\n**Definition 6.4.1.** A non-deterministic algorithm M **accepts** w if there exists **at least one** computation that on input w returns YES.\n**Definition 6.4.2.** M **decides A in time T** if for every w: w ∈ A ⟺ there exists at least one computation that returns YES and takes at most T(|w|) time.\n\n**Theorem 6.4.3 (equivalence of the two NP definitions).** A ∈ NP ⟺ there exists a non-deterministic Turing machine (or Java program) that decides A in polynomial time.\nIntuition: the non-deterministic choices *are* the certificate (guess s, then verify deterministically).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Polynomial-time reductions (Section 6.5)\n\n**Definition 6.5.1.** For languages A, B ⊆ {0,1}*: **A ≤ᴾ B** if there exists a function f : {0,1}* → {0,1}* such that\n1. f ∈ FP (f computable in polynomial time), and\n2. for all strings w: w ∈ A ⟺ f(w) ∈ B.\n\nRead as: \"**A is polynomial-time reducible to B**\" / \"**B is at least as hard as A**\".\n\n### Direction of implication — the #1 exam trap\n- **Theorem 6.5.2.** If B ∈ P and A ≤ᴾ B, then **A ∈ P**. (Easiness flows *backwards* along the reduction.) Proof: decide w ∈ A by computing u := f(w) (time p(n), so |u| ≤ p(n)) and testing u ∈ B (time q(|u|)); total ≤ p(n) + q(p(n)), a polynomial.\n- Contrapositive: if A ≤ᴾ B and A is hard (∉ P), then B is hard. (Hardness flows *forwards*.)\n- To prove a new problem C hard, reduce a **known-hard** problem **to** C — never the other way.\n\n**Theorem 6.5.3.** ≤ᴾ is reflexive (A ≤ᴾ A) and transitive (A ≤ᴾ B and B ≤ᴾ C ⇒ A ≤ᴾ C). [Exercise 6.2]\n\n**Theorem 6.5.4.** If A ∈ P and B is any language with B ≠ ∅ and B ≠ {0,1}*, then A ≤ᴾ B. (Pick u ∈ B, v ∉ B; f(w) := u if w ∈ A, else v — computable in poly time *because* A ∈ P.) So P-languages are the easiest languages in NP.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Definition of NP-completeness (Section 6.5.2)\n\n**Definition 6.5.9.** B ⊆ {0,1}* is **NP-complete** if\n1. **B ∈ NP**, and\n2. **A ≤ᴾ B for every language A in NP.**\n\n**Theorem 6.5.10.** B NP-complete ⇒ (B ∈ P ⟺ P = NP). Proof: if B ∈ P, then any A ∈ NP has A ≤ᴾ B, so A ∈ P by Thm 6.5.2, giving NP ⊆ P. Converse trivial since B ∈ NP.\n\n**Theorem 6.5.11 (the workhorse).** If C ∈ NP, B ≤ᴾ C, and B is NP-complete, then C is NP-complete. (Every A ∈ NP has A ≤ᴾ B ≤ᴾ C; use transitivity.)\n\n### The NP-completeness proof recipe — MEMORIZE\nTo prove language C is NP-complete:\n1. Prove **C ∈ NP** (exhibit a poly-length certificate + poly-time verifier).\n2. Pick a **known NP-complete** language B (ideally \"similar\" to C).\n3. Prove **B ≤ᴾ C** (construct f ∈ FP with w ∈ B ⟺ f(w) ∈ C — prove BOTH directions of the iff).\n4. Conclude by Theorem 6.5.11.\n\nThe recipe needs a **first** NP-complete language whose completeness is proved raw from Definition 6.5.9 (a reduction from *every* A ∈ NP). Historically **Cook (1971)** did this for 3SAT (SAT); the book instead proves **Domino** NP-complete from scratch and derives the rest.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The reductions the book works through\n\n### 7.1 SOS ≤ᴾ KS (Theorem 6.5.5) — warm-up\nKS (knapsack) := {⟨w₁,...,wₘ,k₁,...,kₘ,W,K⟩ : ∃c₁,...,cₘ ∈ {0,1}, Σcᵢwᵢ ≤ W and Σcᵢkᵢ ≥ K}.\n**Construction:** f(⟨a₁,...,aₘ,b⟩) := ⟨a₁,...,aₘ, a₁,...,aₘ, b, b⟩ — use the aᵢ as both weights and calories, and b as both bounds. Then Σcᵢaᵢ = b ⟺ (Σcᵢaᵢ ≤ b and Σcᵢaᵢ ≥ b). An equality is exactly a \"≤ and ≥\" pair. Clearly f ∈ FP.\n\n### 7.2 3SAT ≤ᴾ Clique (Theorem 6.5.8) — the classic gadget\n3SAT: CNF formulas φ = C₁ ∧ ... ∧ C_k, each clause Cᵢ = ℓⁱ₁ ∨ ℓⁱ₂ ∨ ℓⁱ₃ (literals = variables or negations). Clique := {⟨G,k⟩ : G has a clique with k vertices} (clique = vertex subset with all pairs adjacent). Both are in NP (Thms 6.5.6, 6.5.7 — exercises).\n**Construction:** map φ (k clauses) to (G, k): one vertex vⁱₐ per literal occurrence ℓⁱₐ (3k vertices, in k groups of 3). Edge (vⁱₐ, vʲ_b) ⟺ **i ≠ j** (different clauses) **and ℓⁱₐ is not the negation of ℓʲ_b**.\n**⇒:** satisfying assignment makes ≥ 1 literal true per clause; pick one true literal's vertex per clause → k vertices, pairwise consistent (two true literals can't be negations) → clique.\n**⇐:** a k-clique has exactly one vertex per group (no edges inside a group); set each chosen literal true (xⱼ := 1 if ℓ = xⱼ, xⱼ := 0 if ℓ = ¬xⱼ; consistent because clique edges forbid contradictory pairs; unset variables arbitrary) → every clause has a true literal → φ satisfiable.\n\n### 7.3 Domino is NP-complete (Theorem 6.5.12) — the book's \"first\" NP-complete language, proved from the definition\nDomino := solvable tiling games: tiles are squares split into 4 triangles labeled with symbols; fill a t × t frame so that touching triangles of neighboring tiles match, tiles match the frame's boundary symbols, no rotations.\n**Membership:** certificate = the t × t matrix of tile types; poly length, poly-time checkable.\n**Hardness (A ≤ᴾ Domino for every A ∈ NP):** Let M be a 1-tape non-deterministic TM deciding A in polynomial time p (head never left of start cell; accepting computations padded to exactly p(n) steps; state determines head direction). Build a **p(n) × p(n) frame**: each **row = one configuration/computation step**; top row encodes the start configuration ((z₀,a₁) a₂ ... aₙ ⊔...⊔), bottom row encodes the accepting configuration ((z₁,1)). Tile types encode the transition rules: (1) \"head not here\" tiles copying symbol a downwards with # on the sides; (2)–(4) tiles for instructions za → z′bR / z′bL / z′bN carrying (z,a) on top and passing state z′ out the right/left side or (z′,b) down; (5) tiles receiving state z from the left or right and turning cell content a into (z,a). Vertical matching forces each row to follow from the previous by one legal step; side matching passes the head between cells. Then w ∈ A ⟺ ∃ accepting computation of p(n) steps ⟺ the domino game is solvable. All encodings computable in poly time.\n(The book illustrates this with a concrete TM computing x → x+1, input x = 11: 9 steps, 6 cells → a 9 × 6 frame whose unique solution is literally the computation table.)\n\n### 7.4 Domino ≤ᴾ SAT (Theorem 6.5.13) ⇒ SAT is NP-complete\nSAT: CNF with clauses of arbitrary length. SAT ∈ NP is clear.\n**Construction:** variables x_{ijℓ} meaning \"a tile of type T_ℓ sits at position (i,j)\" (1 ≤ i,j ≤ t, 1 ≤ ℓ ≤ k). Clauses:\n- C¹ᵢⱼ := x_{ij1} ∨ ... ∨ x_{ijk} — **at least one** tile per cell;\n- C²ᵢⱼℓℓ′ := ¬x_{ijℓ} ∨ ¬x_{ijℓ′} (ℓ < ℓ′) — **at most one** tile per cell;\n- C³ᵢⱼℓℓ′ := ¬x_{ijℓ} ∨ ¬x_{i,j+1,ℓ′} for every incompatible horizontal pair (right symbol of T_ℓ ≠ left symbol of T_ℓ′) — neighbors fit; symmetric clauses for vertical neighbors;\n- C⁴ⱼℓ := ¬x_{1jℓ} when T_ℓ's top symbol mismatches the frame's upper boundary at j — boundary fits; symmetric for bottom/left/right.\nφ = conjunction of all; size O(t²k²), polynomial ⇒ f ∈ FP; game solvable ⟺ φ satisfiable.\n\n### 7.5 SAT ≤ᴾ 3SAT (Theorem 6.5.14) — clause splitting\n3SAT ∈ NP is clear. Map each clause Cᵢ = ℓ₁ ∨ ... ∨ ℓ_{kᵢ}:\n- kᵢ = 1: C′ᵢ := ℓ₁ ∨ ℓ₁ ∨ ℓ₁ (pad by repetition); kᵢ = 2: C′ᵢ := ℓ₁ ∨ ℓ₂ ∨ ℓ₂; kᵢ = 3: unchanged.\n- kᵢ ≥ 4: chain with **new variables** z₁,...,z_{kᵢ−3}:\n  C′ᵢ := (ℓ₁ ∨ ℓ₂ ∨ z₁) ∧ (¬z₁ ∨ ℓ₃ ∨ z₂) ∧ (¬z₂ ∨ ℓ₄ ∨ z₃) ∧ ... ∧ (¬z_{kᵢ−3} ∨ ℓ_{kᵢ−1} ∨ ℓ_{kᵢ}).\n  The z's act as \"carry\" bits: the chain is satisfiable exactly when some ℓⱼ is true. φ satisfiable ⟺ φ′ satisfiable.\n\n### 7.6 Clique is NP-complete (Theorem 6.5.15)\nNo new work: Clique ∈ NP (Thm 6.5.6) and 3SAT ≤ᴾ Clique (Thm 6.5.8), with 3SAT NP-complete (Thm 6.5.14) ⇒ Theorem 6.5.11 applies.\n\n**Book's reduction chain:** every A ∈ NP ≤ᴾ **Domino** ≤ᴾ **SAT** ≤ᴾ **3SAT** ≤ᴾ **Clique**; also SOS ≤ᴾ KS.\n\n### 7.7 More NP-complete problems (stated without proof, Section 6.5.4)\n- **Traveling salesperson:** m cities, cost matrix M; is there a tour through all cities of total cost ≤ k?\n- **Bin packing:** m objects with volumes a₁..aₘ, k bins of volume ℓ each; do they fit? (Equivalently: schedule m jobs on k processors with makespan ≤ ℓ.)\n- **Time tables:** courses/rooms/professors with constraints.\n- **Motion planning:** path between s,t ∈ Q³ avoiding k polyhedra with length ≤ ℓ.\n- **Map labeling:** place non-overlapping name rectangles, each with its city point at a corner.\nThousands more: Garey & Johnson, *Computers and Intractability* (1979).\n\n---\n\n## 8. P vs NP discussion — exam talking points\n- P ⊆ NP (Thm 6.3.7); NP ⊆ EXP (Thm 6.3.8); P ⊊ EXP is *proven*, but P vs NP and NP vs EXP are open.\n- P = NP is \"one of the most important open problems in computer science\"; Clay Mathematics Institute offers $1,000,000. Most people believe P ⊊ NP.\n- If P ≠ NP, the NP-complete languages are exactly the \"hardest\" languages in NP and none of them is in P. If any single NP-complete language lands in P, then P = NP (Thm 6.5.10) — all thousands of them stand or fall together.\n- P captures \"efficiently decidable\"; verification (NP) is intuitively easier than solution-finding (P), and the open question is whether that intuition is real.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Chapter 7 Summary — the book's final-exam revision map\n\n**Regular languages (Ch. 2).** Equivalent characterizations: DFA ⟺ NFA ⟺ regular expression (via the three conversions NFA→DFA, DFA→regex, regex→NFA). Closed under **union, concatenation, star, complement, intersection**. Pumping lemma for regular languages proves e.g. {aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 0} not regular.\n\n**Context-free languages (Ch. 3).** Every regular language is context-free; {aⁿbⁿ} is context-free but not regular. Equivalent: CFG ⟺ CFG in Chomsky normal form ⟺ nondeterministic pushdown automaton (conversions: CFG→CNF, CNF→NPDA; NPDA→CFG not covered). **Nondeterministic PDAs are strictly more powerful than deterministic PDAs** (witness: {vbw : |v| = |w|}, not proved). Closed under **union, concatenation, star**; **NOT** closed under intersection or complement. Pumping lemma for CFLs proves e.g. {aⁿbⁿcⁿ : n ≥ 0} not context-free.\n\n**Church-Turing Thesis (Ch. 4).** All \"reasonable\" computational devices (multi-tape TMs, Java programs, ...) are equivalent. Thesis: every intuitive algorithm can be converted to a Turing machine.\n\n**Decidable and enumerable languages (Ch. 5).**\n- Every context-free language is decidable; every decidable language is enumerable.\n- Strict separations: {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} decidable but not context-free; the **Halting Problem** enumerable but not decidable; the **complement of the Halting Problem** not enumerable.\n- The class of all languages is uncountable; the class of enumerable languages is countable (so \"most\" languages are not enumerable).\n- **A is decidable ⟺ both A and its complement Ā are enumerable.**\n\n**Complexity classes (Ch. 6).**\n- P = languages decidable in polynomial time by a deterministic TM; NP = decidable in polynomial time by a nondeterministic TM ⟺ poly-length, poly-time-verifiable certificates.\n- Every context-free language ∈ P; P ⊆ NP; NP vs P separation unknown; every language in NP is decidable.\n- B is NP-complete ⟺ B ∈ NP and B is at least as hard (≤ᴾ) as every language in NP; NP-complete languages exist.\n\n**The hierarchy picture (final figure of the book):**\n> regular ⊂ context-free ⊂ P ⊆ NP ⊆ decidable ⊂ enumerable ⊂ all languages\n\nSeparating witnesses to quote: {aⁿbⁿ} (CF, not regular); {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} (in P/decidable, not CF); Halting Problem (enumerable, not decidable); complement of Halting Problem (not enumerable). The P ⊆ NP and NP ⊆ decidable inclusions are not known to be strict... except NP ⊊ \"all decidable\" is not claimed strict by the book either; only P vs NP openness is emphasized.\n\n---\n\n## Key exercises\n\n1. **(Book Ex. 6.5.1–6.5.2) Prove SOS ≤ᴾ SP, and conclude SOS is NP-complete given that SP (set partition) is NP-complete.**\n   *Hint:* For SOS ≤ᴾ SP add two extra numbers to a₁..aₘ that force any balanced partition to encode a subset summing to b — try a_{m+1} := 2Σaᵢ − b and a_{m+2} := Σaᵢ + b, and check both directions of the iff. For the NP-completeness of SOS you need the reduction in the *other* direction: SP ≤ᴾ SOS (take b := ½Σaᵢ) — plus SOS ∈ NP. Watch the direction: \"SOS ≤ᴾ SP\" alone does NOT make SOS hard.\n\n2. **(Book Ex. 6.6) Prove 3Color ≤ᴾ 3SAT.**\n   *Hint (book's):* variable x_{ik} = \"vertex i has color k\" (k ∈ {1,2,3}). Write clauses saying: each vertex has at least one color (x_{i1} ∨ x_{i2} ∨ x_{i3}); at most one color (¬x_{ik} ∨ ¬x_{ik′}); adjacent vertices differ (¬x_{ik} ∨ ¬x_{jk} for each edge (i,j) and each k). Same at-least-one/at-most-one/compatibility pattern as the Domino → SAT reduction.\n\n3. **(Book Ex. 6.7) Prove IP ((0,1)-integer programming) is NP-complete, using that SOS is NP-complete.**\n   *Hint:* IP ∈ NP: certificate is x ∈ {0,1}ⁿ. For SOS ≤ᴾ IP, express Σcᵢaᵢ = b as two inequalities: (a₁...aₘ)x ≤ b and (−a₁...−aₘ)x ≤ −b — the same \"equality = ≤ plus ≥\" trick as SOS ≤ᴾ KS.\n\n4. **(Book Ex. 6.8) DNFSAT vs CNFSAT.** (a) Show DNFSAT ∈ P. (b) Find the flaw: \"any formula can be rewritten in DNF, so CNFSAT ≤ᴾ DNFSAT, hence P = NP.\" (c) Show directly that A ≤ᴾ CNFSAT for every A ∈ P.\n   *Hint:* (a) a DNF formula is satisfiable iff some single term is, i.e. some term contains no variable together with its negation — check each term. (b) the CNF→DNF conversion can blow up the formula *exponentially*, so the mapping is not in FP. (c) use Theorem 6.5.4-style reasoning: since A ∈ P, map w to a fixed satisfiable CNF formula if w ∈ A and a fixed unsatisfiable one (e.g. x ∧ ¬x in CNF) otherwise.\n\n5. **(Book Ex. 6.3/6.4) Prove Clique ∈ NP and 3SAT ∈ NP formally, from Definition 6.3.6.**\n   *Hint:* mimic the NPrim proof. For Clique: certificate s = list of k vertices; B = {⟨G,k,s⟩ : s has k distinct vertices of G, all pairs adjacent}; check |s| polynomial. For 3SAT: certificate = truth assignment (m bits); B checks each clause has a true literal. State p and B explicitly and prove both directions of the ⟺.\n\n6. **(Book Ex. 6.9) Dropping the length bound on the certificate.** Define A ∈ E iff there is B ∈ P with w ∈ A ⟺ ∃y : ⟨w,y⟩ ∈ B (no bound on |y|). Prove E = the class of all enumerable languages.\n   *Hint:* (⊆) enumerate pairs and search over all y — a TM accepting exactly A. (⊇) if A is enumerable via TM M, let y encode an accepting computation of M on w; checking \"y is a valid accepting computation of M on w\" is in P. Moral: the polynomial bound |s| ≤ p(|w|) is exactly what separates NP from mere enumerability.\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Define the running time tM(w) of a Turing machine M, and what \"M decides A in time T\" means.","a":"tM(w) = the number of computation steps made by M on input w; M decides A in time T if tM(w) ≤ T(|w|) for ALL strings w ∈ Σ* (worst-case bound as a function of input length n)."},{"q":"Define the class P (and FP).","a":"P = {A : language A is decidable in polynomial time}, i.e. some algorithm decides A in O(nᵏ) time for a constant k ≥ 1; FP = {F : function F is computable in polynomial time}. Model-independent (TM vs multi-tape TM vs Java) because model conversions cost only polynomial overhead."},{"q":"State the certificate definition of NP (Definition 6.3.6).","a":"A ∈ NP iff there exist a polynomial p and a language B ∈ P such that for every w: w ∈ A ⟺ ∃s with |s| ≤ p(|w|) and ⟨w,s⟩ ∈ B."},{"q":"What is a certificate (\"solution\") intuitively, and what two properties must it have?","a":"A witness string s proving w ∈ A (e.g. a coloring, a Hamilton cycle, a subset, a factorization). It must (1) have length polynomial in |w|, and (2) be verifiable for correctness in polynomial time (via the verification language B ∈ P)."},{"q":"Give the certificates for kColor, HC, SOS, and NPrim.","a":"kColor: the color sequence f₁..fₘ (check colors ∈ {1..k}, endpoints of every edge differ). HC: vertex order v₁..vₘ (check it's all of V and consecutive+closing pairs are edges). SOS: bits c₁..cₘ with Σcᵢaᵢ = b. NPrim: factors a,b with 2 ≤ a,b < x and x = ab."},{"q":"Why is P ⊆ NP, in one line of proof?","a":"For A ∈ P set p(n) := 0 and B := {⟨w,ε⟩ : w ∈ A} ∈ P; then w ∈ A ⟺ ∃s, |s| ≤ 0, ⟨w,s⟩ ∈ B. Intuition: if you can compute the answer, you can verify with an empty certificate."},{"q":"State NP ⊆ EXP and sketch why.","a":"EXP = {A : decidable in time 2^{p(n)} for some polynomial p}. For A ∈ NP, brute-force all ≤ 3^{p(n)} candidate certificates s with |s| ≤ p(n), verifying each in polynomial time; total is 2^{poly(n)}. (Also: P ⊊ EXP is proven; P vs NP and NP vs EXP are open.)"},{"q":"What does \"non-deterministic algorithm M decides A in time T\" mean?","a":"For every w: w ∈ A iff there exists AT LEAST ONE computation of M on w that returns YES within T(|w|) steps. (M accepts w if some computation returns YES; a NO-instance has no accepting computation at all.)"},{"q":"How do the two definitions of NP connect (Theorem 6.4.3)?","a":"A ∈ NP (certificate definition) ⟺ some non-deterministic TM/Java program decides A in polynomial time. The non-deterministic choices play the role of guessing the certificate, which is then verified deterministically."},{"q":"Define A ≤ᴾ B.","a":"There exists f : {0,1}* → {0,1}* with (1) f ∈ FP and (2) for all w: w ∈ A ⟺ f(w) ∈ B. Read: A is polynomial-time reducible to B; B is at least as hard as A. ≤ᴾ is reflexive and transitive."},{"q":"If A ≤ᴾ B, which way do \"easy\" and \"hard\" transfer?","a":"B ∈ P ⇒ A ∈ P (easiness flows from B back to A: decide w ∈ A by testing f(w) ∈ B). Contrapositive: A ∉ P ⇒ B ∉ P (hardness flows from A forward to B). So to show C is hard, reduce a known hard problem TO C, never from C."},{"q":"Define NP-complete.","a":"B is NP-complete iff (1) B ∈ NP and (2) A ≤ᴾ B for EVERY language A ∈ NP (B is at least as hard as everything in NP)."},{"q":"State Theorem 6.5.10 linking one NP-complete language to P vs NP.","a":"If B is NP-complete, then B ∈ P ⟺ P = NP. So a polynomial-time algorithm for any single NP-complete problem collapses all of NP into P; if P ≠ NP, no NP-complete language is in P."},{"q":"Give the 4-step recipe for proving a language C NP-complete.","a":"(1) Prove C ∈ NP (poly certificate + poly verifier). (2) Choose a known NP-complete language B. (3) Prove B ≤ᴾ C: build f ∈ FP with w ∈ B ⟺ f(w) ∈ C, proving both directions. (4) Conclude by Theorem 6.5.11 (C ∈ NP, B ≤ᴾ C, B NP-complete ⇒ C NP-complete)."},{"q":"Sketch the 3SAT ≤ᴾ Clique construction.","a":"For φ with k clauses of 3 literals: one vertex per literal occurrence (k groups of 3); edges between vertices in DIFFERENT clauses whose literals are not negations of each other; ask for a clique of size k. Satisfying assignment → pick one true literal per clause → k-clique; k-clique → one vertex per clause, set those literals true (consistent by construction) → φ satisfiable."},{"q":"What is the book's first NP-complete language and how is its hardness proved?","a":"Domino (the tiling game), proved directly from Definition 6.5.9: for any A ∈ NP with non-deterministic TM M running in poly time p, build a p(n) × p(n) frame where each row encodes one TM configuration (top = start on w, bottom = accepting config) and tile types encode the transition rules; the game is solvable iff M has an accepting p(n)-step computation, i.e. iff w ∈ A. Then Domino ≤ᴾ SAT ≤ᴾ 3SAT ≤ᴾ Clique. (Cook 1971 did SAT/3SAT directly.)"},{"q":"How does SAT ≤ᴾ 3SAT handle clauses with fewer than 3 or more than 3 literals?","a":"Pad short clauses by repeating a literal (ℓ₁∨ℓ₁∨ℓ₁; ℓ₁∨ℓ₂∨ℓ₂). Split a long clause ℓ₁∨...∨ℓ_k (k ≥ 4) into a chain with fresh variables: (ℓ₁∨ℓ₂∨z₁)∧(¬z₁∨ℓ₃∨z₂)∧...∧(¬z_{k−3}∨ℓ_{k−1}∨ℓ_k); the chain is satisfiable iff some ℓⱼ is true."},{"q":"Draw the book's final language-class hierarchy (Ch. 7 figure) and give a separating example at each proper step.","a":"regular ⊂ context-free ⊂ P ⊆ NP ⊆ decidable ⊂ enumerable ⊂ all languages. Witnesses: {aⁿbⁿ} CF but not regular; {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} decidable (even in P) but not CF; P vs NP open; every NP language is decidable; Halting Problem enumerable but not decidable; its complement not enumerable; all languages uncountable vs enumerable languages countable. Also: A decidable ⟺ A and Ā both enumerable."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"s0","title":"Sipser 0 · Intro & Proofs","slides":"Sipser ch. 0 · weeks 1–2","md":"# S0 — Introduction (Sipser, Chapter 0)\n\n> **Sipser companion · course weeks 1–2** — pairs with Book 1 (M&S); Sipser = deeper well of worked problems.\n\nSource: Sipser, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*, 3rd ed., Chapter 0 \"Introduction\" (PDF pages 25–54). These notes do NOT re-derive the basics already in B1 — they capture what Sipser adds: his terminology where it differs from M&S, his proof-writing advice, and every worked/solved problem.\n\n---\n\n## §0.1 Automata, computability, and complexity — Sipser's framing\n\nAll three areas answer one question, each with its own interpretation: **\"What are the fundamental capabilities and limitations of computers?\"**\n\n- **Complexity theory** — central question, verbatim: *\"What makes some problems computationally hard and others easy?\"* Remarkably, still unanswered. Sipser's example pair: sorting a million numbers (easy) vs. university timetable scheduling (hard — a thousand classes could take centuries). His \"periodic table\" analogy: complexity theory's classification scheme for problems is analogous to the periodic table classifying elements by chemical properties.\n- **Four options when facing a hard problem** (quiz-worthy list): (1) alter the problem so it becomes easier; (2) settle for an approximate solution; (3) accept worst-case slowness if it's fast most of the time; (4) use alternative computation types, e.g., randomized computation.\n- **Cryptography** — Sipser's applied example: the unusual field that *wants* hard problems (codes should be hard to break without the key).\n- **Computability theory** — Gödel, Turing, Church (1930s–40s) showed some basic problems can't be solved by computers at all; canonical example: determining whether a mathematical statement is true or false. Complexity classifies easy vs. hard; computability classifies **solvable vs. unsolvable**.\n- **Automata theory** — definitions and properties of mathematical models of computation. Finite automaton → text processing, compilers, hardware design; context-free grammar → programming languages, AI. It's the starting point because computability/complexity need a precise definition of \"computer\" first.\n\nSipser presents the three in reverse order (complexity → computability → automata) \"because by starting from the end you can better understand the reason for the beginning.\"\n\n---\n\n## §0.2 Mathematical notions — Sipser's terminology deltas vs. M&S\n\nOnly what differs or is added; the set/sequence basics match B1.\n\n### Sets and tuples\n- **N = {1, 2, 3, …}** — like M&S, Sipser's naturals start at 1 (relevant for induction bases).\n- **Multiset**: repetition counts — {7} and {7,7} differ as multisets, identical as sets.\n- **Singleton set** = one member; **unordered pair** = two members (Sipser's names).\n- **Proper subset**: A ⊊ B (subset and not equal).\n- Finite sequences = **tuples**; k elements = **k-tuple**; 2-tuple = **ordered pair**.\n- Cartesian product of a set with itself k times: shorthand **A^k**; e.g., N² = all ordered pairs of naturals.\n\n### Functions and relations — Sipser's distinctive treatment\n- Function = mapping; f(a) = b means \"f maps a to b.\"\n- **Domain** = set of possible inputs; **range** = set outputs come from. ⚠️ Sipser's \"range\" is what M&S/others call the *codomain* — f : D → R need not use all of R. A function that does use every element of the range is **onto**.\n- **k-ary function**, **arity** = number of arguments; k=1 **unary**, k=2 **binary**. **Infix** notation (a + b) vs. **prefix** (add(a, b)).\n- **Predicate / property** = function whose range is {TRUE, FALSE}.\n- **Relation** (Sipser's definition): a predicate whose domain is a set of k-tuples A × ··· × A — a \"k-ary relation on A.\" Binary relation aRb means aRb = TRUE. A predicate P : D → {TRUE, FALSE} may equivalently be given as the set S = {a ∈ D | P(a) = TRUE}.\n- **Equivalence relation** = reflexive (xRx), symmetric (xRy ⟹ yRx), transitive (xRy ∧ yRz ⟹ xRz).\n- Worked Example 0.10 — the relation **beats** in Scissors–Paper–Stone, as a TRUE/FALSE table and as the set {(SCISSORS, PAPER), (PAPER, STONE), (STONE, SCISSORS)}.\n- Worked Example 0.11 — **i ≡₇ j iff i − j is a multiple of 7** is an equivalence relation: reflexive (i − i = 0), symmetric (i − j multiple of 7 iff j − i is), transitive (i − k = (i − j) + (j − k), sum of two multiples of 7).\n- Modular arithmetic: **Zₘ = {0, 1, …, m−1}**; Example 0.8 is f(n) = n+1 mod 5 as f : Z₅ → Z₅; Example 0.9 is addition mod 4 as a 2-D table g : Z₄ × Z₄ → Z₄.\n\n### Graphs — Sipser's vocabulary (heavily reused in later chapters)\n- Nodes/vertices, edges; **degree** = number of edges at a node; **self-loop** allowed \"depending on the situation\"; at most one edge between any two nodes.\n- Formal description **G = (V, E)**; undirected edges written (i, j) = (j, i), or as sets {i, j}.\n- **Labeled graph**: nodes and/or edges carry labels (e.g., airfare costs).\n- **Subgraph**: G ⊆ H if G's nodes are a subset of H's nodes and G's edges are H's edges on those nodes.\n- **Path** = sequence of nodes connected by edges; **simple path** = no repeated nodes; **connected** = every two nodes have a path; **cycle** = path that starts and ends at the same node; **simple cycle** = at least three nodes, repeats only first and last; **tree** = connected with no simple cycles; root, **leaves** = degree-1 nodes other than the root.\n- Directed graph: **outdegree** / **indegree**; edge (i, j) points i → j; **directed path**; **strongly connected** = a directed path between every two nodes.\n- Key link: **a directed graph represents any binary relation** — for R on D × D, take G = (D, E) with E = {(x, y) | xRy} (Example 0.17 draws *beats* this way).\n\n### Strings and languages — the definitions the whole course runs on\n- **Alphabet Σ, Γ** = any nonempty finite set; members = **symbols**.\n- **String over Σ** = finite sequence of symbols from Σ; length |w|; **empty string ε** (\"plays the role of 0 in a number system\").\n- **Reverse** wᴿ; **substring** = appears consecutively (cad ⊑ abracadabra); **concatenation** xy; **xᵏ** = x concatenated with itself k times.\n- **Lexicographic order** = dictionary order; **shortlex / string order** = lexicographic except *shorter strings come first*: over {0,1} it's (ε, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10, 11, 000, …). (M&S doesn't name shortlex — Sipser uses it later for enumerators.)\n- **Prefix**: x is a prefix of y if ∃z with xz = y; **proper prefix** if additionally x ≠ y. A **language** is a set of strings; it is **prefix-free** if no member is a proper prefix of another member.\n\n### Boolean logic\n- Values 1/0; operations ¬ (NOT), ∧ (AND), ∨ (OR), ⊕ (XOR: 1 iff exactly one operand is 1), ↔ (equality: 1 iff operands equal), → (implication: 0 only for 1 → 0).\n- **All Boolean operations reduce to AND and NOT**:\n  - P ∨ Q = ¬(¬P ∧ ¬Q)\n  - P → Q = ¬P ∨ Q\n  - P ↔ Q = (P → Q) ∧ (Q → P)\n  - P ⊕ Q = ¬(P ↔ Q)\n- **Distributive laws** (both forms — the dual is easy to forget): P ∧ (Q ∨ R) = (P ∧ Q) ∨ (P ∧ R), and P ∨ (Q ∧ R) = (P ∨ Q) ∧ (P ∨ R).\n\n---\n\n## §0.3 Definitions, theorems, and proofs\n\n- \"Theorems and proofs are the heart and soul of mathematics and **definitions are its spirit**.\"\n- **Definition**: must make precise what constitutes the object and what does not. **Statement**: expresses that some object has a property; may be true or false but must be unambiguous. **Proof**: a *convincing logical argument* that a statement is true — \"airtight,\" beyond ANY doubt (contrast with a murder trial's \"beyond reasonable doubt\"; evidence plays no role in mathematical proof). **Theorem** = statement proved true, reserved for statements of special interest; **lemma** = proved statement whose interest is only in assisting a bigger proof; **corollary** = related statement that follows easily from a theorem or its proof.\n\n### Sipser's \"finding proofs\" strategy (his explicit checklist — exam-relevant)\n1. **Read the statement carefully**; rewrite in your own words; break multipart statements into parts.\n   - **\"P iff Q\"** = two proofs: forward direction P ⟹ Q (\"P only if Q\") + reverse direction P ⟸ Q (\"P if Q\"). One direction is often easier.\n   - **Set equality A = B** = two proofs: A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A (every member of each is in the other).\n2. **Get a gut feeling**: experiment with examples; try to find a **counterexample** — if the statement is true you'll fail, and *where you get stuck reveals why it's true*.\n3. **Try something easier**: prove a special case first (k = 1, then k = 2, …), or a special case of the special case.\n4. **Write it up properly**: a well-written proof is a sequence of statements, each following by simple reasoning from previous ones.\n\n### Sipser's four proof-writing tips (verbatim headers)\n- **Be patient** — finding proofs takes time; researchers can spend weeks or years on one.\n- **Come back to it** — leave it and return later; let the unconscious, intuitive mind work.\n- **Be neat** — simple, clear pictures/text; \"sloppiness gets in the way of insight.\"\n- **Be concise** — brevity expresses high-level ideas; good notation helps; but include enough reasoning for the reader.\n\n### Worked proof — Theorem 0.20 (a DeMorgan law): complement(A ∪ B) = complement(A) ∩ complement(B)\nMethod: mutual inclusion. (⊆) If x ∈ complement(A ∪ B), then x ∉ A ∪ B, so x is in neither A nor B, so x ∈ complement(A) and x ∈ complement(B), hence x ∈ complement(A) ∩ complement(B). (⊇) If x ∈ complement(A) ∩ complement(B), x is in both complements, so x ∉ A and x ∉ B, so x ∉ A ∪ B, hence x ∈ complement(A ∪ B). ∎\n\n### Worked proof — Theorem 0.21 (handshake): in every graph, the sum of node degrees is even\nEvery edge connects two nodes and contributes 1 to each endpoint's degree, hence 2 to the total sum. With e edges the sum is 2e — even. ∎ (Sipser first demos his \"experiment, then hunt for a counterexample\" method on this statement in Example 0.19.)\n\n---\n\n## §0.4 Types of proof\n\n### Proof by construction — Theorem 0.22\nMany theorems assert an object *exists*; prove it by building the object. **Definition**: a graph is **k-regular** if every node has degree k.\n\n**Theorem 0.22.** For each even n > 2, there exists a 3-regular graph with n nodes.\n**Proof (construction).** V = {0, 1, …, n−1};\nE = {{i, i+1} | 0 ≤ i ≤ n−2} ∪ {{n−1, 0}} ∪ {{i, i + n/2} | 0 ≤ i ≤ n/2 − 1}.\nPicture the nodes around a circle: the first two pieces form the ring (each node gets 2 edges), the third adds a \"diameter\" edge to the opposite node (+1 edge). Every node has degree 3. ∎ (Evenness of n is what makes the opposite-node pairing work.)\n\n### Proof by contradiction — Theorem 0.24\nAssume the theorem false; derive an obviously false consequence. Everyday version (Example 0.23): Jill comes in from outdoors completely dry ⟹ it isn't raining, because if it were raining she'd be wet.\n\n**Theorem 0.24.** √2 is irrational. (Rational = ratio m/n of integers; irrational = not rational.)\n**Proof.** Assume √2 = m/n with m, n integers, reduced so that they are **not both even** (divide out the largest common divisor — at least one of m, n is now odd). Multiply by n: n√2 = m. Square: 2n² = m². So m² is even ⟹ m is even (odd² is odd). Write m = 2k: 2n² = 4k² ⟹ n² = 2k² ⟹ n² even ⟹ n even. Now both m and n are even — contradicting the reduction. ∎\n*The load-bearing step students forget: reducing the fraction FIRST so \"both even\" is contradictory.*\n\n### Proof by induction — with Sipser's exact format\nGoal: property P holds for every natural number. Two parts, each a proof on its own:\n- **Basis:** prove P(1) (or P(b) for any starting value b — then P(k) holds for all k ≥ b).\n- **Induction step:** for each i ≥ 1, assume P(i) (**the induction hypothesis**) and prove P(i+1).\n- **Strong induction variant**: assume P(j) for every j ≤ i — still valid, since all earlier cases are already proved by the time you need them.\n\n**Worked induction — Theorem 0.25 (mortgage/bank-loan formula).** Setup: P = principal, I = yearly interest rate, Y = monthly payment, M = 1 + I/12 (monthly multiplier), Pₜ = amount owed after t months; P₀ = P and Pₜ₊₁ = M·Pₜ − Y.\n\n**Theorem.** For each t ≥ 0:  Pₜ = P·Mᵗ − Y·(Mᵗ − 1)/(M − 1).\n**Proof.**\n- *Basis (t = 0):* M⁰ = 1, so formula gives P₀ = P·1 − Y·0 = P. ✓\n- *Induction step:* assume the formula for t = k. Then\n  Pₖ₊₁ = Pₖ·M − Y = [P·Mᵏ − Y·(Mᵏ−1)/(M−1)]·M − Y\n  = P·Mᵏ⁺¹ − Y·(Mᵏ⁺¹−M)/(M−1) − Y·(M−1)/(M−1)\n  = P·Mᵏ⁺¹ − Y·(Mᵏ⁺¹−1)/(M−1). ∎\n(The trick: rewrite the trailing −Y as −Y·(M−1)/(M−1) so the fractions merge.)\n\n---\n\n## Exercises and problems (the full list, for practice targeting)\n\n- **0.1–0.3**: read/write formal set descriptions; subsets, ∪, ∩, ×, power set for A = {x,y,z}, B = {x,y}.\n- **0.4**: |A × B| = ab (each of a choices pairs with each of b choices).\n- **0.5**: |P(C)| = 2ᶜ (each element independently in or out).\n- **0.6**: read function tables (unary f : X → Y, binary g : X × Y → Y); compose g(4, f(4)).\n- **0.7**: exhibit relations that are (a) reflexive+symmetric but not transitive, (b) reflexive+transitive but not symmetric, (c) symmetric+transitive but not reflexive.\n- **0.8–0.9**: draw a graph from V, E; write a formal description from a drawing.\n- **0.10 (fallacy)**: the \"2 = 1 proof\" — error: with a = b, the step *divide by (a − b)* divides by zero.\n- **0.11**: prove by induction S(n) = ½n(n+1) and C(n) = ¼n²(n+1)², hence C(n) = S(n)² — sum of first n cubes equals square of sum of first n naturals.\n- **0.12 (fallacy)**: \"all horses are the same color\" — error: the induction step fails for k = 1 → 2, because H₁ and H₂ share no horse, so there's no common horse to link their colors.\n- **0.13**: every graph with ≥ 2 nodes has two nodes of equal degree. (Pigeonhole: n nodes have degrees in {0, …, n−1}, but degree 0 and degree n−1 can't coexist, leaving n−1 possible values for n nodes.)\n- **0.14⋆, 0.15**: solved in the book — below.\n\n## Solved problems from the book\n\nSipser's own Selected Solutions (pp. 28), transcribed faithfully.\n\n### Problem 0.14 (Ramsey's theorem)\n*Statement.* A **clique** in G is a subgraph in which every two nodes are connected by an edge; an **anti-clique** (independent set) is a subgraph in which every two nodes are NOT connected. Show every graph with n nodes contains either a clique or an anti-clique with at least ½·log₂ n nodes.\n\n*Sipser's solution.* Make space for two piles of nodes, A and B. Starting with the entire graph, repeatedly take a remaining node x: add x to **A** if its degree is more than half the number of remaining nodes, otherwise add x to **B**. Then, if x went to A, discard all remaining nodes x is NOT connected to; if x went to B, discard all nodes x IS connected to. Continue until no nodes are left. At most half the nodes are discarded at each step, so at least log₂ n steps occur before the process terminates. Each step adds one node to a pile, so one pile ends with at least ½·log₂ n nodes. The A pile is a clique (every A-node kept only neighbors afterward, so all later A-nodes are its neighbors); the B pile is an anti-clique (same reasoning with non-neighbors). ∎\n\n### Problem 0.15 (monthly payment from Theorem 0.25)\n*Statement.* Derive a formula for the monthly payment Y in terms of P, I, t, assuming the loan reaches 0 after t payments; then compute the payment for a $100,000, 30-year (t = 360) mortgage at 5% annual interest.\n\n*Sipser's solution.* Set Pₜ = 0 in Theorem 0.25 and solve for Y:\n**Y = P·Mᵗ·(M − 1) / (Mᵗ − 1).**\nFor P = $100,000, I = 0.05, t = 360: M = 1 + 0.05/12, giving **Y ≈ $536.82** per month. ∎\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"What single question links Sipser's three areas (automata, computability, complexity), and how does each area interpret it?","a":"\"What are the fundamental capabilities and limitations of computers?\" Complexity: what makes problems easy vs. hard; computability: which problems are solvable vs. unsolvable at all; automata: precise mathematical models of computation (the needed definition of \"computer\")."},{"q":"Sipser lists four options when you face a computationally hard problem. Name them.","a":"(1) Alter the problem so it's more easily solvable; (2) settle for an approximate solution; (3) accept occasional worst-case slowness if it's usually fast; (4) use alternative computation types such as randomized computation."},{"q":"How does Sipser's use of \"range\" differ from the usual codomain/range distinction, and what does he call a function using all of its range?","a":"Sipser's \"range\" is the set outputs come FROM (i.e., the codomain) — f need not hit every element (abs : Z → Z never outputs −1). A function that does use every element of the range is **onto**."},{"q":"Define relation, k-ary relation, and predicate in Sipser's terms.","a":"A predicate (or property) is a function with range {TRUE, FALSE}. A relation is a predicate whose domain is a set of k-tuples A × ··· × A (a k-ary relation on A); aRb means aRb = TRUE; it can equivalently be written as the set of tuples mapping to TRUE."},{"q":"What is shortlex (string) order, and what are the first seven strings over {0,1} in it?","a":"Lexicographic order modified so shorter strings precede longer ones: ε, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10, 11, …"},{"q":"Define prefix, proper prefix, and prefix-free language.","a":"x is a prefix of y if xz = y for some string z; proper if also x ≠ y. A language is prefix-free if no member is a proper prefix of another member."},{"q":"Sipser: simple cycle, tree, leaves — definitions?","a":"A simple cycle contains at least three nodes and repeats only the first and last node. A tree is a connected graph with no simple cycles. Leaves are the degree-1 nodes of a tree other than the root."},{"q":"How does a directed graph represent an arbitrary binary relation R on D?","a":"G = (D, E) with E = {(x, y) | xRy} — one arrow per TRUE pair (e.g., the graph of *beats* in Scissors–Paper–Stone)."},{"q":"Express ∨, →, ↔, ⊕ using only AND and NOT.","a":"P∨Q = ¬(¬P ∧ ¬Q); P→Q = ¬P ∨ Q; P↔Q = (P→Q) ∧ (Q→P); P⊕Q = ¬(P↔Q)."},{"q":"State Sipser's two standard decompositions of multipart statements to prove.","a":"\"P iff Q\" = prove the forward direction (P ⟹ Q) and the reverse direction (Q ⟹ P). \"A = B\" for sets = prove A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A."},{"q":"Sipser's four tips for producing a proof?","a":"Be patient (proofs take time); come back to it (let intuition work); be neat (sloppiness blocks insight); be concise (brevity + good notation, but keep enough reasoning for the reader)."},{"q":"In the proof that √2 is irrational, which early step makes the final contradiction possible?","a":"Reducing m/n first so m and n are not both even (at least one odd). Then deriving \"m even and n even\" from 2n² = m² contradicts the reduction."},{"q":"Sketch the construction proving a 3-regular graph exists for every even n > 2 (Theorem 0.22).","a":"Nodes 0…n−1 on a circle; edges = the ring ({i, i+1} plus {n−1, 0}) giving degree 2, plus a diameter edge {i, i + n/2} for each i < n/2 giving each node one more — degree 3 everywhere (evenness makes opposite pairing work)."},{"q":"Where exactly does the \"all horses are the same color\" induction fail?","a":"The step from k = 1 to k = 2: the two sets H₁ and H₂ (each formed by removing one horse from a 2-horse set) share no horse, so nothing links their colors; the overlap argument needs k ≥ 2."},{"q":"Outline Sipser's pile argument for Ramsey's theorem (Problem 0.14).","a":"Repeatedly take a node x: pile A if its degree exceeds half the remaining nodes (then discard its non-neighbors), else pile B (discard its neighbors). Each step discards at most half, so ≥ log₂ n steps run; one pile gets ≥ ½·log₂ n nodes; A is a clique, B an anti-clique."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"s1","title":"Sipser 1 · Regular Languages","slides":"Sipser ch. 1 · weeks 3–6","md":"# S1 — Regular Languages (Sipser, Chapter 1)\n\n**Sipser companion · course weeks 3–6** — pairs with Book 2 (M&S); Quiz 1 (wk5) & Tutorial Quiz 1 (wk7) territory.\n\nSource: Sipser, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*, 3rd ed., Chapter 1 (PDF pages 55–124, book pages 31–99).\n\n**What Sipser adds over M&S ch.2:** (1) the \"pretend to be the automaton\" design methodology, made explicit and used everywhere; (2) the **GNFA rip-out method** for DFA → regex (M&S solves equations instead — the professor may use either, know both); (3) a richer pumping-lemma toolkit: five worked nonregularity proofs including two where **condition 3 (|xy| ≤ p) is the whole trick** and one where you must **pump down (i = 0)**; (4) a large set of solved exercises/problems, including the Myhill–Nerode theorem and \"minimum pumping length.\"\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 Finite automata\n\n### Motivating example: automatic door controller\n\nStates {OPEN, CLOSED}; inputs {FRONT, REAR, BOTH, NEITHER}. CLOSED goes to OPEN only on FRONT (BOTH keeps it closed — opening would knock over the person on the rear pad); OPEN stays open on FRONT/REAR/BOTH and closes on NEITHER. Moral: a computer with **one bit of memory**. (Same role as M&S's toll-gate example.)\n\n### Formal definition (Definition 1.5, verbatim)\n\nA **finite automaton** is a 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F), where\n\n1. Q is a finite set called the **states**,\n2. Σ is a finite set called the **alphabet**,\n3. δ : Q × Σ → Q is the **transition function**,\n4. q₀ ∈ Q is the **start state**, and\n5. F ⊆ Q is the set of **accept states** (sometimes called final states).\n\nTwo consequences Sipser highlights: F = ∅ is allowed (0 accept states is fine), and δ being a *function* means **exactly one** transition arrow leaves every state for every symbol.\n\nIf A is the set of all strings M accepts, A is the **language of M**, L(M) = A; M **recognizes** A. A machine accepts many strings but recognizes exactly **one** language; if it accepts nothing, it recognizes ∅.\n\n### Formal definition of computation\n\nM accepts w = w₁w₂⋯wₙ if a sequence of states r₀, r₁, …, rₙ exists with\n1. r₀ = q₀,\n2. δ(rᵢ, wᵢ₊₁) = rᵢ₊₁ for i = 0, …, n−1, and\n3. rₙ ∈ F.\n\n**Definition 1.16.** A language is called a **regular language** if some finite automaton recognizes it.\n\n### The example machines M1–M5\n\n- **M1** (3 states q1,q2,q3; start q1, F = {q2}; δ: q1 —0→ q1, q1 —1→ q2, q2 —0→ q3, q2 —1→ q2, q3 —0→ q2, q3 —1→ q2). L(M1) = {w | w contains at least one 1 and an even number of 0s follow the last 1}. (Identical to M&S's opening machine.) Trace of 1101: q1→q2→q2→q3→q2, accept.\n- **M2** (2 states, F = {q2}; flip to q2 on 1, to q1 on 0): L(M2) = {w | w ends in a 1}.\n- **M3** = M2 with the *other* state accepting (F = {q1}): L(M3) = {w | w is ε or ends in a 0}. Key point: **the start state being an accept state is exactly what makes ε accepted**.\n- **M4** (5 states s, q1, q2, r1, r2 over Σ = {a,b}; two accept states q1 and r1): after the first symbol it commits left (first symbol a) or right (first symbol b) and can never return to s. Accepts strings that **start and end with the same symbol** (a, b, aa, bb, bab yes; ab, ba, bbba no).\n- **M5** (3 states over Σ = {⟨RESET⟩, 0, 1, 2}): keeps the running sum of numeric symbols **mod 3**; ⟨RESET⟩ jumps back to q0; accepts iff sum ≡ 0 (mod 3). Example 1.17 traces w = 10⟨RESET⟩22⟨RESET⟩012 through q0,q1,q1,q0,q2,q1,q0,q0,q1,q0 — accepted.\n- **Example 1.15 (Bᵢ):** generalization to \"sum ≡ 0 mod i\": Qᵢ = {q0,…,q_{i−1}}, δᵢ(qⱼ, 0) = qⱼ, δᵢ(qⱼ, 1) = q_{j+1 mod i}, δᵢ(qⱼ, 2) = q_{j+2 mod i}, δᵢ(qⱼ, ⟨RESET⟩) = q0; start/accept q0. Shows why **formal descriptions** matter: the diagram depends on a parameter i, so you can't draw it.\n\n### Designing finite automata — Sipser's methodology (exam gold)\n\n> \"Put yourself in the place of the machine you are trying to design… **Pretending that you are the automaton** is a psychological trick that helps engage your whole mind in the design process.\"\n\nThe recipe:\n1. You receive symbols **one at a time** and never know when the string ends — so **after every symbol you must be ready to answer** accept/reject.\n2. Ask: **what do I need to remember?** You have finite memory (imagine input \"from here to the moon\"), so you cannot remember everything — only the *crucial* information, expressed as a **finite list of possibilities**.\n3. **One state per possibility.**\n4. Transitions = how reading a symbol moves you between possibilities.\n5. Start state = the possibility for having seen ε; accept states = the possibilities where you'd say yes.\n\n**Worked design E1** (odd number of 1s): possibilities = {even so far, odd so far} → states q_even, q_odd; on 1 flip, on 0 stay; start q_even (0 is even); F = {q_odd}.\n\n**Worked design E2 (Example 1.21, substring 001):** possibilities = haven't seen any of the pattern / just seen 0 / just seen 00 / have seen 001 → states q, q0, q00, q001. δ: q on 1 stays, on 0 → q0; q0 on 1 back to q, on 0 → q00; q00 on 0 stays, on 1 → q001; q001 loops on everything (trap accept). Start q, F = {q001}. (M&S does 101; same idea.)\n\n### The regular operations (Definition 1.23)\n\n- **Union:** A ∪ B = {x | x ∈ A or x ∈ B}.\n- **Concatenation:** A ∘ B = {xy | x ∈ A and y ∈ B}.\n- **Star:** A* = {x₁x₂…x_k | k ≥ 0 and each xᵢ ∈ A} (unary; k = 0 allowed, so **ε ∈ A\\* always**).\n\nExample 1.24: A = {good, bad}, B = {boy, girl}: A ∪ B = {good, bad, boy, girl}; A∘B = {goodboy, goodgirl, badboy, badgirl}; A* = {ε, good, bad, goodgood, goodbad, …}.\n\n**Theorem 1.25 (union, product construction).** Regular languages are closed under ∪. Construction from DFAs M1, M2: Q = Q1 × Q2 (pairs of states — \"simulate both machines at once, one finger in each\"), δ((r1,r2), a) = (δ1(r1,a), δ2(r2,a)), start (q1, q2), F = {(r1,r2) | r1 ∈ F1 **or** r2 ∈ F2} = (F1×Q2) ∪ (Q1×F2). **Footnote 3 (heavily used later):** taking F = F1 × F2 instead (\"and\") proves closure under **intersection**.\n\n**Theorem 1.26 (concatenation)** is *stated* here but the DFA attempt fails: M doesn't know **where to break the input**. This is Sipser's motivation for nondeterminism.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Nondeterminism\n\nDFA vs NFA differences: an NFA state may have **zero, one, or many** exiting arrows per symbol, and arrows may be labeled **ε**. How an NFA computes (three equivalent mental models):\n\n1. **Splitting copies:** at a choice point the machine splits into copies following all possibilities in parallel; a copy with no arrow for the next symbol **dies**; ε-arrows cause splits without reading input; the NFA accepts iff **any** copy ends in an accept state.\n2. **Tree of possibilities:** accept iff at least one branch ends accepting.\n3. **Fingers on states** (this is how you run one by hand): keep a finger on every currently-possible state; per symbol, move/split/remove fingers, then chase ε-arrows; merge duplicate fingers.\n\nWorked trace: N1 (4 states; q1 loops on 0,1 and on 1 also goes to q2; q2 —0→ q3 and —ε→ q3; q3 —1→ q4; q4 loops on 0,1; F = {q4}) on 010110 ends with fingers on {q1, q3, q4} → accept. On 010 ends on {q1, q3} → reject. L(N1) = {w | w contains 101 or 11 as a substring}.\n\n### Formal definition (Definition 1.37)\n\nP(Q) = power set of Q; Σ_ε = Σ ∪ {ε}. An **NFA** is a 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) where δ : Q × Σ_ε → **P(Q)**. Computation: N accepts w if w can be written as y₁y₂⋯y_m with each yᵢ ∈ Σ_ε and states r₀,…,r_m exist with r₀ = q₀, rᵢ₊₁ ∈ δ(rᵢ, yᵢ₊₁), r_m ∈ F. (Note: the *input is rewritten* to interleave ε's — that's how ε-moves enter the formal definition.)\n\n### Example NFAs\n\n- **N2 (Example 1.30):** {w | 1 in the third position from the end}. 4 states: loop on q1, then \"guess\" you're 3 from the end on a 1, then two check states. The smallest DFA needs **8 states** (M&S builds it: remember the last 3 symbols). \"Guess, then check\" is the canonical NFA idiom.\n- **N3 (Example 1.33, unary alphabet {0}):** ε-branch from the start into a 2-cycle or a 3-cycle: accepts {0^k | k a multiple of 2 or 3}. Shows ε-arrows = \"choose which sub-machine to run.\"\n- **N4 (Example 1.35):** 3 states {1,2,3}, start 1, F = {1}; arrows: 1 —b→ 2, 1 —ε→ 3, 2 —a→ {2,3}, 2 —b→ 3, 3 —a→ 1. Accepts ε, a, baba, baa; rejects b, bb, babba. Used below for subset construction.\n\n### Theorem 1.39: every NFA has an equivalent DFA (subset construction)\n\n**Proof idea:** simulate the NFA as the finger game — the DFA only needs to remember **the set of states with fingers on them**; k NFA states → 2^k subsets → the DFA has 2^k states.\n\n**Construction** (N = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F), first ignoring ε-arrows): DFA M = (Q′, Σ, δ′, q₀′, F′) with\n1. Q′ = P(Q);\n2. δ′(R, a) = {q ∈ Q | q ∈ δ(r, a) for some r ∈ R} = ⋃_{r∈R} δ(r, a);\n3. q₀′ = {q₀};\n4. F′ = {R ∈ Q′ | R contains an accept state of N}.\n\n**ε-arrows:** define E(R) = {q | q reachable from R along 0 or more ε-arrows} (includes R itself). Then replace δ′(R,a) by {q | q ∈ E(δ(r,a)) for some r ∈ R} and the start state by **q₀′ = E({q₀})**. (Following ε's *before* each symbol instead also works.)\n\n**Corollary 1.40.** A language is regular **iff** some NFA recognizes it.\n\n**Worked conversion (Example 1.41, N4 above):** D has 8 subset-states; start state E({1}) = **{1,3}** (don't forget the ε-arrow!); accept states = subsets containing 1: {1}, {1,2}, {1,3}, {1,2,3}. Sample transitions: {2} —a→ {2,3}, {2} —b→ {3}; {1} —a→ **∅**, {1} —b→ {2}; {3} —a→ {1,3} (3 →a→ 1 →ε→ 3), {3} —b→ ∅; {1,2} —a→ {2,3}, {1,2} —b→ {2,3}. States {1} and {1,2} have no incoming arrows and can be deleted. **Exam tips embedded here:** ∅ is a legitimate (dead) DFA state; unreachable subset-states may be pruned; in practice only build subsets reachable from the start.\n\n### Closure under the regular operations, NFA-style (the three pictures)\n\n- **Union (Thm 1.45):** new start state q₀ with **ε-arrows to both old start states**; Q = {q₀} ∪ Q1 ∪ Q2; F = F1 ∪ F2. (\"Guess which machine will accept.\")\n- **Concatenation (Thm 1.47):** start state = N1's start; add **ε-arrows from every accept state of N1 to N2's start state**; F = F2 **only** (N1's accept states lose accept status). (\"Guess where to split the input.\") δ formally: δ1(q,a) for q ∈ Q1∖F1; for q ∈ F1, δ1(q,a) when a ≠ ε and δ1(q,ε) ∪ {q2} when a = ε; δ2(q,a) for q ∈ Q2.\n- **Star (Thm 1.49):** add ε-arrows from old accept states back to the old start state, **plus a NEW start state q₀ that is also an accept state, with an ε-arrow to the old start**. Q = {q₀} ∪ Q1, F = {q₀} ∪ F1. **Why the new state:** just making the old start accepting can accept extra junk (a string that returns to the start mid-way without completing a piece) — Exercise 1.15 asks for a counterexample.\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Regular expressions\n\nPrecedence: **star, then concatenation, then union** (like ×, + in arithmetic; ∘ usually implicit). If Σ = {0,1}, \"Σ\" abbreviates (0 ∪ 1).\n\n### Definition 1.52 (inductive)\n\nR is a **regular expression** if R is\n1. a, for some a ∈ Σ (language {a}),\n2. ε (language {ε}),\n3. ∅ (the empty language),\n4. (R₁ ∪ R₂), 5. (R₁ ∘ R₂), or 6. (R₁*),\nwhere R₁, R₂ are (smaller) regular expressions — an **inductive definition**, not circular.\n\nShorthands: R⁺ = RR* (one or more pieces; R⁺ ∪ ε = R*); Rᵏ = k copies of R. L(R) = the language R describes. **Don't confuse ε (language {ε}) with ∅ (no strings at all).**\n\n### Example 1.53 — the regex phrasebook (Σ = {0,1})\n\n1. 0\\*10\\* = {w | w contains a single 1}\n2. Σ\\*1Σ\\* = {w | w has at least one 1}\n3. Σ\\*001Σ\\* = {w | w contains 001 as a substring}\n4. 1\\*(01⁺)\\* = {w | every 0 in w is followed by at least one 1}\n5. (ΣΣ)\\* = {w | w has even length}\n6. (ΣΣΣ)\\* = {w | |w| is a multiple of 3}\n7. 01 ∪ 10 = {01, 10}\n8. 0Σ\\*0 ∪ 1Σ\\*1 ∪ 0 ∪ 1 = {w | w starts and ends with the same symbol} (note the lone 0 and 1 for length-1 strings!)\n9. (0 ∪ ε)1\\* = 01\\* ∪ 1\\*\n10. (0 ∪ ε)(1 ∪ ε) = {ε, 0, 1, 01}\n11. **1\\*∅ = ∅** (concatenating ∅ with anything gives ∅)\n12. **∅\\* = {ε}** (star can pick 0 strings)\n\nIdentities: R ∪ ∅ = R and R ∘ ε = R always; but R ∪ ε ≠ R in general (R = 0) and R ∘ ∅ ≠ R in general (R ∘ ∅ = ∅). Application note: compiler tokens, e.g. numeric constants = (+ ∪ - ∪ ε)(D⁺ ∪ D⁺.D\\* ∪ D\\*.D⁺).\n\n### Theorem 1.54. A language is regular **iff** some regular expression describes it.\n\n**Lemma 1.55 (regex → NFA, the easy direction).** Structural induction on R:\n- R = a: two states, one a-arrow (an NFA, not a DFA — missing arrows are fine).\n- R = ε: single accepting start state, no arrows.\n- R = ∅: single non-accepting state, no arrows.\n- R = R₁ ∪ R₂ / R₁∘R₂ / R₁*: apply the closure constructions of Theorems 1.45/1.47/1.49 to the NFAs for the subexpressions.\n\nWorked: (ab ∪ a)\\* is built bottom-up a → b → ab → ab ∪ a → (ab ∪ a)\\*, giving an 8-state NFA (the minimal equivalent NFA has 2 states — the procedure is **not** state-optimal). Similarly (a ∪ b)\\*aba (Example 1.58).\n\n**Lemma 1.60 (DFA → regex, the GNFA method — SIPSER'S SIGNATURE MOVE; M&S uses equation-solving instead).**\n\nA **GNFA** (generalized NFA) has **regular expressions as arrow labels**; it consumes a whole block of input matching the label to cross an arrow. **Special form** required:\n- the start state has arrows **to** every other state but **no incoming** arrows;\n- there is a **single accept state** (≠ start) with arrows **in from** every other state and **no outgoing** arrows;\n- between every ordered pair of the remaining states (including self-loops) there is **exactly one** arrow.\n\n**Definition 1.64.** GNFA = (Q, Σ, δ, q_start, q_accept) with δ : (Q − {q_accept}) × (Q − {q_start}) → R (all regexes over Σ). It accepts w if w = w₁⋯w_k with each wᵢ ∈ Σ\\* and a state sequence q_start = q₀, q₁, …, q_k = q_accept exists with wᵢ ∈ L(δ(qᵢ₋₁, qᵢ)).\n\n**Step 1: DFA → special-form GNFA.** Add new start state with ε-arrow to the old start; add new accept state with ε-arrows **from all old accept states**; merge parallel arrows into one labeled with the **union** of their labels (e.g. a self-loop \"a, b\" becomes label a ∪ b); add ∅-labeled arrows wherever an arrow is missing (harmless — ∅ can never be crossed; in practice, don't draw them).\n\n**Step 2: rip states out one at a time.** While k > 2, pick any q_rip ∉ {q_start, q_accept} and delete it; then repair **every** pair (qᵢ, qⱼ) with qᵢ ≠ q_accept, qⱼ ≠ q_start (**including qᵢ = qⱼ**): if the old labels were\n\n- R₁ on qᵢ → q_rip, R₂ on q_rip → q_rip (self-loop), R₃ on q_rip → qⱼ, R₄ on qᵢ → qⱼ,\n\nthen the new label is\n\n> **(R₁)(R₂)\\*(R₃) ∪ (R₄)**\n\n**Step 3:** when only 2 states remain, the single arrow q_start → q_accept is labeled with the answer.\n\nCONVERT(G) written as a recursive procedure; **Claim 1.65** proves it correct by induction on k (base k = 2 trivial; inductive step: any accepting run of G either avoids q_rip — then the \"∪ (R₄)\" part covers it — or its runs through q_rip are covered by (R₁)(R₂)\\*(R₃)). Sipser flags this as the model of a **construction proved correct by induction**.\n\n**Worked (Example 1.66, 2-state DFA: state 1 —a→ 1, 1 —b→ 2, 2 —a,b→ 2, F = {2}):** add s, a(ccept); label the 2-self-loop **a ∪ b** (union, not \"a,b\"). Rip state 2: new arrow 1 → accept gets (b)(a ∪ b)\\*(ε) ∪ ∅, simplified to **b(a ∪ b)\\***. Rip state 1: final answer **a\\*b(a ∪ b)\\***. Simplification rules used constantly: R∅ = ∅, R ∪ ∅ = R, Rε = R. Example 1.68 does a 3-state DFA the same way (rip order is your choice; different orders give different-looking but equivalent regexes).\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Nonregular languages\n\nWarm-up warning: intuition (\"this needs unbounded counting\") is **not a proof**. Over {0,1}: C = {w | equal number of 0s and 1s} is not regular, but **D = {w | equal number of occurrences of 01 and 10 as substrings} IS regular** (Problem 1.48; e.g. 101 ∈ D, 1010 ∉ D — D is exactly \"starts and ends with the same symbol\", plus short strings).\n\n### Theorem 1.70 — Pumping lemma (EXACT statement)\n\n> If A is a regular language, then there is a number p (the **pumping length**) where if s is any string in A of length at least p, then s may be divided into three pieces, s = xyz, satisfying the following conditions:\n> 1. for each i ≥ 0, x yⁱ z ∈ A,\n> 2. |y| > 0, and\n> 3. |xy| ≤ p.\n\nNotes: x or z may be ε but condition 2 forces y ≠ ε (without it the theorem is trivially true); i = 0 is allowed (**pumping down**); condition 3 is \"an extra technical condition that we occasionally find useful\" — see Examples 1.74/1.75.\n\n**Proof idea:** Let M be a DFA for A with p states, and take p = |Q|. A string s ∈ A with |s| = n ≥ p induces a state sequence of length n + 1 > p, so by the **pigeonhole principle** some state repeats — and if we take the **first** repetition, it occurs within the first p + 1 states, giving |xy| ≤ p. Cut s at the two visits of the repeated state: x = before first visit, y = the loop between visits, z = the rest. Then x yⁱ z just traverses the loop i times and still ends in the same accept state (i = 0 skips the loop). |y| > 0 since the two visits are distinct positions. *(Formal proof: states r₁,…,r_{n+1}; among the first p+1 pick rⱼ = r_l with j < l ≤ p+1; x = s₁⋯s_{j−1}, y = sⱼ⋯s_{l−1}, z = s_l⋯s_n.)*\n\n### Recipe for a nonregularity proof (Sipser's own summary)\n\n1. Assume B regular; the lemma gives a pumping length p.\n2. **You choose** a \"cannot-be-pumped\" string s ∈ B with |s| ≥ p (this is the creative step — pick one exhibiting the *essence* of B's nonregularity; expect to try several candidates).\n3. The adversary chooses the split s = xyz subject to conditions 2 and 3 — so **you must handle every legal split**, often by cases (use condition 3 to kill cases when convenient).\n4. Exhibit some i with x yⁱ z ∉ B. Contradiction; B is not regular.\n\n### The five worked examples (know all five)\n\n**Example 1.73 — B = {0ⁿ1ⁿ | n ≥ 0}.** s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ. Three cases on y: (1) all 0s → xyyz has more 0s than 1s ∉ B; (2) all 1s → symmetric contradiction; (3) mixed 0s and 1s → xyyz may balance counts but has **1s before 0s**, out of order ∉ B. (Shortcut: condition 3 forces case 1 only.) Any s ∈ B of length ≥ p works here — the easy example.\n\n**Example 1.74 — C = {w | equal number of 0s and 1s}: the |xy| ≤ p showcase.** Same s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ. **Without condition 3 the proof fails:** x = z = ε, y = 0ᵖ1ᵖ pumps fine within C. With condition 3, y lies within the first p symbols, so **y is all 0s**, and xyyz has more 0s than 1s ∉ C. Also a trap: choosing s = (01)ᵖ fails entirely — x = ε, y = 01, z = (01)^{p−1} pumps legally. (\"If you fail on your first attempt to find a string that cannot be pumped, don't despair. Try another one!\") **Alternative proof via closure:** if C were regular, C ∩ 0\\*1\\* = B would be regular (intersection closure, footnote 3) — contradiction with 1.73. This closure trick is often faster than pumping.\n\n**Example 1.75 — F = {ww | w ∈ {0,1}\\*}.** s = **0ᵖ10ᵖ1** (not 0ᵖ0ᵖ, which IS pumpable — it fails to exhibit the essence). Condition 3 again is crucial: y is all 0s within the first block, so xyyz = 0^{p+|y|}10ᵖ1 ∉ F.\n\n**Example 1.76 — D = {1^{n²} | n ≥ 0} (unary, perfect-square lengths).** s = 1^{p²}. Perfect squares have growing gaps. By condition 3, |y| ≤ |xy| ≤ p, and by condition 2, |y| > 0. So p² = |xyz| < |xy²z| ≤ p² + p < p² + 2p + 1 = (p+1)². The length of xy²z is **strictly between consecutive squares p² and (p+1)²**, hence not a square: xy²z ∉ D.\n\n**Example 1.77 — E = {0ⁱ1ʲ | i > j}: pumping DOWN.** s = 0^{p+1}1ᵖ. Condition 3 ⇒ y is all 0s. Pumping **up** fails to contradict (more 0s keeps i > j!). Instead take **i = 0**: xz removes at least one 0, but s had only one more 0 than 1s, so xz has at most as many 0s as 1s — xz ∉ E. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n## Solved problems from the book\n\nSipser marks solved items with a superscript A. Faithful transcriptions of the textual solutions (diagram-only solutions summarized):\n\n**1.1 / 1.2 (reading state diagrams — solutions given as data).** For the pictured M1: (a) start state q1; (b) F = {q2}; (c) on aabb the state sequence is q1, q2, q3, q1, q1; (d) aabb not accepted; (e) ε not accepted. For M2: (a) q1; (b) F = {q1, q4}; (c) q1, q1, q1, q2, q4; (d) aabb accepted; (e) ε accepted. Formal descriptions: M1 = ({q1,q2,q3}, {a,b}, δ1, q1, {q2}) with δ1: q1→(a: q2, b: q1), q2→(a: q3, b: q3), q3→(a: q2, b: q1); M2 = ({q1,q2,q3,q4}, {a,b}, δ2, q1, {q1,q4}) with δ2: q1→(a: q1, b: q2), q2→(a: q3, b: q4), q3→(a: q2, b: q1), q4→(a: q3, b: q4).\n\n**1.4(b), (d) (intersection construction).** (b) {w | exactly two a's AND at least two b's}: build a 4-state DFA counting a's (0,1,2,>2; accept \"exactly 2\") and a 3-state DFA counting b's (0,1,≥2; accept \"≥2\"), take the product with F = F1 × F2, then optionally merge equivalent states. (d) {w | even number of a's AND every a followed by at least one b}: same pattern — product of the 2-state parity machine and the 2-state \"each a followed by b\" machine. (Solutions are diagrams; the method is footnote-3 intersection.)\n\n**1.5(a), (b) (complement by swapping accept states).** (a) Build the DFA for {w | w contains ab} (3 states: nothing, seen a, seen ab-trap), then **swap accepting and non-accepting states** to get {w | w does not contain ab}. (b) Same for baba: 5-state substring tracker, then swap. Works because the machine is a DFA (total, deterministic) — see Exercise 1.14 for why NFA-swapping fails.\n\n**1.7(a), (f) (small NFAs; solutions are diagrams).** (a) {w | w ends with 00}, 3 states: q1 loops on 0,1; guess the final 00 via q1 —0→ q2 —0→ q3 (accept). (f) 1\\*(001⁺)\\* with 3 states: q1 (accept) loops on 1, q1 —0→ q2, q2 —0→ q3, q3 —1→ q1 with q3 looping on 1 — i.e., every 0-block must be exactly 00 followed by at least one 1.\n\n**1.11 (every NFA has an equivalent single-accept-state NFA).** Let N = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) be any NFA. Construct N′ exactly like N but add ε-transitions from the states corresponding to N's accept states to a new accept state q_accept, which has no emerging transitions. Formally N′ = (Q ∪ {q_accept}, Σ, δ′, q₀, {q_accept}) where δ′(q, a) = δ(q, a) if a ≠ ε or q ∉ F; δ′(q, a) = δ(q, a) ∪ {q_accept} if a = ε and q ∈ F; and δ′(q_accept, a) = ∅ for each a ∈ Σ_ε.\n\n**1.23 (B = B⁺ iff BB ⊆ B).** (→) BB ⊆ B⁺ holds for every language, so B = B⁺ gives BB ⊆ B. (←) Assume BB ⊆ B. B ⊆ B⁺ always, so only B⁺ ⊆ B is needed: if w ∈ B⁺ then w = x₁x₂⋯x_k with each xᵢ ∈ B, k ≥ 1. Since x₁, x₂ ∈ B and BB ⊆ B, x₁x₂ ∈ B; then (x₁x₂)x₃ ∈ B; continuing, x₁⋯x_k ∈ B. Formally, induction on k: basis k = 1 trivial; step: x₁⋯x_k ∈ B by hypothesis, so x₁⋯x_kx_{k+1} ∈ BB ⊆ B.\n\n**1.29(a) — A₁ = {0ⁿ1ⁿ2ⁿ | n ≥ 0} not regular.** Assume regular with pumping length p; s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ2ᵖ. Two possibilities: (1) y consists of only one kind of symbol — then xyyz doesn't have equal numbers of 0s, 1s, 2s; (2) y has more than one kind of symbol — then xyyz has them out of order. Contradiction either way.\n\n**1.29(c) — A₃ = {a^{2ⁿ} | n ≥ 0} not regular (powers of 2).** s = a^{2ᵖ}. Condition 3 gives |xy| ≤ p, and p < 2ᵖ so |y| < 2ᵖ (indeed |y| ≤ p). Then |xyyz| = |xyz| + |y| < 2ᵖ + 2ᵖ = 2^{p+1}, and condition 2 gives |xyyz| > 2ᵖ. So 2ᵖ < |xyyz| < 2^{p+1}: the length of xyyz lies strictly between consecutive powers of 2, hence is not a power of 2. Contradiction. (Same \"gap\" pattern as Example 1.76.)\n\n**1.40(a) — NOPREFIX(A) = {w ∈ A | no proper prefix of w is in A} is regular.** Let M = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) be a DFA for A. Build the NFA M′ = (Q, Σ, δ′, q₀, F) with δ′(r, a) = {δ(r, a)} if r ∉ F and δ′(r, a) = ∅ if r ∈ F. (Cutting all transitions out of accept states: once you've accepted, any extension has an accepted proper prefix, so kill the computation.)\n\n**1.44 — closure under B ¹←C = {w ∈ B | some y ∈ C has the same number of 1s as w}.** Let M_B, M_C be DFAs for B, C. NFA M runs M_B on the actual input while **nondeterministically guessing y** and running M_C on it, keeping the 1-counts synchronized: Q = Q_B × Q_C, start (q_B, q_C), F = F_B × F_C, and\nδ((q, r), a) = {(δ_B(q,0), r)} if a = 0 (input 0: only B moves); {(δ_B(q,1), δ_C(r,1))} if a = 1 (a 1 must be matched in both); {(q, δ_C(r,0))} if a = ε (guess a 0 of y for free).\n\n**1.46(b) — {0ᵐ1ⁿ | m ≠ n} not regular (two ways).** *Closure way (slick — note it uses the COMPLEMENT):* observe that B̄ ∩ 0\\*1\\* = {0ᵏ1ᵏ | k ≥ 0}. If B were regular, then B̄ would be regular and so would B̄ ∩ 0\\*1\\*; but {0ᵏ1ᵏ} isn't regular, so B cannot be regular. *Direct pumping (trickier — the p! trick):* choose s = 0ᵖ1^{p+p!}. By condition 3, x = 0ᵃ, y = 0ᵇ, z = 0ᶜ1^{p+p!} with b ≥ 1, a + b + c = p. Pump i + 1 times where i = p!/b (an integer since b ≤ p divides p!): yⁱ = 0^{p!}, so x y^{i+1} z = 0^{p+p!}1^{p+p!} ∉ B. Moral: when \"pump until the counts collide\" needs divisibility, use p!.\n\n**1.50 — no finite state transducer outputs wᴿ.** (FST defined in Ex. 1.24: DFA whose transitions carry input/output symbol pairs, emitting one output symbol per input symbol.) Suppose FST T outputs wᴿ on input w. On input 00 it must output 00; on input 01 it must output 10. Both inputs start with 0, but the required first output bits differ (0 vs 1) — impossible, because an FST produces its first output bit **before reading its second input bit**.\n\n**1.52 — Myhill–Nerode theorem** (via Problem 1.51: x ≡_L y iff no z distinguishes them; index of L = max size of a set pairwise distinguishable by L).\n(a) *DFA with k states ⇒ index ≤ k:* if some pairwise-distinguishable X had > k elements, pigeonhole gives distinct x, y ∈ X with δ(q₀, x) = δ(q₀, y); then δ(q₀, xz) = δ(q₀, yz) for every z, so xz ∈ L ⟺ yz ∈ L — x, y are indistinguishable, contradiction.\n(b) *Index k ⇒ DFA with k states:* let X = {s₁,…,s_k} be pairwise distinguishable. Q = {q₁,…,q_k}; δ(qᵢ, a) = qⱼ where sⱼ ≡_L sᵢa (such sⱼ exists, else X ∪ {sᵢa} would be pairwise distinguishable with k+1 elements); F = {qᵢ | sᵢ ∈ L}; start = the qᵢ with sᵢ ≡_L ε. Then {s | δ(q₀, s) = qᵢ} = {s | s ≡_L sᵢ}, so M recognizes L.\n(c) *L regular ⟺ finite index; the index = size of the smallest DFA for L* (combine (a) and (b): a k-index language has a k-state DFA, and no smaller one can exist by (a)).\n\n**1.55 — minimum pumping lengths** (minimum p that works; note any p′ ≥ p also works):\n(a) **0001\\*: minimum p = 4.** 000 ∈ L can't be pumped (any pumped-up version leaves 0001\\*, and pumping down is also fatal), so 3 fails; any s with |s| ≥ 4 contains 1s — split x = 000, y = first 1, z = rest.\n(b) **0\\*1\\*: minimum p = 1.** p = 0 fails because ε would have to be pumpable but |y| > 0 is impossible for ε. Every nonempty string: x = ε, y = first character, z = rest — pumping a leading 0 or (if none) a leading 1 stays in 0\\*1\\*.\n(d) **0\\*1⁺0⁺1\\* ∪ 10\\*1: minimum p = 3.** p = 2 fails: 11 ∈ L (from 10\\*1) can't be pumped. For |s| ≥ 3: if s ∈ 0\\*1⁺0⁺1\\* and begins 0 or 11, take x = ε, y = first symbol; if it begins 10, take x = 10, y = next symbol; if s ∈ 10\\*1, take x = 1, y = 0 (there is at least one 0 since |s| ≥ 3).\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Sipser Definition 1.5 — the five components of a finite automaton?","a":"(Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F): finite state set Q, finite alphabet Σ, transition function δ: Q × Σ → Q, start state q₀ ∈ Q, accept states F ⊆ Q. Since δ is a total function, exactly one arrow leaves each state per symbol; F = ∅ is allowed."},{"q":"Sipser's automaton design methodology in one sentence, and its first two steps?","a":"\"Pretend to be the automaton\": you read symbols one at a time, never know when the input ends, so you must always be ready to answer. Step 1: determine what crucial finite information must be remembered (you can't store the whole input); step 2: make that a finite list of possibilities and assign one state per possibility (then transitions, start = ε-possibility, accepts = yes-possibilities)."},{"q":"How do DFA and NFA transition functions differ formally?","a":"DFA: δ: Q × Σ → Q (one next state). NFA: δ: Q × Σ_ε → P(Q) — takes a symbol OR ε and returns a SET of possible next states (possibly ∅)."},{"q":"Subset construction (Thm 1.39) without ε-arrows: states, transition, start, accepts?","a":"Q′ = P(Q); δ′(R, a) = ⋃_{r∈R} δ(r, a); q₀′ = {q₀}; F′ = {R | R contains an accept state of N}. k NFA states give a 2^k-state DFA."},{"q":"How do ε-arrows modify the subset construction?","a":"Define E(R) = states reachable from R via 0 or more ε-arrows. Replace δ(r,a) by E(δ(r,a)) in the transition, and set the start state to E({q₀}) (e.g. N4's start became {1,3} via the 1 —ε→ 3 arrow)."},{"q":"NFA constructions for union, concatenation, star (the three pictures)?","a":"Union: new start state with ε-arrows to both old starts; F = F1 ∪ F2. Concatenation: keep N1's start; ε-arrows from N1's accept states to N2's start; F = F2 only. Star: ε-arrows from accept states back to the old start, PLUS a new accepting start state with an ε-arrow to the old start."},{"q":"In the star construction, why can't you just make the old start state accepting?","a":"It would accept ε as desired but may also accept undesired strings — a computation could pass through the (now accepting) start state mid-way without having completed a full piece of A₁ (Exercise 1.15). The new start state has no incoming arrows, so it only accepts ε."},{"q":"Definition 1.52: the six cases of a regular expression, and the difference between ε and ∅?","a":"R is a for a ∈ Σ, ε, ∅, (R₁ ∪ R₂), (R₁ ∘ R₂), or (R₁*). ε describes {ε} (one string); ∅ describes the empty language (no strings). Also: 1\\*∅ = ∅ but ∅\\* = {ε}."},{"q":"GNFA special form — the three conditions?","a":"(1) start state: arrows to every other state, none coming in; (2) a single accept state ≠ start: arrows in from every other state, none going out; (3) between all other ordered pairs of states (self-loops included) exactly one arrow, labeled by a regular expression."},{"q":"How do you convert a DFA into a special-form GNFA?","a":"Add a new start state with an ε-arrow to the old start; add a new accept state with ε-arrows from ALL old accept states; merge multiple/multi-label arrows into one labeled with the union of labels (a,b becomes a ∪ b); add ∅-arrows for missing transitions."},{"q":"The GNFA rip-out repair formula: after removing q_rip, what is the new label from qᵢ to qⱼ?","a":"(R₁)(R₂)\\*(R₃) ∪ (R₄), where R₁ = δ(qᵢ, q_rip), R₂ = δ(q_rip, q_rip) (self-loop), R₃ = δ(q_rip, qⱼ), R₄ = δ(qᵢ, qⱼ) (old direct label). Applied to EVERY pair qᵢ ∈ Q−{q_accept}, qⱼ ∈ Q−{q_start}, including qᵢ = qⱼ. Repeat until 2 states remain; the remaining arrow's label is the regex."},{"q":"State the pumping lemma exactly (Theorem 1.70).","a":"If A is regular, there is a number p (pumping length) where any s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p can be divided as s = xyz satisfying: (1) xyⁱz ∈ A for each i ≥ 0, (2) |y| > 0, (3) |xy| ≤ p."},{"q":"In the pumping lemma proof, what do p, y, and condition 3 correspond to?","a":"p = number of states of a DFA for A. Reading s (length ≥ p) visits ≥ p+1 states, so by pigeonhole a state repeats among the first p+1 visits; y = the substring looping from that repeated state back to itself. Taking the FIRST repetition puts it within the first p+1 states, giving |xy| ≤ p."},{"q":"Prove C = {w | equal numbers of 0s and 1s} is not regular — where exactly is condition 3 needed?","a":"Take s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ. Without condition 3, x = z = ε, y = 0ᵖ1ᵖ pumps fine (proof fails!). Condition 3 forces y within the first p symbols, so y is all 0s and xyyz has more 0s than 1s ∉ C. (Alternative: C ∩ 0\\*1\\* = {0ⁿ1ⁿ}, and regularity is closed under intersection.)"},{"q":"Why is s = (01)ᵖ a bad choice for the equal-0s-and-1s language, and what's Sipser's advice?","a":"It CAN be pumped even respecting condition 3: x = ε, y = 01, z = (01)^{p−1} keeps xyⁱz ∈ C for all i. Advice: pick a string exhibiting the \"essence\" of the nonregularity (0ᵖ1ᵖ), and if your first candidate pumps, try another."},{"q":"Show E = {0ⁱ1ʲ | i > j} is not regular — why must you pump DOWN?","a":"s = 0^{p+1}1ᵖ; condition 3 makes y all 0s. Pumping up ADDS 0s, preserving i > j — no contradiction. Take i = 0: xz loses at least one 0, and s had only one extra 0, so xz has ≤ as many 0s as 1s, hence xz ∉ E. Contradiction."},{"q":"The \"gap\" pattern for {1^{n²}} (and {a^{2ⁿ}}): how does the contradiction work?","a":"With s = 1^{p²}: conditions 2–3 give 0 < |y| ≤ p, so p² < |xy²z| ≤ p² + p < (p+1)². The pumped length falls strictly between consecutive perfect squares, so it isn't one. (Powers of 2, Prob. 1.29c: 2ᵖ < |xyyz| < 2^{p+1}.) Works whenever the language's lengths have growing gaps."},{"q":"For F = {ww | w ∈ {0,1}\\*}, why choose s = 0ᵖ10ᵖ1 rather than 0ᵖ0ᵖ, and how does the proof finish?","a":"0ᵖ0ᵖ ∈ F but can be pumped (it's just 0^{2p}) — it doesn't capture F's essence. With s = 0ᵖ10ᵖ1, condition 3 forces y to be all 0s inside the first block, so xyyz = 0^{p+|y|}10ᵖ1, whose halves can't match: xyyz ∉ F."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"s2","title":"Sipser 2 · Context-Free Languages","slides":"Sipser ch. 2 · weeks 7–9","md":"# S2 — Context-Free Languages (Sipser, Chapter 2)\n\n**Sipser companion · course weeks 7–9 (estimate)** — pairs with Book 3 (M&S); midterm territory.\n\nSource: *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*, 3rd ed., Sipser, Chapter 2 (PDF pages 125–188, book pages 101–162). Covers §2.1 CFGs, §2.2 PDAs and CFG⇄PDA equivalence, §2.3 the CFL pumping lemma, §2.4 deterministic CFLs.\n\n**What Sipser adds over the M&S digest (B3):** a full formal treatment of **ambiguity** (leftmost derivations, Def 2.7, inherent ambiguity — M&S is lighter here); a **complete worked CNF conversion** (Example 2.10) showing every intermediate grammar; the standard **6-tuple PDA with accept states** (M&S uses a different, empty-stack/blank-cell model — know which definition your course uses!); the **A_pq construction** for PDA → CFG with its two induction claims; three **fully worked pumping-lemma proofs** (a^n b^n c^n, the \"pump down\" trick, and the ww condition-3 trick); and an entire section on **deterministic CFLs** (DCFLs) — likely beyond the course but the existence of the class is worth knowing.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Context-free grammars (§2.1)\n\n**Definition 2.2 (CFG, 4-tuple — memorize verbatim).** A context-free grammar is a 4-tuple (V, Σ, R, S), where\n\n1. V is a finite set called the **variables**,\n2. Σ is a finite set, disjoint from V, called the **terminals**,\n3. R is a finite set of **rules**, with each rule being a variable and a string of variables and terminals, and\n4. S ∈ V is the **start variable**.\n\nIf u, v, w are strings of variables and terminals and A → w is a rule, then uAv **yields** uwv, written uAv ⇒ uwv. u **derives** v, written u ⇒* v, if u = v or a sequence u₁, …, u_k exists (k ≥ 0) with u ⇒ u₁ ⇒ … ⇒ u_k ⇒ v. The **language of the grammar** is {w ∈ Σ* | S ⇒* w}. Any language generated by some CFG is a **context-free language (CFL)**.\n\nConventions: rules with the same left-hand variable merge with \"|\"; when only rules are written, the start variable is the left-hand side of the first rule.\n\n**Running examples.**\n\n| Grammar | Rules | Language / point |\n|---|---|---|\n| G1 | A → 0A1 \\| B; B → # | {0ⁿ#1ⁿ : n ≥ 0}; sample derivation A ⇒ 0A1 ⇒ 00A11 ⇒ 000A111 ⇒ 000B111 ⇒ 000#111 |\n| G2 | ⟨SENTENCE⟩ → ⟨NOUN-PHRASE⟩⟨VERB-PHRASE⟩, … (English fragment, 10 variables, 18 rules) | Natural-language example; later shown **ambiguous** (\"the girl touches the boy with the flower\") |\n| G3 | S → aSb \\| SS \\| ε | Properly nested parentheses (a = \"(\", b = \")\") |\n| G4 | ⟨EXPR⟩ → ⟨EXPR⟩+⟨TERM⟩ \\| ⟨TERM⟩; ⟨TERM⟩ → ⟨TERM⟩x⟨FACTOR⟩ \\| ⟨FACTOR⟩; ⟨FACTOR⟩ → (⟨EXPR⟩) \\| a | Arithmetic expressions; the EXPR/TERM/FACTOR layering encodes precedence (× binds before +) and is **unambiguous** |\n\n**Design techniques (four, know as a checklist):**\n1. **Union of simpler CFLs**: build grammars for the pieces, then add S → S₁ | S₂ | … | S_k. E.g. {0ⁿ1ⁿ} ∪ {1ⁿ0ⁿ}: S → S₁ | S₂; S₁ → 0S₁1 | ε; S₂ → 1S₂0 | ε.\n2. **Regular language via its DFA**: variable Rᵢ per state qᵢ; rule Rᵢ → aRⱼ for each δ(qᵢ, a) = qⱼ; Rᵢ → ε for each accept state; start variable R₀. (Same construction as M&S Thm 3.3.1.)\n3. **Linked substrings**: matching counts handled by rules R → uRv (the {0ⁿ1ⁿ} pattern).\n4. **Recursive structure**: put the variable generating a structure wherever that structure can recursively appear (the ⟨FACTOR⟩ → (⟨EXPR⟩) move in G4).\n\n### Ambiguity (Sipser's fuller treatment — read this even if M&S felt sufficient)\n\n- A string is **derived ambiguously** if it has two different **parse trees** (not merely two derivations — derivations can differ only in substitution order without differing in structure).\n- To compare structure via derivations, fix the order: a **leftmost derivation** replaces the leftmost remaining variable at every step. Parse trees correspond one-to-one with leftmost derivations.\n\n**Definition 2.7.** A string w is derived ambiguously in CFG G if it has **two or more different leftmost derivations**. Grammar G is **ambiguous** if it generates some string ambiguously.\n\n- Canonical ambiguous grammar G5: ⟨EXPR⟩ → ⟨EXPR⟩+⟨EXPR⟩ | ⟨EXPR⟩x⟨EXPR⟩ | (⟨EXPR⟩) | a. It generates a+axa with two parse trees (one groups + first, one groups × first). G4 generates the **same language** unambiguously — so ambiguity is a property of the *grammar*; sometimes you can fix it by re-layering.\n- Some CFLs can **only** be generated by ambiguous grammars: **inherently ambiguous** languages. Book example: {aⁱbʲcᵏ | i = j or j = k} (Problem 2.29). Intuition: strings with i = j = k must be derivable \"both ways.\"\n\n### Chomsky normal form\n\n**Definition 2.8.** A CFG is in **Chomsky normal form (CNF)** if every rule is A → BC or A → a, where a is any terminal and A, B, C are variables — except that **B and C may not be the start variable**. Additionally S → ε is permitted, where S is the start variable.\n\n**Theorem 2.9.** Any context-free language is generated by a CFG in Chomsky normal form.\n\n**Conversion procedure — the order matters (START → ε → unit → split/terminal):**\n1. **New start variable**: add S₀ and rule S₀ → S, so the start variable never appears on a right-hand side.\n2. **Eliminate ε-rules** A → ε (A not the start variable): for each occurrence of A on a right-hand side, add a copy with that occurrence deleted (R → uAvAw spawns R → uvAw, R → uAvw, R → uvw); if R → A existed, add R → ε **unless R → ε was previously removed**. Repeat until no ε-rules remain (except possibly S₀ → ε).\n3. **Eliminate unit rules** A → B: for every rule B → u, add A → u **unless A → u is a unit rule previously removed**. Repeat.\n4. **Split long rules**: replace A → u₁u₂…u_k (k ≥ 3) with A → u₁A₁, A₁ → u₂A₂, …, A_{k−2} → u_{k−1}u_k (fresh variables).\n5. **Terminal cleanup**: in any length-≥2 right-hand side, replace terminal uᵢ with new variable Uᵢ and add Uᵢ → uᵢ.\n\nThe \"unless previously removed\" bookkeeping in steps 2–3 is what prevents infinite loops. (M&S digest card 11 makes the same point; Sipser's example below is the one to rehearse.)\n\n**Worked Example 2.10 (know this cold — a favorite exam task).** Convert G6: S → ASA | aB; A → B | S; B → b | ε.\n\n1. New start: **S₀ → S**; S → ASA | aB; A → B | S; B → b | ε.\n2. Remove B → ε: S → ASA | aB | **a**; A → B | S | **ε**; B → b.\n   Then remove A → ε: S → ASA | aB | a | **SA | AS | S**; A → B | S; B → b.\n   (ASA with either A deleted gives SA and AS; both deleted gives S.)\n3. Remove unit rule S → S (yields nothing new). Remove S₀ → S: **S₀ → ASA | aB | a | SA | AS**; S → ASA | aB | a | SA | AS; A → B | S; B → b.\n   Remove A → B: A gains **b**. Remove A → S: A gains **ASA | aB | a | SA | AS**.\n4./5. Split ASA and replace the terminal a in aB. Final CNF grammar:\n   - S₀ → AA₁ | UB | a | SA | AS\n   - S → AA₁ | UB | a | SA | AS\n   - A → b | AA₁ | UB | a | SA | AS\n   - A₁ → SA\n   - U → a\n   - B → b\n\n(The book reuses one variable U → a rather than one Uᵢ per occurrence — allowed simplification.)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Pushdown automata (§2.2)\n\nAn NFA plus a **stack** (LIFO, unbounded): push/pop only at the top. The stack stores unbounded counts, which is exactly why a PDA recognizes {0ⁿ1ⁿ} when an NFA cannot. **Nondeterministic PDAs are strictly stronger than deterministic ones** (unlike the DFA/NFA situation) — and NPDAs are the model equivalent to CFGs.\n\n**Definition 2.13 (PDA, 6-tuple — memorize; note the contrast with M&S's model).** A pushdown automaton is a 6-tuple (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q₀, F), where Q, Σ, Γ, F are all finite sets, and\n\n1. Q is the set of states,\n2. Σ is the input alphabet,\n3. Γ is the stack alphabet,\n4. δ : Q × Σ_ε × Γ_ε → P(Q × Γ_ε) is the transition function (Σ_ε = Σ ∪ {ε}, Γ_ε = Γ ∪ {ε}),\n5. q₀ ∈ Q is the start state, and\n6. F ⊆ Q is the set of accept states.\n\n**Computation / acceptance.** M accepts w if w = w₁…w_m (each wᵢ ∈ Σ_ε) and there exist states r₀, …, r_m and stack strings s₀, …, s_m ∈ Γ* with:\n1. r₀ = q₀ and s₀ = ε (start state, empty stack);\n2. (r_{i+1}, b) ∈ δ(rᵢ, w_{i+1}, a) where sᵢ = at and s_{i+1} = bt for some a, b ∈ Γ_ε, t ∈ Γ* (legal moves: pop a, push b);\n3. r_m ∈ F (accept state at the end of the input).\n\nDiagram notation: an edge labeled **\"a, b → c\"** means: reading a from input (may be ε), pop b (may be ε), push c (may be ε).\n\n**Two idioms with no built-in support — implement them yourself:**\n- **Empty-stack test**: push a marker $ first (ε, ε → $); seeing $ again means the stack is \"empty.\"\n- **End-of-input test**: acceptance only counts at the end of input, so route into the accept state only when you believe the input is done.\n\n**Example 2.14** — M1 recognizes {0ⁿ1ⁿ | n ≥ 0}. Q = {q₁,…,q₄}, Γ = {0, $}, F = {q₁, q₄}. Moves: q₁ --ε,ε→$--> q₂; loop q₂ --0,ε→0-->; q₂ --1,0→ε--> q₃; loop q₃ --1,0→ε-->; q₃ --ε,$→ε--> q₄. (Push a marker, push 0s, pop one 0 per 1, accept on seeing $ at input end.)\n\n**Example 2.16** — {aⁱbʲcᵏ | i, j, k ≥ 0 and i = j or i = k}: push all a's, then **nondeterministically guess** whether to match them against the b's or the c's (two branches; if either accepts, the machine accepts). Nondeterminism is provably essential here (Problem 2.57).\n\n**Example 2.18** — {wwᴿ | w ∈ {0,1}*}: push symbols; at each point nondeterministically guess the middle; then pop and compare against remaining input; accept if stack empties exactly at input end. Also requires nondeterminism (Problem 2.58).\n\n### Equivalence with CFGs\n\n**Theorem 2.20.** A language is context free **iff** some pushdown automaton recognizes it.\n\n**Lemma 2.21 (CFG → PDA).** Given CFG G, build PDA P that guesses a derivation: the stack holds the suffix of the current intermediate string starting at its first variable (terminals before the first variable are matched against input immediately).\n1. Push $ then the start variable S.\n2. Loop forever: (a) top of stack is variable A → nondeterministically replace A by the RHS of some rule A → w; (b) top of stack is terminal a → read next input symbol, match against a (mismatch kills the branch); (c) top of stack is $ → enter the accept state (accepts if all input read).\nFormally three states {q_start, q_loop, q_accept} plus helper states E for the shorthand \"(r, u) ∈ δ(q, a, s)\" that pushes a whole string u one symbol at a time (push u's last symbol first). Transitions: δ(q_start, ε, ε) = {(q_loop, S$)}; δ(q_loop, ε, A) = {(q_loop, w) | A → w ∈ R}; δ(q_loop, a, a) = {(q_loop, ε)}; δ(q_loop, ε, $) = {(q_accept, ε)}.\n**Example 2.25** applies this to S → aTb | b; T → Ta | ε.\n\n**Lemma 2.27 (PDA → CFG) — the A_pq construction.** First normalize P: (1) single accept state q_accept; (2) empties its stack before accepting; (3) every move either pushes or pops, never both/neither. Then for each pair of states p, q, make variable **A_pq generating exactly the strings that take P from p (empty stack) to q (empty stack)** — such strings also take P from p to q leaving any existing stack untouched. Start variable: A_{q₀,q_accept}. Rules:\n1. If δ(p, a, ε) ∋ (r, u) and δ(s, b, u) ∋ (q, ε) — first move pushes u, last move pops the same u — add **A_pq → a A_rs b** (a, b ∈ Σ_ε);\n2. For all p, q, r: **A_pq → A_pr A_rq** (stack empties midway at r);\n3. For all p: **A_pp → ε**.\nCorrectness = two claims proved by induction: Claim 2.30 (derivation ⟹ computation, induction on derivation length; base case App → ε) and Claim 2.31 (computation ⟹ derivation, induction on number of computation steps; case split on whether the stack empties mid-way).\n\n**Corollary 2.32.** Every regular language is context free (an NFA is a PDA that ignores its stack). Picture: regular ⊂ context-free (Fig 2.33).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Non-context-free languages (§2.3)\n\n**Theorem 2.34 (Pumping lemma for context-free languages — EXACT statement).** If A is a context-free language, then there is a number p (the pumping length) where, if s is any string in A of length at least p, then s may be divided into five pieces s = uvxyz satisfying the conditions\n\n1. for each i ≥ 0, u vⁱ x yⁱ z ∈ A,\n2. |vy| > 0, and\n3. |vxy| ≤ p.\n\n(Condition 2: v and y are not both empty. Condition 3: the pumpable window is short — this is the workhorse condition in harder proofs.)\n\n**Proof idea (parse-tree surgery).** A long string forces a tall parse tree; on a longest root-to-leaf path some variable R repeats (pigeonhole). The upper R generates vxy, the lower R generates x. Substituting the larger subtree for the smaller (repeatedly) gives uvⁱxyⁱz for i ≥ 1; substituting smaller for larger gives uxz (i = 0). Details: with b = max RHS length (≥ 2), a tree of height h yields ≤ bʰ leaves, so set **p = b^{|V|+1}**; take a minimum-node parse tree (forces condition 2 — otherwise a smaller tree for s would exist) and choose R repeating among the lowest |V|+1 variables on a longest path (forces condition 3).\n\n**Recipe** (same shape as the regular pumping lemma): assume A is a CFL, get p, pick s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p adversarially, show every split uvxyz meeting conditions 2 and 3 has some i with uvⁱxyⁱz ∉ A, contradiction.\n\n**Example 2.36 — B = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ | n ≥ 0} is not a CFL.** Take s = aᵖbᵖcᵖ. Two cases: (1) v and y each contain only one symbol type → uv²xy²z has unequal counts of a, b, c; (2) v or y contains ≥ 2 symbol types → uv²xy²z has symbols out of order. Both violate membership. ∎\n\n**Example 2.37 — C = {aⁱbʲcᵏ | 0 ≤ i ≤ j ≤ k} is not a CFL (the \"pump down\" example).** Same s = aᵖbᵖcᵖ, but pumping *up* alone isn't enough since unequal counts are allowed. Case 1 (v, y single-typed): one of a, b, c is missing from v and y; subcases:\n- a's missing → **pump down** to uxz: a-count unchanged but fewer b's or fewer c's → some count above a's shrank below... precisely: uxz has the same number of a's but fewer b's or c's, breaking i ≤ j ≤ k.\n- b's missing → if a's appear in vy, uv²xy²z has more a's than b's; if c's appear, u v⁰ x y⁰ z has more b's than c's. Either way out of C.\n- c's missing → uv²xy²z has more a's or b's than c's.\nCase 2 (multi-typed v or y): uv²xy²z out of order. ∎ **Moral: always consider i = 0 as well as i = 2.**\n\n**Example 2.38 — D = {ww | w ∈ {0,1}*} is not a CFL (choosing s is the hard part).** First candidate s = 0ᵖ10ᵖ1 FAILS — it can be pumped (v the last 0 of the first block, y the first 0 of the second block). Correct choice: **s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ0ᵖ1ᵖ**, using condition 3:\n- If vxy lies in the first half of s, uv²xy²z pushes a 1 into the first position of the second half — not ww.\n- If vxy lies in the second half, uv²xy²z pushes a 0 into the last position of the first half — not ww.\n- So vxy **straddles the midpoint**; then pumping **down**, uxz = 0ᵖ1ⁱ0ʲ1ᵖ with i, j not both p — not ww. ∎\nContrast: {wwᴿ} IS a CFL (Example 2.18) — the pumping argument fails for it because matched pairs are nested, not crossed.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Deterministic context-free languages (§2.4) — skim; likely beyond the course\n\nSipser (3rd ed. only) devotes ~25 pages to DCFLs. Headlines worth knowing even if untested:\n\n- **Definition 2.39:** a **DPDA** has δ : Q × Σ_ε × Γ_ε → (Q × Γ_ε) ∪ {∅} with the condition that for every q, a, x, **exactly one** of δ(q, a, x), δ(q, a, ε), δ(q, ε, x), δ(q, ε, ε) is non-∅ (ε-moves allowed but never competing with symbol moves). Languages of DPDAs = **deterministic context-free languages (DCFLs)**.\n- DCFLs are a **proper subclass** of the CFLs: {0ⁿ1ⁿ} is a DCFL (Example 2.40), but {aⁱbʲcᵏ | i = j or i = k} and {wwᴿ} are CFLs that are **not** DCFLs — nondeterminism is essential for them.\n- **Theorem 2.42: DCFLs are closed under complementation** (CFLs are not!). Quick corollary: {aⁱbʲcᵏ | i ≠ j or j ≠ k} is a CFL but not a DCFL, else its complement intersected with a*b*c* = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} would be a CFL. DCFLs are **not** closed under union, intersection, concatenation, star, reversal (Problem 2.53).\n- Machinery: every DPDA can be made to read its whole input (Lemma 2.41); endmarked inputs (⊣) don't change the class (Theorem 2.43); **DCFGs** are defined via forced handles in leftmost reductions (= rightmost derivations in reverse); the **DK-test** (a DFA built from dotted rules that finds handles — DK for \"deterministic K\"/Donald Knuth) decides whether a CFG is deterministic; DCFGs ≡ DPDAs on endmarked languages (Theorem 2.57); **LR(k) grammars** add k symbols of lookahead, DCFG = LR(0), and every LR(k) grammar has an equivalent DPDA — all LR(k) classes describe exactly the DCFLs (endmarked). This is the theory underlying practical parsers. Sipser explicitly says later chapters don't depend on §2.4.\n- Every DCFG is unambiguous (Problem 2.51), and every DCFG generates a prefix-free language (Problem 2.52, solved below).\n\n---\n\n## Solved problems from the book\n\nTranscribed from the chapter's SELECTED SOLUTIONS (book pp. 160–162), lightly compressed.\n\n**2.3 (grammar drill).** For R → XRX | S; S → aTb | bTa; T → XTX | X | ε; X → a | b:\n(a) variables R, X, S, T; (b) terminals a, b; (c) start variable R; (d) three strings in L(G): ab, ba, aab; (e) three strings not in L(G): a, b, ε; (f) T ⇒ aba: **False** (not one step); (g) T ⇒* aba: **True**; (h) T ⇒ T: **False**; (i) T ⇒* T: **True** (zero steps); (j) XXX ⇒* aba: **True**; (k) X ⇒* aba: **False**; (l) T ⇒* XX: **True**; (m) T ⇒* XXX: **True**; (n) S ⇒* ε: **False**; (o) L(G) = all strings over {a, b} that are **not palindromes**.\n\n**2.4 (give CFGs, Σ = {0,1}).**\n(a) {w | w contains at least three 1s}: S → R1R1R1R; R → 0R | 1R | ε.\n(d) {w | |w| odd and middle symbol 0}: S → 0 | 0S0 | 0S1 | 1S0 | 1S1 (add one symbol on each side per step; the base is the middle 0).\n\n**2.6 (give CFGs).**\n(a) Strings over {a, b} with more a's than b's: S → TaT; T → TT | aTb | bTa | a | ε. (T generates all strings with #a ≥ #b; S forces one extra a.)\n(c) {w#x | wᴿ is a substring of x}: S → TX; T → 0T0 | 1T1 | #X; X → 0X | 1X | ε. (T matches w against a reversed copy embedded in x; the X's absorb the unmatched parts of x.)\n\n**2.7 (informal PDAs for 2.6).**\n(a) Use the stack as a **counter of #a − #b**: scanning the input, if the input symbol and the top of stack are an a/b or b/a pair, pop; otherwise push the input symbol. Accept iff a is on top at the end. (State-based version: accept whenever the count is positive.)\n(c) Push every symbol until reading # (reject if no # ever appears). Then nondeterministically skip over a prefix of x; at the guessed point, compare input symbols against popped stack symbols (this checks reversal automatically); any disagreement, or input ending with a nonempty stack, rejects that branch; if the stack empties, read the rest of the input and accept.\n\n**2.8 (ambiguity of the English grammar G2).** \"the girl touches the boy with the flower\" has two leftmost derivations: one expands ⟨VERB-PHRASE⟩ → ⟨CMPLX-VERB⟩⟨PREP-PHRASE⟩ (the girl **uses the flower** to touch the boy — \"with the flower\" modifies the touching), the other expands ⟨VERB-PHRASE⟩ → ⟨CMPLX-VERB⟩ and attaches ⟨PREP-PHRASE⟩ inside the object ⟨NOUN-PHRASE⟩ (the girl touches **the boy who is holding the flower**). Structural ambiguity = two parse trees = two meanings.\n\n**2.18 (CFL ∩ regular is a CFL; the key closure fact).**\n(a) Let PDA P recognize C and DFA D recognize R. Run them **in parallel**: build PDA P′ with state set Q_P × Q_D that does whatever P does while tracking D's state on the same input; accept states F_P × F_D. Then L(P′) = C ∩ R, so C ∩ R is context free.\n(b) A = {w ∈ {a,b,c}* | equal numbers of a's, b's, c's} is not a CFL: if it were, A ∩ a*b*c* = {aⁿbⁿcⁿ} would be a CFL by (a), contradicting Example 2.36. (Moral: intersect with a regular \"shape filter\" to expose a known non-CFL.)\n\n**2.30 (pumping-lemma proofs).**\n(b) B = {0ⁿ#0²ⁿ#0³ⁿ | n ≥ 0}: take s = 0ᵖ#0²ᵖ#0³ᵖ. Neither v nor y may contain # (else uv²xy²z has > 2 #'s). The #'s split s into three 0-segments; since |vxy| ≤ p... in fact v and y together touch at most two of the three segments, so pumping up breaks the 1 : 2 : 3 length ratio. Not in B — contradiction.\n(c) C = {w#t | w a substring of t}: take s = aᵖbᵖ#aᵖbᵖ. Neither v nor y may contain # (else pumping **down** removes the #). If both v, y sit left of #, uv²xy²z has a longer left side than right — not in C. If both sit right of #, u v⁰ x y⁰ z again has the longer side on the left. (If one of v, y is empty, treat as the same-side case.) Remaining case: v, y nonempty straddling # — then by condition 3 (|vxy| ≤ p), v ⊆ b's and y ⊆ a's, so uv²xy²z has more b's on the left of # than the right. Contradiction in every case.\n\n**2.38 (CFLs not closed under perfect shuffle).** A = {0ᵏ1ᵏ} and B = {aᵏb³ᵏ} are CFLs; their perfect shuffle is C = {(0a)ᵏ(0b)ᵏ(1b)²ᵏ | k ≥ 0}. If C were a CFL, pump s = (0a)ᵖ(0b)ᵖ(1b)²ᵖ: strings in C are exactly one-fourth 1s and one-eighth a's, so vxy would need to contain both 1s and a's to preserve the proportions — impossible since the a's and 1s are separated by 2p symbols and |vxy| ≤ p.\n\n**2.52 (every DCFG generates a prefix-free language).** Suppose w and wz (z ≠ ε) are both in L(G) for a DCFG G. Both are valid strings; since handles are forced, w = xhy and wz = xhyz share the same handle h, so their first reduce steps yield u and uz. Iterating, we eventually reduce w to the start variable S₁, leaving S₁z — but S₁ appears on no right-hand side, so S₁z cannot be reduced further. Contradiction. (This is why endmarkers ⊣ are needed for DCFG ≡ DPDA equivalence: endmarked languages are automatically prefix-free.)\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Sipser's Definition 2.7: when exactly is a grammar ambiguous, and why isn't \"two derivations\" the right notion?","a":"G is ambiguous if some string has two or more different **leftmost derivations** (equivalently, two parse trees). Two derivations may differ only in the order variables are replaced while having identical structure, so plain derivation-counting overcounts; fixing leftmost order makes derivations correspond one-to-one with parse trees."},{"q":"What is a leftmost derivation?","a":"A derivation in which, at every step, the leftmost remaining variable is the one replaced."},{"q":"What is an inherently ambiguous language? Give Sipser's example.","a":"A CFL generable only by ambiguous grammars — no unambiguous CFG exists for it. Example: {aⁱbʲcᵏ | i = j or j = k}. Contrast: a+axa is ambiguous in G5 but the same language has the unambiguous grammar G4, so that language is not inherently ambiguous."},{"q":"How does grammar G4 (EXPR/TERM/FACTOR) avoid the ambiguity of G5 for arithmetic expressions?","a":"By layering: ⟨EXPR⟩ → ⟨EXPR⟩+⟨TERM⟩ | ⟨TERM⟩; ⟨TERM⟩ → ⟨TERM⟩x⟨FACTOR⟩ | ⟨FACTOR⟩; ⟨FACTOR⟩ → (⟨EXPR⟩) | a. Each precedence level gets its own variable, so × groups below + and every string has a unique parse tree."},{"q":"Define Chomsky normal form exactly, including the two side conditions.","a":"Every rule is A → BC or A → a (a a terminal; A, B, C variables), where B and C may not be the start variable; additionally S → ε is permitted for the start variable S only."},{"q":"List Sipser's CNF conversion stages in the correct order.","a":"(1) Add new start variable S₀ with S₀ → S; (2) eliminate ε-rules A → ε (A ≠ start), patching all rules containing A; (3) eliminate unit rules A → B, copying B's rules to A; (4) split right-hand sides of length ≥ 3 into chains of length-2 rules; (5) replace terminals in length-2 right-hand sides with new variables Uᵢ → uᵢ."},{"q":"In CNF conversion, removing B → ε when S → ASA and A → B | S exist: which rules get added when A → ε is then removed?","a":"From S → ASA: add S → SA, S → AS, and S → S (both A's deleted); from S → aB earlier: B → ε adds S → a. This is Example 2.10 — every occurrence of the nullable variable spawns a copy with it deleted, and R → A becomes R → ε only if R → ε wasn't already removed."},{"q":"What loop-prevention rule keeps the ε-rule and unit-rule elimination steps from cycling forever?","a":"Never re-add a rule that was previously removed: when B → ε removal would create R → ε, add it only if R → ε wasn't removed before; when removing A → B, add A → u for B → u only if A → u isn't a previously removed unit rule."},{"q":"Give Sipser's 6-tuple PDA definition, including the transition function's type.","a":"(Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q₀, F): states Q, input alphabet Σ, stack alphabet Γ, δ : Q × Σ_ε × Γ_ε → P(Q × Γ_ε), start state q₀ ∈ Q, accept states F ⊆ Q. It accepts if some computation branch consumes all of the input and ends in a state of F (stack contents irrelevant, but it starts empty)."},{"q":"PDAs have no built-in empty-stack or end-of-input test. How do you implement both?","a":"Empty stack: push a marker $ at the start (ε, ε → $); seeing $ again means the stack is effectively empty. End of input: acceptance only counts when the whole input is read, so enter the accept state only on the branch that believes the input is exhausted."},{"q":"Sketch the PDA for {aⁱbʲcᵏ | i = j or i = k} and say why nondeterminism is essential.","a":"Push all a's; then nondeterministically branch: one branch pops an a per b (then reads c's freely), the other reads b's freely and pops an a per c. A DPDA can't do it — it would have to commit before knowing whether the b's or the c's match, and the language is provably not a DCFL (Problem 2.57)."},{"q":"In the CFG → PDA construction (Lemma 2.21), what is kept on the stack and what are the three loop actions?","a":"The stack holds the current intermediate string from its first variable onward (earlier terminals are already matched), on top of $. Loop: variable A on top → nondeterministically replace it by the RHS of a rule A → w; terminal a on top → read the next input symbol and match (mismatch rejects the branch); $ on top → move to the accept state."},{"q":"In the PDA → CFG construction (Lemma 2.27), what does variable A_pq generate, what normalizations are imposed on the PDA, and what are the three rule types?","a":"A_pq generates exactly the strings taking P from state p with empty stack to state q with empty stack. Normalize P to have a single accept state, to empty its stack before accepting, and to make every move a pure push or a pure pop. Rules: A_pq → a A_rs b when δ(p,a,ε) ∋ (r,u) and δ(s,b,u) ∋ (q,ε) (same symbol pushed first and popped last); A_pq → A_pr A_rq (stack empties midway at r); A_pp → ε."},{"q":"State the CFL pumping lemma exactly, and give the value of p from the proof.","a":"If A is a CFL, there is a pumping length p such that any s ∈ A with |s| ≥ p splits as s = uvxyz with (1) uvⁱxyⁱz ∈ A for all i ≥ 0, (2) |vy| > 0, (3) |vxy| ≤ p. Proof sets p = b^{|V|+1} (b = max rule RHS length, ≥ 2): a repeated variable R on a longest path lets you swap the two R-subtrees. A minimum-node parse tree forces (2); choosing R among the lowest |V|+1 path variables forces (3)."},{"q":"For D = {ww}, why does s = 0ᵖ10ᵖ1 fail and how does s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ0ᵖ1ᵖ succeed?","a":"0ᵖ10ᵖ1 can be pumped (v = last 0 of block one, y = first 0 of block two keeps the ww form). With 0ᵖ1ᵖ0ᵖ1ᵖ, condition 3 forces vxy either into one half — pumping up shifts a wrong symbol across the midpoint — or straddling the midpoint, where pumping **down** gives 0ᵖ1ⁱ0ʲ1ᵖ with i, j not both p. Every case leaves ww, so D is not a CFL (yet {wwᴿ} is one — nesting vs. crossing)."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"s3","title":"Sipser 3 · Church–Turing Thesis","slides":"Sipser ch. 3 · weeks 10–11","md":"# S3 — The Church–Turing Thesis (Sipser Ch. 3)\n\n**Sipser companion · course weeks 10–11 (estimate)** — pairs with Book 4 (M&S).\n\n*Source: Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation, 3rd ed., Chapter 3 (PDF pp. 189–216; book pp. 165–191).*\n\nWhy read this alongside M&S ch. 4: same machine, but Sipser adds four things the M&S digest treats lightly or not at all — (1) a full worked **nondeterministic-TM ⇒ deterministic-TM simulation** (breadth-first tree search), (2) the **enumerator** variant and its equivalence with recognizability (why \"recursively enumerable\" is called that), (3) **Hilbert's tenth problem** as the historical motivation for defining \"algorithm\" at all, and (4) the **three levels of TM description** — the vocabulary your exam answers should use.\n\n**Watch the definitional differences from M&S:** Sipser's basic TM is **single-tape**, the tape is **one-way infinite** (infinite to the right only; a left move at the left end leaves the head in place), and the head must move **L or R every step — no \"stay put\" (N)** in the base model. M&S's base model is k-tape, doubly infinite, with N allowed. The variants section shows all these choices are equivalent in power, so nothing downstream changes — but quote the definition of whichever book the question cites.\n\n---\n\n## 3.1 Turing machines\n\n### Setup and the differences from finite automata\n\nAn infinite tape holds the input; a read–write head moves over it; the machine writes notes on the tape as its unlimited memory. Sipser's list of differences from a DFA:\n\n1. A TM can **write** on the tape as well as read it.\n2. The head moves **both left and right**.\n3. The tape is **infinite**.\n4. The accept and reject states **take effect immediately** (mid-string, not only at the end of input).\n\nMotivating machine M1 for B = {w#w | w ∈ {0,1}\\*}: you can't remember a mile-long input, but you can walk back and forth and make marks. So **zig-zag**: match the symbol left of # against the corresponding symbol right of #, crossing off (writing x over) each matched pair; if a mismatch or no # → reject; when the left side is fully crossed off, check nothing uncrossed remains on the right → accept.\n\n### Formal definition (Definition 3.3) — the 7-tuple\n\nA **Turing machine** is a 7-tuple **(Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q0, q_accept, q_reject)**, with Q, Σ, Γ all finite, where\n\n1. **Q** is the set of states,\n2. **Σ** is the input alphabet, **not** containing the blank symbol ␣,\n3. **Γ** is the tape alphabet, with **␣ ∈ Γ and Σ ⊆ Γ**,\n4. **δ : Q × Γ → Q × Γ × {L, R}** is the transition function,\n5. **q0 ∈ Q** is the start state,\n6. **q_accept ∈ Q** is the accept state,\n7. **q_reject ∈ Q** is the reject state, with **q_reject ≠ q_accept**.\n\nδ(q, a) = (r, b, L) means: in state q reading a, write b over a, enter state r, move head left.\n\nComputation: input w = w1…wn sits on the leftmost n squares, rest of the tape blank, head on the leftmost square. Since ␣ ∉ Σ, **the first blank marks the end of the input**. A left move at the leftmost square leaves the head where it is. The machine halts exactly when it enters q_accept or q_reject; otherwise it runs forever.\n\n### Configurations\n\nA **configuration** = (current state, tape contents, head position), written as a single string **u q v**: state q, tape contents uv (blanks after), head on the **first symbol of v**. E.g., 1011 q7 01111 = tape 101101111, state q7, head on the second 0.\n\n**Yields relation** (one legal step):\n- Left move: ua qᵢ bv **yields** u qⱼ acv when δ(qᵢ, b) = (qⱼ, c, L).\n- Right move: ua qᵢ bv **yields** uac qⱼ v when δ(qᵢ, b) = (qⱼ, c, R).\n- Left edge: qᵢ bv yields qⱼ cv on a left-moving transition (head pinned), and c qⱼ v on a right-moving one.\n- Right edge: ua qᵢ is treated as ua qᵢ ␣.\n\n**Start configuration** on w: q0 w. **Accepting/rejecting configurations** have state q_accept / q_reject; both are **halting configurations** (yield nothing further).\n\n**M accepts w** iff there is a sequence of configurations C1, …, Ck with C1 the start configuration on w, each Cᵢ yields Cᵢ₊₁, and Ck accepting. L(M) = the language **recognized** by M.\n\n### Recognizable vs. decidable (Definitions 3.5, 3.6)\n\nOn any input, three outcomes are possible: **accept, reject, or loop** (never halt). Failing to accept = rejecting **or** looping.\n\n- **Definition 3.5:** A language is **Turing-recognizable** if some TM recognizes it. (Other books: *recursively enumerable*.)\n- A **decider** is a TM that halts on all inputs. A decider that recognizes L is said to **decide** L.\n- **Definition 3.6:** A language is **Turing-decidable** (or just **decidable**) if some TM decides it. (Other books: *recursive*.)\n\nEvery decidable language is Turing-recognizable; recognizable-but-undecidable examples wait for Ch. 4.\n\n### Examples 3.7–3.12 (all decidable) — know the strategies\n\n**Example 3.7 — M2 decides A = {0^(2^n) | n ≥ 0}** (strings of 0s of power-of-2 length). Strategy: repeatedly **sweep left to right crossing off every other 0**, halving the count each pass. If a pass sees exactly one 0 → accept; if it sees an odd number > 1 → reject; else return to the left end and sweep again. (A power of 2 halves down to 1 without ever passing through an odd number > 1.) Formal description given in the book: Q = {q1,…,q5, q_accept, q_reject}, Σ = {0}, Γ = {0, x, ␣}; the first 0 is overwritten with ␣ so the machine can find the left end later. Sample run on 0000 ends in q_accept.\n\n**Example 3.9 — formal M1 for B = {w#w}**: Q = {q1,…,q8, q_accept, q_reject}, Σ = {0,1,#}, Γ = {0,1,#,x,␣}. State-diagram convention worth stealing: **the reject state and its transitions are omitted; any missing arrow implicitly goes to q_reject**.\n\n**Example 3.11 — M3 decides C = {aⁱbʲcᵏ | i·j = k, i,j,k ≥ 1}** (multiplication check). Strategy: (1) one left-to-right pass checks the shape a⁺b⁺c⁺ (acting like a DFA — no writing needed); (2) return to the left end; (3) cross off one a, then shuttle between b's and c's crossing off one c per b until the b's are gone (c's exhausted early → reject); (4) restore the b's and repeat for the next a; when the a's are gone, accept iff all c's are gone. — i.e., k must equal j added up i times.\n\nTwo techniques for **finding the left end of the tape**: (i) mark the leftmost cell with a special symbol at the start (3.7 uses ␣ as the marker); (ii) exploit the pinned-head rule — write a special symbol, try to move left; if you still see the special symbol, the move failed, so you're at the left end (restore the original symbol before continuing).\n\n**Example 3.12 — M4 decides the element distinctness language E = {#x1#x2#…#xl | all xᵢ distinct}**: compare every pair xᵢ, xⱼ by keeping **dotted marks** on two of the # separators and zig-zag-comparing the strings after them; advance the marks through all pairs; reject on any equal pair, accept when all pairs are checked. Technique introduced: **marking a symbol = adding a dotted copy (e.g., #̇) to Γ** for every symbol you may need to mark.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 Variants of Turing machines\n\n**Robustness:** every reasonable variant recognizes the same class of languages. Master method for all equivalence proofs: **show each model can simulate the other**. Warm-up: adding a \"stay put\" option S is powerless — replace each S-transition by a right move followed by a left move.\n\n### Multitape TMs (Theorem 3.13)\n\nA k-tape TM has k tapes, each with its own head; input starts on tape 1, others blank; δ : Q × Γᵏ → Q × Γᵏ × {L, R, S}ᵏ (all heads read/write/move simultaneously; S allowed here).\n\n**Theorem 3.13.** Every multitape TM M has an equivalent single-tape TM S.\n\n*Simulation:* S stores all k tapes on its one tape, **separated by # delimiters**, and marks each virtual head position by a **dotted version of the symbol** under it. To simulate one step of M: scan from the first # to the (k+1)-st # to read the k dotted symbols; second pass updates tape contents and dot positions per M's δ. If a virtual head moves right onto a # (M moved onto unread blank tape), S writes ␣ there and **shifts everything from that cell to the rightmost # one cell right**, then resumes.\n\n**Corollary 3.15.** L is Turing-recognizable iff some multitape TM recognizes it. (⇐ is Thm 3.13; ⇒ because a single-tape TM *is* a 1-tape multitape TM.)\n\n### Nondeterministic TMs (Theorem 3.16) — Sipser's big add over M&S\n\nAn NTM's transition function is **δ : Q × Γ → P(Q × Γ × {L, R})** — a set of options per (state, symbol). Its computation on w is a **tree**: nodes are configurations, root = start configuration, branching = the nondeterministic choices. **N accepts w iff some branch reaches q_accept.**\n\n**Theorem 3.16.** Every nondeterministic TM has an equivalent deterministic TM.\n\n*Proof idea:* D searches N's computation tree for an accepting configuration. **Depth-first search is the classic wrong answer** — DFS can dive down one infinite branch forever and miss an accepting configuration on a sibling branch. Use **breadth-first search**: explore all branches to depth d before any branch to depth d+1, so every node is eventually visited.\n\n*Proof (3-tape D, legitimate by Thm 3.13):*\n- **Tape 1:** the input w, never altered.\n- **Tape 2:** N's tape contents along the branch currently being simulated.\n- **Tape 3:** the **address** of the current node: a string over Γ_b = {1,…,b}, where b = max number of choices δ ever offers. Address 231 means: from the root take child 2, then child 3, then child 1. Addresses with too-large digits for the actual choices available are **invalid**; ε addresses the root.\n\nD's loop: (1) tape 1 holds w, tapes 2–3 empty; (2) copy tape 1 to tape 2, set tape 3 to ε; (3) simulate N on tape 2, consuming one tape-3 symbol per step to pick the branch — if the address runs out, is invalid, or the branch rejects, go to (4); if the branch accepts, **accept**; (4) replace tape 3's string with the **next string in string order** and go back to (2). Replaying every branch from the root in string order = breadth-first search.\n\n**Corollary 3.18.** L is Turing-recognizable iff some NTM recognizes it.\n\n**Corollary 3.19.** L is **decidable** iff some NTM **decides** it — where an NTM is a decider when **all branches halt on all inputs**. (Proof = solved Exercise 3.3 below.)\n\n### Enumerators (Theorem 3.21)\n\nAn **enumerator** E is a TM with an attached **printer**: it starts on a blank tape and prints strings, possibly forever, **in any order, possibly with repetitions**. The language enumerated by E = the set of strings it ever prints. (This is where the name *recursively enumerable* comes from.)\n\n**Theorem 3.21.** A language is Turing-recognizable iff some enumerator enumerates it.\n\n*⇒ (enumerator E ⟹ recognizer M):* M on input w runs E and compares every printed string with w; if w ever appears, accept. (If w ∉ A, M runs forever — a recognizer, not a decider.)\n\n*⇐ (recognizer M ⟹ enumerator E):* the naive \"run M on s1, print if accepted; run M on s2, …\" **fails** because M may loop on some sᵢ and never get to sᵢ₊₁. Fix by **dovetailing**: with s1, s2, s3, … a list of all of Σ\\*, E repeats for i = 1, 2, 3, …: run M for **i steps** on **each of s1, …, sᵢ**; print every sⱼ accepted within those i steps. Every accepted string eventually shows up (infinitely often, in fact) — the effect of running M in parallel on all inputs.\n\n### Equivalence with other models\n\nAll models with **unrestricted access to unlimited memory** (and only finite work per step) turn out equivalent — analogy: any algorithm programmable in LISP is programmable in Pascal and vice versa, since each compiles into the other. Philosophical upshot: individual models are somewhat arbitrary, but **the class of algorithms they define is unique and natural**.\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 The definition of algorithm\n\n### Hilbert's tenth problem — why \"algorithm\" needed a definition\n\nIn 1900 Hilbert posed 23 problems; the **tenth**: devise an algorithm (his phrase: \"a process according to which it can be determined by a finite number of operations\") that decides whether a given polynomial with integer coefficients has an **integral root** (an assignment of integers to its variables making it evaluate to 0; e.g., 6x³yz² + 3xy² − x³ − 10 has the integral root x=5, y=3, z=0). Hilbert asked that the algorithm *be devised* — he assumed one existed.\n\nThere is none — but proving **no algorithm exists** is impossible with only an intuitive notion of algorithm; the intuition suffices for exhibiting algorithms, never for ruling them all out. The precise definition arrived in the **1936 papers of Alonzo Church (λ-calculus) and Alan Turing (his machines)**, which were shown equivalent.\n\n> **The Church–Turing thesis:** the intuitive notion of algorithm **equals** Turing-machine algorithms. (Figure 3.22.)\n\nWith the definition in hand, **Matijasevič (1970)**, building on Davis, Putnam, and Robinson, proved no algorithm decides D = {p | p is a polynomial with an integral root}: **D is undecidable**. But D **is Turing-recognizable**: try all integer assignments in some enumeration; accept if one evaluates to 0 (loop forever otherwise).\n\nOne-variable warm-up D1 = {p over x with an integral root}: the recognizer tries x = 0, 1, −1, 2, −2, … . This one **can** be upgraded to a decider, because every root satisfies **|x0| < (n+1)·c_max / |c1|** (k terms/degree bound, c_max = largest |coefficient|, c1 = leading coefficient — Problem 3.21), so the search can be cut off. Matijasevič's theorem says no such bound is computable in the multivariable case — that's exactly what separates D1 (decidable) from D (undecidable).\n\n### The three levels of TM description — USE THESE WORDS ON EXAMS\n\n1. **Formal description** — the full 7-tuple: every state, the whole transition function/state diagram. Lowest level.\n2. **Implementation description** — English prose about **how the head moves and how data is stored on the tape** (\"mark the leftmost symbol\", \"zig-zag comparing\"), but no states or δ. (Examples 3.7–3.12 are at this level.)\n3. **High-level description** — English prose for the **algorithm itself**, ignoring tape and head entirely (\"select a node and mark it\"). From Ch. 4 on, this is the default — the exercise is to know which level a question wants.\n\n### Encoding conventions\n\nTM input is always a **string**; any other object O (polynomial, graph, grammar, automaton, tuples of these) is fed in as an encoding **⟨O⟩**, several objects as **⟨O1,…,Ok⟩**. The particular encoding scheme never matters, since a TM can convert between reasonable encodings. Format convention: algorithms are quoted, indented, broken into stages; input line \"On input w\" means a plain string, while \"On input ⟨A⟩\" means the machine **first implicitly checks the input is a well-formed encoding and rejects if not**.\n\n**Example 3.23 — connectedness.** A = {⟨G⟩ | G is a connected undirected graph}. High-level decider M: (1) mark the first node of G; (2) repeat until no new marks: (3) mark every node attached by an edge to an already-marked node; (4) accept iff all nodes end up marked. The book then drills down to implementation level: ⟨G⟩ = list of nodes (distinct decimal numbers) then list of edges (pairs); the input check uses M4's element-distinctness routine for \"no repeated nodes\" and a similar check that edge endpoints appear in the node list; \"marking\" = dotting a digit, and the stage-2 search underlines a candidate undotted node n1 and a dotted node n2 and scans the edge list for {n1, n2}.\n\n---\n\n## Solved problems from the book\n\n*(Answer-key exercises marked A in the text; condensed from the Selected Solutions, pp. 190–191.)*\n\n**3.1(b) — run M2 (Example 3.7) on input 00.** Configuration sequence:\nq1 00 → ␣ q2 0 → ␣x q3 ␣ → ␣ q5 x ␣ → q5 ␣x␣ → ␣ q2 x ␣ → ␣x q2 ␣ → ␣x␣ q_accept. (Two 0s: cross off one, count is even, sweep back, second pass sees a single 0 → accept.)\n\n**3.2(a) — run M1 (Example 3.9) on input 11.** q1 11 → x q3 1 → x1 q3 ␣ → x1␣ q_reject. (No # is ever found, so the machine rejects.)\n\n**3.3 — decidable ⟺ some NTM decides it (Corollary 3.19).**\n(⇒) A deterministic decider is automatically a nondeterministic one.\n(⇐) Let NTM decider N decide L; modify Theorem 3.16's simulator D: renumber old stage 4 as stage 5 and insert a new stage 4 — **reject once all branches of N have rejected**. Call the result D′. If N accepts, D′ finds an accepting branch and accepts. If N rejects, every branch halts and rejects (N is a decider), so every branch is finite; by the given tree fact (finitely many children per node + finite branches ⟹ finite tree), N's whole computation tree is **finite**, so D′ finishes exploring it and rejects. Hence D′ always halts: a decider for L.\n\n**3.5 — probing Definition 3.3.**\n(a) Can a TM write ␣? **Yes** — ␣ ∈ Γ, and the machine may write any symbol of Γ.\n(b) Can Γ = Σ? **No** — ␣ ∈ Γ always, ␣ ∉ Σ ever, so they can't be equal.\n(c) Can the head be in the same place in two successive steps? **Yes** — attempt a left move at the left end; the head stays put. (Only there — otherwise it must move.)\n(d) Can a TM have a single state? **No** — q_accept ≠ q_reject forces at least two states.\n\n**3.8(a) — implementation-level decider for {w | equal number of 0s and 1s}.** On input w: (1) scan and mark the first unmarked 0; if none, go to (4); else return the head to the front. (2) scan and mark the first unmarked 1; if none, **reject**. (3) return to the front; go to (1). (4) return to the front and scan for unmarked 1s: none → **accept**, some → **reject**. (Pairs off 0s with 1s one at a time.)\n\n**3.10 — write-once TMs are equivalent to ordinary TMs.** Step 1, **write-twice** version: simulate one step of the original machine by recopying the entire used tape onto fresh tape to its right, character by character, marking each character as it's copied (write once, mark once = two writes per cell); the simulated head position is marked on the tape, and the cells at/next to it are updated per the original δ during the copy. Step 2, down to **write-once**: represent each original cell by **two cells** — one for the symbol, one reserved for the copying mark — so no cell is written twice. (First copy pass is special: the input arrives one-cell-per-symbol, so its copy-marks go directly on the input symbols.)\n\n**3.15(a) — decidable languages are closed under union.** Given deciders M1, M2 for L1, L2, build decider M′: on w, run M1 on w — accept if it accepts; else run M2 on w — accept if it accepts, otherwise reject. Sequential running is fine **because deciders always halt**.\n\n**3.16(a) — Turing-recognizable languages are closed under union.** Given recognizers M1, M2, build M′: on w, **run M1 and M2 alternately, step by step**; accept if either accepts; reject if both halt and reject. The interleaving is essential: run sequentially, M1 could loop and starve M2. (If both fail to accept and one loops, M′ loops — fine for a recognizer.) Compare with 3.15(a): decidable closure runs machines in sequence, recognizable closure must run them in parallel.\n\n**3.22 — the Mars language.** A = the one-string language {0} (if life will never be found on Mars) or {1} (if it will). **A is decidable either way**: both candidates are finite languages, and every finite language is decidable. We just can't say *which* two-state decider is the right one — decidability requires that a decider **exist**, not that we can identify it. (A favorite trick question: nonconstructive existence still counts.)\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Sipser's Definition 3.3: a Turing machine is a 7-tuple — name all seven components and the type of δ.","a":"(Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q0, q_accept, q_reject): states Q; input alphabet Σ with ␣ ∉ Σ; tape alphabet Γ with ␣ ∈ Γ and Σ ⊆ Γ; transition function δ : Q × Γ → Q × Γ × {L, R}; start state q0; accept state q_accept; reject state q_reject ≠ q_accept."},{"q":"Three ways Sipser's base TM differs from M&S's base TM.","a":"Sipser: single tape, one-way infinite (left moves at the left end leave the head in place), and no stay-put option — the head must move L or R each step. M&S: k tapes, doubly infinite, stay-put (N) allowed. Equivalent in power by the variants theorems."},{"q":"What does the configuration notation \"u q v\" mean, and what is the start configuration on input w?","a":"Current state q, tape contents uv (blanks beyond), head on the first symbol of v. Start configuration: q0 w."},{"q":"State the \"yields\" rules for one step.","a":"If δ(qᵢ, b) = (qⱼ, c, L): ua qᵢ bv yields u qⱼ acv. If δ(qᵢ, b) = (qⱼ, c, R): ua qᵢ bv yields uac qⱼ v. Left edge: qᵢ bv yields qⱼ cv (L, head pinned) or c qⱼ v (R). Right edge: treat ua qᵢ as ua qᵢ ␣."},{"q":"Turing-recognizable vs. Turing-decidable — definitions, and the three possible outcomes of a TM on an input.","a":"Outcomes: accept, reject, or loop. L is Turing-recognizable (= recursively enumerable) if some TM recognizes it — non-members may be rejected or looped on. A decider halts on every input; L is decidable (= recursive) if some decider recognizes it. Every decidable language is recognizable."},{"q":"Strategy of M2 deciding {0^(2^n) | n ≥ 0}.","a":"Sweep left to right crossing off every other 0, halving the count each pass. One 0 left → accept; an odd count > 1 on any pass → reject; else return to the left end and sweep again."},{"q":"Two techniques a single-tape TM can use to find the left-hand end of its tape.","a":"(1) Mark the leftmost cell with a special symbol at the start. (2) Write a special symbol, try to move left: still reading that symbol ⟹ the move failed ⟹ at the left end (then restore the original symbol)."},{"q":"How does a single-tape TM S simulate a k-tape TM M (Theorem 3.13)?","a":"Store all k tape contents on one tape separated by #s; mark each virtual head position with a dotted symbol. Per step of M: one scan reads the k dotted symbols, a second pass updates them per δ. A virtual head hitting a # (fresh blank cell needed) → write ␣ and shift everything to the right of it one cell right."},{"q":"In the NTM ⇒ DTM simulation (Theorem 3.16), why is depth-first search wrong, and what do the three tapes of D hold?","a":"DFS can descend one infinite branch forever and miss an accepting configuration elsewhere; BFS visits every node of the computation tree. Tape 1: the input (never altered). Tape 2: N's tape on the branch being replayed. Tape 3: the branch's address — a string over {1,…,b} (b = max choices) picking one child per step, replaced by the next string in string order after each branch."},{"q":"What is an enumerator, and what is Theorem 3.21?","a":"A TM with an attached printer; started on a blank tape, it prints strings (any order, repetitions allowed); its language = everything it ever prints. Theorem 3.21: L is Turing-recognizable iff some enumerator enumerates L."},{"q":"In proving recognizer ⇒ enumerator, why can't E simply run M on s1, then s2, … and print the accepted ones?","a":"M might loop on some sᵢ, so E would never reach later strings. Fix: dovetail — for i = 1, 2, 3, …, run M for i steps on each of s1, …, sᵢ and print whatever accepts within those steps; every accepted string eventually appears."},{"q":"State the Church–Turing thesis and its role in Hilbert's tenth problem.","a":"Thesis: the intuitive notion of algorithm equals Turing-machine algorithms (Church's λ-calculus and Turing's machines, 1936, provably equivalent). Hilbert's tenth asked for an algorithm deciding whether a polynomial has an integral root; only with the precise definition could Matijasevič (1970, after Davis–Putnam–Robinson) prove none exists. D = {p | p has an integral root} is undecidable yet Turing-recognizable (try all integer assignments)."},{"q":"Why is the single-variable version D1 decidable when D is not?","a":"Roots of a one-variable polynomial are bounded: |x0| < (n+1)·c_max/|c1|, so a decider searches only finitely many integers and rejects past the bound. Matijasevič's theorem shows no computable bound exists for multivariable polynomials."},{"q":"Name and characterize Sipser's three levels of Turing-machine description.","a":"Formal description: complete 7-tuple, all states and transitions. Implementation description: prose about head movement and tape data layout, no states/δ. High-level description: prose for the algorithm alone, ignoring tape and head. Exam answers should name and match the requested level."},{"q":"The Mars problem (3.22): A = {0} if life will never be found on Mars, {1} if it will. Decidable?","a":"Yes. Whichever it is, A is a finite language, and every finite language is decidable — some two-line decider exists even though we can't tell which one. Decidability asks for existence of a decider, not our ability to exhibit it.\n\n---\n\n*Companion file: study/book/B4-turing-machines.md (M&S ch. 4). Next Sipser chapter: decidability (pairs with B5).*"}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"s4","title":"Sipser 4–5 · Decidability & Reducibility","slides":"Sipser ch. 4–5 · weeks 12–13","md":"# S4 — Decidability & Reducibility (Sipser, Ch. 4–5)\n\n**Sipser companion · course weeks 12–13 (estimate)** — pairs with Book 5 (M&S); In-Class Quiz 2 territory. Sipser's reducibility chapter has no M&S counterpart — read this one fully.\n\nWhat Sipser adds over M&S Ch. 5:\n- The **named-language zoo** (A_DFA, E_DFA, EQ_DFA, A_CFG, E_CFG, A_TM, HALT_TM, E_TM, REGULAR_TM, EQ_TM, A_LBA, E_LBA, ALL_CFG, PCP). Professors love these names — learn each one's definition AND status (decidable / undecidable / not even recognizable).\n- **Mapping reducibility ≤m** as a formal tool, with clean direction rules (§5.3 here).\n- The **computation-history method** for reductions to \"does something exist?\" problems (LBA emptiness, ALL_CFG, PCP).\n- Convention: ⟨X⟩ = string encoding of object X; ⟨X, Y⟩ = encoding of a pair.\n\n---\n\n## Ch. 4 §1 — Decidable languages (the zoo, decidable wing)\n\nSipser's move: express each decision *problem* as a *language* of encodings; \"the problem is decidable\" ⟺ \"the language is decidable.\"\n\n### Regular-language problems\n\n**Thm 4.1 — A_DFA = {⟨B, w⟩ : B is a DFA that accepts w} is decidable.**\nDecider M: check the input encodes a DFA + string (else reject); simulate B on w by tracking B's current state and input position on the tape; accept iff simulation ends in an accept state. Always halts because a DFA reads w in |w| steps.\n\n**Thm 4.2 — A_NFA = {⟨B, w⟩ : B is an NFA accepting w} is decidable.**\nDecider N: convert NFA B to an equivalent DFA C (subset construction, Thm 1.39 — an algorithm), then run the A_DFA decider M on ⟨C, w⟩. Illustrates Sipser's key habit: **use earlier deciders as subroutines**.\n\n**Thm 4.3 — A_REX = {⟨R, w⟩ : regex R generates w} is decidable.**\nConvert R to an NFA (Thm 1.54), then call the A_NFA decider. Moral: DFA / NFA / regex encodings are interchangeable for decidability — the TM can convert between them.\n\n**Thm 4.4 — E_DFA = {⟨A⟩ : A is a DFA, L(A) = ∅} is decidable.**\nL(A) ≠ ∅ iff some accept state is reachable from the start state. Decider T: **marking algorithm** — mark the start state; repeatedly mark any state with an incoming transition from a marked state until stable; accept iff no accept state got marked.\n\n**Thm 4.5 — EQ_DFA = {⟨A, B⟩ : DFAs with L(A) = L(B)} is decidable.**\nBuild DFA C for the **symmetric difference** L(C) = (L(A) ∩ L(B)̄) ∪ (L(A)̄ ∩ L(B)) using the regular-closure constructions (complement, intersection, union — all algorithmic). Then L(A) = L(B) ⟺ L(C) = ∅, so run the E_DFA decider on ⟨C⟩. Pattern to remember: **equivalence reduces to emptiness of symmetric difference**.\n\n### Context-free problems\n\n**Thm 4.7 — A_CFG = {⟨G, w⟩ : CFG G generates w} is decidable.**\nNaive \"try all derivations\" only gives a recognizer (infinitely many derivations if w ∉ L(G)). Fix: convert G to **Chomsky normal form**; then any derivation of w with |w| = n takes exactly **2n − 1 steps** (1 step if n = 0). Check the finitely many derivations of that length. (Same trick as M&S Thm 5.1.4. Inefficient but a decider; CYK in Ch. 7 does it efficiently.)\n\n**Thm 4.8 — E_CFG = {⟨G⟩ : CFG G, L(G) = ∅} is decidable.**\nCan't test all w's. Instead a marking algorithm on the grammar, dual to Thm 4.4: mark all terminals; repeatedly mark any variable A having a rule A → U1…Uk with every Ui already marked; accept iff the **start variable never gets marked** (i.e., it cannot derive a terminal string).\n\n**EQ_CFG = {⟨G, H⟩ : L(G) = L(H)} is NOT decidable.** The symmetric-difference trick fails because CFLs are **not closed under intersection or complement**. (Proved undecidable via Thm 5.13 → Exercise 5.1.)\n\n**Thm 4.9 — Every context-free language is decidable.**\nBad idea: convert a PDA for A into a TM — nondeterministic stack branches may run forever, so no decider. Good idea: hardwire a CFG G for A and, on input w, run the A_CFG decider S on ⟨G, w⟩.\n\n**Figure 4.10 hierarchy:** regular ⊂ context-free ⊂ decidable ⊂ Turing-recognizable (each containment proper).\n\n---\n\n## Ch. 4 §2 — Undecidability: A_TM and diagonalization\n\n**Thm 4.11 — A_TM = {⟨M, w⟩ : M is a TM and M accepts w} is undecidable.**\nBut A_TM **is Turing-recognizable**: the **universal TM** U simulates M on w and accepts/rejects if M does — U loops when M loops, which is exactly why it's a recognizer, not a decider. So recognizers are strictly more powerful than deciders.\n\n### The diagonalization method (Cantor)\n\n- **Def 4.12.** f : A → B is *one-to-one* (injective) if distinct inputs map to distinct outputs; *onto* (surjective) if every b ∈ B is hit; a **correspondence** (bijection) if both. A and B are the **same size** if a correspondence exists.\n- **Def 4.14.** A set is **countable** if it is finite or the same size as N.\n- Ex 4.13: N and the even naturals E are the same size via f(n) = 2n (proper subset, same size — that's fine for infinite sets).\n- Ex 4.15: **Q⁺ is countable** — arrange m/n in an infinite grid (row = numerator, column = denominator) and list along the diagonals, skipping repeats.\n\n**Thm 4.17 — R is uncountable** (the diagonalization argument itself).\nAssume a correspondence f : N → R. Build x ∈ (0,1): make the nth fractional digit of x differ from the nth fractional digit of f(n) (avoid digits 0 and 9 to dodge the 0.1999… = 0.2000… ambiguity). Then x ≠ f(n) for every n — f isn't onto. Contradiction.\n\n**Cor 4.18 — Some languages are not Turing-recognizable** (a pure counting argument):\n- The set of all TMs is **countable**: Σ* is countable (list by length), each TM has a string encoding ⟨M⟩, drop illegal encodings.\n- The set of all languages is **uncountable**: each language A ↔ its **characteristic sequence** χ_A ∈ {0,1}^∞ (bit i = 1 iff s_i ∈ A, where Σ* = {s1, s2, …}); this is a correspondence with the set B of infinite binary sequences, and B is uncountable by diagonalization.\n- Uncountably many languages, countably many TMs ⟹ some language has no recognizer.\n\n### Proof of Thm 4.11 (the D-machine)\n\n1. Suppose TM H decides A_TM: H(⟨M, w⟩) = accept if M accepts w, reject otherwise.\n2. Build D: on input ⟨M⟩, run H on ⟨M, ⟨M⟩⟩ and **output the opposite**. So D accepts ⟨M⟩ ⟺ M does not accept ⟨M⟩.\n3. Run D on ⟨D⟩: D accepts ⟨D⟩ ⟺ D does not accept ⟨D⟩. Contradiction — neither D nor H can exist. ∎\n\nWhere's the diagonal? Table with rows M_i, columns ⟨M_j⟩, entries = H's answer on ⟨M_i, ⟨M_j⟩⟩. D computes the **opposite of the diagonal entries**; since D must itself appear as some row, the (D, ⟨D⟩) entry must be the opposite of itself (Figs 4.19–4.21).\n\n### A Turing-unrecognizable language\n\n**Def.** A language is **co-Turing-recognizable** if its complement is Turing-recognizable.\n\n**Thm 4.22 — A is decidable ⟺ A is Turing-recognizable AND co-Turing-recognizable.**\n(⇒) Decidable ⟹ recognizable, and decidable languages are closed under complement.\n(⇐) Run recognizer M1 for A and recognizer M2 for Ā **in parallel** (two tapes, alternate steps). Every w is in A or Ā, so one of them eventually accepts; accept if M1 does, reject if M2 does. Always halts ⟹ decider.\n\n**Cor 4.23 — Ā_TM (complement of A_TM) is not Turing-recognizable.** A_TM is recognizable; if Ā_TM were too, A_TM would be decidable, contradicting 4.11. **First example of a non-recognizable language.**\n\n---\n\n## Ch. 5 — Reducibility\n\n**The concept.** A reduction converts problem A into problem B so that a solver for B yields a solver for A (\"finding your way around a city reduces to obtaining a map\"). If A reduces to B:\n- B decidable ⟹ A decidable;\n- equivalently (contrapositive), **A undecidable ⟹ B undecidable** — the workhorse for undecidability proofs.\nStandard proof shape: assume a decider R for the new problem B, use R to build a decider S for A_TM (or E_TM), contradiction.\n\n### §5.1 The undecidable zoo\n\n**Thm 5.1 — HALT_TM = {⟨M, w⟩ : TM M halts on w} is undecidable** (the halting problem).\nReduce A_TM to HALT_TM. Assume R decides HALT_TM; build S deciding A_TM: on ⟨M, w⟩, run R on ⟨M, w⟩; if R rejects (M loops), reject; if R accepts, simulate M on w — now safe, it must halt — and answer as M does. S would decide A_TM ⟹ contradiction.\n\n**Thm 5.2 — E_TM = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) = ∅} is undecidable.**\nRunning the assumed E_TM-decider R on ⟨M⟩ alone isn't enough (nonempty tells you M accepts *something*, not w). Trick: build the **filter machine** M1 = \"on input x: if x ≠ w, reject; if x = w, run M on w and accept if M does.\" Then L(M1) ≠ ∅ ⟺ M accepts w. S runs R on ⟨M1⟩ and **inverts** the answer. (Note the inversion — this is why A_TM ≤m Ē_TM but NOT A_TM ≤m E_TM; see Ex 5.27.)\n\n**Thm 5.3 — REGULAR_TM = {⟨M⟩ : L(M) is regular} is undecidable.**\nBuild M2: \"on input x: if x has the form 0ⁿ1ⁿ, accept; otherwise run M on w and accept if M accepts.\" Then L(M2) = Σ* (regular) if M accepts w, and L(M2) = {0ⁿ1ⁿ} (nonregular) if not. Feed ⟨M2⟩ to the assumed decider; its answer decides A_TM. **Key confusion to avoid:** M2 is never actually run — it's constructed only so its *description* can be fed to the decider. Similar proofs work for \"is L(M) context-free / decidable / finite\"; the general statement is **Rice's theorem** (Problem 5.28, solved below).\n\n**Thm 5.4 — EQ_TM = {⟨M1, M2⟩ : L(M1) = L(M2)} is undecidable.**\nReduction **from E_TM** (not A_TM — a deliberate example of reducing from another undecidable language): E_TM is the special case of EQ_TM where one machine is fixed to reject everything. S on ⟨M⟩ runs the assumed EQ_TM-decider on ⟨M, M_rej⟩ (M_rej rejects all inputs) and copies the answer.\n\n### Reductions via computation histories\n\n**Def 5.5.** An **accepting computation history** for M on w is the sequence of configurations C1, …, Cl where C1 is the start configuration q0w, each C_{i+1} follows legally from C_i, and Cl is accepting. (Rejecting history: Cl rejecting.) Finite sequences; exists iff M halts on w with that outcome; deterministic M has at most one. Use this method when the target problem tests **existence of some object** — make that object the accepting computation history.\n\n**Def 5.6 — Linear bounded automaton (LBA):** a TM whose head may not move off the input portion of the tape (memory linear in n, up to the constant factor from a larger tape alphabet).\n\n**Lemma 5.8.** An LBA with q states, g tape symbols, tape length n has exactly **q · n · gⁿ** distinct configurations (state × head position × tape contents).\n\n**Thm 5.9 — A_LBA is decidable** (contrast with A_TM!). Simulate M on w for q·n·gⁿ steps; if it hasn't halted, it repeated a configuration ⟹ it loops forever ⟹ reject. Loop detection is possible precisely because the configuration space is finite.\n\n**Thm 5.10 — E_LBA is undecidable.** Reduce A_TM: from ⟨M, w⟩ construct an LBA B that accepts exactly the strings x that are accepting computation histories for M on w (written #C1#C2#…#Cl#). B checks: (1) C1 = q0w, (2) each C_{i+1} legally follows C_i — verified by **zig-zagging** between corresponding positions of adjacent configurations, marking positions with dots, (3) Cl contains q_accept. Then L(B) ≠ ∅ ⟺ M accepts w; invert the assumed E_LBA-decider's answer. (An LBA suffices — checking a history needs no extra space.)\n\n**Thm 5.13 — ALL_CFG = {⟨G⟩ : CFG G, L(G) = Σ*} is undecidable.**\nFrom ⟨M, w⟩ build CFG G (actually build a PDA D, convert via Thm 2.20) generating all strings that **fail** to be an accepting computation history for M on w: D nondeterministically checks one of (1) doesn't start with C1, (2) doesn't end in an accepting configuration, (3) some C_i doesn't properly yield C_{i+1} (push C_i, pop against C_{i+1}). Twist: the stack pops in reverse, so **every other configuration is written reversed** in the history format. Then L(G) = Σ* ⟺ M does not accept w. This is the main step toward EQ_CFG undecidable (compare G with a grammar for Σ*).\n\n### §5.2 PCP (brief)\n\n**Post Correspondence Problem:** given a collection of dominos [t_i / b_i] (top/bottom strings), does some sequence of dominos (repeats allowed) make the top concatenation equal the bottom concatenation (a **match**)? PCP = {⟨P⟩ : instance P has a match}.\n\n**Thm 5.15 — PCP is undecidable.** Reduction from A_TM via computation histories: dominos are engineered so that any match is forced to spell out an accepting computation history of M on w (top string lags one configuration behind the bottom; transition dominos like [qa / br] for δ(q,a) = (r,b,R) force correct single steps; copy dominos [a/a] and [#/#] carry unchanged cells; q_accept dominos let the top \"catch up\" by eating symbols). Technically: first build a **Modified PCP** instance (match must start with domino 1), then convert MPCP → PCP with the ⋆-interleaving trick (stars force the designated first domino). So A_TM ≤m MPCP ≤m PCP.\n\n---\n\n## §5.3 Mapping reducibility (THE formalism — memorize this)\n\n**Def 5.17 — computable function.** f : Σ* → Σ* is computable if some TM, on every input w, halts with exactly f(w) on its tape. (Arithmetic is computable; so are transformations of machine descriptions, e.g. ⟨M⟩ ↦ ⟨M′⟩.)\n\n**Def 5.20 — mapping reducibility.** A ≤m B (\"A is mapping reducible to B\"; a.k.a. many–one reducibility) if there is a computable f with, for every w:\n  **w ∈ A ⟺ f(w) ∈ B.**\nf is called *the reduction*. To test w ∈ A: compute f(w), test f(w) ∈ B. Read \"A ≤m B\" as \"A is no harder than B.\"\n\n### The direction rules (exam gold)\n\n| Rule | Statement | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Thm 5.22 | A ≤m B and B decidable ⟹ **A decidable** | inherit easiness downward |\n| Cor 5.23 | A ≤m B and A undecidable ⟹ **B undecidable** | prove undecidability |\n| Thm 5.28 | A ≤m B and B recognizable ⟹ **A recognizable** | inherit recognizability |\n| Cor 5.29 | A ≤m B and A NOT recognizable ⟹ **B NOT recognizable** | prove non-recognizability (typically A = Ā_TM) |\n\nProof of 5.22: decider N for A = \"compute f(w), run B's decider on f(w), copy its answer.\" (5.28: same with recognizers.)\n\nFacts that ride along:\n- **Complement symmetry:** A ≤m B ⟺ Ā ≤m B̄ (same f!). So to show B not recognizable, show Ā_TM ≤m B, equivalently A_TM ≤m B̄.\n- **Transitivity:** A ≤m B and B ≤m C ⟹ A ≤m C (compose the reductions — Ex 5.6 below).\n- ≤m is **not symmetric** in general, and the direction of the ⟺ in the definition must hold both ways — a common exam mistake is only checking one direction.\n\n### Worked mapping reductions (Examples 5.24–5.27)\n\n- **Ex 5.24, A_TM ≤m HALT_TM:** f(⟨M, w⟩) = ⟨M′, w⟩ where M′ = \"on x: run M on x; if M accepts, accept; if M rejects, **loop**.\" M′ halts on w ⟺ M accepts w. (Convention: malformed inputs map to some fixed string outside B.)\n- **Ex 5.25:** A_TM ≤m MPCP ≤m PCP, so A_TM ≤m PCP by transitivity.\n- **Ex 5.26, E_TM ≤m EQ_TM:** f(⟨M⟩) = ⟨M, M_rej⟩.\n- **Ex 5.27, the subtle one:** the Thm 5.2 construction gives f(⟨M, w⟩) = ⟨M1⟩ with M accepts w ⟺ L(M1) ≠ ∅ — that is **A_TM ≤m Ē_TM**, NOT A_TM ≤m E_TM (the decider's answer was inverted). Still proves E_TM undecidable (decidability is complement-blind), but mapping reducibility is **complement-sensitive**, and in fact A_TM ≤m E_TM is impossible (Ex 5.5 below).\n\n**Thm 5.30 — EQ_TM is neither Turing-recognizable nor co-Turing-recognizable.** (First problem in the book with BOTH sides non-recognizable.)\n- Not recognizable: show A_TM ≤m E̅Q̅_TM, i.e. Ā_TM ≤m EQ_TM... concretely f(⟨M, w⟩) = ⟨M1, M2⟩ with M1 = \"reject everything\", M2 = \"on any input, run M on w; accept if it accepts.\" M accepts w ⟹ L(M2) = Σ* ≠ ∅ = L(M1) (not equivalent); M doesn't ⟹ both empty (equivalent). So f shows A_TM ≤m E̅Q̅_TM, hence Ā_TM ≤m EQ_TM; apply Cor 5.29.\n- Not co-recognizable: g identical except M1 = \"accept everything.\" Now M accepts w ⟺ L(M1) = L(M2) = Σ*, so g : A_TM ≤m EQ_TM, hence Ā_TM ≤m E̅Q̅_TM; apply Cor 5.29 to the complement.\n- The only change between f and g is M1's fixed behavior — a classic exam question.\n\n### Status scoreboard (know cold)\n\n| Language | Status |\n|---|---|\n| A_DFA, A_NFA, A_REX, E_DFA, EQ_DFA, A_CFG, E_CFG, every CFL, A_LBA | decidable |\n| A_TM, HALT_TM | undecidable, but Turing-recognizable |\n| E_TM | undecidable; co-recognizable (Ē_TM is recognizable, Ex 4.5), not recognizable |\n| REGULAR_TM, EQ_CFG, ALL_CFG, E_LBA, PCP, MPCP | undecidable |\n| Ā_TM | not Turing-recognizable (Cor 4.23) |\n| EQ_TM | neither recognizable nor co-recognizable (Thm 5.30) |\n\n---\n\n## Solved problems from the book\n\n(Faithful summaries of Sipser's own \"Selected Solutions\", condensed.)\n\n**Ex 4.1** (given a specific DFA M): ⟨M, 0100⟩ ∈ A_DFA — yes, M accepts 0100. ⟨M, 011⟩ — no, M doesn't accept it. ⟨M⟩ ∈ A_DFA — no: **wrong format** (only one component). ⟨M, 0100⟩ ∈ A_REX — no: first component isn't a regex, wrong format. ⟨M⟩ ∈ E_DFA — no, L(M) ≠ ∅. ⟨M, M⟩ ∈ EQ_DFA — yes, any DFA equals itself. *Lesson: format-checking questions are fair game.*\n\n**Ex 4.5 — Ē_TM (complement of E_TM) is Turing-recognizable.**\nRecognizer, on ⟨M⟩: for i = 1, 2, 3, …, run M for i steps on each of s1, …, s_i (the first i strings of Σ*); if M has accepted any of them, accept; otherwise continue. This **dovetailing** finds an accepted string iff L(M) ≠ ∅. (Memorize dovetailing — it recurs everywhere.)\n\n**Ex 4.6:** f with f(1) = f(3) = 6 is not one-to-one; the table's g (values 10, 9, 8, 7, 6) is one-to-one.\n\n**Prob 4.10 — INFINITE_DFA = {⟨A⟩ : DFA A, L(A) infinite} is decidable.**\nLet k = number of states of A. Build DFA D accepting all strings of length ≥ k; build M with L(M) = L(A) ∩ L(D); test L(M) = ∅ with the E_DFA decider; answer \"infinite\" iff nonempty. Correctness via pumping: A accepts some string of length ≥ k ⟺ it accepts infinitely many.\n\n**Prob 4.12 — A = {⟨M⟩ : DFA M accepts no string with an odd number of 1s} is decidable.**\nBuild DFA O accepting exactly the odd-number-of-1s strings; build B with L(B) = L(M) ∩ L(O); accept iff the E_DFA decider says L(B) = ∅.\n\n**Prob 4.14 — {⟨G⟩ : CFG G over {0,1}, 1* ∩ L(G) ≠ ∅} is decidable.**\nCFL ∩ regular is context-free (Prob 2.18), so construct CFG H with L(H) = 1* ∩ L(G); run the E_CFG decider on ⟨H⟩ and invert its answer.\n\n**Prob 4.23 — AMBIG_NFA = {⟨N⟩ : NFA N accepts some string along two different branches} is decidable.**\nSouped-up subset construction with **pebbles**: red pebble = state reached along one branch, blue = reached along ≥ 2 branches (merge red+red → blue; colors propagate along transitions). DFA D (3ⁿ states: each NFA state has red / blue / no pebble) accepts iff a blue pebble sits on an accept state or two accept states hold red pebbles — i.e., iff the input is ambiguously accepted. Then test L(D) = ∅ with the E_DFA decider.\n\n**Prob 4.25 — BAL_DFA = {⟨M⟩ : DFA M accepts some string with equally many 0s and 1s} is decidable.**\nThe balanced-strings language is a CFL (grammar S → 1S0S | 0S1S | ε), with PDA P. Intersect P with the DFA B (CFL ∩ regular is a CFL, effective construction) to get PDA R; accept iff L(R) ≠ ∅ (E_CFG decider on the equivalent grammar).\n\n**Ex 5.5 — A_TM is NOT mapping reducible to E_TM.**\nSuppose A_TM ≤m E_TM via f. The same f gives Ā_TM ≤m Ē_TM. But Ē_TM is recognizable (Ex 4.5), so Thm 5.28 would make Ā_TM recognizable — contradicting Cor 4.23. *This is THE example of ≤m's complement-sensitivity.*\n\n**Ex 5.6 — ≤m is transitive.** From f (A to B) and g (B to C), h(x) = g(f(x)) is computable (run f's TM, then g's TM on the output) and x ∈ A ⟺ f(x) ∈ B ⟺ g(f(x)) ∈ C.\n\n**Ex 5.7 — If A is Turing-recognizable and A ≤m Ā, then A is decidable.**\nA ≤m Ā gives (same reduction) Ā ≤m A; A recognizable + Thm 5.28 ⟹ Ā recognizable; recognizable + co-recognizable + Thm 4.22 ⟹ decidable.\n\n**Ex 5.8** (PCP without the \"never moves off the left end\" assumption): add dominos [#qa / #rb] for each left-moving transition δ(q,a) = (r,b,L) (head bounces at the wall), and change the start domino's bottom to ##q0w1…wn so a first-move bounce is also covered.\n\n**Prob 5.10 — B = {⟨M, w⟩ : two-tape TM M writes a nonblank on tape 2 when run on w} is undecidable.**\nAssume decider R. From ⟨M, w⟩ build two-tape T: \"on x: simulate M on x on tape 1; if M accepts, write a nonblank on tape 2.\" Run R on ⟨T, w⟩: T marks tape 2 on w ⟺ M accepts w — decides A_TM, contradiction.\n\n**Prob 5.11** (same but \"on **some** input\"): build T_w that **ignores its input**, simulates M on w on tape 1, and marks tape 2 iff M accepts. Run the assumed decider on ⟨T_w⟩. The ignore-your-input trick is the standard move for \"on any/some input\" problems.\n\n**Prob 5.28 — Rice's theorem.** Let P be a set of TM descriptions that is (i) **nontrivial** — some TM description is in P and some isn't — and (ii) a **property of the language**: L(M1) = L(M2) ⟹ (⟨M1⟩ ∈ P ⟺ ⟨M2⟩ ∈ P). Then P is undecidable.\nProof: assume decider R_P. Let T_∅ reject everything; WLOG ⟨T_∅⟩ ∉ P (else work with P̄). By nontriviality pick T with ⟨T⟩ ∈ P. From ⟨M, w⟩ build M_w = \"on x: simulate M on w; if it rejects, reject; if it accepts, simulate T on x and answer as T does.\" Then L(M_w) = L(T) if M accepts w, and ∅ otherwise; so ⟨M_w⟩ ∈ P ⟺ M accepts w, and R_P would decide A_TM. ∎\n*Instant corollaries (Prob 5.30):* INFINITE_TM, {⟨M⟩ : 1011 ∈ L(M)}, ALL_TM all undecidable — just check nontrivial + language-property. (Both conditions are necessary: 5.29.)\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"Define A_DFA, and state its status with the one-line reason.","a":"A_DFA = {⟨B, w⟩ : B is a DFA that accepts w}. Decidable — simulate B on w; a DFA always halts after |w| steps (Thm 4.1)."},{"q":"How do the deciders for A_NFA and A_REX work, and what's the moral?","a":"Convert: NFA → DFA (subset construction) then call the A_DFA decider; regex → NFA then call the A_NFA decider. Moral: for decidability, DFA/NFA/regex encodings are interchangeable, and deciders can be used as subroutines (Thms 4.2–4.3)."},{"q":"How is E_DFA decided, and how does EQ_DFA reduce to it?","a":"E_DFA: mark states reachable from the start state (marking algorithm); L(A) = ∅ iff no accept state is marked. EQ_DFA: build the symmetric-difference DFA C — L(C) = (L(A) ∩ L(B)̄) ∪ (L(A)̄ ∩ L(B)); then L(A) = L(B) ⟺ L(C) = ∅, so run the E_DFA decider (Thms 4.4–4.5)."},{"q":"Why is A_CFG decidable even though \"try all derivations\" fails, and why can't the EQ_DFA trick prove EQ_CFG decidable?","a":"Convert to Chomsky normal form; a string of length n then has derivations of exactly 2n − 1 steps — finitely many to check (Thm 4.7). The symmetric-difference trick needs closure under complement and intersection, which CFLs lack; EQ_CFG is in fact undecidable."},{"q":"State the four-class hierarchy sealed by Thm 4.9.","a":"regular ⊂ context-free ⊂ decidable ⊂ Turing-recognizable; Thm 4.9 (every CFL is decidable, by hardwiring a grammar into the A_CFG decider) supplies the CFL ⊂ decidable link."},{"q":"Define A_TM. Is it recognizable? Decidable?","a":"A_TM = {⟨M, w⟩ : TM M accepts w}. Recognizable — the universal TM U simulates M on w (loops if M loops). Undecidable (Thm 4.11) — so recognizers are strictly stronger than deciders."},{"q":"Sketch the diagonalization proof that A_TM is undecidable.","a":"Assume decider H for A_TM. Build D: on ⟨M⟩, run H on ⟨M, ⟨M⟩⟩ and output the opposite. Then D accepts ⟨D⟩ ⟺ D doesn't accept ⟨D⟩ — contradiction. D negates the diagonal of the table \"does M_i accept ⟨M_j⟩\", and D is itself a row."},{"q":"Why must non-Turing-recognizable languages exist (counting argument)?","a":"TMs are countable (each has a string encoding, Σ* is countable) but languages are uncountable (language ↔ characteristic sequence, and infinite binary sequences are uncountable by diagonalization). More languages than TMs (Cor 4.18)."},{"q":"State Theorem 4.22 and its corollary about Ā_TM.","a":"A is decidable ⟺ A is Turing-recognizable and co-Turing-recognizable (run recognizers for A and Ā in parallel; one must accept). Corollary 4.23: Ā_TM is not Turing-recognizable — A_TM is recognizable and undecidable, so its complement can't also be recognizable."},{"q":"State the two ≤m direction rules for decidability.","a":"If A ≤m B and B is decidable, then A is decidable (Thm 5.22). Contrapositive form (Cor 5.23): if A ≤m B and A is undecidable, then B is undecidable. Reduce the KNOWN-hard language TO the new one; \"A ≤m B\" = \"A is no harder than B.\""},{"q":"Define A ≤m B precisely.","a":"There is a computable function f : Σ* → Σ* (some TM halts with f(w) on its tape for every w) such that for every w: w ∈ A ⟺ f(w) ∈ B (Defs 5.17, 5.20). Both directions of the iff are required."},{"q":"State the ≤m rules for recognizability, and the standard way to prove a language B is not recognizable.","a":"A ≤m B and B recognizable ⟹ A recognizable (Thm 5.28); so if A ≤m B and A is NOT recognizable, B isn't either (Cor 5.29). Standard move: show Ā_TM ≤m B — equivalently A_TM ≤m B̄ (a reduction f for A ≤m B is also one for Ā ≤m B̄)."},{"q":"Give the mapping reduction A_TM ≤m HALT_TM.","a":"f(⟨M, w⟩) = ⟨M′, w⟩ where M′ = \"on x: run M on x; accept if M accepts; if M rejects, loop forever.\" Then M accepts w ⟺ M′ halts on w. So HALT_TM is undecidable by Cor 5.23 (Thm 5.1 / Ex 5.24)."},{"q":"In the E_TM and REGULAR_TM proofs, what machine is constructed from ⟨M, w⟩, and is it ever run?","a":"E_TM: filter machine M1 that rejects everything except w and accepts w iff M does (L(M1) ≠ ∅ ⟺ M accepts w). REGULAR_TM: M2 that accepts {0ⁿ1ⁿ} always, plus everything else iff M accepts w (L(M2) regular ⟺ M accepts w). Never run — only their descriptions are fed to the assumed decider (Thms 5.2–5.3)."},{"q":"Why is A_TM ≤m Ē_TM true but A_TM ≤m E_TM false?","a":"The Thm 5.2 reduction satisfies M accepts w ⟺ L(M1) ≠ ∅, i.e. maps into the complement of E_TM (the decider's answer gets inverted). If A_TM ≤m E_TM held, the same f would give Ā_TM ≤m Ē_TM; Ē_TM is recognizable (dovetailing, Ex 4.5), so Thm 5.28 would make Ā_TM recognizable — contradicting Cor 4.23 (Exs 5.27, 5.5). ≤m is complement-sensitive even though decidability isn't."},{"q":"What is the computation-history method and when do you use it?","a":"An accepting computation history is the configuration sequence #C1#…#Cl# of M accepting w (C1 = q0w, legal steps, Cl accepting). For problems that test existence of an object, build a machine/grammar whose accepted/generated objects are exactly (or exactly not) these histories: E_LBA (LBA B accepts only the history; L(B) ≠ ∅ ⟺ M accepts w), ALL_CFG (G generates all strings except the history — with alternate configurations reversed for stack comparison), and PCP (dominos force a match to spell out the history)."},{"q":"A_LBA vs E_LBA — statuses and reasons.","a":"A_LBA is decidable: an LBA on input length n has only q·n·gⁿ configurations (Lemma 5.8), so simulate that many steps — no halt means a repeated configuration, i.e., an infinite loop; reject (Thm 5.9). E_LBA is undecidable via computation histories (Thm 5.10). Acceptance easy, emptiness hard — opposite feel to the DFA world where both are easy."},{"q":"Thm 5.30: what is EQ_TM's exact status and what are the two reductions?","a":"EQ_TM is neither Turing-recognizable nor co-Turing-recognizable. Both reductions map ⟨M, w⟩ to ⟨M1, M2⟩ with M2 = \"ignore input, run M on w, accept if it accepts.\" For non-recognizability, M1 rejects everything (machines equivalent ⟺ M does NOT accept w, giving Ā_TM ≤m EQ_TM). For non-co-recognizability, M1 accepts everything (equivalent ⟺ M accepts w, giving A_TM ≤m EQ_TM, i.e. Ā_TM ≤m E̅Q̅_TM). Only M1 changes between the two."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]},{"id":"s5","title":"Sipser 7 · Time Complexity & NP","slides":"Sipser ch. 7 · weeks 14–15","md":"# S5 — Time Complexity (Sipser, Chapter 7)\n\n**Sipser companion · course weeks 14–15 (estimate)** — pairs with Book 6 (M&S); final-exam (35%) territory. Note: Sipser chapters 6, 8, 9, 10 (advanced computability, space complexity, intractability, advanced complexity) are beyond this course's likely scope — skim only if the professor signals otherwise.\n\nSource: Sipser, *Introduction to the Theory of Computation*, 3rd ed., Ch. 7 (pp. 275–330, PDF pages 299–354).\n\n**What Sipser adds over M&S Ch. 6 (B6-complexity.md):**\n1. The **verifier/certificate definition of NP** (Def. 7.18–7.19) and the proof it equals the NTM definition (Thm. 7.20) — M&S goes straight to NTMs.\n2. The **Cook–Levin tableau proof** in full (φ_cell ∧ φ_start ∧ φ_move ∧ φ_accept over a tableau of configurations, 2×3 legal windows).\n3. The **standard gadget reductions**: 3SAT→CLIQUE, 3SAT→VERTEX-COVER, 3SAT→HAMPATH, HAMPATH→UHAMPATH, 3SAT→SUBSET-SUM.\n4. Worked exercises with official solutions (below).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Measuring complexity (§7.1)\n\n**Definition 7.1 (running time).** For a deterministic TM M that halts on all inputs, the running time is f : ℕ → ℕ where f(n) = the **maximum** number of steps M uses on **any** input of length n (worst-case analysis; n = input length). M \"runs in time f(n)\".\n\n**Definition 7.2 (big-O).** For f, g : ℕ → ℝ⁺: f(n) = O(g(n)) if positive integers c and n₀ exist such that for every n ≥ n₀, **f(n) ≤ c·g(n)**. g is an *asymptotic upper bound* for f (\"≤ up to a constant factor\").\n- Log bases don't matter inside O: log_b n = log₂ n / log₂ b, so O(log n) needs no base.\n- 2^{O(n)} means ≤ 2^{cn} for some c; 2^{O(log n)} = n^{O(1)} = polynomial. Bounds nᶜ (c > 0) are **polynomial**; 2^{(n^δ)} (δ > 0) are **exponential**.\n\n**Definition 7.5 (small-o).** f(n) = o(g(n)) if lim_{n→∞} f(n)/g(n) = 0; equivalently, for **every** c > 0 there is n₀ with f(n) < c·g(n) for all n ≥ n₀. Big-O is \"≤\", small-o is \"<\" (strictly asymptotically smaller). f(n) is never o(f(n)). Chain worth memorizing: √n = o(n), n = o(n log log n), n log log n = o(n log n), n log n = o(n²), n² = o(n³).\n\n**Definition 7.7 (time class).** TIME(t(n)) = { L | L is decidable by an **O(t(n))-time** (deterministic, single-tape) TM }.\n\n**Running example A = {0ᵏ1ᵏ | k ≥ 0}** — model-dependence in miniature:\n- M₁ (cross off one 0 and one 1 per pass): O(n²) → A ∈ TIME(n²).\n- M₂ (parity trick: check total parity, then cross off every other 0 and every other 1 — the parities read off the binary representation of the count backwards): **O(n log n)**. This is optimal for 1-tape TMs: any language decidable in o(n log n) time on a single-tape TM is **regular** (Problem 7.49).\n- M₃ (two-tape: copy 0s to tape 2, match against 1s): **O(n)** — linear, obviously optimal.\nSo the time complexity of A depends on the model — unlike computability (Church–Turing), complexity is **model-sensitive**, but only polynomially so for reasonable deterministic models.\n\n**Theorem 7.8 (multitape → single-tape).** Let t(n) ≥ n. Every t(n)-time multitape TM has an equivalent **O(t²(n))**-time single-tape TM. *Proof idea:* the Thm. 3.13 simulation stores all k tapes on one tape; the active portion has length O(t(n)), and each of M's t(n) steps costs two scans + up to k shifts = O(t(n)); total t(n)·O(t(n)) = O(t²(n)). Only a **squaring** (polynomial) overhead.\n\n**Definition 7.9 (NTM running time).** For a nondeterministic decider N: f(n) = the maximum number of steps N uses **on any branch** on any input of length n.\n\n**Theorem 7.11 (NTM → DTM).** Let t(n) ≥ n. Every t(n)-time nondeterministic single-tape TM has an equivalent **2^{O(t(n))}**-time deterministic single-tape TM. *Proof idea:* the computation tree has depth ≤ t(n) and branching ≤ b, so ≤ b^{t(n)} leaves; deterministic breadth-first search costs O(t(n)·b^{t(n)}) = 2^{O(t(n))}; converting the 3-tape simulator to 1 tape only squares this (still 2^{O(t(n))}). — An **exponential** overhead.\n\n**The moral (sets up P vs NP):** deterministic model changes cost at most a *polynomial* (square); determinizing nondeterminism costs (as far as we know) an *exponential*.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The class P (§7.2)\n\n**Definition 7.12.** **P** = ∪_k TIME(nᵏ) = languages decidable in polynomial time on a deterministic single-tape TM. P matters because it is (1) **invariant** across all reasonable deterministic models (they are polynomially equivalent), and (2) roughly the class of problems **realistically solvable** on a computer. Exponential algorithms typically come from **brute-force search** of a solution space; being in P means brute force was avoided by some insight.\n\n**Encoding convention:** ⟨·⟩ must be a *reasonable* encoding — polynomial-time convertible to natural representations. **Unary encoding of numbers is NOT reasonable** (exponentially longer than binary/base-k, k ≥ 2). For graphs, size-of-encoding is polynomial in #nodes, so analyzing in terms of #nodes is fine.\n\n**Theorem 7.14 — PATH ∈ P.** PATH = {⟨G,s,t⟩ | directed graph G has a directed path s→t}. Brute force (all ~mᵐ potential paths) is exponential; instead do breadth-first search: mark s, repeatedly mark any b with an edge (a,b) from a marked a, ≤ m rounds; accept iff t marked. Polynomially many polynomial stages.\n\n**Theorem 7.15 — RELPRIME ∈ P.** RELPRIME = {⟨x,y⟩ | gcd(x,y) = 1}, x, y in **binary**. Trying all divisors is exponential in the input length. The **Euclidean algorithm** E (repeat: x ← x mod y; swap x,y; until y = 0) runs in O(n) stages: every second execution of \"x ← x mod y\" at least **halves** x (case x/2 ≥ y: mod value < y ≤ x/2; case x/2 < y: x mod y = x − y < x/2), so ≤ 2log₂x iterations ~ length of input.\n\n**Theorem 7.16 — every context-free language is in P** (Sipser's dynamic-programming / CYK argument; M&S states CFL ⊆ P too, but Sipser gives the table algorithm). Trying all 2n−1-step CNF derivations is exponential. Instead: for G in Chomsky normal form, build an n×n table where table(i,j) = the set of variables that generate substring w_i…w_j.\n- Length-1 substrings: put A in table(i,i) iff rule A → w_i exists.\n- Length l = 2…n: for each start i (j = i+l−1), each split point k, each rule A → BC: if B ∈ table(i,k) and C ∈ table(k+1,j), add A to table(i,j).\n- Accept iff S ∈ table(1,n) (w = ε handled separately by checking rule S → ε).\nTriple loop over (l, i, k) × constantly many rules ⇒ **O(n³)** stages, each polynomial. This is the flagship example of **dynamic programming**: solve every subproblem once, record it, reuse it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The class NP (§7.3)\n\n**HAMPATH** = {⟨G,s,t⟩ | directed G has a Hamiltonian path from s to t} (a path visiting **every node exactly once**). No polynomial algorithm is known — but the problem is **polynomially verifiable**: given a claimed path, checking it is easy. Same for **COMPOSITES** = {x | x = pq for integers p,q > 1}: a divisor is easy to check. By contrast, the complement HAMPATH has no obvious short evidence of membership.\n\n**Definition 7.18 (verifier) — Sipser's signature definition, not in M&S.**\n> A **verifier** for a language A is an algorithm V where\n> **A = { w | V accepts ⟨w, c⟩ for some string c }.**\n> V's running time is measured **only in terms of |w|**; a polynomial-time verifier runs in time polynomial in |w|. A is **polynomially verifiable** if it has a polynomial-time verifier.\n\nThe extra string c is the **certificate** (or **proof**) of membership. For a polynomial-time verifier the certificate automatically has polynomial length (V can't read more within its time bound). Certificates: for HAMPATH, the Hamiltonian path itself; for COMPOSITES, a nontrivial divisor.\n\n**Definition 7.19.** **NP** = the class of languages that have **polynomial-time verifiers**. (Name from \"**n**ondeterministic **p**olynomial time\" — the equivalent NTM characterization.)\n\n**Theorem 7.20 (the two definitions coincide).** A ∈ NP ⟺ A is decided by some nondeterministic polynomial-time TM.\n- (verifier ⇒ NTM) N on input w: nondeterministically **guess** a certificate c of length ≤ nᵏ, run V on ⟨w,c⟩, accept iff V accepts.\n- (NTM ⇒ verifier) V on ⟨w,c⟩: simulate N on w treating the symbols of c as the **script of nondeterministic choices** to make at each step; accept iff that branch accepts. (The accepting branch *is* the certificate.)\n\n**Definition 7.21.** NTIME(t(n)) = { L | L decided by an O(t(n))-time NTM }. **Corollary 7.22:** NP = ∪_k NTIME(nᵏ).\n\n**Slogan to remember:**\n> **P = membership can be *decided* quickly. NP = membership can be *verified* quickly.**\n\n**More NP examples (Thms. 7.24, 7.25):**\n- **CLIQUE** = {⟨G,k⟩ | undirected G has a k-clique (k nodes all pairwise connected)} ∈ NP — certificate: the clique; verifier checks c is k nodes of G and all connecting edges present. NTM version: guess a k-subset, check edges.\n- **SUBSET-SUM** = {⟨S,t⟩ | S = {x₁,…,x_k} (a multiset) has a subcollection summing to t} ∈ NP — certificate: the subset. E.g., ⟨{4,11,16,21,27}, 25⟩ ∈ SUBSET-SUM since 4+21 = 25.\n- Complements: CLIQUE-bar and SUBSET-SUM-bar are *not obviously* in NP (verifying absence seems harder). **coNP** = { complements of NP languages }; whether NP = coNP is open.\n\n**P vs NP (Fig. 7.26).** P ⊆ NP. Either P = NP or P ⊊ NP — nobody knows which; it's the central open problem. Best known deterministic bound: **NP ⊆ EXPTIME = ∪_k TIME(2^{nᵏ})**. Most researchers believe P ≠ NP because enormous effort has failed to find polynomial algorithms for problems like HAMPATH/CLIQUE; but no NP language has been *proved* outside P.\n\n---\n\n## 4. NP-completeness (§7.4)\n\n**SAT** = {⟨φ⟩ | φ is a satisfiable Boolean formula} (some 0/1 assignment makes φ evaluate to 1). **Theorem 7.27:** SAT ∈ P ⟺ P = NP.\n\n**Definition 7.28.** f : Σ* → Σ* is a **polynomial-time computable function** if some poly-time TM halts with exactly f(w) on its tape on every input w.\n\n**Definition 7.29 (polynomial-time reduction).** A is **polynomial time (mapping) reducible** to B, written **A ≤_P B**, if a polynomial-time computable f exists with\n> **w ∈ A ⟺ f(w) ∈ B** for every w.\n(Elsewhere: \"polynomial-time many–one reducibility\". The efficient analogue of mapping reducibility ≤_m from Ch. 5.)\n\n**Theorem 7.31.** If A ≤_P B and B ∈ P, then A ∈ P. (Compute f(w), run B's decider; composition of polynomials is polynomial.) **Direction discipline:** to show a *new* problem C hard, reduce **known-hard → C**; to show C easy, reduce **C → known-easy**.\n\n**3SAT:** literal = variable or negated variable; clause = OR of literals; **cnf-formula** = AND of clauses; **3cnf** = exactly three literals per clause. 3SAT = {⟨φ⟩ | φ a satisfiable 3cnf-formula}. A cnf-formula is satisfied iff **every clause has ≥ 1 true literal**.\n\n**Theorem 7.32 — 3SAT ≤_P CLIQUE (the model gadget reduction).** From φ with k clauses build ⟨G, k⟩:\n- One **triple** of nodes per clause, one node per literal occurrence (labeled with its literal).\n- Edges between **all** pairs of nodes **except**: (i) nodes in the same triple, (ii) nodes with contradictory labels (x and x̄).\n- φ satisfiable ⟺ G has a k-clique. (⇒) pick one true literal per clause; those k nodes are pairwise compatible, hence a clique. (⇐) a k-clique has exactly one node per triple (same-triple nodes aren't adjacent) and no contradictory pair; setting those literals true satisfies every clause.\nGadget vocabulary: individual nodes simulate **variables/literals**; triples simulate **clauses**; clique size k forces one chosen literal per clause.\n\n**Definition 7.34.** B is **NP-complete** if (1) **B ∈ NP**, and (2) **every A ∈ NP satisfies A ≤_P B**. (Condition (2) alone = \"NP-hard\".)\n- **Theorem 7.35:** if B is NP-complete and B ∈ P, then P = NP.\n- **Theorem 7.36 (the workhorse):** if B is NP-complete, B ≤_P C, and **C ∈ NP**, then C is NP-complete. (Reductions compose.) So after one NP-complete problem exists, new ones need only: membership in NP + one reduction *from* a known NP-complete problem.\n\n### Cook–Levin theorem (Theorem 7.37): SAT is NP-complete\n\n**Proof idea.** SAT ∈ NP: guess an assignment, check it. Hardness: for **any** A ∈ NP with NTM decider N running in time nᵏ, build in polynomial time a formula φ that \"simulates N on w\": φ is satisfiable ⟺ N has an accepting computation on w ⟺ w ∈ A. Boolean AND/OR/NOT are the basis of computer circuitry, so encoding a TM in a formula is plausible; the work is in the details.\n\n**The tableau.** A **tableau** for N on w is an **nᵏ × nᵏ table** whose rows are configurations of one branch of N's computation: row 1 = start configuration of N on w; each row follows from the previous by N's transition function; every row starts and ends with #. The tableau is **accepting** if any row is an accepting configuration. N accepts w ⟺ an accepting tableau exists.\n\n**Variables.** C = Q ∪ Γ ∪ {#}. For each cell (i,j) and symbol s ∈ C, a Boolean variable **x_{i,j,s}**, meaning \"cell[i,j] contains s\". φ = **φ_cell ∧ φ_start ∧ φ_move ∧ φ_accept**:\n- **φ_cell**: for every cell, *at least one* symbol variable is on (big OR) AND *no two* are on simultaneously (AND over pairs of (x̄_{i,j,s} ∨ x̄_{i,j,t})). ⇒ every satisfying assignment describes exactly one symbol per cell, i.e., an actual table.\n- **φ_start**: a conjunction of literals forcing row 1 to be exactly the start configuration # q₀ w₁…w_n ␣…␣ #.\n- **φ_accept**: big OR of x_{i,j,q_accept} over all cells — q_accept appears somewhere.\n- **φ_move**: every **2×3 window** of cells is **legal** — i.e., consistent with N's transition function (a window is legal if it *could* appear when one configuration correctly follows another). Written as an AND over all window positions of an OR over all legal 6-tuples (a₁,…,a₆) of the AND of the six corresponding variables.\n\n**Claim 7.41:** if row 1 is the start configuration and *every* 2×3 window is legal, then each row legally follows the previous one. (Cells away from the head sit at the center of a state-free window, so they persist; the window around the state symbol enforces the transition.) The 2×3 size is essential — 2×2 windows would not suffice (Problem 7.41).\n\n**Polynomiality:** the tableau has n^{2k} cells; each of φ's parts has size O(n^{2k}) (φ_start only O(nᵏ)), and the formula is highly repetitive, so f(w) = φ is computable in polynomial time. ∎\n\n**Corollary 7.42 — 3SAT is NP-complete.** The Cook–Levin formula is nearly cnf already (φ_cell is an AND of clauses; φ_start an AND of unit clauses; φ_accept one big clause; φ_move becomes cnf by distributing OR over AND — constant-factor blowup since window size depends only on N). Then normalize to exactly 3 literals per clause: pad short clauses by **repeating a literal**; split a long clause (a₁ ∨ … ∨ a_l) into l−2 clauses **(a₁∨a₂∨z₁) ∧ (z̄₁∨a₃∨z₂) ∧ … ∧ (z̄_{l−3}∨a_{l−1}∨a_l)** with fresh variables z_i — satisfiability is preserved both ways.\n\n**Corollary 7.43:** CLIQUE is NP-complete (Thm. 7.32 + Thm. 7.36).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Additional NP-complete problems (§7.5)\n\nEmpirically, most natural NP problems turn out to be either in P or NP-complete. Standard strategy: show membership in NP, then reduce **from 3SAT** (or another convenient NP-complete problem), building **gadgets** — structures that simulate variables (two states ↔ true/false) and clauses (force ≥ 1 true literal).\n\n**Theorem 7.44 — VERTEX-COVER is NP-complete.** VERTEX-COVER = {⟨G,k⟩ | undirected G has a k-node set touching every edge}. In NP (certificate = the cover). Reduction 3SAT ≤_P VERTEX-COVER, φ with m variables, l clauses:\n- **Variable gadget:** an edge between two nodes labeled x and x̄ — any cover must pick one endpoint; picked node = the true literal.\n- **Clause gadget:** a triangle of three nodes labeled with the clause's literals, each also joined to the identically labeled variable-gadget node. A cover needs ≥ 2 triangle nodes; the third triangle edge to the variable gadgets is covered \"for free\" only if some variable-gadget node in the cover matches a literal of the clause (i.e., the clause is satisfied).\n- Total: 2m + 3l nodes, set **k = m + 2l**. φ satisfiable ⟺ cover of size k exists (one node per variable edge + two per triangle, exactly exhausting the budget).\n\n**Theorem 7.46 — HAMPATH is NP-complete** (3SAT ≤_P HAMPATH; directed). φ with l variables, k clauses:\n- **Variable gadget:** a **diamond** per variable x_i containing a horizontal row of 3k+1 nodes (a pair per clause + separators), traversable **zig-zag (left→right) = TRUE** or **zag-zig (right→left) = FALSE** — two truth values, two traversal directions.\n- **Clause gadget:** a **single node** c_j per clause. If x_i ∈ c_j, add a detour edge pair from the jth pair of diamond i out to c_j and back oriented for a **left-to-right** detour; if x̄_i ∈ c_j, orient the pair for a **right-to-left** detour.\n- Diamonds are chained top to bottom from s to t. A satisfying assignment yields a Hamiltonian path: traverse each diamond in the direction of its truth value, detouring once into each clause node via one true literal. Conversely any Hamiltonian path must be **normal** (a detour that returned to a *different* diamond would strand a separator/pair node — the a₂ argument of Fig. 7.54), so it reads off an assignment; each clause node visited ⇒ each clause satisfied.\n\n**Theorem 7.55 — UHAMPATH (undirected version) is NP-complete**, by HAMPATH ≤_P UHAMPATH (reduce from the directed problem, not 3SAT):\n- Replace each node u (except s, t) by a **triple** u^in — u^mid — u^out; s becomes s^out = s′, t becomes t^in = t′. Each directed edge (u,v) becomes the undirected edge {u^out, v^in}.\n- Directed Hamiltonian path s,u₁,…,t ↦ undirected path s^out, u₁^in, u₁^mid, u₁^out, u₂^in, …, t^in. Conversely, any undirected Hamiltonian path must pass through triples in in–mid–out order (u^mid's only neighbors are u^in, u^out, forcing the pattern), so it projects back to a directed Hamiltonian path. The degree-2 \"mid\" node is what enforces direction.\n\n**Theorem 7.56 — SUBSET-SUM is NP-complete** (3SAT ≤_P SUBSET-SUM). φ with variables x₁…x_l, clauses c₁…c_k. Build large **decimal** numbers (table of Fig. 7.57):\n- Per variable x_i, a pair **y_i, z_i**: both have a 1 in \"variable digit\" i; y_i has a 1 in clause digit c_j iff literal x_i ∈ c_j; z_i has a 1 in c_j iff x̄_i ∈ c_j.\n- Per clause c_j, a pair of **slack numbers g_j = h_j** = 1 in digit c_j only.\n- **Target t = 1 1 … 1 (l ones) 3 3 … 3 (k threes)**.\n- Each column has ≤ five 1s, so **no carries** — digits behave independently. Variable digit i sums to 1 ⟺ exactly one of y_i/z_i chosen (a truth assignment). Clause digit c_j must reach 3; slacks contribute ≤ 2, so ≥ 1 must come from a chosen literal number ⟺ clause c_j has a true literal. Satisfying assignments ⟺ subsets hitting t. Note this uses **binary/decimal magnitude**: with unary encoding the reduction's output would be exponentially long (see Problem 7.17 — UNARY-SSUM ∈ P).\n\n**Reduction map (all directions matter for the exam):**\n- Cook–Levin: every A ∈ NP ≤_P SAT ≤_P 3SAT.\n- 3SAT ≤_P CLIQUE, 3SAT ≤_P VERTEX-COVER, 3SAT ≤_P HAMPATH, 3SAT ≤_P SUBSET-SUM.\n- HAMPATH ≤_P UHAMPATH.\n\n---\n\n## Solved problems from the book\n\n*(Sipser's \"Selected Solutions\" for Ch. 7, transcribed; exercise statements paraphrased.)*\n\n**7.1 (c), (d)** — Big-O true/false. Is n² = O(n log² n)? **FALSE.** Is n log n = O(n²)? **TRUE.**\n\n**7.2 (c), (d)** — Small-o true/false. Is 2ⁿ = o(3ⁿ)? **TRUE.** Is 1 = o(n)? **TRUE.**\n\n**7.16 — NP is closed under star.** Let A ∈ NP. Construct NTM M deciding A* in nondeterministic polynomial time:\n> M = \"On input w:\n> 1. Nondeterministically divide w into pieces w = x₁x₂⋯x_k.\n> 2. For each x_i, nondeterministically guess the certificates that show x_i ∈ A.\n> 3. Verify all certificates if possible, then accept. Otherwise, if verification fails, reject.\"\n\n**7.23 — HALF-CLIQUE is NP-complete.** HALF-CLIQUE = {⟨G⟩ | G has a clique on ≥ m/2 of its m nodes}. Membership in NP: certificate is the clique. Hardness by CLIQUE ≤_P HALF-CLIQUE: on input ⟨G,k⟩ (G has m nodes) output ⟨H⟩ where\n- if k = m/2: H = G;\n- if k < m/2: add j = m − 2k new nodes connected to **every** node (original and each other); H has 2m − 2k nodes, and G has a k-clique iff H has a clique of size k + j = m − k = half of H's nodes;\n- if k > m/2: add j = 2k − m isolated nodes (no new edges); H has 2k nodes, and G has a k-clique iff H has a k-clique = half of H's nodes.\nIn every case ⟨G,k⟩ ∈ CLIQUE ⟺ ⟨H⟩ ∈ HALF-CLIQUE. *(Pattern: padding a graph to move the threshold to exactly half.)*\n\n**7.33 — SOLITAIRE is NP-complete.** (m×m board of blue/red stones; remove stones until each **column** is single-colored and each **row** still has ≥ 1 stone.) In NP: verify a proposed solution in polynomial time. Hardness by 3SAT ≤_P SOLITAIRE: given φ with variables x₁…x_m and clauses c₁…c_k (assume no clause contains both x_i and x̄_i — such clauses can be deleted), build a k × m board: **blue** stone at (row c_j, column x_i) if x_i ∈ c_j; **red** stone there if x̄_i ∈ c_j; pad to square harmlessly.\n(→) From a satisfying assignment: if x_i is true, remove the **red** stones in column i; if false, remove the **blue** ones. Stones of true literals remain; every clause has a true literal, so every row keeps a stone.\n(←) From a winning play: column i ended blue-only ⇒ set x_i true; red-only ⇒ false. Every row retains a stone ⇒ every clause has a true literal ⇒ φ satisfied.\n*(Pattern: columns = variables with a binary color choice; rows = clauses needing a survivor.)*\n\n**7.40 — If P = NP, a largest clique can be *found* (not just detected) in polynomial time.** Assuming P = NP, CLIQUE ∈ P, so test ⟨G,k⟩ for k = 1,…,m to learn the maximum clique size t in polynomial time. Then, for each node x of G in turn: delete x and recompute the maximum clique size; if it drops below t, put x back; if it stays t, keep x deleted permanently. After all nodes are processed, the remaining nodes form a t-clique. *(Pattern: a decision oracle bootstrapped into a search algorithm — same idea as Problems 7.38 on SAT assignments and 7.39 on factoring.)*\n\n---\n","lesson":"","cards":[{"q":"State Sipser's Definition 7.2 of f(n) = O(g(n)) precisely.","a":"Positive integers c, n₀ exist such that for every n ≥ n₀, f(n) ≤ c·g(n). g is an asymptotic upper bound for f (constant factors suppressed)."},{"q":"State the small-o definition (7.5) and its relation to big-O.","a":"f(n) = o(g(n)) iff lim_{n→∞} f(n)/g(n) = 0 (equivalently: for every c > 0 there is n₀ with f(n) < c·g(n) for all n ≥ n₀). Big-O is like ≤, small-o like strict <. f(n) is never o(f(n))."},{"q":"What are the costs of (a) simulating a t(n)-time multitape TM on a single tape (Thm. 7.8), and (b) simulating a t(n)-time NTM deterministically (Thm. 7.11)? Why does the contrast matter?","a":"(a) O(t²(n)) — each step costs a scan of the O(t(n)) active tape. (b) 2^{O(t(n))} — search the computation tree of ≤ b^{t(n)} branches. Contrast: deterministic model choice costs only a polynomial; nondeterminism (apparently) costs an exponential — this is the seed of P vs NP."},{"q":"Define TIME(t(n)), P, NTIME(t(n)), and NP as unions.","a":"TIME(t(n)) = languages decidable by an O(t(n))-time deterministic single-tape TM; P = ∪_k TIME(nᵏ); NTIME(t(n)) = same with nondeterministic TMs; NP = ∪_k NTIME(nᵏ)."},{"q":"State the verifier definition of NP (Defs. 7.18–7.19) exactly — including whose length the time bound is measured in.","a":"A verifier for A is an algorithm V with A = { w | V accepts ⟨w,c⟩ for some string c }. V's time is measured in |w| only; NP = languages with polynomial-time verifiers. c is the certificate (proof) of membership, automatically of polynomial length."},{"q":"Sketch both directions of Theorem 7.20 (verifier definition ⟺ poly-time NTM definition of NP).","a":"Verifier→NTM: nondeterministically guess a certificate c (|c| ≤ nᵏ) and run V on ⟨w,c⟩. NTM→verifier: on ⟨w,c⟩ simulate N on w using c as the sequence of nondeterministic choices; the accepting branch is the certificate."},{"q":"Give the certificates that put HAMPATH, COMPOSITES, CLIQUE, and SUBSET-SUM in NP.","a":"HAMPATH: the Hamiltonian path itself. COMPOSITES: a nontrivial divisor. CLIQUE: the k nodes of the clique. SUBSET-SUM: the subcollection summing to t. In each case the verifier checks the certificate in time polynomial in |w|."},{"q":"One-line slogans distinguishing P and NP, and the best known deterministic upper bound on NP.","a":"P = membership decidable quickly; NP = membership *verifiable* quickly. Known: NP ⊆ EXPTIME = ∪_k TIME(2^{nᵏ}); whether P = NP is open."},{"q":"Define A ≤_P B (Def. 7.29) and state Theorem 7.31. Which direction do you reduce to prove a new problem hard?","a":"A ≤_P B iff a polynomial-time computable f exists with w ∈ A ⟺ f(w) ∈ B for all w. Thm. 7.31: A ≤_P B and B ∈ P imply A ∈ P. To prove C hard: reduce known-NP-complete → C (never the reverse)."},{"q":"Define NP-complete (Def. 7.34) and state the two theorems (7.35, 7.36) that make the notion useful.","a":"B is NP-complete if B ∈ NP and every A ∈ NP has A ≤_P B. Thm. 7.35: NP-complete B ∈ P ⇒ P = NP. Thm. 7.36: B NP-complete, B ≤_P C, C ∈ NP ⇒ C NP-complete (reductions compose) — the standard recipe for new completeness proofs."},{"q":"In the Cook–Levin proof, what is a tableau and what are its dimensions for an nᵏ-time NTM N on w?","a":"An nᵏ × nᵏ table whose rows are the successive configurations of one computation branch of N on w, each row delimited by # at both ends, row 1 = start configuration; it is accepting if some row is an accepting configuration. N accepts w iff an accepting tableau exists."},{"q":"Name the four parts of the Cook–Levin formula φ and what each enforces.","a":"φ_cell: exactly one symbol variable x_{i,j,s} on per cell (assignment ↔ actual table); φ_start: row 1 is the start configuration of N on w; φ_move: every 2×3 window of cells is legal (consistent with N's transition function), forcing each row to follow the previous; φ_accept: some cell contains q_accept. φ = φ_cell ∧ φ_start ∧ φ_move ∧ φ_accept, of total size O(n^{2k})."},{"q":"Why do 2×3 windows suffice in Cook–Levin, and how are clauses of the wrong length fixed when converting to 3SAT (Cor. 7.42)?","a":"A cell not adjacent to the state sits atop a state-free window so must persist; the window covering the state enforces the transition — 2×2 windows would fail (Prob. 7.41). To 3cnf: repeat a literal in clauses with < 3 literals; split (a₁∨…∨a_l) into (a₁∨a₂∨z₁)∧(z̄₁∨a₃∨z₂)∧…∧(z̄_{l−3}∨a_{l−1}∨a_l) with fresh z's, preserving satisfiability."},{"q":"Describe the 3SAT → CLIQUE gadgets and the correctness equivalence.","a":"One triple of nodes per clause (nodes labeled by literals); edges join all pairs except same-triple pairs and contradictory pairs (x, x̄); ask for a k-clique, k = #clauses. Satisfying assignment → pick one true literal per triple = k-clique; k-clique → one node per triple, labels non-contradictory, set them true → every clause satisfied."},{"q":"Describe the 3SAT → VERTEX-COVER gadgets and the value of k.","a":"Variable gadget: edge x—x̄ (cover picks one = truth value). Clause gadget: triangle of the clause's three literals, each also joined to its identically labeled variable node. k = m + 2l (m variables, l clauses): one node per variable edge + two per triangle; the triangle's third connection is covered free only when a chosen variable node matches a literal, i.e., the clause is satisfied."},{"q":"In the 3SAT → HAMPATH reduction, what encodes a variable's truth value, and what encodes clause satisfaction?","a":"Each variable is a diamond with a horizontal row (3k+1 nodes: a pair per clause + separators); traversing zig-zag (left→right) = TRUE, zag-zig = FALSE. Each clause is a single node reachable by a detour from its pair — oriented left-to-right for literal x_i, right-to-left for x̄_i — so a detour is possible exactly when the traversal direction makes that literal true. Separator nodes force any Hamiltonian path to be \"normal\" (return to the same diamond)."},{"q":"How does the HAMPATH → UHAMPATH reduction remove edge directions?","a":"Replace each node u by a triple u^in — u^mid — u^out (s ↦ s^out, t ↦ t^in); each directed edge (u,v) becomes undirected {u^out, v^in}. Since u^mid has only u^in and u^out as neighbors, any undirected Hamiltonian path must traverse in→mid→out, recreating the direction; paths correspond exactly."},{"q":"In the 3SAT → SUBSET-SUM reduction, what do the numbers y_i/z_i and g_j/h_j encode, what is the target t, and why do carries never occur?","a":"y_i (resp. z_i) has a 1 in variable-digit i and 1s in the clause digits where literal x_i (resp. x̄_i) appears — choosing y_i or z_i sets x_i true or false; g_j = h_j are slack numbers with a lone 1 in clause digit j. Target t = l ones followed by k threes. Each column contains at most five 1s, so decimal addition never carries: digit i = 1 forces exactly one of y_i/z_i, and digit c_j = 3 needs ≥ 1 from a true literal (slacks give ≤ 2) — exactly \"every clause satisfied\". Crucially the numbers are in decimal/binary; in unary the instance would be exponentially large (UNARY-SSUM ∈ P)."}],"figs":[],"slideText":[]}],"deliverables":[]};
